import type { V205BookData } from '../types';

export const DATA: V205BookData = {
  book: "Genesis",
  display: "Genesis",
  chapters: {
    "1": {"chapter":1,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"The first five books of the Bible are, arguably, the most important books of the Bible. The gospels, of course, are perfect, and serve a far greater purpose, but without these opening books, we would not have a clue as to the needs of the gospel and why we would ever want to trust in the Lord or in Jesus Christ for salvation. This first book displays the lives and lineages of the most prominent characters of the faith starting with Adam and Eve. Just in this first book alone, we see the layout and purpose of the four gospels, and why Jesus had to come to this Earth and save us from our sins. Even furthermore, we see the ultimate purpose of the end of all things which we learn about in Revelation. A full circle will be made from beginning to end with humanity as we currently see it."},{"kind":"para","text":"This book is laid out pretty plainly, spiraling through generations from the beginning and ending. Starting with the generations of Heavens and Earth, then going to the generations of Adam, to the generations of Noah, to the sons of Noah, to the sons of Shem, to Torah, to Ismael, to Isaac, to Esau, to Jacob, and so on. Some have labeled this book of the Bible as “the Book of Families.” Though we will be reading on specific people and their faith, followed by som of their downfalls in the faith, we will also see a common thread, which will be proven throughout the generations that follow this book, even into the end times of Revelation. Even still, there are a lot of people in this world today who have tried to warp, or twist the meaning of this first book, and have tried to write their own versions or concepts of what actually happened in the beginning, copying what Moses started with in the Garden and going completely off course to match scripture with their religions, rather than religions with scriptures."},{"kind":"para","text":"Mormonism has taken the fall of man and has changed it into a redemption story specifically through their religious viewpoints, by twisting the happenings of Sodom and Gomorrah into a place in which it wasn’t. Not to mention the promises made by God to Adam, and to Abraham and his kids, to make Jesus out to be nothing more than a man who could obtain salvation because of His own goodness. Muslims have taken the same stance against this first book, sighting that the first people sinned, but then did good works to earn God’s favor again, giving way for the lack of necessity for the Savior to come and die for our sins. The stories of the Quran give off the notion that you can choose between doing good and doing evil. Some people choose evil, and therefore won’t be with God. Some people choose good and God accepts them. This is completely against scriptural evidence and it really falls apart when you point out that even good people do bad things (like stealing, lying, using God’s name, coveting after things that don’t belong to them (including but not limited to women), giving themselves over to multiple women at a time even, which is adultery, murder, and the list goes on."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will see the issues with these religions and their viewpoints as we read through this amazing historical event of the beginning of all things, and we will also see the necessity of the rest of scripture and the lineup for all the rest of the events to take place in the way that they did and the reason for the timing of all things. From Genesis 1 to Genesis 11 is about 2000 years (Possibly more). From Genesis 12 to Matthew 1 is about 2000 years, and from Matthew 1 to today is about 2000 years. This will help us kind of get the picture of God’s moving every 2000 or so years. He is ramping up for the next big event (which is more than likely the tribulation)."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"The Original Creation","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This single verse alone gives us all of the details we need about God. First, it proves that GOD created. He didn’t just dictate what needed to be done; He did it Himself. It also says “In the beginning,” which doesn’t insinuate God’s beginning. One of the biggest questions that atheists ask about the existence of God is, “If God created everything, then who created God?” That question is so childish because it doesn’t take into account the fact that if God was created by something, then God would cease to be God, and rather the one that created Him would be God. Because God doesn’t stand in our definition of time (like this first verse suggests), then God is an eternal being that created creation."},{"kind":"para","text":"Creation is a human concept; God invented it for us. This beginning isn’t speaking about God’s beginning; it is speaking about ours. The structure of time “in the beginning” was not meant for Him, but for us, which is why I believe that the Earth was completed in six literal days rather than thousands of years in between each day."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But, beloved, be not ignorant of this one thing, that one day is with the Lord as a thousand years, and a thousand years as one day.","ref":"2 Peter 3:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"When we allow this verse to dictate the beginnings of creation, we do two things wrong. Firstly, we allow the evidence of creation outlined here in the book of Genesis to be completely wrong and void of any proof or validity by calling Moses a liar in his opening statements. Secondly, we are calling God stupid because He doesn’t have the proper grasp of time in His creation, and therefore is late to every other circumstance in the pages to follow."},{"kind":"para","text":"God was with His people every single second of every single day. If a thousand years was a day to the Lord, Adam would have been one thousand years old by the time God got around to appropriating the next steps to create Eve. Or let’s take it a step further, when Abraham was walking with God and God spoke to him (which we will get into later in this book) it would have taken one thousand years for Abraham to receive his first child, let alone for him to take his first child and offer him as a sacrifice to the Lord. Abraham only lived to be one hundred and seventy-five years old. The math doesn’t add up. In fact, all the Bible’s writings wouldn’t historically add up if God was only able to talk to us every one thousand years..."},{"kind":"para","text":"Let’s take it a step further. If a day was one thousand years to the Lord, then our prayers would take one thousand years to be answered. Yet we see people’s prayers answered all the time right in front of them. This concept of “one thousand years is like a day” doesn’t mean that God has no grasp on time (I mean He invented it for crying out loud), it simply means He is able to be through all times, at once, and He has the patience to endure time because He knows the end and beginning of all things. He stands OUTSIDE of time. Time has no control over Him. That’s the key thing we have to remember when we view God, and if we are going to view Him in the proper light."},{"kind":"para","text":"God is above time and space. God also starts His creation before the first day by making the heaven and the earth void:"}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Because of the way in which verse two is structured, we have the notion to believe that either God created the Heaven and the Earth without form and void on purpose, leaving it in darkness (meaning His presence was not there, for the Lord is light), and He had still a purpose for building what was there, OR (as many scholars believe) there was some sort of catastrophe that took place between verses 1&2."},{"kind":"para","text":"Though not a surprise by God, the fact that the earth was without form and void gives credence to the idea that maybe God allowed a catastrophe to take place so He could start the Earth with something new. There are a lot of theories that can go into this (that I won’t get into), but I think it’s safe to say that the creation of the Earth that will be taking place in the next few verses will display the importance that God took to create a universe that is self-sustaining yet reliant upon Him."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":5,"title":"The First Day","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In the first day of creation, God starts with light. He spoke light into existence, and not only did He just speak it and it was so, He also allowed it by saying, “Let there be...” This indication suggests that all of the things in which we see here on Earth to this day prove that it was created and made available to us because of a divine authority who provided it and allowed it."},{"kind":"para","text":"The fact that the sun has not yet burned out, exploded, or gotten closer to the Earth gives acceptable proof that there is a divine creator holding it all together. Though, this scripture does not talk about the sun. This light is a self-sustaining light without a source. The light, here, was granted access to this Earth because of God’s authority to allow light in a dark and void place. Though, some scholars are under the impression that this is speaking about the sun specifically, and it won’t be in its proper place until a later day."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":8,"title":"The Second Day","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A “firmament” is an air space. It’s what gave separation between the water and the sky. God allows the sky to be divided from the water, and the water from the sky. When God divides the waters by the waters, it is speaking about the water being taken into the sky, and being above the water of the earth. Then He makes the water that was above the sky into the sky, and the water that was on the earth was kept as the water of the Earth."},{"kind":"para","text":"It was gathered together into the sky and is now a source of dew, rain, and providence. The scripture speaks of three heavens. When verse 8 speaks about the “heaven” it is speaking about the sky we see here. There is then the heaven above the sky (where the stars and planets dwell) and then there’s a third Heaven where God dwells. The Heaven spoken about here is the first layer, the one in which the waters (clouds or otherwise) are layered."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":13,"title":"The Third Day","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"On the third day, God created dry land, grass, and trees with all kinds of fruits. We need to take special note that Moses writes “after his kind” about every single part of multiplied creation. He speaks about this with fruits, stating that there is no way to cross-breed a banana with a potato, for instance. There’s no way to have a potatnana, nor would that be something that you would want. The trees themselves cannot be turned into grass and thrive. Grass could never become a tree. They have to stick and repopulate with their own kind. Now, once we get to the third day, the Earth seems pretty complete. We have light, with no sun. Water and sky, with no fish and birds. Dry land and greenery with no animals or man. This is where it becomes interesting, and why so many people are divided on the actuality of the biblical creation."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":19,"title":"The Fourth Day","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God is now finishing His creation with the fulfilling things that it needs from the last three days, and He starts by fulfilling day one on day four, day two on day five, and day three on day six. He starts by making two large lights that will give source to the light that was already there on Earth. Of course, these sources may have already been here since day one if scholars are correct, but they will not be positioned into their proper places to give off their proper purposes of light. One for day, and one for night. The moon itself not even giving off its own light! I believe the sources of these lights were created on this day. I think light was existing without a source before, and God gave that source right here on this day to COMPLETE His creation for it. That is what we will see in these six accounts. God is going to complete His creation."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":23,"title":"The Fifth Day","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Let the waters bring forth abundantly the moving creature that hath life...” is this an indication of evolution? Did the waters just create life? Did God just give that authority to water? Did the sky just produce birds? No... Of course not, the language here is that God is using those things that are already present to create those things to fill those spaces. The purpose of the sky is for the birds. The purpose of the water is for the fish. The purpose for the land is for the animals and man. All three of these things work together for one common purpose, and that’s for glorifying God."},{"kind":"para","text":"Also, notice that they are created to breed with their own kind. There is no ape-fish. No bird-fish. That is impossible to do, even if you forced it. This continues to give further credence to the falsities of evolution…"}]}]},{"start":24,"end":27,"title":"The Sixth Day","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here God makes animals and seems to finish off creation... But there was one thing missing that God knew needed to be on Earth. He could have finished it with animals. In fact, He could have made evolution legitimate, but that’s not what He wanted in His creation. He wanted people that would reflect His image, just like the Moon reflects the Sun."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God creates man here, and this was the most important part of his creation. He will be using and focusing on man for the remainder of this book. We will get glimpses and looks at the rest of His creation, but man was the thing that needed the most help. Actually, God will have an easy time using every other part of creation to help man get along. It will be man that the Lord will have a difficult time using."}]}]},{"start":28,"end":31,"title":"The Edenic Covenant","entries":[{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Multiplying was the main focus that God had for His creation. There are four main ways in which God proved creation and produced life:"},{"kind":"para","text":"1. By direct creation, which produced Adam. 2. By indirect creation, which produced Eve. 3. By virgin birth, and this was how Jesus came into the human family. 4. By natural generation, and that is what is common creation in our day."},{"kind":"para","text":"God made everything at this point and looked at it, understanding that everything that was made was “very good.” Not just “good” but “very” good. It was perfect. It couldn’t be better! But this will be the last time for a long time that the Earth and everything in it was perfect... And we see the start of the downfall of perfection right in the very next chapter…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":31}]},
    "2": {"chapter":2,"intro":[],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":null,"entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We can sometimes view the Sabbath in an improper light. There are many today who have the idea that when we (as mere human beings) keep the Sabbath holy (as the Lord commands in Exodus), we are not to do any work in that day that may subtract from the Lord’s work. It’s as if to say that cooking a meal, watching TV, spending time with family and friends, going to the park, taking a holiday getaway, or simply walking too far of a distance would be a sin on the Lord’s Day. They misunderstand the purpose and point of it."},{"kind":"para","text":"God does not need rest. In fact, in these verses here, it does not say that God rested because of His work on the seventh day. It says He rested, FROM His work. In other words, He was done with His work. There was no more work to be had. We are not supposed to take a break for the week because we are “done” with work. The idea of the Sabbath is to refocus your week to prepare you for more work. That’s the way to keep it holy. We are to go to church and learn how to take the message of the Lord into the world for the week."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he said unto them, The sabbath was made for man, and not man for the sabbath: Therefore the Son of man is Lord also of the sabbath.","ref":"Mark 2:27-28"}]}]},{"start":4,"end":6,"title":null,"entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are many who believe that there were billions of years present before the world actually started on these seven days because it starts with it being void and formless, with nothing but darkness ruling over it. If this is the case, I cannot claim truth to it, because I was not there, and I am not God. It is sufficient to say that the days in which God created everything give homage to the fact that God didn’t just leave the Earth in disorder and darkness. He obviously had a timing and a plan for this Earth that far surpasses even our understanding."},{"kind":"para","text":"See, when we form ideas or plans, we sometimes sit, think, and work out the details before even initiating the start of a plan. God doesn’t work that way though. He just knows. It’s hard to wrap our brains around, but everything was planned to a “t” within an instant. That’s how God works! He thought of everything before a single thing was created. This includes Heaven, angels, Satan, humans, and even animals! He has a purpose for it all, and it boggles my mind that He created everything, yet He stands outside of our concepts and thoughts of creation and motives. He also uses the term “generations” in this verse here. He gives the picture that these beginning days were the start of “families.” That’s what “generations” means, it means “families.”"}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Take this into consideration when thinking about the creation of all things. We know that God created light. He creates the sun, moon, and stars. He creates fish, birds, and animals. He creates plant life, trees, and dry ground. He creates a sky that’s made out of the water of the Earth. But what’s it all for? Is it for Him to find a place to dwell? Is it an experiment for Him to see how life can grow and be without Him? No. All the things we see that are created were created with US in mind! He created all things for His pleasure, sure, but they were all created so we can have dominion and rule over them. He created US to be like Him in character, and to love life and love creation. These things exist for man! And that is where the Lord is leading us in the remainder of this book."},{"kind":"para","text":"The whole rest of the Bible, from this point forward, focuses on man. It will focus on the creation that was built around him, and for him, and then it will focus on the fall and sin of man, all leading to the redemption of man. The ultimate purpose of this story is to show God’s constant grace and mercy towards a people who is constantly working against Him."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":15,"title":null,"entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I LOVE this particular verse. There’s so much source here that we could dive into. Firstly, evolutionists claim we come from stardust. We certainly come from dust, but it’s not the dust they think it is. Honestly, if we were to take the idea that we are made from dust (not even dirt specifically, but the “dust” of the ground, which means we are made of just the leftover residue of falling particles) then we amount to nothing more than “space dust,” or leftover space residue. But God took that dust, molded it into a human being, and breathed literal LIFE into it, and man became a living soul."},{"kind":"para","text":"What’s even more fascinating to me, is if we did an exam of our bodies to see what we were truly made out of, even today throughout generations since Genesis started, we would find these elements (the four main elements) are: Oxygen, Carbon, Hydrogen, and Nitrogen. We also consist of smaller amounts of calcium, phosphorus, potassium, sodium, chlorine, magnesium, sulfur, and a few others. These are all elements that are literally found in the ground. With inflation, we are probably worth around the range of $8.50. My math is not precise, and I don’t want to pretend it is, but doing some basic addition and division, I was able to divide the cost per element into one percentage, and then add them together into total cost per percentage, and it rounds to about that much."},{"kind":"para","text":"Though, there are a lot of these elements that are worth more today, like Oxygen. With insurance, it would cost about $35 for a tank of oxygen. An oxygen TANK is worth more than a human’s body... That’s getting away from the overall purpose of this passage, however. It’s not about the value we are on the outside, because we obviously aren’t worth much. God puts extreme value on the “living soul” that He breathed into man. It’s the life that has real value. This is why it’s integral that Jesus died on the cross. He gave His life for mine, because my life (given by God) has the most value. It is worth more than all the elements this world can muster up."},{"kind":"para","text":"God could have made us with gold dust, or the dust of the finest diamonds. He could have given our physical bodies real value, and it still wouldn’t have been nearly as valuable as the soul He has placed into each and every one of us."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the life of the flesh is in the blood: and I have given it to you upon the altar to make an atonement for your souls: for it is the blood that maketh an atonement for the soul.","ref":"Leviticus 17:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"When Jesus shed His blood for us on the cross, He was not only demonstrating the value of our lives over His, but also demonstrating the real importance that God has placed on each and every one of us to live FOR Him. A living soul is worth an eternal value (or an everlasting, never-ending value). A body is only worth a few dollars. Even our caskets will be worth more than our physical bodies. This is why we must find the necessity to increase our knowledge of eternity, because that’s where the real value is placed. He could have just told us to breathe, but rather He breathed His own life into us. It’s His breath that we breathe, and that’s why it’s so valuable. We have the breath of God in our living souls, and when we accept Christ, He breathes new life into us again."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Garden of Eden, at this point, was the center of the Earth. Where that is, scholars disagree. I like this idea from one scholar saying that it could have been in the Tigris-Euphrates valley, and they come off of that conclusion because of some of the locational language that is used here (which we will see in a moment), and because it is known as the “Fertile Crescent.” Apparently, the land was so fertile (and still is) to the point that people wouldn’t even plant their own grain there; they simply just harvested it because it grew by itself! That’s pretty miraculous! Of course, once we see the fall of man, this garden disappears, and access to it is cut off. A good belief is that it now dwells with the Lord until the time of the New Jerusalem. More on that later."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is when we are first introduced to the tree of life. This same tree is found in the New Jerusalem. The tree of the knowledge of good and evil seems to disappear forever. It seemed to have served its purpose, but I’m getting ahead of myself. What I find interesting in this verse is that ALL the trees were “pleasant to the sight.” There wasn’t a single tree that looked bad to eat from. Honestly, Adam and Eve should have been content with the fruit they knew they could eat... but they weren’t..."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The river in Ethiopia would be the Nile, and the Hiddekel would be the Tigris, which is where the assumption that this garden was in the Tigris-Euphrates valley."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Man had the good life. He was not to grow the garden. He was not to work the garden, though his job was still to work in “dressing and keeping” the garden clean and tidy. It, honestly, was a simple job. There was no back aching, no leg cramping, no physical exertions yet. The first job on Earth was tending to a garden. This is a very important symbol of what the Lord will be doing for the rest of time. WE (that is, saved Christians) are a part of the Lord’s garden. He will always be working on us, and He will give reminders of that throughout the rest of Scripture. Everything was quite simple, and man was good for the work, knowing it was pleasing to God, and God only gave him one rule:"}]}]},{"start":16,"end":20,"title":null,"entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This tree of “the knowledge of good and evil” was only there for this one particular purpose. All we knew was good. We knew God, and God created everything good, but we didn’t understand what evil even meant. Adam didn’t really care to know, honestly. He was “commanded” to stay away from that one fruit. Maybe there was something in the back of his mind that wondered, “Why did God put it in the garden then if He doesn’t want me to eat from it? What is its purpose?” The purpose has yet to be revealed, and I won’t jump the gun quite yet."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is our first “not good” sentence from the Lord. Everything was good up until this point. God didn’t want man to be alone. But God also knew that the creation of woman would ultimately lead to mankind’s downfall. Not because women, in themselves, are inherently bad (as we are all sinners, for man took a bite of the fruit as well), but because He knew that she would be enticed easily. But Adam, himself, didn’t even know that it wasn’t good for him to be alone. He was just taking God’s word for it. That’s how much trust man had in God in the beginning. No matter what God said, even if we didn’t know the full nature of it, it must be absolutely true. If only we were this trusting in the Lord today."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Adam must have thought that the animals were there to keep him company, but none of them seemed right to help him in his life. God already knew that Adam wouldn’t find the right helper there. It was Adam that took it upon himself to try and find a helper from God’s current creation. God needed to form a helper that would be closer related to Adam. Male birds had female birds. Male animals had female animals. Male fish had female fish. Male plants had female plants. Male humans had... well, they didn’t have anything, yet. But God was planning that."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":25,"title":null,"entries":[{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We understand that woman was taken from man. She is equal with man because of that purpose. The perfection of man was now the perfection of woman! What is still a mystery to me is why God would wait to make woman? Maybe He knew that she would take the fruit from the tree and would eat from it, and there would be a disconnect, so He tried to prolong it. But even the snake (which will be the one that causes the temptation to begin with) had a specific timing that God prepared for. Maybe God needed the rest of creation to have names before creating woman? But Adam still could have named the animals while Eve was there."},{"kind":"para","text":"I do find it interesting, though, that God says, “it’s not good for man to be alone” when man was not alone. Man had God. God knew something that man didn’t, I believe that is likely the reason why He is creating woman here. He is anticipating that the man will be alone soon. However, it’s the creation of woman that forces man to disconnect from God. That’s a spoiler though. I suppose we won’t ever fully know, but God certainly had a plan for this the whole time, and nothing is ever off timing with the Lord. There were some things that needed to be worked out with Adam, perhaps, before he could really appreciate having a helper in his life. Woman is the other half of man."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Hebrew word for “man” is “ish,” and the Hebrew word for “woman” is “ishshah.” In the Hebrew tongue, it is almost as if the word “ish” isn’t quite complete. The “shah” part is what completes the word, and it’s woman that completes man."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is an indication that the man is now responsible for the woman, and is not dictated on how to treat his marriage or his life based on control or concepts from his father and mother."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"To completely throw off the whole concept of superiority in culture today (race, gender, “science,” and many more...) marriage was meant for man and woman for a reason, and it’s the most beautiful picture of oneness and uniqueness ever created. God created this with the intention and image of unity with His church, which will be displayed through the life and death of Jesus Christ. Right here (in the book of Genesis) is the first time someone is giving His life for the sake of others. It’s the first time true love is shown, and it’s the first time that unity is displayed without a fork or wrench thrown into the picture to get them off track. But of course... this won’t last forever."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":25}]},
    "3": {"chapter":3,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we get a really great look at the rest of God, a recap of the days of creation, and a look at the creation of man and woman. God was putting everything into perfect places, and everything was in harmony. Nothing was useless. Everything had life, and purpose. Man was one of the last creations of God in these six days, and it was His most precious of all creation. He knew the coming issues that would arise seemingly quickly, and so He's setting things up for that occassion."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see the first issue of man come into the picture, which is the disobedience of God's command. It's the first sin ever committed, and it is what will be passed down through all the generations of the Earth. Of course, this doesn't throw God off, but it also doesn't make God happy. This will happen, though, ultimately to bring in the Savior!"}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"The Ultimate Fall of Man","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here, we are introduced to Satan. One of the most dangerous, deadly, and (apparently) most subtle of all creatures that God has designed is the snake, and how fitting it is for Satan to show up in this manner. What is interesting to me, however, is that this was a talking snake. I don’t know about you, but if a snake talked to me, I would think I was either going crazy or there was something wrong with that snake!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the great dragon was cast out, that old serpent, called the Devil, and Satan, which deceiveth the whole world: he was cast out into the earth, and his angels were cast out with him.","ref":"Revelation 12:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, we can get into the origin of Satan and how he ends up here in this garden paradise, but I don’t want to draw away from the main focus of Scripture in this chapter of Genesis. There may be time for that when we go through Isaiah 14 or Ezekiel 28. Apparently, Eve didn’t think it was too big of a deal seeing as she is seen literally conversing with the snake. This could be the reason that she was easily tempted into what is about to come next."},{"kind":"para","text":"She might have thought that the fact that the snake could talk meant that God has a special purpose for this snake. Honestly, they aren’t even warned about Satan beforehand. They are just warned about what would happen to them if they take from that tree. They KNEW God was telling the truth about that tree, yet they still had a hard time believing it."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think Satan was wise to go to Eve in this matter. Eve is not totally to blame here. God had told Adam about the tree in which he was not allowed to eat from, and then created woman. The fact that Eve knows about this says that Adam had to warn her about that tree after she was created. She was getting secondhand information here. If it had come from God Himself to Eve’s ears, then she might have been more inclined to accept it and trust it, but even already she had some doubts because it came from Adam specifically. This is why Satan is able to get away with what he’s about to get away with."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is the same lie he speaks of today. He questions the ethics of God in the lives of human beings who are seeking God and serving Him. This is why many don’t come to know Him today. He says, “If God is good, why would He put a tree in this garden that you are bound to and restrict you from eating it?” Today, he says, “If God is good, why would He allow murder, rape, and abortion? Why would He send people to a place of eternal torture and torment? That can’t possibly be what He means when He says those things!”"},{"kind":"para","text":"The sad part is, fools are tricked by it, and they end up rejecting the truth and the proof of it to their own demise and frustration. They experience pain and loss because they had chosen to disbelieve what God actually said. Even further ADDING to His word to try and support their personal claims. God warns against adding to His Word, time and time again."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Like trying a new drug, they felt different! They felt alive! They wondered “Why was God trying to keep this away from us?” Even further more, they had a new knowledge that they didn’t have before. They now knew “evil.” They already knew what was good, and rightfully could deem what was good and not good. God said that He made man in His own image, and they had the same mindset and judgement to deem what was good and not good. They, however, didn’t know evil."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now that they have taken a bite of this fruit, their eyes were opened to a whole new idea of life. One where people had the opportunity to do gross, grotesque, heinous, and inappropriate things. Remember they had the option for the “Tree of Life” in the midst of the garden. They had the opportunity to literally CHOOSE LIFE. But that was meant for a righteous and perfect people."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s a good thing Satan took them to the tree of the knowledge of Good and Evil rather than straight to the tree of life. Satan knew what he was doing. He wanted them to question God, and he wanted them to be disconnected from God, and the best way to do that is to give them thoughts of evil. Thoughts that go against the character of God. This will ultimately make it easier for Satan to use humankind for his own sinful purposes."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":13,"title":"God's Rest Broken","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It should be realized and understood that right off the bat (after sin had befallen the human race), it was not man seeking God to try and make amends and fix the disobedience. It was God seeking man, and God continues to seek until we are all called into His presence, or openly reject His calling."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Adam doesn’t just try and blame someone else for the sin he caused. He literally, and openly tries to blame GOD for allowing it. He says, it was “the woman whom THOU gavest to be with me...” he didn’t even try and repent from his sin. He didn’t try and confess and let it be known that what he did was wrong, and understand that it was wrong. He openly disobeyed God, and because He took of the fruit of evil, he also understood that what he ultimately did was evil in the sight of the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you don’t think disobedience is evil, then you should not get after your children or others when they disobey. Adam was trying to hide his sin right here at the beginning, and he was doing it in a way that simply placed blame back onto the one who did not allow sin in the lives of His children. The sovereignty of God plays a big role here in this chapter of Genesis, and this will be the first chapter where Jesus becomes the focal of redemption. Jesus has already been mentioned, right from the first chapter, and we’ve talked about Him several times already, but now things are set up for history."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Once again, there’s no repentance, and no ownership of their own sin nature. They do not want to confess what they have done. They continuously shift the blame onto someone else. That won’t work. But God still helps them, and gives them hope that they don’t have to take the full brunt of their sin, which is what we will see in the verses and chapters to come."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":19,"title":"The Adamic Covenant","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, the serpent that is being spoken of here is not the same serpent as what we see today. This serpent was Satan’s spirit. It was how he portrayed himself. He was a snake, slithering his way to divide and kill and destroy anything that seemed like a threat to him. Did you know we are a threat to Satan? Satan hates us, not because we can become Christians. Satan hates those that don’t become Christians too. He pretends like he cares for them, but ultimately just to keep them away from true love in the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"No, Satan hates us, he’s afraid of us, because he knows that God gives those that follow Him power unlike Satan has ever seen before. We have a higher power than Satan himself. Satan has the power to destroy, to steal, to kill, and to divide. But God gives us the power to overcome every single one of those issues. We have the power to literally overthrow Satan. Not just resist him, but to literally control him. Many of us don’t use that power, and we still fall for the tricks of Satan, but next time Satan tempts you with something, remember that God gave you more power than Satan could ever have."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here’s the second promise of God. This verse is wonderful because it gives all of mankind the promise of the Savior. God is promising Satan that he will lose. He literally warns Satan about what is going to take place, and it won’t be good for him. He will strike a blow at Christ’s heel. It will hurt and pain God, but the blow to the head will be much stronger than what Satan can do. As a serpent, Satan can only attack the heel. Snakes don’t aim for the face when they try to bite someone. They aim for the only part of the body that is closest to them on the ground. But if you step on the snake’s head, it won’t live. We see Christ bring victory over Satan on the day of Judgement, and we see Satan wound Christ on the cross of Calvary. But that will come in due time. God has a plan and a timing for everything."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Babies are joyous, but only after the sorrow of labor. Pain must come first before joy can take place. Once again, a picture of Christ. This is still the truth today. Childbearing could have been easy and painless, but now it won’t be because that is the judgement of women for the sin of Eve."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are now faced with death because of our sin, and we are faced with burdensome work. Work was supposed to be burdenless and simple. Now we sweat, and we ache, and we grow old and die. That is the judgement of man now. We understand that death is the separation of the spirit from the body."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then shall the dust return to the earth as it was: and the spirit shall return unto God who gave it.","ref":"Ecclesiastes 12:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"Multiple times throughout the Bible we are told that our spirit doesn’t rest. It goes somewhere. It goes back to God."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And as it is appointed unto men once to die, but after this the judgment:","ref":"Hebrews 9:27"},{"kind":"para","text":"Because of sin, we will all be judged after death, but God has given us victory and grace for judgement through the bruised heel of His Son."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":21,"title":"The Faith of Adam","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Woman was now named. The first mention of man’s name is found in our previous chapter:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And out of the ground the LORD God formed every beast of the field, and every fowl of the air; and brought them unto Adam to see what he would call them: and whatsoever Adam called every living creature, that was the name thereof.","ref":"Genesis 2:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"That name literally means “man” in the Hebrew language. Now we see women get their names, but Eve doesn’t mean “woman” or “first woman” like some assume it means. Though she certainly is, the “first woman,” the name Eve means “Living.” This is why Adam says this here and calls her Eve. There was originally no name for Eve until now."},{"kind":"para","text":"This also provides proof that Cain and Able were born AFTER the fall of Adam and Eve, because they were born unto the “living mother.” Not a spiritual one. You may be thinking, “Well duh. Who thought they were born into a spiritual birth?” Actually, there are many. Some believe this is when Cain and Abel are born. They will not be born in the garden; they will be born outside of the garden, and that should be understood because they weren’t protected by God in the garden like Adam and Eve were in the beginning. The sin nature that Adam and Eve had caused sin to be inherited to every single person that comes along after them. It’s not inherited simply by Adam and Eve teaching them how to sin, but rather it’s inherited through the disobedience of man. We are born into sin!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Behold, I was shapen in iniquity; and in sin did my mother conceive me.","ref":"Psalm 51:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"Eve is the mother of all the living, and that is really the reason we are all born into sin..."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The first sacrifice is made for the sinful nature of man. It didn’t take long before God represented the ultimate sacrifice that would take place. May it be understood that the clothing that was given was not a representation of God agreeing with their naked form, but rather a representation of God preparing His children to come before Him. It has always been the case!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But put ye on the Lord Jesus Christ, and make not provision for the flesh, to fulfil the lusts thereof.","ref":"Romans 13:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"Through this understanding, we know that Adam and Eve could only be fitted for the presence of God by God Himself, not based on anything that Adam and Eve did. He replaced the ratty fig leaves that Adam and Eve made with proper apparel. We can try and dress ourselves up with what we think is acceptable for the Lord all we want, but if we are not relying on His providence, we will never be able to stand in His presence. Our works are like fig leaves compared to His clothes of hide. God must provide the covering for us, and that can only be obtained through Jesus’ death."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":24,"title":"The Conscience","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The knowledge of good and evil is written in our hearts. This is called a “conscience.” When people say, “Well, what about those people that are in tribes and are living in jungles away from Christians? Will God allow them to suffer in Hell?” They are born with a conscience. They know the difference between good and evil. They are without excuse. Not to mention, God always sends someone."},{"kind":"para","text":"Thank God for missionaries that are sent out to tell others about the goodness of God to those that reject their conscience. Also, thank God He sent Adam and Eve out of the garden so that they wouldn’t live for eternity in their sins. We would be no different from Satan if God allowed us to eat from the tree of Life..."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This indicates that the Tree of Life can no longer be the acceptable way to come to God. The only way to come to Him now is through a sacrifice. Our sins have separated us from Him, and we must have a sacrifice to bring us into His presence."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":24}]},
    "4": {"chapter":4,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In the last chapter, we spoke heavily on the fall of man, and the first sin ever taking place."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter we continue to see what life is like after the fall of man..."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"The Sons of Adam and Eve","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What I love about this verse is that Adam and Eve don’t look at their acts as if forming a child is something they could control. No, rather they praise the Lord for allowing a child to be born to them, and they are saying, “Look what the Lord has done for us!” As we will see in a moment, I believe that Eve had more kids than what is mentioned right here. We will be getting into angels having children with humans and all sorts of strange beliefs of false religions later on in this book, but I honestly and seriously do believe that Eve had a lot more children than what is being spoken of here. My guess is that she had a lot of girl children, and these were her first boys that were born."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Both works were noble and important to the life and lineage that would be taking place for years to come. It should never be misconstrued that one particular work or the other was more important, which we will get to in a moment."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Both of these offerings are regarded as holy and important later in the Levitical Law. We know that an animal offering is important, and we see that as the basis most times throughout the Old Testament as the offering for sin, and that is what it is. But the Lord sees the fruit of the ground as an important offering as well multiple times over!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"That thou shalt take of the first of all the fruit of the earth, which thou shalt bring of thy land that the Lord thy God giveth thee, and shalt put it in a basket, and shalt go unto the place which the Lord thy God shall choose to place his name there.","ref":"Deuteronomy 26:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"The animal sacrifice was for sin, the fruit and plant sacrifice was for gratitude. We still have the same offerings today, just not in the same way. Christ was our sin sacrifice, our atonement in place of the animal sacrifice. Animals were sacrificed because of the blood. Jesus was sacrificed because of His perfection. Because Jesus was perfect and animals are not, Jesus’ payment on the cross is sufficient for the sacrifice we need to be forgiven from sin forever."},{"kind":"para","text":"The first fruits sacrifice is a gratitude sacrifice that every believer should give to thank and honor the Lord. This would be our money these days, for we work for cash payment rather than food today. But God is displaying (in these verses) that a gratitude sacrifice (tithing or money sacrifice today) is not acceptable when you have unpardoned sin in your life. Those days (before Christ) in order to be pardoned by God, there had to be blood given for the sins of the particular person. Plants don’t bleed. His carrot wouldn’t give him forgiveness. An innocent and spotless lamb would. If he made the right sacrifice first, his plant sacrifice would have been counted as worthy."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, you can kind of tell the spirits of each sacrifice just by reading this passage, and we will see it obviously open up more in the coming verses. Abel was excited to sacrifice to the Lord, while Cain didn’t really seem to care. Cain didn’t seem to really enjoy life. He seemed to be kind of callous to the idea that he was even around. The biggest issue with Cain’s sacrifice was his heart, it was sinful and in need of renewal."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Not as Cain, who was of that wicked one, and slew his brother. And wherefore slew he him? Because his own works were evil, and his brother's righteous.","ref":"1 John 3:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"Taking it a step further (even still today), giving the life of his firstborn of his flock is far more costly than giving a vegetable from the garden. You go to the grocery store today; meat pricing is inflated big time. Vegetable pricing is too, but it’s far more costly to purchase meat than it is vegetables. Abel was giving something far more valuable (through the life of his sheep) than Cain was (through the life of his garden). God has always found offering life in obedience more precious than offering gifts."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Samuel said, Hath the Lord as great delight in burnt offerings and sacrifices, as in obeying the voice of the Lord? Behold, to obey is better than sacrifice, and to hearken than the fat of rams.","ref":"1 Samuel 15:22"},{"kind":"para","text":"Our reasonable service to the Lord is to offer ourselves for His service."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I beseech you therefore, brethren, by the mercies of God, that ye present your bodies a living sacrifice, holy, acceptable unto God, which is your reasonable service.","ref":"Romans 12:1"}]}]},{"start":6,"end":7,"title":"Cain Exhorted, Given Correction","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God doesn’t give up on Cain. He knows what Cain is about to do, but He still pushes Cain to do better and gives Cain correction on how to do it. He warns Cain, “If you continue to be angry over this and not correct it to do what is right, you will be swallowed by your bitterness, and sin will take you hostage.” God tells Cain that he still has the possibility to make the right sacrifice and still please God. The only way to do that is to overcome sin’s power, however, which is impossible to do if you don’t have the appropriate heart and sacrifice."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":15,"title":"The First Murder","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I’m very curious to know what the conversation was. Either the conversation was civil, and it was a trick from Cain which led his brother to the field where he killed him, or the conversation was very rocky, and there was a fight that broke out, ultimately leading to Cain killing his brother. Either way, we know and understand that this was unnecessary. Cain had God’s forgiveness and presence in his life, and he rejected it by following after sin."},{"kind":"para","text":"This act is so cruel and disgusting, and it shouldn’t just be glanced over. I can’t stand it when preachers and pastors just talk about the killing of Abel as if it was just a part of a story that was told long ago. This act set the course of history away from the Lord even further, which is something we will get into later when we get to the flood, and the Exodus from Egypt, and so much more."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord knows what happened. Honestly, God didn’t have to ask. I think it’s important for US (the readers) to know this conversation took place, because it shows the lack of remorse and the depths of Cain’s sin. He literally just killed his own brother with his bare hands and doesn’t seem to care in the slightest. That is what sin does though, and this is important to note."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sin makes the follower of it extremely callous. If you have no sympathy or care for others, or you don’t have the ability to uplift others in your walk and way, then you may be surrounded by sin in your life. Feeling depressed, or feeling down, and focused on yourself is a sign that your walk isn’t right, and something needs to take place. Sometimes it’s as simple as repentance from a sin that you know about. Other times it’s more complicated and requires a sabbatical of some sort where you must get away and plead with the Lord. It doesn’t matter the length of time it takes, it just matters that you don’t ignore it like Cain does, because all that does is bring you deeper into sin. And yes, it’s as simple as your words being misspoken that can cause death to those around you. You don’t have to physically kill someone in order for them to feel hurt and killed spiritually by you."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Death and life are in the power of the tongue: and they that love it shall eat the fruit thereof.","ref":"Proverbs 18:21"},{"kind":"para","text":"Be cautious about your Spirit, and take care of it through the washing of the Word of the Lord."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is very interesting to me. Abel’s blood cries out from the ground, which actually isn’t the interesting part. God knows the pain and turmoil of His children, so this is not a surprise that God knows what happened to Abel after Cain’s sin. God may not have been able to look on the sin of Cain, but He was certainly able to look on the righteousness of Abel."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Precious in the sight of the Lord is the death of his saints.","ref":"Psalm 116:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"No, what’s interesting to me here is that God tells Cain (because of Cain’s sin) that he won’t be able to cultivate the soil and produce fruit for himself or his family. The reason I find that interesting is because God continuously does this throughout history because of the sin of the people. When the people turn back to Him, we see an increase of abundance for necessary products that will keep us moving forward."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe we are experiencing such a high inflation rate, and so much lack of products (especially oil and the like) because of the sin of our nation. The only way we will see a renewal of supply is if people turn back to the Lord. Honestly, I think the world is ripe (right now) for a revival. If a revival takes place, it may be the last one, and it gives significant signs to the rapture happening sooner than we realize. If not, we will be as helpless as Cain is here because our sins have kept God back from blessing us. This goes to further prove God’s existence. Farmers must depend upon the Lord for growth even still today."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Even after Cain’s sin and hatred towards the Lord’s anointed, God still gave him grace. Sure, he wouldn’t be able to come to the Lord anymore for what he did, and he wouldn’t be able to farm (which seemed to be the only thing he was good at), but he was able to continue to live, AND he was able to receive a mark from God that symbolized God’s provision if anyone caused him harm. God loved Cain still. In fact, I truly believe that God was still open to accepting Cain had Cain just repented. But God knew that Cain wouldn’t, and that Cain was too far gone to his sin to ever come back to the Lord. Cain left God with no choice but to be cut off..."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":24,"title":"The First Civilzation","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Cain moves to a land called Nod, which just sounds like a place where everyone is either falling asleep or agreeing with everything. “Nod” actually and literally means “Wandering.” Cain wandered in as a wanderer in the land called wandering. It’s what happens to all who are enticed by sin. We have no direction; we just wander."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, during this time, there was no one else in the land to be with, so this is literally insinuating that Cain had a child with one of his sisters. Of course, this was before the Levitical Law, which would forbid this practice."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The nakedness of thy sister, the daughter of thy father, or daughter of thy mother, whether she be born at home, or born abroad, even their nakedness thou shalt not uncover.","ref":"Leviticus 18:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"That’s presumably for a reason, seeing as life would not be able to continue if this didn’t take place. They have their first son, and they name him Enoch. “Enoch” means “Dedicated.” Dedicated to what? I don’t know... I don’t believe Cain was dedicating his firstborn to the Lord, seeing as he seemingly has a hard time dedicating living things to God, including himself. Enoch was probably dedicated to himself or to his own family lineage. He names the city after Enoch, and it seems that Cain is making quite a name for himself there."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Interestingly, we are given the skills and occupations of those that came after Cain, and they are things that are still relevant today. These are noble trades, but they were being used for the wrong reasons. Lamech’s immediate descendants are associated with animal breeding, music, and metalwork. Abel’s association was with sheep; Jabal was the only one that seemed to find the significance in raising livestock. It took a few generations before they were accepting of tending to sheep and animals again, probably because Cain hated the works of Abel so much."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, sin is tied to all generations. Someone simply hit Lamech, and Lamech killed him. Lamech is under the assumption that because he did it out of revenge rather than hate, he is safer than Cain who had only God’s protection. But that’s not how this works, of course... God’s protection is far greater than our fleshly protection that we apply to ourselves..."}]}]},{"start":25,"end":26,"title":"The Birth of Seth (The Spiritual Seed Renewed)","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course (as we will see), it is through the lineage and bloodline of Seth that our Savior comes. It may have been through the bloodline of Abel, had Cain not killed him. But the reason that God allowed the death of Abel was to signify the ultimate sacrifice that would take place. Abel was an innocent young man who was simply pleasing the Lord in his sacrifice like he was commanded. Cain didn’t like that and killed him. This is the exact same picture of Jesus Christ. He lived a perfect life and pleased God in everything He did."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And lo a voice from heaven, saying, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.\" Matthew 3:17 \"While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear ye him.","ref":"Matthew 17:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"Seth’s lineage would have to be the one that redeemed the bloodline. It could not be Cain’s because of his lack of heart for the Lord. Obviously, through the bloodline of Seth, we see a renewal of the Spirit in our last verse of the chapter."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And lo a voice from heaven, saying, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.","ref":"Matthew 3:17"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear ye him.","ref":"Matthew 17:5"}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"At this point, it would be wrong of me not to define the names of each person that comes into the picture here. Names are vital in the Old Testament because they give purpose to the person that is mentioned. “Seth” means “compensation.” Compensation because Abel was taken from them, so the Lord allowed another Son to be born. “Enosh” means “man.” The biblical importance of that name is not certain..."},{"kind":"para","text":"The fact of the matter is, God had allowed a fresh start to this lineage by His own grace, knowing that it would mean the salvation of all of mankind, not just once through Christ, but multiple times through those that would turn to Him. Christ died for all of mankind for the salvation of the world. Noah saved mankind by obedience. The prophets will save those before Christ by their preaching and testimonies. The Israelites will be saved through the leadership of Moses. The children of God will continue to get chance after chance after chance, and still to this day continue to get chance after chance to turn to the Lord and to follow after righteousness."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":26}]},
    "5": {"chapter":5,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we looked at the first murder in history (which didn’t even take place against an enemy but rather over jealousy and in the same blood related family). We saw the chances for Cain to continuously make the right choice, and his rejection to God’s providence and forgiveness, ultimately leading to his exile and his separation from God. We are then introduced to the salvation lineage of Seth, leaving us with hope for the future generations to come."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we will be reading a lot about the lineage of Adam. Honestly, that is pretty much all this chapter focuses on, so I am going to use this as an opportunity to define the names that are given so we have a clear idea and representation of this lineage coming out of Adam and Eve, and how it leads to the story of salvation through Jesus Christ."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"The Generations of Adam","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A constant reminder throughout the Bible is that God made them (since the beginning of time) male and female. There was no “gender spectrum” or crazy ideology that would make people believe that they could be anything they desired to be."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"So God created man in his own image, in the image of God created he him; male and female created he them.\" Genesis 1:27 \"But from the beginning of the creation God made them male and female.","ref":"Mark 10:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"Pretend to be a bird all you want, you are still a male or a female, and no amount of cosmetic surgery will change that. What’s really funny to me is that our culture today is strongly trying to attack this idea of gender, further proving the necessity that Jesus and the Bible place on this. It’s almost as if God knew that people would try and warp this idea, so God emphasizes continuously that there is no excuse not to know what is male and what is female. They didn’t have surgeries like this back then, so it wouldn’t seem like it was all that important for Jesus to even bring this up, or for God to continuously remind everyone that He made them male and female since the beginning of time. But we see that God always emphasizes and repeats things that are going to be an important issue so that we know for sure where God stands on the subject."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A very interesting correlation is placed here between the birth of Seth and the birth of Adam. Of course, we don’t know how old Adam was when God created him. We understand though that the important fact of the creation of Adam was that he was made in God’s likeness. But we see that Seth was not made in God’s likeness. Seth was made in the likeness of Adam. I do believe that is why Adam names him Seth. He is the “compensation” for the loss of Able. The new man, who will bring along the lineage of salvation."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is where we see God’s first promise come true. He promised to Adam and Eve that if they ate from that tree, they would die."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, thou shalt not eat of it: for in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die.","ref":"Genesis 2:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"The words are clear here. God knew they would eat of it, and He knew that they would die because of it, and He already knew what had to happen to reconnect with His creation. Grace was shown before Adam sinned."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Moreover the law entered, that the offence might abound. But where sin abounded, grace did much more abound:","ref":"Romans 5:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"God’s grace hasn’t shrunk over the years as some might like you to believe. It didn’t just end with Adam’s disobedience. God’s grace grows with each sin we commit. This does not mean we should sin to obtain grace, but it means that if we do sin, God’s grace is enough!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he said unto me, My grace is sufficient for thee: for my strength is made perfect in weakness. Most gladly therefore will I rather glory in my infirmities, that the power of Christ may rest upon me.","ref":"2 Corinthians 12:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"The issue isn’t with God’s grace abounding. The issue is with our disobedience to His Word and Will. This is ultimately why Adam died, because of His disobedience to God’s Word. He had a son, and as we read the remaining passages, we see what ultimately happens to them as well because of the sin of Adam."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":32,"title":"The Family of Seth","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Enos” means “Man” in the Hebrew language. This was a fresh start for the lineage of Adam. Enos was the “new” man. The second chance for humanity. As if we deserved it. Yet God is not willing that any should perish, and it goes to show His deep love for us that He would provide salvation and more chances for us than we deserve."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here’s the sad truth about sin. Because of it, death passed onto everyone because we all NOW sin due to what Adam and Eve did."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherefore, as by one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin; and so death passed upon all men, for that all have sinned:","ref":"Romans 5:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"Enos (though a fresh start to the lineage) would still have to suffer the same fate as the rest of humanity because he was made in Seth’s likeness, who was made in Adam’s likeness. Of course, Seth had other sons and daughters as we read a minute ago, but the highlight is through this lineage because it leads to the ultimate savior (which is, of course, Jesus Christ)."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Cainan” means “Possession.” It can also mean “Fixed.” Whichever definition was used here for this child is not clear, though either can work for the fact that we see this lineage moving in a fixed position for the Lord, and that the possession of the lineage was through the promise of God."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Yet again, another death through natural causes. The curse was being realized (at least I hope at this point) through this lineage. If their sons and daughters were not born, they would have nothing to provide for the next generation."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Mahalaleel” means “Praise of God.” So far, the names continuously bring honor to God through this lineage. They are keeping their eyes on the same important image that they were supposed to follow to begin with."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, we know at this point it’s inevitable. These people were living for a long time, though. A record life of nine hundred and ten years through Cainan. I’m, honestly, thankful we no longer live that long."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Jared” means “Descent.” Jared wasn’t necessarily as grandiose of a name as “Praise of God.” But Jared is ultimately the descendant of that praise. God’s praise was passed down to the next generation!"}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Eight hundred and ninety-five years is less than Cainan, but still a long time considering."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is where it gets interesting. “Enoch” means “Dedicated.” Interesting enough (if you find it interesting at all), my mother’s name is Denise. “Denise” means “Dedicated to God.” Remember in the last chapter that Cain also had a son whom he named “Enoch”..."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Cain knew his wife; and she conceived, and bare Enoch: and he builded a city, and called the name of the city, after the name of his son, Enoch.","ref":"Genesis 4:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"Cain’s Enoch was dedicated to a city. Jared’s Enoch was dedicated to the Lord. The city of Enoch will not go down in history to have ever served any particular grandiose purpose. Enoch, however, has an incredible testimony."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course (sadly), the same fate belonged to Jared as it did to all of his ancestors before him. Death swallowed up everybody, even in the line of salvation. Someone asked, “If Jesus didn’t die, would He have died from old age?” The answer is no. He would have to die for humanity, or we would all die in our own sins. Without His sacrifice (that is what His death was, by the way), we would all serve our sentence in eternity. That is not what God wanted. But I’ll take the bait and warp a reality to the question. My answer is still no. I believe God would have just taken Him (just like we will see happen in a moment)."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Methuselah” means “Sending Forth.” I believe Methuselah will certainly send forth Enoch to be a great child of God in what follows in the verses after this. Enoch has the more incredible testimony."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"No one can read this and just be callous to the fact that God literally loved spending time with Enoch so much (because of his dedication to the Lord) that God literally just took him. Strange enough, nothing else is mentioned of this situation. His family doesn’t seem to speak about it. The Bible doesn’t give much more clarification after this. That’s pretty much all we get of Enoch. He’s mentioned in the gospel as a lineage account."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Which was the son of Mathusala, which was the son of Enoch, which was the son of Jared, which was the son of Maleleel, which was the son of Cainan,","ref":"Luke 3:37"},{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord, obviously, allowed him to stay for a time just to have kids and continue the lineage, but God wanted Enoch with Him immediately. We either avoided something that could have come to pass with Enoch, or Enoch was a very special person to the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"By faith Enoch was translated that he should not see death; and was not found, because God had translated him: for before his translation he had this testimony, that he pleased God.","ref":"Hebrews 11:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"He pleased God immensely in the things he did. No one (other than Elisha and Jesus) was ever treated like this because of their relationship with God. The only other time that the Bible gives a little further clarification is..."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"And Enoch also, the seventh from Adam, prophesied of these, saying, Behold, the Lord cometh with ten thousands of his saints,\" Jude 14"},{"kind":"para","text":"What is Jude talking about here? I will not pretend to act as if I know what Jude is talking about here. I have guesses, but that’s all it can be. Judging by the way Jude presents this, it’s as if Enoch knew a little more about the future than we previously thought. The only time that the Lord comes with ten thousand of His saints is mentioned in Matthew:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And then shall appear the sign of the Son of man in heaven: and then shall all the tribes of the earth mourn, and they shall see the Son of man coming in the clouds of heaven with power and great glory. And he shall send his angels with a great sound of a trumpet, and they shall gather together his elect from the four winds, from one end of heaven to the other.\" Matthew 24:30-31 \"And I saw heaven opened, and behold a white horse; and he that sat upon him was called Faithful and True, and in righteousness he doth judge and make war. His eyes were as a flame of fire, and on his head were many crowns; and he had a name written, that no man knew, but he himself. And he was clothed with a vesture dipped in blood: and his name is called The Word of God. And the armies which were in heaven followed him upon white horses, clothed in fine linen, white and clean.","ref":"Revelation 19:11-14"},{"kind":"para","text":"Both of these times mention end-time prophecies. The Holy Spirit seemed to guide Jude to write something that had not been revealed in any other way. It’s a very interesting verse that I won’t spend too much time on because of the lack of time."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Lamech” means “Powerful.\""}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Interestingly enough, Methuselah lived longer than anyone else, including Adam (or at least in the time frame that we are given about Adam). I believe that (because of Methuselah’s long life) he could have given a proper understanding to Noah about the things that were taking place since the beginning of time. Methuselah would have died right around when Noah was six hundred years old. Adam died when Methuselah was two hundred and forty-three."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Noah” means “Rest.” They were hopeful that the Lord would provide them with rest through Noah. Little did they know that the rest they were longing for would only come through the hard work and obedience of Noah and his family."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, from Noah’s generation there is a bit of a rest from the rest of the generations because of the great flood that takes place (spoiler alert)."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These kids, and their spouses, would help to further the salvation lineage. “Shem” means “name.” “Ham” means “hot.” “Japheth” means “opened.” Maybe this family was running out of ideas for names…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":32}]},
    "6": {"chapter":6,"intro":[],"previously":["In our last chapter, we got a good look at the linage of Seth leading to Noah. We explained the definition behind each name as we went down the list, and ended with Noah having children.","In this chapter, we will learn more about Noah, the way he helped form the future generations, and the link between his story and that of the saviors."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"The Marriage","entries":[{"start":1,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We will be addressing some interesting and common issues throughout this chapter that many have falsely interpreted here as far as what Moses meant when writing these passages. Many, today, are under the presupposition that when the Bible refers to these “sons of God” it is referring to angels. They translate that as there were half angel, half human creatures living on earth during this time. This could not be further from the truth. The “sons of God” reflection comes from the idea that they were men who were striving to reverence the Lord as we’ve read about already before!"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"And to Seth, to him also there was born a son; and he called his name Enos: then began men to call upon the name of the Lord.\" Genesis 4:26"},{"kind":"para","text":"That would leave the question as to why women were being considered as “the daughters of men.” Well these were daughters of the line of Cain. Once again (as previously mentioned) we have looked at the lineage of man since the beginning of this book. While wives may be mentioned, it is usually the man’s name that seems to matter a little more."},{"kind":"para","text":"We assume because they may have had more than one wife, (not exactly sure if that is the reason why or not). A greater assumption would be (if I am so bold to speak in this manner) that it was the MEN who were the vessels used for God’s Work and labor, whereas the women were to simply give honor and uplift the man for that work. Women will be mentioned quite a bit through scripture, and I think it’s important to point them out when they appear. But Woman was to be the HELPMEET, not just the MEET, if you catch my drift."},{"kind":"para","text":"The sons of God, would be those that were set apart for the Lord’s labor and work, and were not to find themselves in fornication, or in monogamy (or marrying more than one wife). Yet, here they are, being distracted from their ultimate purpose, and I believe this is why it’s highlighted here."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"title":"The Warning","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Peter actually addresses this exact and important note in 1 Peter, which is another reason so many today believe that the sons of God were angels..."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"For Christ also hath once suffered for sins, the just for the unjust, that he might bring us to God, being put to death in the flesh, but quickened by the Spirit: By which also he went and preached unto the spirits in prison;\" 1 Peter 3:18-19"},{"kind":"para","text":"These “spirits” are referenced as being “in prison” because they were in bondage to their sins during the time of Christ. Most believe this meant that Christ went down into hell and preached the gospel to angels, which has no stance or reason to be true, especially if you read the whole chapter in proper context. More importantly, Peter then links that directly to this time in Genesis in the very next verse."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Which sometime were disobedient, when once the longsuffering of God waited in the days of Noah, while the ark was a preparing, wherein few, that is, eight souls were saved by water.\" 1 Peter 3:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"The same spirit was being preached and witnessed by Noah (according to this chapter) for one hundred and twenty years. God’s Spirit was given to these people so that they would repent and turn to Him rather than to the lustful attraction they felt for the daughters of men. However, they don’t turn, and things seemingly get worse. God knows they don’t turn, and that is why He says, “My spirit shall not always strive with man...”"}]}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"title":"Before the Flood","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The giants, and the sons of God’s offspring MUST be separated. This verse purposely separates this idea because the giants were not the children of God’s sons. In fact, the children of God’s sons were simple men of mighty valor. They were not half breeds, they were men. This can’t be expounded enough. God makes it painfully clear that angels were not breeding with earthly humans. If they had been, they would either have to be good angels (which would not sin against God in doing this) or bad angels, which would create monstrosities instead of men, nor could they be considered “sons of God” because they are evil... The sons of God can only mean the godly lineage of Seth through the generations. Those born through Cain would not be considered the sons of God, for his lineage rejected Him."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":7,"title":"The Purpose of Judgement","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This line is scary, and it’s not because we understand that the ultimate flood is coming in their presence soon, but it’s because we know that it will be the same in the end of times..."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"But as the days of Noah were, so shall also the coming of the Son of man be. For as in the days that were before the flood they were eating and drinking, marrying and giving in marriage, until the day that Noe entered into the ark, And knew not until the flood came, and took them all away; so shall also the coming of the Son of man be.\" Matthew 24:37-39"},{"kind":"para","text":"Having already read the end of the book gives us a clarification that the end times will be even worse than the days of Noah. At least in this time, there was saved eight people..."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Sin was literally grieving God. It hurt Him so much that He felt like there was no other choice but to destroy man. If He knew that salvation wouldn’t work and it didn’t matter, He very well could have destroyed man, and it would all be over from there. We wouldn’t exist today. However, He (obviously) loves His creation, and desires for them to come to Him and love Him back. Grief for others is a sure sign that you care. If God had enough compassion to continue creation because of His love for us, than it stands to reason that we could never treat sinners any other way."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Interestingly enough, it doesn't mention fish here, because they live in the water, and God is going to send more water."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":22,"title":"The Purpose of Grace","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here’s where things become interesting, and most Calvinists would throw a fit..."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The reason Noah was able to see God’s grace while the whole world was looking out for themselves and their own desires, was because Noah walked with God in faith!"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"By faith Noah, being warned of God of things not seen as yet, moved with fear, prepared an ark to the saving of his house; by the which he condemned the world, and became heir of the righteousness which is by faith.\" Hebrews 11:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"With the reality and understanding of the amount of flack I might get for suggesting this, but it must take our understanding to receive salvation. God saves us, but only when we trust Him by faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast.\" Ephesians 2:8-9"},{"kind":"para","text":"Yes, we do not earn salvation. Yes, we do not have to do anything to receive salvation. Yes, it is all of God that we are saved. However, may we read this verse in Ephesians more accurately, not missing the most important details."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God:\" Ephesians 2:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"Grace is what God gives to us, and faith is what we give to Him. I do not believe it is possible to have faith without grace first, but I certainly believe that it’s not possible to receive salvation without our faith being placed in God’s grace. Noah’s faith is ultimately what saves him here, and it’s what helps him see God’s grace through the sinful and lustful world around him."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have already been through the meanings and thoughts of Noah’s Sons in our last chapter."}]},{"start":11,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These were supposed to be godly children. They were being anything but Godly. They took what God gave them and corrupted it. They abused their God given freedoms, and ran the opposite way then where they were supposed to go. So because of this, God knew it would only continuously become worse, and if God didn’t put a stop to it, the earth would become chaotic and evil, which was the opposite of what God desired for His creation."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Man had a promise of redemption since the fall took place, but they were not looking for the redeemer. They were pleasing their own flesh. Seth’s lineage would still be the lineage used for the Savior (simply because of Noah) whereas Cain’s lineage was to be the lost lineage. Cain’s lineage had chosen far before Seth’s that they were going to be as opposite of God’s Will and Way as humanly possible. Seth’s lineage was not supposed to be that way, yet it’s the way they eventually choose, maybe because it looks easier and sin is fun to them. Noah believed God, and for one hundred and twenty years may have been bullied and ridiculed for his faith in God, especially as he continuously warned his neighbors that there would be destruction coming, and they must turn to God in repentance and turn from their wickedness (does this sound familiar?)"}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We may need to break up this particular instruction of the Ark so we get a gist picture of how it may have looked (though Ken Ham has done most of the work for us)... Obviously, during that time, Gopher Wood was nearly indestructible and sturdy. That was the best wood Noah was going to get for the situation that was to take place. “Rooms” would describe “places” (rather than actual separated living quarters) for each different type of animal. For instance, an Elephant would need a fairly large space, whereas a squirrel wouldn’t need more than a small nest. Pitching it would mean to seal it so that water could not get in or out."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Biblical measurements are not as easy to understand because they use a different measuring system than us. I will try my best to help illustrate what that would equal to in the US modern measurements we would use today. For instance, three hundred cubits, would equal to four hundred and fifty feet (long). Fifty cubits would be seventy-five feet (wide). Thirty cubits would be forty-five feet (tall). This ship was too look, honestly, fairly similar in style to our ships today. Not much has changed as far as the way boats are built. I do not imagine they had many boats at this time, or ships of any kind. If they did (which is not spoken of here) then I guarantee they had nothing that was even remotely close to this big..."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Basically, the whole top of the ship would be opened for ventilation. I don’t imagine that the stench in the ark would be great. Not to mention, if there was no ventilation, those fumes could cause an explosion for that length of time being built up in that ship with all those animals. I’ve seen the linking of this ONE door in the ark to Christ’s mention of Him being the only door to salvation. That one door was the salvation factor to the faith of Noah and His family, plus the animals that Noah had saved on His ark."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God gives Noah His concern for the earth, and His plan for finishing the evil taking place (knowing full well that it will happen again and again in the future, leading to an ultimate evil that will come during the end times). Noah needed to understand this salvation testimony, because it would be something that is passed down through the future generations so that they are without excuse when God ends all things in His final judgement."}]},{"start":18,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You may not believe it, but Noah didn’t have to go get the animals. The animals would actually, just simply, come to Him. God controls animals a lot through His Word (like giving the ability for a donkey to speak, or for a whale to swallow Jonah and take him to Nineveh, etc.) God does not force man to do anything. Noah was not forced to build this ark. Noah would only work on this by faith alone that God was serious about what was going to take place. The animals simply obeyed God’s moving and Will, and went to Noah’s ark for rescue."}]},{"start":21,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We’ve mentioned this before, but there was no meat eating in the beginning here. The first sacrifice of an animal was with Adam and Eve, and even after that they were still focused on tilling the ground and growing fruits and vegetables. They have not mentioned eating meat, and so I believe that the animals in the Ark more than likely didn’t eat meat either. It was fruits, vegetables, and hay that Noah had to collect. Even for what is commonly known as carnivorous animals like lions and tigers, and bears."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"The wolf also shall dwell with the lamb, and the leopard shall lie down with the kid; and the calf and the young lion and the fatling together; and a little child shall lead them. And the cow and the bear shall feed; their young ones shall lie down together: and the lion shall eat straw like the ox.\" Isaiah 11:6-7"},{"kind":"para","text":"This was how it was to be with all creation before the fall of man... Noah took God at His word and was obedient to every one of God’s suggestions. Not because Noah knew God was really going to flood the whole earth and kill everyone, but because Noah simply believed God would not lie about this. This is the same way WE should approach God’s Will and calling. We won’t know the end, or what God really means in the matter, but we must believe that God will do what He says, and follow Him simply because we believe Him without even seeing the proof of what is to come."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":22}]},
    "7": {"chapter":7,"intro":[],"previously":["Noah and the flood is a common knowledge reference of the Bible. Most people, when they hear the word “Bible,” think of either Noah, or they think of Jesus. In the last chapter, we saw what life was like before the flood. There was a season when the sons of God were on fire for the Lord, but as the days progressed they slowly became wicked and sinful. Moses (the writer of Genesis) identified what the purpose of judgement would be for, and what the purpose of grace is for. Noah spent a good portion of his time before building the ark to let people know about the coming judgement of God. Not one single person listened. We still find a few today who would accept the message of salvation, but not many. I believe when the world comes to an end, it will be just as impossible to find any who would listen to the gospel message, let alone accept it.","The last chapter ended with Noah providing the salvation that the earth needed in order for life to continue, which was found in the Ark. It gives the dimensions of the ark, and we see that (without hesitation) Noah worked on God’s Will to provide salvation from the coming judgement. In this chapter, we will pick back up in the happenstance of the flood, and see what happens to all of creation as we know it. There is a lot of parallels to look at through this as to the end times, or even the purpose and salvation of Jesus for our souls."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":9,"title":"Entering the Ark","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Noah’s righteousness wasn’t explicitly set because God just chose him and his household to be saved. God found favor with Noah because Noah was willing to listen to the Lord while no one else would. He believed in what the Lord was doing. He knew that what God was planning was for His own Will, and simply moved by faith to accomplish what the Lord desired. It was by Noah’s faith alone, and God’s grace alone, which saved this family and these animals."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: 9 Not of works, lest any man should boast.\" Ephesians 2:8-9"},{"kind":"para","text":"Faith was the ultimate factor to the saving grace of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"By faith Noah, being warned of God of things not seen as yet, moved with fear, prepared an ark to the saving of his house; by the which he condemned the world, and became heir of the righteousness which is by faith.\" Hebrews 11:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"Contrary to hyper-Calvinistic belief, faith is the means to salvation. Now, I do not walk in the same line as some who believe that faith is a work. Faith in itself is not a work, and it cannot be. Faith is the letting go of yourself, and accepting what God has already done without actually seeing it personally."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Now faith is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen.\" Hebrews 11:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"But it is required to obtain salvation. Without faith, we cannot be saved."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"For whosoever shall call upon the name of the Lord shall be saved. How then shall they call on him in whom they have not believed? and how shall they believe in him of whom they have not heard? and how shall they hear without a preacher? And how shall they preach, except they be sent? as it is written, How beautiful are the feet of them that preach the gospel of peace, and bring glad tidings of good things!\" Romans 10:13-15"},{"kind":"para","text":"It is God’s part to provide grace, it is the preachers part to tell others about that grace, and it is our part to hear the preacher so that we might obtain that grace through faith. In the days of Noah, it was no different. Those that desired to be saved from God’s punishment and wrath had an open invitation to the ark. Yet everyone rejected it, and God only saw righteousness in Noah because of his faith in God."}]},{"start":2,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The purpose for the more abundance of clean beasts was the providence of a holy and spotless sacrifice which we will see Noah make right after everything is finished."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"And Noah builded an altar unto the Lord; and took of every clean beast, and of every clean fowl, and offered burnt offerings on the altar.\" Genesis 8:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"There were to be more “clean” beasts for the sacrifices and offerings later on as well. The sacrifices were meant as a cleansing offering for the sins of the people, but also as a thanksgiving offering for what the Lord had provided. Sacrifices will be more understood later in this book, and especially in Exodus, but the gist of the purpose of them were to display the sin of man, and the ultimate end of that sin by the sacrifice of the just and holy King whom we refer to as Jesus, the Christ."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When we read through Revelation, we see multiple chances for salvation given to the people, even during the destruction of everything. Angels (of a sort; or two fiery preachers depending on which end of the argument you land on) are sent to preach the gospel to everybody in the earth, and they literally killed these beings. Look what it says about Jesus even during the last days..."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Behold, I stand at the door, and knock: if any man hear my voice, and open the door, I will come in to him, and will sup with him, and he with me.\" Revelation 3:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"God gave a whole week (after the ark was finished being built) for the world to repent and go to the door of Noah but they never did. They could have knocked at that door but they chose to continue to reject God and Noah’s message. I’d have started freaking out at about day three or four thinking that maybe Noah was onto something, but apparently the rest of the world was brain dead. Or just exceedingly sinful and rebellious (culminating from an immense amount of pride)."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Noah remained faithful, even while the floods were pouring in. No doubt, Noah worried about this ark making it through the flood. Even if he followed the instructions of God verbatim (which I’m sure he did), the work of his own hands were still flawed, and he was probably just hoping there would be no leaks, and the ship would actually float rather than sink. Apparently it does, and I believe it’s partly because Noah kept faith in the Lord. He had provenly provided for Noah before, why would He stop providing now?"}]},{"start":6,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Judging by the verses, we do not find the necessity for Noah to go out and bring in the animals. A lot of Hollywood pictures portray Noah doing the work of gathering the animals, but the animals were simply obedient to the will of God. We find that often throughout scripture. Even the whale that swallows Jonah is found to be more obedient than Jonah."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":24,"title":"Destruction & Salvation","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They had been warned for years at this point. Noah, I believe gave them a time frame. He probably told the people that they had seven days left but they still wouldn’t listen. And now... we come to day seven."}]},{"start":11,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The LORD shut them in. They didn’t have to close the door because the Lord closed it. They didn’t even have the chance to reject entrance to anyone else because the Lord rejected entrance. They had plenty of time, and now it was too late. I am under the impression that none of them even desired to board Noah’s ark anyway. They probably called out after they were floating in the deep but it was too late by then. This will be the same in the end."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"And shall cast them into the furnace of fire: there shall be wailing and gnashing of teeth.\" Matthew 13:50"},{"kind":"para","text":"Their cries for help will be too late by then. They had their chance, and they didn’t take it..."}]},{"start":17,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems to have taken about forty days before the ark started to finally rise. I imagine, at that point, the water had to be about half way up the side of the ark. It would have been too deep for any humans to continue to stand or find ground unless they climbed a mountain and stood at the top. Even then, the rain and cold would probably waste them away. No food or shelter. No way to escape. It had been raining nonstop for forty days already, and the mountains were probably close to being covered as well."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"As the water increased, the chances of survival decreased. Even if they found a mountain to stand on, they wouldn’t have survived. There was nothing left for them other than the safety of the ark which they had rejected long before the flood."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is roughly twenty-two feet deep, that’s above the TOPS of the mountains. The peak of Mount Everest (which is the tallest mountain in the world) is twenty-nine thousand and twenty-nine feet ABOVE sea level. If the sea level reached all the way up to twenty-nine thousand and twenty-nine feet, and then there was added twenty-two feet extra, you can kind of get the picture of how much water there was on the Earth. It’s a seemingly impossible amount of water. It takes about one hundred and fifty days (according to the time period here) for the water to rise and then abate. Seventy days for the Earth to dry. So for three hundred and seventy days, Noah and his family are floating in this ark. We will see more of that timeline in the next chapter, but for one hundred and fifty days the earth floods."}]},{"start":21,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Nothing survived. This is not just a guess. There was no mountain for anyone to hide on top of. No submarine they could live in for a while. No air pockets they could stay in for over a year and survive. God is not lying when He says, everything with “the breath of life” was killed off."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There have been some who have tried to claim this flood was only located in the vicinity in which Noah and these great and terrible people were. I don’t believe Moses made a mistake when writing that the WHOLE EARTH was flooded."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"And the waters prevailed exceedingly upon the earth; and all the high hills, that were under the whole heaven, were covered.\" Genesis 7:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"God won’t exaggerate the truth."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Living in the desert has it’s advantages and disadvantages. One disadvantage is the lack of rain we receive. Then we have the advantage of God providing rain. Then there’s the disadvantage of floods occurring, and people seem to lose all sense of meaning when it starts to rain, causing car accidents and drifting, and issues of all sorts. After a day of rain (though much needed) we are over it. One hundred and fifty days of rain would be quite painful. That’s nearly five months of rain! Five months of living with animals in a boat with a family in no other direction to go but to sit and wait. And it’s not even over yet."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":24}]},
    "8": {"chapter":8,"intro":[],"previously":["Our last chapter saw the final preparations of the ark and the last warnings of Noah before getting on the Ark. Sadly, no one listened to Noah’s pleads for mankind and it was only his family that would be saved from the coming flood. We saw the flood start to take over the earth, and we studied how wide and deep the flood became. This chapter continues with life after the flood, and details how Noah comes to find out that it’s safe to finally leave the ark."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":12,"title":"The Rain Stops","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The first part of this sentence is more important than the provision that God gives. I think I need to say that again... The fact that God remembered Noah, had His thoughts on Noah (a mere and insignificant man) is FAR ABOVE AND BEYOND important than the provision that God gives to Noah. I have a strong argument to say that this is still the most important thing to consider today. We desire and look out for God’s provision... We think that if God isn’t providing for us that we are either doing something wrong, or God is upset with us for something. This MUST be a reminder to us! God does not forget us!"},{"kind":"para","text":"We had best remember that God thinks on His children, because it makes all the difference in our walks and way of life even if we don’t see God’s provision! God could have allowed Noah to stay on the ship for the rest of his life! But if Noah KNEW the fact that God remembered him, Noah would have been content to do it. Maybe Noah didn’t know God was thinking about him. This may have put Noah in a position of uncertainty and worry. But Noah can look back through the oracles of history and know for a certainty that he was remembered by God. Obviously Moses understood this, and that is why he writes it this way. God MUST have remembered, otherwise Noah would still be on that boat, and the earth would not be like it is today."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"For I know the thoughts that I think toward you, saith the Lord, thoughts of peace, and not of evil, to give you an expected end. Then shall ye call upon me, and ye shall go and pray unto me, and I will hearken unto you. And ye shall seek me, and find me, when ye shall search for me with all your heart.\" Jeremiah 29:11-13"}]},{"start":2,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Assuage” means to “make an unpleasant feeling less intense.” In other words the storm was calmed, and the ship was steady over the water. It was far more bearable for the ship to rest on the water. With as big of a ship as it were, I don’t know if Noah really felt the brutality of the waters or not. I’ve been on large boats in the past (we went on a week long cruise ship) and never felt the waters below me at all. Actually, you didn’t really even know you were on the water until you went outside and saw it for yourself. Of course, today’s ships were much different than what Noah built, but I believe (due to the size mostly) that Noah, his family, and the animals sat pretty comfortably on the ship even during the storm. I couldn’t say, however, because I wasn’t there. It took one hundred and fifty days for the waters to rise, and now there will be about two hundred and sixty-one days of this “assuaging” to come."}]},{"start":5,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The flood is starting to end at this point, and there is hope in Noah’s eyes of being able to get off the boat and back onto land to live out the rest of his time on Earth. Perhaps Noah had it in the back of his mind that he would die on the boat. He really wanted to see his family get to safety I’m sure, and so he anticipated that the Lord would allow him that grace."}]},{"start":7,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don’t imagine that Noah can see out of the boat at this particular time. There’s a large opening at the top of the boat, but that’s about it. There’s no way for him to know if it’s safe to open the door or not, so he uses a fair resource to make sure it was safe. Honestly, this is pretty smart. The birds would have no choice but to come back if there was no where for them to land, because Noah’s ark was the only resting place. The birds would be dumb to not come back to the safety of the ark... There’s a lot of symbolism there that I won’t get into at this moment..."}]},{"start":9,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A dove is used as a symbol several times over in the Bible..."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"And John bare record, saying, I saw the Spirit descending from heaven like a dove, and it abode upon him.\" John 1:32"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Behold, I send you forth as sheep in the midst of wolves: be ye therefore wise as serpents, and harmless as doves.\" Matthew 10:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"Doves are a sign of peace. Olives and their branches were used often as well throughout scripture..."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"And if some of the branches be broken off, and thou, being a wild olive tree, wert grafted in among them, and with them partakest of the root and fatness of the olive tree;\" Romans 11:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"But I am like a green olive tree in the house of God: I trust in the mercy of God for ever and ever.\" Psalm 52:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"His branches shall spread, and his beauty shall be as the olive tree, and his smell as Lebanon.\" Hosea 14:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, from the beginning, God has taken special care and note of doves and olives. What did this symbolize for Noah? It symbolized freedom and promise! He can finally leave the safety of the ark to start life over again. The real issue, however, was the fact that his ark didn’t transform him into anything else. We see man literally stay the course in the same sinful way they did before the flood. All this flood did was give evidence to the fact that God pays attention to what we do here, that God is righteous, and that we are sinful, terrible beings. There’s also a lot of insight on the two birds."},{"kind":"para","text":"Remember that Noah sent out a raven in verse 7, but the raven never came back to the ark. Ravens are satisfied with feeding on the flesh of dead things. There were, no doubt, plenty of dead things for the raven to eat. It enjoyed this new world because there was enough for him to indulge himself in that he didn’t have to worry about anything else. The DOVE, however, went out into the world and found nothing worth resting on and returned back. It wasn’t until there was absolute safety and peace that the dove does not return."},{"kind":"para","text":"If we were to compare ourselves with these two birds, many would find themselves in this same issue. Our flesh likes the filth of the world, and we gravitate towards that. We enjoy going against God’s commands. Skipping church on a Sunday for a party, or for television is proof of that. That’s our sinful nature. That’s the nature of the raven. Our spiritual nature thrives and thirsts for Godly provision and peace. That was the nature of the dove."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"For I know that in me (that is, in my flesh,) dwelleth no good thing: for to will is present with me; but how to perform that which is good I find not.\" Romans 7:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"There’s much more I could go into in these verses, even accounting for the last days of Revelation, because much of what happens then is pictured here with these two birds, except there will be an ultimate change at that time, for sin and hell will be cast into the lake of fire."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"And death and hell were cast into the lake of fire. This is the second death.\" Revelation 20:14"}]}]},{"start":13,"end":19,"title":"The Earth Dries","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So this says it took about two hundred and sixty-one days, which equals out to three hundred and seventy-one days in total on this ark. When it uses this six hundred and one year terminology, it is referencing the lifespan of Noah. This would have been the six hundred and first day of Noah’s life. Noah took off the covering of the ark first and foremost, and was excited to see the ground! Much of us after a few minutes of a crazy driver, or a wild rollercoaster are happy to plant our feet back on solid ground. Some are deathly terrified of flying in planes, and the first thing they do when they get off of one is thank God for the ground. Noah was in this same boat (no pun intended)."}]},{"start":14,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Noah hears the Lord’s command to take everything out of the ark. Would Noah have forgotten to let everything lose if God didn’t say something? Maybe Noah was so enamored by the event that had taken place, and now by the fact that he gets to stand on dry land that he couldn’t even think straight about what to do next. So God interrupts and says, “Noah, don’t forget about the animals and the rest of your family on the ark. This was the whole reason I saved you from the judgement to begin with!”"}]},{"start":18,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was a triumphant day, no doubt about it. They had just experienced one of the craziest historical events known to man. Even crazier, we have a very accurate timeline and timeframe of when it took place. There are many dates and numbers thrown into these few chapters, specifically because God didn’t want any one to question (later on) that this actually happened. God knew there would be many who would rob this occasion as nothing more than a story for little kids. This is not just a story. There is too much evidence to state that."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":22,"title":"Noah Offers Sacrifice","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Noah was astounded, and it shows in this verse alone. This is why Noah took clean beasts into the ark. He has the opportunity, now, to show his thankfulness to the Lord by sacrificing something extremely valuable and rare at this particular time. There wasn’t many beasts left on the Earth. It was, however, quite evident to Noah that God could always provide more, and there was nothing too large to offer to the Lord. This, of course, is a small picture of Jesus Christ and His sacrifice for us. Noah is sacrificing to God for praise. God sacrifices His Son for salvation."}]},{"start":21,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are many today who have rejected this promise of God. There is not a mention of the proof of this promise being kept (yet) but you can take God’s Word as absolute even without the proof. God always proves Himself though, and He will on this occasion, and I think it’s specifically because of Noah’s act of worship and trust here. If Noah didn’t worship the Lord in the way he did (without even being taught how to do it mind you), the promise may have not been made. But God also already knew that the promise was going to be made."},{"kind":"para","text":"So the question is really begged, does God make promises based off of man’s acts of worship and faith, or does God promise whether people are faithful or not? I have an argument for both, but I do think that God makes promises whether people are obedient or not."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"That ye may be the children of your Father which is in heaven: for he maketh his sun to rise on the evil and on the good, and sendeth rain on the just and on the unjust.\" Matthew 5:45"},{"kind":"para","text":"I’d argue that God would have still kept His promise to never flood the earth again, whether Noah did this or not, but it just made it easier for God to make that promise because man showed care back to God. While the world tries to talk about global warming, or global cooling, or climate change, God’s Word says He will keep the seasons and the weather the way it needs to be until the Earth is over and His final judgement comes."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":22}]},
    "9": {"chapter":9,"intro":[],"previously":["We’ve been going through the story of Noah and the flood, how the ark was built, what it might have looked like, how the animals went in, and how long he had been floating in the depths of the water. The last chapter saw the water decrease, and Noah finally found himself and his family on dry land. The first thing he does is sacrifice to the Lord and thank Him for saving his family alive during this crazy event in history. If only the rejoicing lasted forever..."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":17,"title":"God's Covenant With Noah","entries":[{"start":1,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God gave Adam and Eve the command to “replenish” the earth with his offspring."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"And God blessed them, and God said unto them, Be fruitful, and multiply, and replenish the earth, and subdue it: and have dominion over the fish of the sea, and over the fowl of the air, and over every living thing that moveth upon the earth.\" Genesis 1:28"},{"kind":"para","text":"I find that terminology fascinating. “Replenish” In chapter 1 comes from the Hebrew “mala” which means “to fill” or “to satisfy.” “Replenish” in Chapter 9 comes from the same Hebrew word. To our understanding the prefix “re” insinuates that there was a filling prior to that. For instance, you don’t usually refill an empty cereal container with Mac and Cheese. You would either fill it for the first time with Mac and Cheese, or “RE” fill it with cereal (if there had been cereal in it before). You get the picture. God is saying in the beginning with Adam that there must have been something that was living before Adam that Adam needed to “re” fill the Earth with. Human or not, I could only speculate. Either way, the command is still given here with Noah, in the same way it was given to Adam. I have no doubt, things were much quieter for Noah and his family during this time, and it may have been somewhat eerie and depressing (more on that in a moment)."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is the first time that God has given man meat as provision. Before the flood, everyone was vegetarian. After the flood, animals were not just given for sacrifice, but for provision. This will tie in well with Christ later. More interesting, however, is the next verse..."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Consuming blood was NOT allowed in this new Earth. God confirms that blood is proof of life, and there was no way someone could drink blood and be satisfied. In fact, God knew that if you drank blood, it would only cause increased problems. Why does this matter? Because God cares for His creation. He doesn’t want the sacrifices of animals to be wasteful, and the best way to be sure the animal is dead, is to drain the blood from the animals first."}]},{"start":5,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I grouped these two verses together, because by themselves it doesn’t make much sense. We can get confused and say that eating blood from animals would (or could) be considered such a high offense to the Lord that animals will kill and attack you if you are not careful. But really, God is making a next point in this new covenant, which is the importance of abstaining from murder! We don’t just kill to kill. There’s a purpose for killing, and it’s not to callously take life. Rather, taking life requires the life of the person who took it."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is the first protection of man that God implements upon man. He says that justice must be served for someone who commits murder, and if that person is guilty of taking life, than it is justified that his life should be taken. This will ultimately make people think twice before committing heinous crimes like murder. Our country today has largely gotten away from this idea of capital punishment. It’s either life sentences or a slap on the wrist these days. This doesn’t decrease the response of sin and evil, but only increases it. Because people know there is no serious punishment for their crimes, they have an easier time committing those crimes, and so crime rates spike due to lack of authoritative control. There are times when people are found innocent from a crime they were accused of, and God speaks about that in later chapters (specifically Exodus). But the gist of it is, you should not murder, and that includes purposely killing animals just for the fun of it."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God makes it abundantly clear what Noah’s mission is. I don’t believe that God thinks or assumes that Noah will become a murderer. But God wants Noah to understand that the important thing to pass down to the following generations is the importance of life, because the future generations (especially in the generations today) will have very little interest or importance laid upon life (both of man and animals)."}]},{"start":8,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is us! We are from the seed of Noah. We are from the seed of Adam. But Noah (being the benefactor of restarting the human race) is our direct descendant, and the promise that was given to Noah here, is the same promise that is given to us today. The promise is also made to the animals. The animals don’t know it, and they don’t understand it, but God is promising all of creation something very important, and it’s proven even still today. We see signs of it in animals (not all animals are murderous beasts who feed on flesh just for the fun of it), and we see signs of it in humans (not all humans are murderous beasts who feed on flesh just for the fun of it). Isaiah even prophesy’s about the lion and the lamb laying down together rather than the lion killing the lamb."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"The wolf also shall dwell with the lamb, and the leopard shall lie down with the kid; and the calf and the young lion and the fatling together; and a little child shall lead them. And the cow and the bear shall feed; their young ones shall lie down together: and the lion shall eat straw like the ox. 8 And the sucking child shall play on the hole of the asp, and the weaned child shall put his hand on the cockatrice' den. They shall not hurt nor destroy in all my holy mountain: for the earth shall be full of the knowledge of the Lord, as the waters cover the sea.\" Isaiah 11:6-9"},{"kind":"para","text":"The promise doesn’t just stop there though..."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This promise has been given to Noah specifically, but it was a promise that is passed down to all generations. The next time God destroys the Earth, it will not be by flood. His promise will stay true. Rather it will be by fire. Furthermore, God does something He doesn’t even have to do and it still astounds scientists today..."}]},{"start":12,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That promise is still evident today. The bow of God is still a sign that God takes care of us. Scientists have studied rainbows for years. They have a very interesting grasp on what causes rainbows which is namely an infraction of light. Light peers through the rainbow causing the colors to shine. This has given us understanding that our eyes don’t see color in a normal way. For instance, color is solemnly based on the eye’s perspective. All color we see is actually white light, but the light reflecting from our eyes translates it to different colors. It’s all very complicated and interesting, and I won’t spend a great deal of time on that particular viewpoint (no pun intended), but the rainbow shows colors in a very different and complex way."},{"kind":"para","text":"Still, today, scientists don’t fully understand why rainbows show the way they do. They don’t know why they arch the way they do, or why It’s always those colors that appear in the way they do. The picture here, though, is that God made a promise and PROVED His promise tangibly. It’s something we can physically see. This isn’t always how He displays His promises, but it is how He displays His most important ones for us to have faith."}]},{"start":14,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The fascinating picture here, is not so much that God has proven His promise to us. That, in itself, is quite fascinating. Rather, the most fascinating part is that God uses this as a reminder to Himself that He won’t destroy Earth in this way again. You say, “Why would God need to remind Himself not to destroy the Earth like that again? Would He really forget?” The answer is simple, and obviously He wouldn’t forget. Even people who don’t know Christ personally know the story of Noah and the flood. This sign is not for God’s remembrance, but it’s a sign for God’s mercy. He is going to see the world for the next (however) many years, and man will fall quickly all over again (we see it, in fact, within the next few verses with NOAH). God will see the sin of man (which is the betrayal of man towards God) and it will hurt."},{"kind":"para","text":"God does not like to see His children reject Him, scorn Him, mock Him, or hate Him, but that is exactly what man does every day. God is just, and holy, and has a right to enact judgement on His creation (it’s HIS creation, the keyword being “HIS”), and so He makes promises not just for us, but for Himself so that He can justify His mercy. We don’t deserve to be justified for our sins. But He finds every means possible to justify us. Then it was a rainbow (and that promise was not meant to justify us from sin, but it was to justify Him from never flooding the whole Earth to kill everything again). It was a sacrifice for sins (a constant work seeing as there was no meaningful sacrifice that could be given (an animal might have been killed in our place, but could never live a perfect life for us))."},{"kind":"para","text":"Today it is the sacrifice of Christ which justifies us. God see’s Christ’s sacrifice and it helps Him to remember mercy, not sin. If we are not under the sacrifice of Christ, God will only see sin, and there’s no promise given to those under sin for God’s mercy... There’s no bow to cover their sin."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The promise lasts for all generations. You could bet the bank on it."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":29,"title":"Sin Continues","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I would love to end this chapter off in verse 17 because the process that unfolds in this last half are really just disappointing. Focusing on verse 18, though, we have a little bit of a lineage story to Noah after the flood. Canaan being mentioned would have been a great sign for the children of Israel, seeing as they were heading to the land of Canaan while Moses was writing this account. It probably gave the readers hope as they were traveling."}]},{"start":19,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I have the daunting and undesirable task of speaking of this difficulty with Noah. There are men far more capable and better studied than me that could access the sin here and deal with it applicably. I will say, though, that when a faithful man in the Bible is found in sin, it leaves a rather large impact on society today. A lot of people justify their sins based on the sins of others from the Bible. For instance, a large section of the Mormon church has justified polygamy because David and Solomon had hundreds and thousands of wives so therefore God must be okay with it. This is not true. When God points sin out in the Bible from the most faithful characters, it is not to try and give off an idea that things are okay, but rather lessons that should be learned."},{"kind":"para","text":"When you go to Hebrews 11, Noah is mentioned (along with other great characters of the faith) and given a brief, yet useful, synopsis of his testimony. This was not the testimony that is given, but rather his FAITH is all that is mentioned. The real sin here, is not so much that he was uncovered in his tent. The sin here was of his drunkenness. Why? Well being intoxicated will cause you to say and do some really stupid things, and with the favor of God on your life, you are not to make big mistakes with your words or actions being under the influence of something else entirely... It is also associated that being “uncovered” has more explicitly sexual implications in scripture."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"A man shall not take his father's wife, nor discover his father's skirt.","ref":"Deuteronomy 22:30"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"None of you shall approach to any that is near of kin to him, to uncover their nakedness: I am the LORD. The nakedness of thy father, or the nakedness of thy mother, shalt thou not uncover: she is thy mother; thou shalt not uncover her nakedness. The nakedness of thy father's wife shalt thou not uncover: it is thy father's nakedness. The nakedness of thy sister, the daughter of thy father, or daughter of thy mother, whether she be born at home, or born abroad, even their nakedness thou shalt not uncover. The nakedness of thy son's daughter, or of thy daughter's daughter, even their nakedness thou shalt not uncover: for their's is thine own nakedness. The nakedness of thy father's wife's daughter, begotten of thy father, she is thy sister, thou shalt not uncover her nakedness. Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy father's sister: she is thy father's near kinswoman. Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy mother's sister: for she is thy mother's near kinswoman. Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy father's brother, thou shalt not approach to his wife: she is thine aunt. Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy daughter in law: she is thy son's wife; thou shalt not uncover her nakedness. Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy brother's wife: it is thy brother's nakedness. Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of a woman and her daughter, neither shalt thou take her son's daughter, or her daughter's daughter, to uncover her nakedness; for they are her near kinswomen: it is wickedness. Neither shalt thou take a wife to her sister, to vex her, to uncover her nakedness, beside the other in her life time. Also thou shalt not approach unto a woman to uncover her nakedness, as long as she is put apart for her uncleanness. Moreover thou shalt not lie carnally with thy neighbour's wife, to defile thyself with her.","ref":"Leviticus 18:6-20"},{"kind":"para","text":"Whether or not this was what was happening I could not say. It seems absolutely absurd if it is, but I suppose it wouldn’t be too far fetched with the way in which sin runs rampant today. There are far more heinous crimes today (sadly). One such crime recently was about a homosexual father awaiting to adopt a child as soon as it was born to do disgusting acts with it (speaking about it through text messages to a friend of his). Either way this was not a good thing for either Noah or his son to experience and we can blame it entirely on the sin of alcohol abuse."}]},{"start":22,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It is far more likely that Ham just saw his father and made fun of him to his brothers. That is likely the play here rather than something sexual. His sons try to cover the sin. But what happens next is where the drunkenness becomes the issue, because what he is about to say will be a promise of the covenant..."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The curse was specifically on Canaan, even though it was Shem and Japheth who placed the garment. Ham was the one that saw the issue, (which is the father of Canaan) and so Noah put the full blame on his grandson Canaan. The Canaanites were now cursed for the sake of God’s appointed purpose (which we will study out more thoroughly through Moses in Deuteronomy, and Joshua). Obviously, the Canaanites have disappeared, so the curse that Noah speaks of here was initiated and kept."},{"kind":"para","text":"The ultimate purpose of this particular story, however, is to show us the weakness of flesh. This man (Noah) could be labelled as a “Titan of the Faith.” People know Noah and the ark, but they often reject or dismiss Noah and his sin. People know David and Goliath, but they neglect David and his sin. People know Peter and the Apostle, but they often neglect Peter and the Apostles sins. If these Titan’s of the faith can be found falling (some continuously I might add) then why should it be any surprise to us when we fall?"},{"kind":"para","text":"We have spent millions of dollars flying people into outer space. There is millions (if not billions) of dollars spent on preparing for a civilization on Mars. This sounds amazing, and it sounds interesting, but in the end, the issues of the Earth will still translate over to another planet. God did not place us on other planets. He placed us on this one, and gave us a manual about how to live here. We can’t do that very well as it is, why would we try to inhabit other planets when we can’t even follow God’s order for this one? We are sinful by nature, and it’s something that God has been working on for generations, including in the most notable characters of the faith (save Jesus Christ of course)."}]},{"start":26,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The important thought to remember is that Moses is giving the account here. This was a promise to the children of Israel because they came from the line of Shem. So the thought and the promise that Canaan was going to become the servants of Shem gave them courage, because that was the land that is promised to them to inhabit."}]},{"start":28,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Noah lived to a ripe young age of nine hundred and fifty years old before he died. That’s quite impressive. Length of life was given to him specifically to see the world be replenished, and for the story of God’s work to be passed down correctly through his children and grandchildren (and possibly great grandchildren (something that will be spoken about in the next chapter)). Many people know the surface level of Noah’s story, but this is the real uncut reality of Noah’s life, and it is left off on a bit of a sour note. Of course that sour note means hope for the future, but to that time it meant curses from your drunken grandfather who was to be the express image of God."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":29}]},
    "10": {"chapter":10,"intro":[],"previously":["We saw God make a covenant with Noah which is still evidenced today. Everything seemed to look well. The flood was over, life was still preserved, and Noah was given the commission to multiple and restart life... however, sin did not cease and so Noah (though started off strong) ended poorly.","In this chapter, we will get an account of Noah’s lineage leading to the next big character of this historical book of the Bible Abram (which will be picked up in chapter 12)."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"The Sons of Japheth","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Often when we look at genealogies, they are easily skipped over. We can’t pronounce the names, so therefore we just skim through it, or we just skip it entirely. I don’t want to do that when we go through this. Especially this lineage here. We will see some very interesting and important people and locations that will pop up later on in other books that we need to take note of where they came from and why they ended up where they are later."}]},{"start":2,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The first highlighted child’s lineage is on Japheth. Some names to remember and focus on would be Magog (which is mentioned throughout scripture, including in the last book of the Bible)..."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"And shall go out to deceive the nations which are in the four quarters of the earth, Gog and Magog, to gather them together to battle: the number of whom is as the sand of the sea.\" Revelation 20:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"Tarshish will be another name that may sound familiar to most. Tarshish was a land known for their idolatry and evil. It was not a land that was very prosperous by means of the Lord. It was the land that Jonah was planning to run to so he didn’t have to preach to Nineveh..."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"But Jonah rose up to flee unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD, and went down to Joppa; and he found a ship going to Tarshish: so he paid the fare thereof, and went down into it, to go with them unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD.\" Jonah 1:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems this is where the Gentiles got their start and where they were divided away from the children of Israel. Of course, salvation still belongs to them, but it was through the children of Israel where salvation would be proclaimed and shown first, which takes place in the book of Acts, and throughout the rest of the Old Testament."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":20,"title":"The Sons of Ham","entries":[{"start":6,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Next up is the lineage of Ham. Some more prominent names are mentioned in this lineage as well. Canaan will be a place that is commonly referred to throughout scripture. It is the place that is promised to the children of Israel, which we learned through the curse that Noah brings to him in the last chapter..."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"And he said, Cursed be Canaan; A servant of servants shall he be unto his brethren.\" Genesis 9:25"},{"kind":"para","text":"Canaan will give lineage to the Phoenicians, Hittites, the Jebusites, the Amorites, the Girgashites, the Hivites, and so on... Cush will bring the lineage of the Africans such as the Ethiopians, the Egyptians, the Libyans and so on. What God’s Word seems to highlight here, however, is on Cush’s son Nimrod. It seems the term for “hunter” here is not set on the idea that he was a “game” hunter. It seems the hunter that is being referred to is “in the Lord.” In other words, he may have been a soul winner."},{"kind":"para","text":"Nimrod is not a name that is commonly associated with good character today. Nimrod wanted his name to be great among the Earth, and he was positioning his life specifically to do that. However, we notice one thing he is responsible for, and it wasn’t something that was very Godly in nature. It will be displayed in the next chapter, but it is loosely mentioned here, as it says he founded the kingdom of Babel. His intentions may have been on the good of unity, but ultimately his motives were selfish. He was desiring to be the one on top. He wanted his name to be great. This is a great tie-in to the antichrist and last world ruler. This would be the first great civilization to have ever existed, and history is just starting to catch up to what the Bible has already said for thousands of years..."}]},{"start":16,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"More names that will become more familiar in the chapters ahead. Gaza, Sodom, Gomorrah. These are places that will be visited during the life of Abram which will take place in a couple of chapters."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":32,"title":"The Sons of Shem","entries":[{"start":21,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When we come to Shem, it seems there aren’t many here to recognize. Uz made a name for himself in the book of Job (which was actually written BEFORE Genesis believe it or not)."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"There was a man in the land of Uz, whose name was Job; and that man was perfect and upright, and one that feared God, and eschewed evil.\" Job 1:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"It is quite likely that Job is a descendent of Shem, and therefore the reason that he is found living in that land. In fact, it is said that the book was set during the time of the Patriarchs (which was Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob). We cannot be sure, but it would be my guess seeing as the family lineage doesn’t usually go too far from the land they grew up in (at least until Abram (spoiler alert))."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It is suffice to use this chapter to give an accurate lineage to the line of Noah, and could be thoroughly used to draw down the line towards your own family history, or draw up to his. It would take years and it would be complicated, but it would be accurate, and historically proven. This will not be the last time we get this much information about Noah either. They will give the lineage again through 1 Chronicles."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":32}]},
    "11": {"chapter":11,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter we explored the names of multiple people through the line and lineage of Noah and his sons. Some names were familiar, while others will eventually get lost by a dying out (something that will be spoken on more thoroughly through Exodus, Leviticus, Numbers, and Deuteronomy). We saw the three different sons of Noah and their lineage, and talked about the promises made to Noah’s son (through his drunkenness) Shem and Ham."},{"kind":"para","text":"This chapter opens up on a very interesting and important Bible story that is often just glanced at yet everyone knows well."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":9,"title":"The Tower of Babel","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We can never really know what that language might have been. It is assumed that the language was one that was far more adequate, exuberant, and flattering than our current languages today. It’s possible that this language is a language that will never be used again except for in eternity. Would we even know we were speaking that language? Maybe our speech would have a lot more depth to it in Heaven? Who knows, the point is Moses is pointing this out because there was coming a very important change that many of the Israelites might have been questioning at this point. “Why were there so many different cultures, and why do they have different languages than we do?”"}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This plain was located in the Tigris-Euphrates Valley. Interestingly enough, they formed stones from the material that was there because apparently there was no stone for them to use for building. They were the first to make this material? They seemed to already know what it is, so maybe it was something they had learned along their journey of life? Whatever it may have been, it’s pretty obvious that brick making has been important for a very long time. Then, it was necessary. Now, it’s just easy to get."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Notice the terminology used here. “THEY said...let US build US a city and a tower...let US make US a name, lest WE be scattered abroad...” They focused, primarily, upon themselves. They didn’t seek the Lord’s guidance in this, and they were mostly worried about their self-image and their own family lineage. It had always been God that made the promise of continuance in the family lineage, but now these people are taking it upon themselves to be something greater and reject the leading of God. Nimrod was the man who apparently led this charge, and he desired to be a world power that no one had ever seen before. He wanted to have control, and everyone to trust in HIS strength and wisdom. He wanted the world under HIS thumb and power."},{"kind":"para","text":"This tower was to represent unity and prosperity for the people. It is almost like the representation of a church, if you will. It was a false church, that’s to be said. The city was to be great and mighty and powerful, and there will be many more like it that come through the woodworks in the coming chapters, but every city that rejects the Lord suffers grievously. This tower and city was a representation of rejection. It was the exact image of going against what the Lord wanted. It displayed defiance by saying “We will do what we want. God tells us to scatter and inhabit the whole Earth? No, we will meet in one place and make it mighty, even reaching up to Heaven to be above God.”"}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Some would misinterpret what God is ultimately saying here. He’s not putting a cap on knowledge. He’s not restricting their abundance of growth and capabilities. He’s not keeping them from science and solutions. Rather, He sees their sinful nature, and He sees that they are all using their sinful nature to try and do things that would ultimately lead to evil and disgusting acts, and the best way for that to stop is if God restricts their language."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we know where the term “babbler” comes from. These people suddenly didn’t make sense to each other. Certainly, God was saving their lives by keeping them from building a tower that reached so high as to see them die, but He was ultimately keeping them from becoming destructive to the world again. He wanted them to trust in Him and move in His Will, seeking a relationship with Him, not just each other to form diabolical and terrible feats that would amount to nothing more than murder and adultery (and that of every other type of sin imaginable which will be displayed in chapters to come). A great juxtaposition is during the day of Pentecost when they spoke in tongues for the first time. You can read more about that in Acts 2:4-8. In Genesis, the purpose of speaking in tongues was to divide the people so that they couldn’t sin against God. In Acts, the purpose of speaking in tongues was to unite the people so that they could be saved in God. This purpose was out of a love from God, and an ultimate plan for His children, which will be seen in the coming verses..."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":32,"title":"From Shem to Abraham","entries":[{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I hate to bunch so many verses into one small remark, but these names are merely important to the point of leading to Abram and Lot. I do want to highlight, however, that there is usually only one name mentioned from each generation, even though they all had other “sons and daughters.” It’s highlighting these key names because they are important to the lineage of Abraham, which will be a known and important character, not just to the Old Testament and the family that comes from him, but also to the Jews during the days of Christ and even still today."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"As a mental note, Lot is labelled here as Abram’s nephew. That, really, will only be useful later on, and it’s not really that important to remember, but it is something to take into consideration and understand how close they were in relation to each other."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We’ve come to yet another important detail. Women were known to be prosperous through having children. If they didn’t have children, they felt like they had a failed life. If they only had one child, it was a poor life. Children were extremely valuable to them, and they were always thankful for having children. But, we see here, that Abram’s wife, Sarai, cannot have children. This is a heavy conflict in this family, and Abram and Sarai may have felt abandoned by the Lord. Especially when Abram sees his other brother Nahor and his wife Milcah are having no problem childbearing. Their first thoughts probably went to “What did I do wrong that the Lord would do this to us?”"}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This sets us up for the chapters to come. We are now switching stories from Noah and his children to Abram and his struggle with the Lord. We notice that Abram and Sarai (along with Lot) are led by Abram’s father Terah to a new place in the land of Canaan (which is obviously the place where Canaan and his family lived; remember that this is the land in which they will inherit). That promise hasn’t come yet, because as of right now, the lineage of Shem has halted with the issue of Sarai being barren. But we know that God always keeps His promises, and that was something these people were going to have to learn as well."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":32}]},
    "12": {"chapter":12,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter we were able to see the setup of and destruction of the Tower of Babel. We saw the vain goal of human kind to make themselves god. We are still out to try and make ourselves better than we think others are. The Tower of Babel is a great representation of pride. The higher we build our pride, the further our fall will be... We also saw the lineup leading to the account of Abram and his wife Sarai. That’s where this chapter picks up."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will see the theme of faith-building throughout all of scripture, and it usually happens the same way every single time. Abram has such small faith, and will need to be built up to become the man that God needs him to be."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"God's Calling","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God is calling out this man “Abram,” who is to leave everything he knows and bring glory to God through His lineage. Remember, this is a book of lineages. The book of families. We have left the family of Adam to get to the family of Noah, and the family of Noah, now coming to the family of Abram. Abram was from the lineage of Shem (Noah’s son, who received the promise of a righteous lineage from Noah’s drunkenness (which was spoken about in previous chapters)). Shem may not have known the full extent of this promise, nor was it something he might have held tightly to, but this promise is being realized through Abraham now."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will be moving on to yet another promise from God. We saw the Noahic covenant (and we know how it comes to fruition), but now we will see a new covenant that is made, and this time it’s through the lineage of Abram. He tells Abram to get out of the country, away from everything he knows, and he’s going to start a new land, a better land than any other people could even fathom. Not only so, but through Abram’s leadership and lineage, God will make Him a great nation, and will bless his work, allowing Abram’s name to be a blessing."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Jewish lineage still gives credence and honor to the name of Abraham. As well, blessings and curses are given to those that don’t accept (or reject) the lineage account. If you ask a Jewish person about the legitimacy of Abraham’s lineage, you would quickly realize the accuracy of God’s promise here. It wouldn’t be hard to prove the accuracy of Scripture here."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":9,"title":"Abram's Response","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So where is he going? Is he just wandering, and wherever sounds fit for the time is the place in which he will be blessed? No, God had a place set up for him, and this will be the land promised to the Israelites later on (which of course hasn’t happened yet in the story, but as Moses was writing this, I’m sure the children of Israel were excited about this man named Abram). The part that catches me is that Abram disobeyed already... God said, “Get out of the country, and away from your kindred,” and Abram takes his nephew Lot with him."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, taking his wife was fine; it was just taking his nephew that wasn’t in full surrender and obedience to God. It looks as if Ham’s family was already in the land, as is explained in this verse. The land was not better than where they came from. They had a good thing going for them where Abram was living in the land of Ur. We know it was a good place because it’s spoken about highly in the book of Job. Job lived there and was extremely prosperous. He even owned homes for his sons and was rich in cattle and prosperous in everything he did. He was also an extremely faithful man, even during a time of immense testing."},{"kind":"para","text":"What Abram and Sarai were doing here was walking by faith. Sometimes God brings us to a land that is far less than where we are currently, and we seem completely confused as to why. God is doing that with Abram here, and really the purpose was to grow his faith, because Abram was going to need to be strong in faith in order to do what God needed him to. He had an overarching purpose for Abram, and Abram couldn’t complete his purpose by staying where he was. God needed him to increase his faith. Job stayed in the land because his faith was strong. Abram moved from the land because his faith needed more strength."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Sarai, remember, couldn’t have children."},{"kind":"para","text":"Comment 2 \"But Sarai was barren; she had no child.\" Genesis 11:30"},{"kind":"para","text":"This was a faith-building promise from God already. Abram didn’t put much thought into the promise, it seems, and just built an altar there, understanding the fact that he was going to own that land someday. Maybe he thought it would be him (personally) that would own the land, or maybe he missed the part where God says “Unto thy seed...” Either way, this was a big deal, and Abram’s faith would be stretched on this issue alone (but that will come later)."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Knowing it wasn’t time for that land to be his yet, he moved on to a nearby mountain where he could see two different lands, Bethel (which means “House of God”) and Hai (which means “The Heap”). Everywhere he went, he set up an altar for worshiping God. He was grateful for where God was leading him, and was observant about the locations God was taking him. This should be a testimony to us as well."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s okay for us today to build a spiritual altar to the Lord where He leads you. If He has led you to a church house today, build up a spiritual altar there to worship Him. If He has led you to your job today, build up a spiritual altar there to worship Him. If He has led you to the hospital bed today, build up a spiritual altar there to worship Him. If He has led you to the graveside today, build up a spiritual altar there to worship Him. Is that too dark? We should be able to worship in every location the Lord takes us, and if we are not willing to build the altar (or leave a testimony rather), then we can never see how God is moving."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There was still more movement to be had. Of course, we can read this account and think “What a man of faith! He obviously doesn’t need to grow that much in faith, because he seems to be heading in the right direction for the Lord. His faith seems pretty full.” Keep those thoughts in mind as we read the next verses…"}]}]},{"start":10,"end":20,"title":"Abram's Faith Falls","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The most common way for God to test His people has always been through famine and inflation. You will see this over and over again throughout Scripture, and this will happen again on a great scale when Joseph comes into the scene. The point is, people rely too much on money and possessions. How does God suppose He can get our minds set right again? By making money and possessions less valuable. You will either give up all you have to gain very little, or you will give up the little you have to lean on God. Famine is a great way to stop as many people as possible from going in the wrong direction."},{"kind":"para","text":"Think of it this way. There aren’t many people who would be willing to drop everything they are doing to go to a foreign land. Even fewer people would do it with no people around for miles. Even fewer people would do it if you aren’t able to even grow anything in that land. So what do you do? Plant a little closer to towns. You would try to find a local civilization so you have something to fall back on. Abram was supposed to stay in the land where he was, but instead saw an issue and ran to the nearest country for provision."},{"kind":"para","text":"This isn’t even the worst part. I can sympathize with Abram on this part, thinking that there is no way for him to survive if there was a famine (and he probably said “God wants me to work for what I can get”) so he moved on to where he is able to support his wife. That’s the issue with us today. We rely too much on money and provision, and not enough on the help of the Lord. It has always been a problem, obviously. But here’s where the issue really escalates..."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is outright deception. He was assuming the people of the land would take her and kill him if she said he was her husband. Abram wasn’t exactly wrong, but he should have told the truth and allowed the Lord to determine the outcome. He was in conversation with the Lord already, trusted Him this far, and is now taking situations into his own hands. If it were me, and I was worried about being killed or mistreated, I would have just headed back up north where God had told me to go to begin with. But I suppose this was the way in which Abram’s faith would need to be strengthened..."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In those days, it was custom for a woman to be taken and prepared for leadership to be the wife of the Pharaoh, so Abram had assumed this would be the case with his wife, and I believe he feared more for his life being taken than he did for his wife being taken... Abram, in this instance, was living pretty. He traded his most valuable possession (his wife, and YES she was his possession as much as he was hers), and traded her for some sheep, oxen, donkeys, servants, and camels... Today’s equality would probably be for a few million dollars. You can see how much someone truly means to you when money is involved."},{"kind":"para","text":"Again, the issue was (which is the issue still) that Abram was relying on monetary gain and provision from his own hands. He was not simply trusting in the Lord. The Lord, at this point, has every right to just say, “You selfish moron, good luck getting out of this one... you’re on your own.” But God is gracious, and protects them..."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abram left the land more full than he went in, that’s for sure. But at what cost? There’s no amount of marriage counseling that would heal this conversation he will likely have with his wife Sarai. Either way, God protects them. Notice, though, that God doesn’t speak to them in this state. He just provides protection, and it is assumed by Pharaoh that the things that were happening to them were because Abram had lied about his “sister.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"God had been speaking to Abram for some time now, but not in this incident. God has been quietly working to get Abram back where he is supposed to be, and this was the only way for Abram to see how weak and little his faith was. No doubt, this gave Abram a lot to think about (or at least you would hope that he would (more on that later))."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":20}]},
    "13": {"chapter":13,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Abram received a calling from God to leave everything he knew, take his family with him, and go to a new place where the Lord would grow his family. For some reason, he thought it was reasonable to take Lot (his nephew) with him, and now they are wandering for what seems like into nothingness. Because it seems that they are wandering and they are afraid that God won’t provide for them (something that God’s children always worries about), he turns off course from where he is suppose to be going and so he goes to the nearest city (which was Egypt) that has food and supplies to live there. This wasn’t nearly as big of a deal as the deception Abram uses against the leaders there by saying his wife is his sister so that he can be in better terms with the leadership and not have to be kicked out."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see Abram and his family back on course, and we see where it will ultimately lead them allowing God to give the providence and build the faith and trust of Abram and Lot."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":4,"title":"Abram and Lot","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Don’t let it be misconstrued. What Abram did during this time was a great sin, yet his sin paid off for him because now he had great riches. Honestly, the easiest way to become rich in this life is to allow sin to control you rather than being led by the Lord. There will be plenty of examples of that to come throughout Scripture, and it will be quite evident that the people who are rich in this world are the least likely to be saved in the next. Revelation shows a significant number of rich people, and they are all doing heinous and wicked things in order to stay rich and reject the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Because thou sayest, I am rich, and increased with goods, and have need of nothing; and knowest not that thou art wretched, and miserable, and poor, and blind, and naked: I counsel thee to buy of me gold tried in the fire, that thou mayest be rich; and white raiment, that thou mayest be clothed, and that the shame of thy nakedness do not appear; and anoint thine eyes with eyesalve, that thou mayest see.","ref":"Revelation 3:17-18"}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They are now getting back on track and have accepted the direction of the Lord. He is just north of Jerusalem around the location of Bethel. He finally comes back to God and calls out to him in hopes that God will incline an ear to his words. He messed up, and he let his ADD get the best of him, but now he’s back and he’s ready to follow where God would lead and trust in Him a little more. This should be a reminder to all of us."},{"kind":"para","text":"God could easily hear Abraham out and completely reject him now. He could say, “You had your chance, and you blew it, Abram. You were on the right path and you stumbled and turned. There is nothing I hate more than disobedient children. There’s nothing that gets me more upset than when my children leave me and live a sinful life. You’re on your own now.” That, of course, is not what happens. It’s not what happened then, and it’s not what happens now. It doesn’t matter how far we may stray from God, there is always a way back to the altar. God is always there waiting and willing to forgive and move forward."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":13,"title":"The Build of Tension","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Their accumulated wealth was so large that their servants started to argue with each other. Another rippling effect of sin. Sin roots itself deep and causes something that seems valuable to become extremely burdensome. Of course, had they offered this wealth up to the Lord to bless and take care of, it could have been a very different circumstance. That is not, however, what they do. They take this matter into their own hands AGAIN and disregard the Lord’s leading on the issue."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is not to be an issue between brothers of the same family, and if you are a child of God, there must not be quarrels between those you will be spending the rest of eternity with. Sadly, there are many quarrels happening in the church, and it seems that bitterness and strife rise quite fervently over really small and menial things. This fight is over cattle... CATTLE! There has to be a separation now over animals! How they fought and the purpose of it, I don’t know. But it’s the same issue today except with other forms of currency."},{"kind":"para","text":"It could be fights over a lesson or sermon that was given. It could be fights over money (pieces of paper that we value over God’s work). It could be fights over presence (how long so-and-so stays at the church every day). It could be fights over power, acceptance, love, tenderness, grace (or lack thereof), events, gossip, slander... The list really goes on forever. The point is, the church (the body of Christ) is not supposed to be that way!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"By this shall all men know that ye are my disciples, if ye have love one to another.","ref":"John 13:35"},{"kind":"para","text":"We have issues with one another because we have let our greed for more blind our compassion for others. We think we are okay without these brothers in Christ in our lives, and so we cut them out and split them up. We go in separate directions. A church cannot pull in opposite directions without severing a body part."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the eye cannot say unto the hand, I have no need of thee: nor again the head to the feet, I have no need of you.","ref":"1 Corinthians 12:21"},{"kind":"para","text":"Abram is facing a dilemma over something so small and trivial, and it is time to take this to the Lord and allow God to lead... Except that won’t be what happens..."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A very good and important verse to consider, and underline. Why should they not fight? Is it because they love each other? Maybe. Is it because they have a lot in common? Probably. Is it because they are living near each other? Possibly. Is it because they are doing the same work together? Doubtful. No, the reason they shouldn’t fight is because as Abram says, “We be brethren.” We are family. We are co-laborers. We have the same mission, goal, and purpose, and there’s no reason we should be at odds with each other. So what should they do to make this work? GO TO THE LORD..."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"NO! GO TO THE LORD! Why is it that when things fall apart, or when there is a difficult course or task, we always neglect the leading of God? Maybe we think that God will make us do something we are not willing to do (like make amends, or get rid of the contentious workers who are actually causing the division to begin with). Too many churches, places of business, families, and friends all have the same exact issue in common. Instead of dealing with the problem, it’s easier just to get rid of everyone around the problem."},{"kind":"para","text":"The problem will continue to exist, but now we have the problem in smaller groups, ready to multiply at any given time. Mind you, the issue is not between Abram and Lot. The issue is between their workers. They could have easily assessed the issue, and then formed a solution... Instead, Abram says, choose your land. There’s plenty of space for you and your team to venture. You go one way, I’ll go the other. Maybe that’s what the Lord would have told them to do anyway. We will never know because they never went to the Lord about this."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This dynamic plays exactly into the issue that they were experiencing with the herdsmen. The reason they accumulated this wealth to begin with was because they basically tricked the leaders of Egypt into sinning against the Lord. Now they are here with so much wealth that it’s causing issues between them. The wealth looked good, and it probably felt amazing to have because now they had a huge head start on life, but just like the wealth they accumulated, Lot chose the land based off of looks rather than based off of guidance. All of their decisions at this point have been made on their own free volition, and it will not work out for Lot (spoiler alert) even though the grass literally looks greener in the land in which he is going."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abram was in the land that was promised to him, and he lived where God would provide. Obviously, that was not intentional. Abram just went the opposite way of Lot. Even though they did not confer with the Lord about any of this, there was God’s guiding to this moment. Lot makes a HUGE mistake here. He chooses what LOOKS better. Who could blame him? We always desire the nicer plans and the lusher land. No one desires to live in the sand dunes in the desert. We want easy access to the necessities, not to have to work for everything we have. Lot was getting what he wanted, but little did he know who was there..."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I have no doubt in my mind that if Lot knew this, he would have stayed away from this area. He doesn’t know this, though. It will be some time before he even figures this out, and by that time, he will be so settled in and so used to life there that it will be too late for him to go anywhere else. He might even think that this is the only place that was worth staying in because the rest of the land was desert and wilderness. If they had conferred with the Lord, they might have made better decisions to stay where they are or to make things work. That will not be what happens, of course..."},{"kind":"para","text":"May it be noted and well understood. None of this is lost on God. God was watching. He was seeing what they were doing. He kept quiet because He already knew what would take place. He certainly didn’t want Lot to go this direction, but he also didn’t want them to continually make their own decisions without praying to Him first. Their lack of communication with the Lord led to their own separation."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":18,"title":"God's Promise to Abram","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God knew that Lot was separated. He knew that Lot went his own way. He knew that Abram was still in the right place. He tells Abram to look at all the land before him. While Lot is taking the land that was already cultivated, it would be a small acreage compared to the thousands of miles of land that Abram just inherited. Nobody touched the land before he got there. It was all for him. This land was significant to the Lord. It was a land that was rich in God’s eyes. It didn’t seem like much to anyone else, especially at this point, but to God it was the perfect place for His children."},{"kind":"para","text":"Many people think of Heaven in regards to the same thing. “What will be there? Who will be there? It doesn’t sound like a fun place to live. It sounds like a confusing place. Why does it matter if we try to go to that land?” The truth is, we don’t see the land as something valuable because we don’t see what God sees. God sees with eyes of care, desire, and dwelling. He sees the land as a perfect place for Him to be with His people. Heaven is also that perfect place for Him to be with His people. He wants to be amongst us. He wants to have fellowship with us. He wants us to be able to see Him face to face, eye to eye. He wants to be able to talk directly to us. We can’t do that, though, because of our sinful separation. This is why Christ came, and this was the promise that is given to the generations to come. More on that later."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It’s the land that was promised to the lineage of Shem. It’s the land that Noah said would be taken from Ham (through the Canaanites). The children of Canaan may not have known it yet, but Israel was being built up to take that land which God will accept the promise for. He gives this promise to Abram here and says, “This is yours, and it will be for you, your children, and for their children after that!” There was nothing that would be able to separate or keep God’s children from inheriting the Land that God promises. Even if it seems bleak and dismal (and it will), God still keeps His promise here, and they WILL eventually take this land just as God had said."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abram doesn’t batt an eye at this... Remember his wife can’t have children."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But Sarai was barren; she had no child.","ref":"Genesis 11:30"},{"kind":"para","text":"He may have thought “Well, obviously the Lord is going to provide me with someone who is going to be able to have kids.” We know this will be his thought later because he tries to take this issue into his own hands, and it doesn’t work out in his favor. But it’s evident to God (and will be proven later) that Abram was going to have a multitude of children."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abram continues to leave a testimony to the Lord. He builds a new altar to the promise that God had just made to him, and it will be a reminder for him and everyone around him that God had done something huge that day. Sadly, it doesn’t seem Abram really looks back on these altars that he’s building, and so the promises become forgotten, and the trust begins to dwindle."},{"kind":"para","text":"“Mamre” means “richness” and “Hebron” means “communion.” The land that Lot took seemed rich in wealth and prosperity. But the land that Abram took was rich in communion with the Lord. That is the best land to be in. I’d rather have rich communion with God than to have all the riches of this world..."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Charge them that are rich in this world, that they be not highminded, nor trust in uncertain riches, but in the living God, who giveth us richly all things to enjoy;","ref":"1 Timothy 6:17"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":18}]},
    "14": {"chapter":14,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: We lost see Abram and Lot (and their collective family) continue their journey to the promised land (before it is the promised land of course). Abram and Lot have a lot of wealth from where they had just left in Egypt (they kind of stole it but that's a discussion for a different time), and now they are travelling to find a new place to dwell (in the direction they were suppose to go to begin with). Issues arose between their servants, and it made it difficult for Lot and Abram to stay together, so they decide to split up and go in separate ways. Lot takes the land that looks good and easy to live in. Abram accepts the land that has nothing but potential."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we continue to see how Abram grows, the cities around him grows, and ultimately where the Lord is going to lead these two families and how it will affect the future generations."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":12,"title":"The First War","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Remember that Lot took the land in Sodom and Gomorrah because it looked pleasant. Now he's going to be in the middle of a leadership war. The kings of the east seemed to think this land was valuable as well and were willing to do whatever it took to take it. This is the first recorded war we have in history. I'm guessing this wasn't the first war, but it's the first one we have an accurate depiction of."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems the war started off of a rebellion. They were tired of serving this city just to see it prosper more than their own. So the best plan of action was to take it by force."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Mount Seir is a place that will be more widely known to those wandering in the wilderness while this was being written. It is where the Israelites will be familiar with God's leading them."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he said, The LORD came from Sinai, and rose up from Seir unto them; he shined forth from mount Paran, and he came with ten thousands of saints: from his right hand went a fiery law for them.","ref":"Deuteronomy 33:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"At this time, however, Seir is a place of war."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was not a small war or battle. There was a lot of ruling authority coming into the scene to try and stop this overthrow. The kings of this time must have been pretty fierce men, and they must have been quite strong to enter battle with their people. It is usually the king that goes into battle last, but there must have been a very large commotion that took them from their places of authority to enter this battle."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You can be ruler of the greatest nation known to history and still end up in the slimepits. May it be a reminder to us. When we think we have it all, and we are everything and then some, we must be cautious about the war that is looming, because the only place to hide is in the darkest and dirtiest places that are left..."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Yes, it's very sad that they took a bunch of material possessions, but the saddest part is they took Lot. This record is important because we have one of the main characters found here being held captive, and Abram needs to do something to protect his family, whom he loves."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":16,"title":"Abram's New Boldness","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abram was scared of the king of Egypt not that long ago. He lied to him so that his wife wouldn't be taken. Now his nephew is taken, and he suddenly develops enough boldness to gather three hundred and eighteen trained servants (servants of war? Probably not) to join him to get back his nephew. It seems he might love his nephew Lot more than he loves his own wife. Maybe I should just give him the benefit of the doubt here, though. The important part to note here is that this army of servants was \"confederate\" with Abram. They were in it to death."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Strangely enough, they were successful. It almost sounds too unreal, but I also know that God watches out for His children. This was not about killing and making war. This was about protecting His children. Abram was successful because he planned out the attack quickly. He surrounded these men and took them down before they even knew what was happening."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abram, the vigilante. The Savior of the people. They were taking these people to be slaves. The material was precious cargo, but the people would provide more for them than just the material. They were going to be treated as property, but Abram stopped that from happening. He didn't just rescue his nephew this day."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":24,"title":"Enter King/Priest Melchizedek","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This king is something spectacular. Abram is the focus of this story, of course, but this king means quite a lot to us today. You might think to yourself, \"I don't get it. He’s just happy to be with the victor of this war. What's so special about this king?\" He was the priest of the most high God. There weren’t many in this day that were looking to the Lord. Even Abram had his faith dilemmas. This king priest is a type of Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are very few of these characters in the Bible who display this kind of characteristic of Christ. Boaz in Ruth is a type of Christ. It is said that Noah is a type of Christ. Enoch. Abraham. Isaac. Jacob. Joseph. Moses. We can go down a whole list. This king Melchizedek is the most fascinating to me, and it's widely talked about in Hebrews to represent some very important factors. We don't know where he comes from. We don't know how he became a priest (before there were any priests ever created or made). We don't know how he knows the Lord so well. We don't see anything about his death. He is also a kind king, gracious, and thankful (as we will see in a moment). There's a lot of conversation to be had about this amazing and random king priest that we could ask a lot of questions about. I think it's best to leave those questions with Hebrews, however, because it is very deeply outlined in that book."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For this Melchisedec, king of Salem, priest of the most high God, who met Abraham returning from the slaughter of the kings, and blessed him; To whom also Abraham gave a tenth part of all; first being by interpretation King of righteousness, and after that also King of Salem, which is, King of peace; Without father, without mother, without descent, having neither beginning of days, nor end of life; but made like unto the Son of God; abideth a priest continually.","ref":"Hebrews 7:1-3"}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This king knew that God was watching over Abram. He knew that Abram was something special to the Lord. He was being extremely gracious to Abram at this time. Abram wasn't trying to be a hero; he just wanted to protect his own family, but in so doing, he met an extremely astounding king and was rewarded for his victory."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was a humble thing for Abram to do. Of course, he didn't need anything of the spoil. Just by the fact that he had three hundred and eighteen servants ready for battle means he had a lot of riches already. The king of Egypt had already set him up well. He didn't need any of this spoil; he just needed his nephew Lot to be taken care of. The only thing that he couldn't give back was the food and supplies that his servants had already taken for themselves after the battle. It would seem that Abram was happy. That there was nothing else he needed... At least until the next chapter…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":24}]},
    "15": {"chapter":15,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Our last chapter saw the amazing triumph of Abram rescuing Lot and the others captured by an evil ruler trying to take over his land. Abram seems to grow a new boldness, gets his servants to go with him into battle, and is victorious! He then meets King Melchizedek who is a very interesting and strange king. One that will be talked about much more in the book of Hebrews."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see Abram make a covenant with Lord, accepting God's promises on his life, through a tradition that is not common today."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":11,"title":"Abram Requests God's Provision","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abram was a great warrior at this point. He had just finished rescuing his family, and was sitting pretty well off. The King Melchizedek (this random priest of God, when priests weren't even created yet) gave Abram a very high honor. He reminded Abram that the Lord was with him in this journey, and that Abram would be greatly used of God. Yet, here is Abram, and it seems like he's down on his luck. He still feels as if God isn't fully paying attention to him. He may have been successful at saving his nephew, but he didn't have something that he had been wanting for a long time and it was something that only the Lord could provide."},{"kind":"para","text":"God shows up to him in a vision and says something pretty astounding! He says \"I am your shield, and your exceeding great reward.\" In other words, God has provided him with protection (that was pretty obvious in the last chapter) but God is also everything that Abram needs. In fact, God is MORE than what Abram needs. That is a great lesson for us. We don't have much, or maybe we are struggling with what we do have, but God says, \"Don't worry, I am your protection, and I am MORE than enough for you.\""}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abram tells God that he doesn't want more wealth (he turned all the wealth he could have down in the last chapter). He wants children. He wants a son that can pass down his family name. God had already promised Abram that he would have children."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"for all land which thou seest, to thee will I give it, and to thy seed for ever. And I will make thy seed as the dust of the earth: so that if a man can number the dust of the earth, then shall thy seed also be numbered.","ref":"Genesis 13:15-16"},{"kind":"para","text":"Abram was now wondering where that promise was? He's not getting any younger, and it doesn't seem possible to even have children at this point, so what's the hold-up? The only other person that could give him a child would be a servant that lives in his house with him."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God makes it abundantly clear here that Abram is to wait for the Lord to allow a child. This is something we must remember because Abram makes a mistake in the future, not paying attention to God's Word here. The promise was going to come true (either way), Abram just needed to wait and be patient."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Can you number the stars? I remember when I was a little kid, when we would go camping, my mom would sing this song about the stars. I don't remember the lyrics, but the jest of it is that we would sit and count the stars, and never be able to add up the number. There are just too many. Even if you took the time to count them, you would lose count because you would lose your place with all the other stars surrounding. God is making it clear to Abram that he will have too many children to even count. Maybe we can count them today, but we aren't absolute about the children of some of his offspring, a story for a different time."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Faith is the most important trait for God's children to have. Abram has great faith at this moment. We, too, will see God make a promise, and we will have great faith at the moment of the promise. But we will do as Abram does in the next few chapters and lose patience in that faith, and take things into our own hands. We think, \"God is taking too long to keep His promise. Why am I waiting around?\" We make the greatest mistakes because we lose patience in our faith."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The land is given to Abram, and God has also made it clear that the seed of Abram will own this land and prosper in it. Of course, that doesn't last because sin comes into the picture with Joseph, but that's later. Right now, the promise is proven through God. Noah made the promise after his drunkenness, but God is reiterating that promise through His own grace and love. He has a plan, and though Noah may not have understood what was happening when he was drunk (even possibly just being mean to his children), there was a purpose for that promise and a reason that Noah's word must be kept. Obviously, when Noah made the promise, God was working through him as well; otherwise, God could have stopped Noah's mouth from ever saying anything at all."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is the opposite of faith. This is what we desire most of the time as well. If we truly believe that the Lord is who He says He is, then we must have faith to believe that He will do what He says He'll do. However, we want proof first. We want written documentation that God will keep His promise to us. Abram's written documentation will come in a moment. Our written documentation is Christ crucified and resurrected. He signed our faith and dealings with God the moment He was put on that cross."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abram wants proof, so God tells him what to do to prepare the proof. He was going to make a sacrifice to the Lord. He had to get a heifer (or a cow), a goat, a ram, a turtle dove, and a pigeon, split them down the middle, and put each half on each side to make a pathway. The birds he didn't divide but put one on each side of the path. This is how men made contracts in that time. If they were to agree to buy land or animals, they would prepare a sacrifice in this manner to show their dedication to this cause. There were no written contracts, of course. They would shake hands in the middle and walk through the sacrifice together, stating they completed their contract."},{"kind":"para","text":"God is using something that would be familiar to Abram, and Abram would be comforted by the fact that God is giving Him this approval and proof in this way. This was binding, and nothing could be taken from it. The dividing of the animals was to prove the dividing of the portion that was agreed upon. The agreed-upon portion was that Abram would have children (too many to count), and God would have... well, Abram..."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So Abram sits after the sacrifice is prepared. He sits, and he waits. He waits so long that the birds are coming down to eat the sacrifice before the Lord can get there, and Abram has to shoo them off."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":21,"title":"God Reveals Future Plans","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abram waits long enough for him to fall asleep near this sacrifice that was prepared. What was God doing? Why was God waiting so long, and why did God tell Abram to do this and then not show up? God paralyzes Abram in his sleep by bringing \"great darkness\" upon Abram. He doesn't know what is happening or why it's happening, and this is more like a nightmare."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God knows the future. He knows about the issue in Egypt. Everything will go quite well for Abram and his child. His child will have children. His children will fight, and one will be sent into slavery. When that happens, a series of events will befall, and all of his children's children will be in Egypt prospering. However, that's not the land they are supposed to be in. They are supposed to be in this land that is promised to Abram. They will be stuck in Egypt for four hundred years EXACTLY, and then they will be set free (by a man named Moses). God does not give the exact details, but he gives the most important parts of this prophecy."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Israelites do leave Egypt with an amazing amount of resources and material. Actually, they have enough gold to make a whole cow out of it. They have food to survive for quite some time on their own. They have victory after victory as well. I don't want to get ahead of myself though. Abram will die at an old age and won't see any of this take place. This is a warning that will be passed down from Abram to his children. I think his children knew this warning. I think it is passed onto their children and so forth. But when we get to Egypt, we see they have been enslaved for four hundred years, and they question where God is. They think that the Lord forsakes them in fact."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now the sojourning of the children of Israel, who dwelt in Egypt, was four hundred and thirty years.","ref":"Exodus 12:40"}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Amorites have a chance to turn to the Lord still. The Lord knows they won't. He knows that the Amorites’ time will come, and so Abram can't be put in the land yet because that time has to be fulfilled first. In those four hundred something years, the Amorites don't turn to God. Only one does, and that is Rahab the Harlot. Again, I will not get ahead of myself here. It's sufficient to say that God's waiting has a purpose, and our patience needs to match His. We might not be alive by the time His promise is made, but we will be guaranteed that His promise will be kept."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is how God represents Himself through the sacrifices. It makes you wonder, why did God have Abram wait so long, though? What was the purpose of having Abram make the sacrifice and then just sit and wonder if God would show up at all? He waited so long that he fell asleep. I believe this was a faith-building exercise as well. God does not work on our timing; He works on our faith. If we have little faith, God will make your faith bigger. If we have big faith, God will work quite quickly. All in all, God works on His own schedule and has a purpose for the waiting. The purpose this time was for Abram's faith in the Lord to grow."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here is all the land that is given to Abram and his offspring. God has sealed the covenant promise with Abram by walking through the sacrifice with him, and is now pointing out everything that Abram will own. This promise will be passed down to his children as well. They will know for certain that this is where they are to dwell, and this is the land that God has given to them. God is giving Abram a lot in this chapter. He has promised Abram His protection, His presence, an innumerable offspring, and land far above and beyond what he can even fathom. And Abram has given God, literally, nothing. He just simply believed God. God warned him about what would happen in the land in the future, but the land is Abram's children's. It will be given to them when the time is right. That time would be when Joshua comes on the scene (you can read that in the book of Joshua)."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":21}]},
    "16": {"chapter":16,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Over and over again we have seen God provide for Abram and his family. They have gone through battle and survived, they have travelled through a famine and survived. They have lived off the land and survived. God has made promises to Abram and Abram was just to accept those promises and comply. God gives Abram a covenant promise, one represented by a sacrifice in our last chapter, revealing very specific future plans for Abram and his family."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, however, we see that Abram doesn't accept the promises, and he makes a very dumb move that he really shouldn't have made. However, nothing is lost on God, and God knows exactly what Abram will do which makes this chapter even more interesting."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"Abram Marries Hagar","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here's where the issue of wealth comes in. He became an extremely wealthy man. They seemed to really enjoy the things they were able to keep from Egypt. So much so that he had to split with his nephew and that family because there was fighting occurring with their servants. Having money is not the issue. Relying on that money is the issue. Abram and Sarai were relying on their wealth here, and we will see them make a grave mistake because they did not rely on the word of God."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's always the woman who makes the wrong decision, isn't it? I'm just kidding; we see that Abram was to be the man of the house. He was to have the final say, and she was to respect his decision. Abram will not make a wise decision here and instead thinks with his emotions and flesh rather than with his spirit. God made a promise to Abram that Sarai would be the one that gave them kids, and they rejected that promise for something else instead."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abram and Sarai thought that giving this maid to Abram for marriage would make the process acceptable. They seemed to understand that marriage was a necessity for a family to be in unity with the Lord, but they failed to understand that it was between one man and one woman. There was a great big world out there doing this, and it was not what God wanted for His children at all. This maid now became a concubine, and the children she will have will be rejected by God."},{"kind":"para","text":"You say, \"That seems a little harsh, don't you think?\" No. God gave them a promise, and they were treading their own path to accomplish that promise on their own timing. God did not want this at all. He knew this would happen, but He doesn't want it to happen. This will ultimately make Abram and Sarai learn a few lessons, but it will also make us, the readers, understand a few things as well. God is very specific about who is chosen for what. He knows exactly what He's doing when He leads His people. There are many who have read this story and would say, \"Okay, so God has rejected them, and that is a sure sign that they will not receive salvation from the Lord.\" That is not what it is saying at all. Let's read further."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":6,"title":"Hagar and Sarai Fight","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have an even bigger issue here. Hagar must have known that Abram loved Sarai more. I mean, how could he not? That was his true wife. So now Hagar hates Sarai... This is why nobody should have more than one wife."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Sarai is standing up for her family (finally), and it doesn't end well. How can it end well? There is no way a story like this could ever have a happy ending. Even if everyone got along and everyone was a servant to one another and rejoiced in the fact that they had a child, it still wouldn't end well because they were dwelling in sin. That sin separates them from God. The only way to grow in connection with God again is to separate that sin. Hagar hates this new life, so she takes her child and runs."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":14,"title":"The Lord Speaks With Hagar","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I'm going to tread lightly on this subject, because there is not quite enough evidence to determine whether or not this is so. There are a great many scholars who believe that when the Bible says, \"the angel of the Lord\" it is talking about Jesus Christ pre-incarnate. Before He came to Earth, He was the angel of the Lord. Whether or not that is true, I can't hold much weight to it. There are a lot of factors that I would believe most scholars about."},{"kind":"para","text":"For instance, in this verse we see the angel of the Lord looking for this lost maiden \"Hagar.\" There aren't any other angels mentioned that seek the lost. Actually, this angel is the only one throughout the Old Testament that seems to do so. In the New Testament, it seems this angel disappears and never comes again. There are a lot of assumptions as to why that is, but most scholars agree that this is Christ and He is looking out for this maiden."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I mean this angel already knew her name. He had to have also known that Hagar was on the run for that particular reason. He didn't have to ask. This was more of a ploy to get her to realize who it was that she was talking to. This was a big deal. She was talking to an angel, and if scholars are right, she was talking to CHRIST! What a big deal! He doesn't tell her to keep running, but rather to go back because she is owned by them. What she was doing was wrong by running. What they were doing was wrong by making her a concubine. The whole situation is pretty messed up and muddy."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In other words, God will take care of her. This is another reason why scholars believe this is Christ. No angel can make this kind of promise. They can't promise people anything that will happen, including childbearing. They are to protect and relay messages; that is it. Their design is from God, and anything they are to say and do must come from Him first. This angel, however, makes a promise to Hagar that she will have a multitude of children and she will be looked after."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The descendants of Ishmael certainly are wild. In fact, if you were to travel to the Middle East (or just look this up online), they would admit that they are the sons of Abraham. But really, they are the sons of Ishmael. They are quite the wild bunch; that is all I can and will say about that."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a very big deal to Hagar. The Egyptians served many gods, but none were like the God of the Bible. They were more like Satanic beings. Deities that would be harsh or kind depending on your circumstances. The God of the Bible obviously doesn't care about your circumstances to show you kindness and compassion. He cares about your future and Spirit. He desires that all would come to know Him, even this Egyptian woman \"Hagar\" who seems to be in the wrong place. Hagar is thrilled that God (the true God that is) would speak to her and give her direction. This changes her whole mood."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":16,"title":"Ismael is Born","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This eighty-six-year-old man just had his first son, and he was not the son that was promised to him. Hagar was the recipient of a promise from God, though. You might read this and say, “Well, why would God allow this to happen now? Why would He give her children and ignore Abram and Sarai?\" That will be revealed better in the next chapter. But God knew exactly what they were doing. In fact, He knew it before they did it. He is letting this play out because He has a very distinct purpose for it, and it shows the difference between getting what you want with your own hands and waiting on the Lord to provide like He has promised. We have this same issue as Abram does today. It might not be with having children, but it's certainly with having something else we want. Instead of waiting on the Lord, we do what we can, and it ends up being a small blessing compared to the extremely large blessing that God can provide if we just wait on Him."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":16}]},
    "17": {"chapter":17,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: We left off our last chapter in a kind of an awkward position. Abram did something that the Lord did not desire nor command with his handmaiden Hagar in order to force God's promise through having a child. This ultimately leads to a fight between Hagar and Sarai, and Hagar tries to leave. However, the Lord sends a messenger to give her a promise for her child, which God will reiterate in our chapter today to Abraham."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is quite bit happening in this chapter, and I certainly want to do every chapter justice, but there are some things that will be spoken about here that will make a lot more sense later. I will try my best to clarify without leaving anything open ended, but having a good study of the Bible through would certainly benefit you more because of the promises that God will make."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":8,"title":"God Makes Abraham","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So it has been about fourteen years after Ishmael was born at this point. Abram was eighty-six, and now it says he is ninety-nine. Imagine having your first child at eighty-six years old. I can't imagine having my second child at thirty even at this point. But 86?! That seems like a hectic time to have children. The interesting part here is how God refers to Himself. He tells Abram, \"I am the Almighty God.\" There has not been a time before this where God refers to Himself as the Almighty. \"Almighty\" means \"All-powerful.\" There is nothing more powerful than the Lord, and the Lord Himself makes that well known. It's a fantastic name that God refers to Himself here, and He's not doing it as a boast (though God certainly CAN boast for He is worthy), but He's doing it as a reminder, as we will see in a moment (and in future chapters to come)."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God always keeps His promises. Abram might have been trekking through life at this point thinking that God has already made His promise through Ishmael, but that was not how God wanted to keep His promise. God was very specific with the promise He made to Abram..."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And, behold, the word of the LORD came unto him, saying, This shall not be thine heir; but he that shall come forth out of thine own bowels shall be thine heir.","ref":"Genesis 15:4"},{"kind":"para","text":"God wanted Abram and Sarai to have a child. Interestingly enough, Abram does what we all do and defined the terms in which God meant by that. He went ahead and decided to rush the Lord's promise by marrying and sleeping with someone who was not to be his wife. May it also be reminded (and we've talked about this before and will continue to talk about this throughout Scripture), no human was to have more than one wife."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And they TWAIN shall be one flesh: so then they are no more twain, but one flesh.","ref":"Mark 10:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"Adam never married another. Noah never married another. Yet we will see great men of faith doing some despicable sins, like marrying more than one woman at a time. It is said that Moses had two wives. David had many wives. Solomon had four hundred(ish) wives. Solomon was quite foolish for someone who is claimed to be so wise..."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The promise is clear. Abram (or rather Abraham now) will be the sole source of providence leading up to our salvation today. If God did not keep His promise with Abraham here, it is quite unlikely that any of us would be born, and that the Savior would come to give Himself for our sins. We would have no eternal security if this chapter were missing today. \"Abram\" means \"Exalted father.\" Abram was not a father, so the only father that would be exalted to Abram was his Heavenly Father. \"Abraham\" means \"Father of a multitude.\" Abraham was not a father of a multitude (at least not yet), but that is where God's promise comes in."},{"kind":"para","text":"Why does his name have to change? Well, this will be an interesting theme throughout Scripture. Not everyone's name changes, but there are quite a few. Everyone's name has significance. They all mean something to the Lord. The book of Ruth illustrates this extremely well (with every name having a very unique and specific purpose to it). The Apostle Paul's name was changed from Saul without any warning or without any kind of explanation."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then Saul, (who also is called Paul,) filled with the Holy Ghost, set his eyes on him,","ref":"Acts 13:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham doesn't go back to his old name again after this. Once it was changed by the Lord, it was changed for all eternity. It didn't matter what Abraham's parents named him at birth. Abraham's purpose was new in the Lord. This is a great description of our new birth as well. When we are born again, we are completely new. We have a new purpose. A new “name,\" if you will. Of course, our names are not changed, but the names we are given are not just given to us by our parents. The Lord defines our names in the second birth."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I want to establish a couple of things here before just glossing over these verses and moving on. Notice the way that God phrases this promise here... He says, \"I will MAKE thee exceeding fruitful...\" He says, \"I will MAKE nations of thee, and kings shall come out of thee.\" The kings of Israel are certainly in direct lineage to Abraham (including the first king of Israel, which is Saul). That promise was kept by the Lord, and history proves that."},{"kind":"para","text":"History also proves (as we will eventually read) that God keeps His promise in MAKING them as well. They will be stubborn. They will fight against everything the Lord has to offer them. They will be scared, and they will go through some heavy burdens and trials because of their rejection of God's work, but ultimately that is how God makes a person. God makes a person by fire. He tries His children with heat."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But he knoweth the way that I take: when he hath tried me, I shall come forth as gold.\" Job 23:10 \"For thou, O God, hast proved us: thou hast tried us, as silver is tried.\" Psalm 66:10 \"I indeed baptize you with water unto repentance: but he that cometh after me is mightier than I, whose shoes I am not worthy to bear: he shall baptize you with the Holy Ghost, and with fire:\" Matthew 3:11 \"Behold, I have refined thee, but not with silver; I have chosen thee in the furnace of affliction.","ref":"Isaiah 48:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"If you are going through a hurt or trial, it is God's way of \"making\" you. He is shaping you. He is forming you. If gold could cry, they would probably scream in agony because of the refinement process. It is being melted over and over again to make it useful and to purify it. It would be painful. It is painful to us to go through a trial, but God wants to MAKE His children. He wants us to thrive, and not by \"force.\" There has to be a very good understanding here of God's Sovereignty before we move on."},{"kind":"para","text":"God has a Will. There is no doubt about that. But His Will also allows US to have a Will as well. We get to make choices (otherwise, Adam would have never eaten from that tree, and Abram would have never done this great sin with his handmaiden). Yes, God has a Will, but He has never (not one time throughout Scripture or in anyone's lives today) forced His Will upon people. His Will is to be accepted. If we do not accept His Will, we suffer consequences outside of His Will and desire. If we accept His Will, there might be consequences from the world, but God will bless those for being IN His Will. That is how God makes us. It's the pressures from the outside trying to destroy our faith because we are living in God's Will, and God protecting us and growing us because He is pleased for standing with Him. I'd rather have the whole world against me and standing with the Lord alone than to have the whole world for me and standing against the Lord. God will MAKE Abraham. God will MAKE His nation. God will MAKE His children."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is going back to the promise of Noah here. God makes it clear that He is keeping what Noah had promised. Noah's promise was a good promise for God to keep because it meant that God was able to make these people. We will see them being made when they get into the wilderness, and we will see how that goes for them in Deuteronomy as they are about to see the promise (which is verified here) preparing for the land of Canaan."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":14,"title":"God's Symbolic Covenant","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God has initiated the \"making\" process with these words. Keeping God's covenants will not be something that the Israelites will be very good at. The covenant that God will ask for here is a very peculiar one, and one that I am not extremely comfortable talking about, but it seems that the Lord has left me with not much choice in the matter. He is MAKING me as well... The covenant is this:"}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This seems like a strange covenant requested by the Lord. This is a symbol of their belonging to God. They did not get circumcised as a means to enter into God's covenant; they got circumcised as a representation of already having God's covenant. The circumcision belonged to the Jews specifically, for the memorial and representation of God's promises to Abraham. Today, there are circumcisions still happening, but the purpose has been lost. It's the same with our salvation today."},{"kind":"para","text":"Before we are God's children, we must enter into His covenant. How do we do that today? Is it through good works or religious traditions? Of course not. It is through accepting His invitation into the family. Adoption today is a very tricky and difficult process in the lives of Christians. I've seen a great deal of foster parents desire adoption for the children they foster only to have to give up their foster children back to broken families. But when it works, it is an acceptance of familial acuity. In other words, the children are accepted by this family without restraint. The child does not have to work to earn membership in the family. Actually, it's the parents that do the work here."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we are adopted by the Father, it's an open invitation to Him, and all we must do is accept the adoption. He does the rest of the work. There is no work we must do to be accepted into the family of God. No baptism gets us accepted. No religion gets us accepted. No good deed gets us accepted. No circumcision gets us accepted. When we are accepted, we are then able to lovingly be baptized, freely express our religion, do good deeds out of a desire for our Father, and any other type of work that is given to us will only be a badge that we wear because we have already been accepted. Circumcision is that badge here. It's a symbol of God's covenant to Abraham and his descendants. It's the same symbol we have through the Spirit today as children of God."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"How would they know they are uncircumcised? Certainly the parents would know, but no one else is going to check. This must be between them and the Lord, and if the parents are not dedicating their children in this way at this time, they are openly rejecting God's covenant here, which will only be effective to the child."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":22,"title":"God's Covenant Promise to Abraham","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Sarai\" means \"princess.\" I've met a lot of people named \"princess\" in my life, and they are not very princess-y. \"Sarah\" means \"noblewoman.\" God is making Sarah now. He is forming her from a princess to a woman of nobility. Someone who is able to help Abraham as he leads great nations under him. Someone who will be noble for her husband, and will be remembered for her desire to please her husband and please the Lord. And she certainly is remembered for such. Not one time do we hear much of a complaint from Sarah about the things her husband is doing. Including when she was almost lost from him because he tells her to lie to the pharaoh (a few chapters back). That's a conversation for a different time, though."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What an astounding promise from the Lord. Abraham did not remember the promise was going to be through Sarah for the lineage of his people, and so Abraham did something against the Lord's promise in the last chapter. There was use for this, of course, but it was not for the use of MAKING God's people. God will certainly make a people through thirteen-year-old Ishmael, but it won't be the Godly heritage that Sarah's child will lead. This promise is quite incredible, and it's something that should have humbled Abraham, but that is not how Abraham sees it..."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, Abraham didn't say anything out loud, but can certainly be seen laughing here. He falls upon his face (as he's done before), but instead of falling to worship God, he's falling to cover his laughter. I think he believes that God doesn't know his thoughts or his countenance. He believes he can hide his concern from the Lord. God still has a long process to \"make\" Abraham. Here's what Abraham believes God means..."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham was defining God's promise (again) by his own terms and understanding. He is thinking that God has to be talking about Ishmael. But God made it clear, and Abraham didn't mishear. Abraham was thinking, \"God, you're funny. You said that Sarah would have a son, but obviously you didn't mean Sarah. You must have meant the real son that I already have, Ishmael. You made a mistake here, Lord.\""}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"As a rule of thumb (something I believe will be helpful as we continue to study God's Word), numbers are not extremely significant, and I try not to pry into the symbolism of numbers. But I think addressing this here will be of huge benefit (especially when we get into Revelation). Throughout Scripture, you will see two numbers pretty significantly. One number is seven, and I truly believe that is God's number. It's His number to display His perfection. The second number is twelve. That is our number. It's our number of perfection through God."},{"kind":"para","text":"Ishmael will have twelve princes that come out of his lineage. That will be depicted as God's perfect amount for his lineage. God has a bigger and more special purpose for the lineage of Isaac than He does for Ishmael. Does that mean that God doesn't care about Ishmael or other people? Absolutely not. We will see God save over and over again throughout all of Scripture (especially in Acts) people who are not in the lineage of Abraham. Another topic for a different conversation."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Another clarity is that the covenant promise of circumcision was primarily meant for the Jews as a symbol of God's covenant to them. This is an issue that will be brought back up in the book of Acts through the Gentiles not being circumcised, but the leaders of the church will have to make it understood that the covenant was not meant as a form of salvation. It was meant as a symbol of God's promise to the Jews."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here's what Abraham was left with to think about: God promised to make a great nation through his and Sarah's children. The circumcision was the symbol of God's promise to them. Abraham was not circumcised... Now, I don't know about you, but that would be a very scary thing for me to think about. More troublesome than having my second child at ninety-nine years old... He will also have to figure out how to tell Sarai (now Sarah) about all of this as well..."}]}]},{"start":23,"end":27,"title":"The Circumcision Observed","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You might be thinking, \"Okay, God just said that the covenant promise was made to Abraham through Sarah’s children. Why is Abraham circumcising his son Ishmael, when God made it clear that it was not through the lineage of Ishmael that the promise would be kept?\" I think there are a few reasons for this. One is that Abraham really desired that his son Ishmael (whom he seems to really love) would be included in this promise, even though God made it clear he wouldn't be. Two is that Abraham was commanded to circumcise, and so Abraham circumcised whoever was there and willing. Another assumption (and this is what I believe to be true) is that God still made a promise to Ishmael through his bloodline. He was receiving a covenant promise from God too, and that covenant promise was for God to work in that nation as well. But it was not the same promise as was made to Isaac’s bloodline."},{"kind":"para","text":"Isaac’s bloodline was for kingdoms and mighty nations. People that would be set apart for the work of the Lord. Ishmael's bloodline would be an unruly one. It will be mighty as well, but not for the works of the Lord. It will be for the works of themselves, and it will be a lineage that will ultimately fall away from God. The Israelites have greatly fallen away from God as well. Israel is a nation that has given itself over to all sorts of idolatry and has highly rejected Christ. There is still hope for them, however, for Revelation makes it known that a good majority of the group that will receive salvation after the rapture takes place will be mostly Jewish Israelites."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The question really comes down to, \"So why does God choose Israel to prosper instead of the Ishmaelites?\" Well, God knows the future. He knows that the children of Ishmael will turn away from Him and greatly reject Him. He knows that the Israelites will do this too, but He also knows that the Israelites will come to Him in the end. So it is through this lineage that He has chosen to put His name, and it's through this lineage in which the Lord will come to us. Here's the key thing to remember, however, is that (though the promise comes through the lineage of Isaac) the promise of salvation is given to the world. Israel will have plenty of promises given to them, but we (reading this today) have plenty of promises given to us as well, starting with the promise of redemption. The children of Ishmael may not have had the covenant promise of the land and prosperity of the Lord, but they can certainly have the new covenant promise of salvation through Christ."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":27}]},
    "18": {"chapter":18,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see the kind of man that Abraham is turning out to be since those promises were made. Three strangers appear, and they will change the lives of Abraham and Sarah forever."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, we see God make this man Abraham. He becomes a servant of the Lord. He is given several promises, and he is tasked with the objective of circumcising the people who were chosen of God. One promise that was made to him was the promise of children."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":8,"title":"Going Above And Beyond For Others","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What was so interesting about these three men? Did they seem heavenly? Did it seem to Abraham that these men were something special from the Lord? I really don't have the slightest idea, but it seems that Abraham has learned the necessary trait of grace. He bows himself to the ground at the sight of these three strangers and gives them extreme hospitality."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are not many Christians today who are hospitable. I've gone to a church with thousands of \"Christians\" attending, and have only been invited to two people's houses. When I was working in ministry, it was my prerogative to make our home a place for people to be welcomed. Our teens came over quite a bit, and we had dear friends constantly coming and going. We should be a people of hospitality."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Be not forgetful to entertain strangers: for thereby some have entertained angels unawares.\" Hebrews 13:2 \"Use hospitality one to another without grudging.\" 1 Peter 4:9 \"A bishop then must be blameless, the husband of one wife, vigilant, sober, of good behaviour, given to hospitality, apt to teach;","ref":"1 Timothy 3:2"}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Washing feet may seem like a very little thing to most people. It was a sign of grace and welcoming back then. It was a lesson on servanthood."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"So after he had washed their feet, and had taken his garments, and was set down again, he said unto them, Know ye what I have done to you? Ye call me Master and Lord: and ye say well; for so I am. If I then, your Lord and Master, have washed your feet; ye also ought to wash one another's feet.","ref":"John 13:12-14"},{"kind":"para","text":"There's a lot of symbolism here that I think is best left for the book of John, but I will say that Abraham was showing a sign of Christ here by offering him a place to wash his feet. This was Abraham's way of saying, \"You are welcome to relax in my home and stay as long as you'd like.\""}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He was even feeding these men. You'd think it would be enough for him to just simply give them a place to rest, but Abraham didn't feel satisfied enough to leave it at that. He wanted to take care of these men. I've heard it said, \"The best way to a man's heart is through his stomach.\" and every Baptist who reads this quote is rejoicing. Truly, you can reach the man's heart by feeding him. Men like good food, and we certainly do feel more welcome when it is offered to us out of love."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham and his family really went to work for these people. It wasn't just a little meal. This was a feast. He found one of his best cattle, and he made bread, and churned butter, and poured them a glass of milk. He dressed the cow, and made it as perfect as he could for these men. Mind you, Abraham still doesn't know who these men are. He was simply being hospitable to them, and they seemed to find it quite eloquent and joyous to be entertained by Abraham. Abraham had a sure faith that made him desire to entertain everyone he could meet."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":15,"title":"The Promise of a Child","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't think Abraham ever told these people his wife's name. More so, in the East like this (and still today), it was not custom for the wife to come out and entertain. Her job was always behind the scenes. Yet, these men are asking about her, and they are calling her by name."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So here's the strange and awkward picture here. These three men are traveling by, and they happen to travel by the most hospitable man in all of Mamre. This man sees them and knows that they will have a long journey ahead of them, so he offers them a place to rest. Not just offers them rest, but feeds them a meal for the ages and allows them to wash their feet. Then these men call out Sarah, and make a very direct assumption that Sarah (mind you at an old age) was to have a child. No, not just a child, specifically a son! Sarah hears this from her tent door, and is more than likely confused. Abraham might have looked at Sarah with a \"are these guys serious\" face."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It is quite funny from their perspective. They have lived a very long time and it's been a very difficult trek, and yet they were still found to be joyous and hospitable. Abraham was already told about this matter, but it seems that Abraham just didn't believe it. He probably didn't even tell Sarah. Sarah is hearing this for the first time and probably ashamed of the fact that she couldn't have any children. This almost seems (to Sarah) like a slap in the face."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know about you, but when I am placed in awkward situations, I can't help but find the humor in it. I tend to laugh a little bit at the prospect and think, \"Is this guy serious?\" It has gotten me into trouble in the past, especially when the person I thought was making a joke was actually very much serious. They don't like the idea of them being laughed at about something that they really thought was important to mention."},{"kind":"para","text":"When I was in elementary school, I was working with a group of kids for a science project. The particular group that I chose were not scholarly by any means. In fact, I couldn't have picked a worse group to sit with. One of the boys that was in my group was a troublemaker (to put it kindly). He stole things from teachers, mocked teachers behind their backs, and would pick fights with other kids on the playground. He was a bully through and through. I didn't pick the group because of him, but he was placed in our group later on. One particular day, he did something to upset the teacher (said something out loud that made her quite upset). The teacher turned and said, \"I can easily give you detention if you don't knock it off.\" \"Good,\" I thought to myself with a smile, \"he's finally getting caught for something that he's done.\" The teacher got right in my face and said, “ I don't know why you're smiling, I don't find what you said funny at all.\" My smile dropped. She thought it was ME that said it to her. I looked over at the other kids at my table and they were laughing at how awkward the situation was."},{"kind":"para","text":"Be careful when you laugh, it might get you into trouble. Sarah laughs at this awkward situation, and this is what happens to her..."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Suddenly, the Lord steps in and makes it clear that the thing which was mentioned was not a mistake. It was not a joke. It was not something that can just be passed off as an idea or as some person hoping and wishing they had children. No, this was a plan from the Lord, and it was not funny. This was serious business."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"And this makes ME laugh because Sarah tried to deny the fact that she laughed. It was plainly obvious to everyone there, and nothing can be hidden from the Lord. I feel you, Sarah..."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":22,"title":"The False Image of Sodom","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Where Abraham was, was a perfect location to overlook Sodom. They were able to see the vast city and the splendor of it. Abraham, perhaps, still didn't quite know who these men were. He must have had his suspicions that they were angels at this point."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God had a secret. He wanted Abraham to know what would happen next because He didn’t want Abraham to follow the same paths as Sodom had."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God reminds Abraham of the promise that was made. He reminds him that the land he was possessing was for something far greater than just himself. He gives the assurance of the fact that this nation (that is, the Israelites) will be the opposite of what Sodom and Gomorrah stand for. They are looking on this great city; Abraham can see it while this is being said. He can see the splendor of it all. He can see what prosperity looks like. He can almost feel it, as he stands on his property, which is not a part of Sodom at all. He might be thinking to himself, “We can never be as great as Sodom is.\" They will certainly be greater, but not because of the richness of it. They will be greater because they will have something that Sodom never had. They will have the Lord."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God already knows what is happening in Sodom and Gomorrah. It is not as if His eyes cannot see their hearts. Abraham sees the splendor, but God sees their wickedness. These angels are being sent down to give a message, and it will be up to the Sodomites to either accept or reject it. God will not do anything without a warning first, and He will never do it in a hurry. There is no jumping the gun with God. He has everything planned up to the exact moment. He knows what will happen to Sodom and Gomorrah before his angels go there, and He knows exactly what must be done to them in order for Abraham to see the prosperity he needs to see."}]}]},{"start":23,"end":33,"title":"Abraham Wants To Save The City","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We can tell that Abraham has a concern for this city. Not because of the splendor of it. But rather, he knows that the Lord might destroy this city, and there is one person (in particular) that is on his mind. His nephew Lot was still there, and he did not want him to be destroyed with the rest of the city."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God ALWAYS does what's right. God knows that there are not \"fifty\" righteous people in this city, but He knows there is at least one whose name is Lot. God has a track record of always rescuing the just from the wicked. You say, “Well, what about the disciples and martyrs? Were they not just?\" Yes, but it was not God's judgement that killed them. It will be God's judgement that kills this extremely wicked city of Sodom. God saves the righteous from His wrath."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For God hath not appointed us to wrath, but to obtain salvation by our Lord Jesus Christ,","ref":"1 Thessalonians 5:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"The faith of the people is what will save them from the wrath of God. That is how it has always been, and that is how it will be in the end as well."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Done deal. Fifty righteous people will be saved if God can find them. If God can’t, well then they won't be saved..."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham has decreased the number to forty-five. Abraham didn't want the city saved; he just wanted his nephew saved. I have no doubt that he cared about the people as well, but his main concern was his nephew."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He's decreased it to forty now..."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now thirty..."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Twenty..."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ten... You might think to yourself, \"Why is Abraham lowering the number?\" I believe Abraham knew that these cities were extremely wicked. I believe he felt good about not taking that land when Lot decided to take it. I believe he understood that the Lord knew there were not going to be many there that would be worth saving. The only people worth saving were Lot and his family. Some would say, “Well, Lot couldn't have been very righteous; he was living in a very bad land, surrounded by very bad influences. How is it that Lot can live there and not be affected by the culture around him?\" I don't believe he was. I believe God took care of him. Why do I believe that? Well, we will see Lot's lifestyle in the next chapter, but I will show what Peter says about him..."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And delivered just Lot, vexed with the filthy conversation of the wicked: (For that righteous man dwelling among them, in seeing and hearing, vexed his righteous soul from day to day with their unlawful deeds;) The Lord knoweth how to deliver the godly out of temptations, and to reserve the unjust unto the day of judgment to be punished:","ref":"2 Peter 2:7-9"}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Before the number gets any lower, God had to leave the conversation. It is likely that Abraham would have brought that number down to one. \"If there was only one just person, would you save the whole city, Lord?\" God would save that one just person, but He cannot allow the city to prosper any longer. They were gaining a lot in this world, but they were doing it in a very ungodly and inhumane way. Besides that, if God only saves one just person, it would be a lot alone, and his family would be left behind because of their wickedness."},{"kind":"para","text":"It is a noble thing to desire a whole city to be saved because of ONE just person. We should have the same heart as Abraham, but not because we know someone in the city. We should have the same heart because we want everyone to be saved from their wickedness."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":33}]},
    "19": {"chapter":19,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Abraham has a meeting with a few random wandering strangers. He takes special care of them and encourages their journey. Because he is so hospitable to them, they tell him that they are sent from the Lord, and they were going to Sodom to destroy it. Of course, I don't think it's any accident that they happened to be travelling by the tent of Abraham that particular day. Abraham pleads with the Lord to save the righteous people in Sodom (namely his nephew Lot, if Lot is righteous at all)."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see where the angels end up going after they enter into Sodom, and how the Sodomites ultimately treat these strangers to their land."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"Lot's Hospitality","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that Lot recognized who they were. He, at the very least, understood they were sent from the Lord. There are a lot of assumptions made about Lot and his character, but there is a lot of information stating that Lot was a righteous man, living in a very unrighteous neighborhood. We’ve already mentioned this, but I think it’s important to recap."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And delivered just Lot, vexed with the filthy conversation of the wicked: (For that righteous man dwelling among them, in seeing and hearing, vexed his righteous soul from day to day with their unlawful deeds;) The Lord knoweth how to deliver the godly out of temptations, and to reserve the unjust unto the day of judgment to be punished:","ref":"2 Peter 2:7-9"},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe there need to be some righteous people in hard places. Missionaries are often sent to some of the most dangerous areas to reach the most heinous types of people. Lot, perhaps, didn't know that this place was going to be spiritually evil, but he certainly could use that as a means to witness to the lost. I believe Lot was \"just\" because he didn't live like the rest of the people in this place. He lived like he belonged to a different God, and he certainly didn't belong where he was."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Lot wanted to be hospitable like Abraham. It seems that this family has a knack for taking care of complete strangers. I believe there was something about these angels that made Lot understand they did not belong here. He knew they were going to be taken advantage of because the people in Sodom were not friendly people. How do we know this? Well, Abraham pled with them in the last chapter about not destroying Sodom if there were any just people in it. Ten just people was all it would take to save the whole city. They will not find ten as we will soon see. But we also know that Sodom isn't any good because of how Lot reacts next."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems they were getting just as good of a meal at Lot’s house as they did at Abraham's. Abraham wasn't living the high life in the city, yet he was just as well-fed as Lot. Lot, honestly, should have taken his family and left this place a long time ago. He, perhaps, got comfortable and didn't feel like it was wise for them to move on from where they were. I don't know. That's usually how it is though with God's children."},{"kind":"para","text":"California is a beautiful state. I have nothing against it except for the politics. The people in California are mostly fleshly people. They aren't much different from the Sodomites as we will soon see. This is not the best place to be for Christians who want to live Godly lives for the Lord. Most of the people in California HATE Christians, and especially the Bible. I refer to California as \"The Front Lines of Faith.\" This is where the battle actually happens. People in the Bible Belt of Texas have no idea what spiritual warfare really looks like. I would know, I've been in both places for battle. I don't believe God will wipe out California (He certainly could). I believe He will allow California to become as big as possible before He takes the pleasure of destroying it. Even in His wrath, He will protect those that are \"just.\" God wants to take care of His own, and Lot is one of His own. Lot proves that by the way he begs these angels to come into his home and stay there rather than on the streets."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":11,"title":"The Sodomites Order","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Word traveled fast that there were some good-looking strangers entering into the house of Lot. They might have thought that these people were rich. They might have had other fleshly desires for them. Rape was a real issue in Sodom, and it seems that this is more than likely what they were referring to when they said they wanted to get to \"know\" these men. They likely wanted to have their way with these men and then treat them as slaves or kill them and get rid of them. There was not much law and order in Sodom, it seems. Some people might question why I believe it was rape they wanted at this moment. Lot will reveal this in a moment."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He pleads with this crowd. He is trying to protect these angels of the Lord. He has it on good authority that these angels don't deserve the type of treatment that the Sodomites want to give them."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is why I believe they wanted to rape the angels. Lot is letting it be made known that his daughters were virgins, and the crowd could have them. I couldn't imagine giving up my daughters in this moment. Lot had to be certain these were actually angels; otherwise, he wouldn't be giving up his own family that easily for a few strangers. Lot lived like a just man before the people. It was Abraham that rescued these people before, if you don't recall. These people, likely, had a lot of respect for Lot and his family. They didn't want Lot or his family; they wanted the strangers that came into his house. Sodom and their acts are also where we get the word for \"sodomy\" from. I won’t define that here; you can look that up on your own time."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The angels were not going to let Lot do this alone. They knew what had to be done. If they had not intervened, Lot would be out there in a much worse position. These people are now threatening to do worse with Lot because of his protection of these strangers. Again, they knew that Abraham was a very close relative to Lot, and it was Abraham that rescued them. They didn't seem to care about that, though, at the moment. They cared about their fleshly desires more than remembering what the Lord had done for them. That seems to be the theme of mankind throughout all of history."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now Lot can be assured these men were sent from the Lord. No one had power like that to suddenly blind people out of nowhere. I don't believe the angels threw something out the door (like a flash grenade). I believe they simply had to think it, and it was done. God granted the angels power to do some pretty incredible things, it seems."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":14,"title":"Gathering The Family","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are not \"Ten\" just people in Sodom. There are a few though, and it was in the house they were currently in. The angels tell them to gather any other family they have and take them out of the city. They give Lot the game plan, and I don't think Lot was ready to hear this. He probably thought he would die in Sodom. He never could have imagined that there would be a time where it would be completely destroyed. Sodom was a big city, with a lot of amazing things. The grass was greener over here. Yet, there were a lot of terrible people in it, and so God must do away with it. He is looking to protect Lot and his family though before doing what needs to be done."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"His daughters were married. Yet he tells the crowd in verse 8 that his daughters have not known any men. He might not have liked who his daughters married, honestly. They are here thinking Lot had lost his mind by the way he was talking and didn't want to listen to him tell them the city would be destroyed. But did Lot lie to the people about his daughters? It's possible that he did. He could have used this lie because he knew what they were attempting to do to these innocent strangers from the Lord. However, Lot had a testimony, and God gives us a means to tell the truth in a way that won't escalate the situation. He was a just man."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe that Lot's son-in-laws made fun of Lot because Lot had a testimony. I don't believe it was simply because he lived like them or however pastors perceive it today. I think that Lot did speak to them about the God of Abraham, but I think he became the laughingstock of the people. He was probably made fun of often. His sons-in-law didn't believe it either, and that really shows here as he tries to warn them about their impending doom. It's also possible that his daughters were too young to have been with their husbands, and so they remained virgins. That happened often back then."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":16,"title":"Leave the City","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Lot had to hurry before he and his family were consumed in the destruction of the city. Lot believed that everything would be destroyed. Yet, we find him in the next verse taking his time to go..."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Why did he linger? I think he was taking everything in. All the things he had, and the work he built up in his home. When you make a home, it is hard to leave it. Yet this was an urgent matter. He was told to leave the city before its destruction. The angels gave them the night to pack their things, and it seems they just simply slept through the night. They then gave them a little time in the morning, but instead of getting ready to go, Lot lingered. The angels grabbed them all and took them to the city limits. They were far enough away from the destruction where they wouldn't be consumed, but they were also too close to be tempted back to the city. They could not come back here. They had to leave this behind."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":22,"title":"Escape For Your Life","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"scripture","text":"Watch ye therefore, and pray always, that ye may be accounted worthy to escape all these things that shall come to pass, and to stand before the Son of man.","ref":"Luke 21:36"},{"kind":"para","text":"The call is the same for us today. We are told to \"escape for your life and don't look back.\" When we have been set free from sin, we cannot deem it worthy to look back on. We have a means of escape, a way out, and it wouldn't make much sense for us to think there was something to look back to..."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Lot takes God's mercy a step further and requests to go to a small city outside of Sodom. He wants to be in comfort, not in a cave. He wants to be close to what he knew before he was taken out of the city, and he doesn't want to lose everything he had. Yet, either way, he will not find comfort outside of where God wants him. God is yet, still, more merciful to Lot."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Zoar\" means “insignificance.\" It's funny; this is where Lot will end up for a time. This was actually already mentioned as Lot was trying to figure out which land to take."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Lot lifted up his eyes, and beheld all the plain of Jordan, that it was well watered every where, before the LORD destroyed Sodom and Gomorrah, even as the garden of the LORD, like the land of Egypt, as thou comest unto Zoar.","ref":"Genesis 13:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"It wasn't too much different from Sodom. It was still not a good place for him to be. It was insignificant to Lot's life. His refuge cannot be found in a city or a building. His refuge must be found in the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"God is our refuge and strength, a very present help in trouble. Therefore will not we fear, though the earth be removed, and though the mountains be carried into the midst of the sea;","ref":"Psalm 46:1-2"}]}]},{"start":23,"end":28,"title":"A Pillar of Salt","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Not unlike how it would be in the last days (except on a much wider scale, of course). God's wrath is poured out on this city’s evil and disgusting lifestyle. They would not change. They were becoming bigger and bigger by the moment. They were much like \"Egypt\" as Moses writes it in Genesis 13. They were prosperous, and only growing bigger by the second. God could not let it go on any longer. If He did, the city would become so big that it would start to invade Abraham and his land, and perhaps even Egypt and their land. God gave them prosperity through Abraham once before, and because of it, the city continued to grow, but it grew deeper into sin too, and there were plans that the Lord had for Abraham and that land that was already promised to Abraham beforehand."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Lot's wife looked back. The angels told them not to look back. Zoar was too close for them. It was an insignificant city, and there was no reason for Lot to take his family there. He knew Abraham. He knew where Abraham lived. It would have been much wiser for him to go there. You might read this and think to yourself, \"Why a pillar of salt?\" I don't know if it was a literal pillar of \"salt\" or if it was \"like\" a pillar of salt. She was likely \"salty\" at the fact that she had to leave everything she loved behind. She became her emotions in that moment, and Lot lost his wife."},{"kind":"para","text":"Her heart was still in Sodom. She didn't look back to see what the destruction was like. She looked back because she didn't want to leave it behind. That is how it is with many of us today. God has called us somewhere else, and we have left our hearts behind. Our hearts shouldn't be set on anything here on Earth anyway. We will always be let down if we set our hearts on anything here. No, our hearts should be set in Heaven. That is our home, and home is where the heart is."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham's heart must have sunk in that moment. He knew it was coming, but he didn't know anything about his nephew Lot. Would his family be okay? Would they get out of this alive? What would the Lord do with them?"}]}]},{"start":29,"end":38,"title":"A Great Sin","entries":[{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Zoar wasn't cutting it for Lot. He tried to replace his life and receive comfort in this small city, but he didn't feel like that was where he belonged. Notice the way this plays out though. God remembers Lot, and it seems that God allows Lot some emotional turmoil which causes Lot to go back and see the destruction, and then live in the mountains. It should have made Lot go to Abraham. I think that was the connection God was trying to convey, but Lot doesn't think of this for some reason."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I'm sorry, but Lot's daughters are stupid. Maybe there wasn't anyone in Zoar during that time? But they thought that the whole world was Sodom and Gomorrah. They didn't think there was anything left if that city wasn't left for them. They thought they had to take things into their own hands and continue the family lineage. What other choice would they have? They couldn't surely just wait on the Lord and His provision. They couldn't possibly think about Lot's uncle Abraham and hope that there was more out there. This is such an absurd moment in the book of Genesis that it boggles my mind every time I read it."}]},{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The question we are left with is, why? Well, there will be a purpose for it, but for now, it was strictly because of the environment they grew up in. This was not strange to them. This was probably something that was encouraged in Sodom. They, perhaps, went to school, and the teachers told them how normal it was. Their friends probably talked about it, and they had influences of all sorts around them talking about the normalcy of this."},{"kind":"para","text":"These are things that our society is trying to call normal today. It's heinous and disgusting. This is not something new, though. Abraham made a mistake in much of the same way with his handmaiden. God blesses the offspring of the handmaiden in a particular way, but that lineage becomes an enemy of God's children. Now we will see the children of Lot's daughters..."}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Moab is a city that will be mentioned later in scripture quite a bit. It's funny, because they also become an enemy to the Israelites. Yet without this lineage, we would not have David."}]},{"start":38,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ammon is another culture that becomes the enemy of Israel. These lineages will play a prominent role in leading the Israelites in the right direction, and proving God can give His people victory even through their disgusting sins."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":38}]},
    "20": {"chapter":20,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Sodom was recognized as being a very prosperous land, and Lot and his family were living it luxury, yet around a lot of wickedness. The angels had come to appoint judgement, and Lot takes them into his household for protection. The people of the town find out about it and chaos starts to ensue until the angels take Lot and his family to safety and destroy the cities of Sodom and Gomorrah. Lot loses his wife, and the he loses his daughters purity. It's a real shame of a story, and it leaves us, as the readers, feeling sick and ashamed."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we pick up with Abraham and his goings after the incident occurs, and we see Abraham make some serious mistakes as well. Instead of seeking comfort while things are falling apart spiritually (like Lot) Abraham will have to seek the Lord to build his faith."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"God Warns The King","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Kadesh\" means “Holy.\" It was a city that was in the south of Judah. \"Shur\" means \"wall.\" It was southwest of Palestine, right on the border of Egypt. \"Gerar\" means \"a lodging place.\" This will be a Philistine town south of Gaza. So Abraham brought his family from the hill country near Sodom (he could see the destruction from where he was) and moved to a place between a wall and holiness called \"the lodging place.\" If he jumped \"the wall,\" so to speak, he would be going away from holiness. Abraham was getting his family dangerously far from where they needed to be..."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The first time this happened, they were much younger. It seems that as an older woman, Sarah was still just as beautiful and desirable. You'd think Abraham would have learned his lesson the first time he did this, but it seems the protection of the Lord taught Abraham nothing... Maybe it's because they still haven't had their child, which was promised by the Lord? I don't know, but it's something that is keeping God from providing the promise because they have not addressed this sin, and it's something that continues to come up, obviously."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems this king knew the Lord quite well. He is being warned by God not to touch Sarah and to return Sarah back to Abraham. God intervened in a much different way last time. This time he is speaking directly to the king. This tells us the Pharaoh of Egypt didn't know the Lord, and so it worked against his favor because of it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the LORD plagued Pharaoh and his house with great plagues because of Sarai Abram's wife.","ref":"Genesis 12:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"I'd much rather have the Lord just warn me about the issue instead of being plagued with diseases and distress. God was not pleased by Abraham and Sarah's actions of doing this, and they were trying to cause the king to sin against the Lord personally. Abimelech was a wise king, however, and entreats the Lord at this time."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":16,"title":"Abimelech Confronts Abraham","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's a great question. If the king was Abraham's enemy, I could see Abraham maybe trying this to have the Lord take him out. The king didn't do anything to Abraham, however, and Abraham still tried to destroy the testimony and the life of this king. This was a terrible thing for Abraham to do the first time he did it, and it's still a terrible thing the second time."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems Abimelech had a better relationship with the Lord than Abraham did at this time. Abraham claims he did it because the city seemed like a disgrace to the Lord. He thought that God was not in this place, but he was proven wrong immediately. He might have thought it was similar to Sodom and had the reputation to rape and kill whomever they pleased. But they were not like this, and there was no sign of them ever being like this. Yet, Abraham just assumes they needed the fear of God. The next verse is where it gets interesting though:"}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He is opening up about his lie here. He says, \"It's not a complete lie, but it's not a complete truth either. She is my half-sister in a way, and so I wasn't wrong about that.\" Does it make it okay now? Of course not... Yet Abraham continues:"}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Sarah obeyed the desire of Abraham so that he wouldn't be attacked over her. The sad part is, he didn't need to lie about any of it. Then the sadness gets worse:"}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham was forgiven, and then given more riches again. Abimelech wanted his hands cleaned of the matter, and so paid off Abraham and Sarah, showing them mercy and grace when they truly don't deserve it. That is the way of Christ, mind you. This king was a good, Godly fellow, and it seems he was willing to make the best out of an awful situation. Abraham is being humbled here for the matter. When Abraham left Egypt with spoils, he must have felt pretty great. He thought that his sin was nothing too concerning. Here, however, the king knows the Lord, and it must have made Abraham feel pretty awful for what he had done and the way the king gives mercy to him. I believe Abraham knew, at that moment, he did not deserve to be forgiven. Sarah, likewise..."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let's remember that Abimelech now knows that Abraham is married to Sarah. Instead of saying, \"I have given thy husband a thousand pieces of silver...\" (Which is done to commemorate the fact that Sarah is pure), he says, \"I have given thy 'brother'...\" He is actually kind of mocking them here, and you can feel it. This surely makes Sarah feel ashamed, and it says that she was \"reproved\" for it. \"Reproved\" means \"convicted.\" She feels convicted for what she had done."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":18,"title":"Abraham Realizes His Mistake","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that God was prepared for this moment. He obviously knew which direction Abimelech would go, and they may not have even known that they couldn't bear children, but Moses knew it and so he writes it here to remind the readers that God does not take kindly to evil. He was prepared to make the people of Gerar disappear. Abimelech did what was right, however, and Abraham's faith in the Lord helped restore his family, and Abimelech's as well. God needed Abraham and Sarah to move forward. He needed them to be in the right place. He could have easily just spoken to Abraham and Sarah, mind you, about their sin, but instead you will notice He goes straight to Abimelech. Why? Because Abimelech was innocent here, and Abraham was not trusting in the Lord like he was supposed to be."},{"kind":"para","text":"The best way to get Abraham where he needed to be was not by force, but by his own faith and circumstances. We will not see Abraham and Sarah make this mistake again after this humiliation. God worked on Abraham's faith by using the people around Abraham instead of just directly confronting Abraham in the middle of his sin. Sometimes that is how God will work on us as well. He works on our surroundings while we are ignoring Him, and that will ultimately get us in the right direction. He works on the storm to break the ship, and a whale to swallow us whole. He works on the increase of faith in others while we are decreasing our leniency on Him. He works on the deconstructing of a nation, while His people wander from their lack of faith. He works on paying the fine while the rest of us reject ever knowing Him. We see God using our environments and the people around us when we are being disrespectful and disobedient to Him, and it's because those are the things we can see, while we anticipate His Spirit to work in our lives (which is what we cannot see). If you are struggling in your relationship with the Lord, or you see someone struggling, I guarantee you can look around and see God working through others and your environment to get you back with Him."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":18}]},
    "21": {"chapter":21,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Abraham meets the king of Gerar whose name is Abimelech. Abraham tries the same trick on him as he did with the Egyptians leader by calling Sarah his sister. This time, however, the Lord is with this king and warns him that Abraham is lying. The king confronts Abraham about the issue and wonders why God's chosen man would try and trick him like this. Abimelech lets him go in good faith and Abraham realizes the mistakes he has made in his faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see the story continue with Abraham, and we see the promise kept by the Lord for a son to be born. There's a lot of twists and turns in this chapter, and I'm hoping to the best I can to make it clear and evident for the set up of the rest of scripture."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"Isaac Is Born","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a moment. I couldn't imagine having a child at such an old age. This is a very good image of God's timing, though. We might not know why God is not answering our prayers right away. Honestly, it might seem that God is taking far too long to answer our needs and meet with us. It might even seem as if He is not listening or that He doesn't actually care. To Abraham and Sarah, it took time. Of course, there were some issues in their faith that needed to be addressed, but God also had a waiting period planned. He knew when He was going to answer their prayers, and it had to be at this particular time. Sometimes it will be like that with us. If we stay fervent and committed in prayer, we might not see it answered until we are at a good old age, but we can bet any money that God will answer it in His way and time."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Isaac\" means \"he laughs.\" Who laughs? Well, Sarah laughed when she heard that she would have a child at her old age. Abraham laughed when he heard that he would have a child at his old age (and also took matters into his own hands because he thought God was playing a joke on him). But it was God who has the last laugh. And it's God who names Isaac, if you don't remember."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And God said, Sarah thy wife shall bear thee a son indeed; and thou shalt call his name Isaac: and I will establish my covenant with him for an everlasting covenant, and with his seed after him.","ref":"Genesis 17:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"God certainly has a sense of humor, and it's written all throughout Scripture. When we find something a little funny in Scripture, it is more than likely intended to be funny because God finds humor in it. Especially when it revolves around God proving Himself in the most unorthodox of ways."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham was keeping God's covenant promise, and there was even more proof that God would keep His promise through the birth of Isaac."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But my covenant will I establish with Isaac, which Sarah shall bear unto thee at this set time in the next year.","ref":"Genesis 17:21"}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It is quite humorous. It is also quite the miracle. The birth of this child is nothing short of miraculous and unheard of. Even during that time, it was a very strange thing to happen. It was exciting, but it was extremely rare. For Sarah to even survive giving birth to a child at such an old age is a miracle in itself. But she wasn't even supposed to have children because she was barren. The whole world, at this point, seemed to know it, and now she is amazed by the miracle of birth. It seems God uses the miracles of birth often throughout Scripture, and we will see it time and time again, including when Christ is born."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":13,"title":"Ishmael Mocks Isaac","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A momentous occasion. It's a good idea to celebrate good things. When a child is weaned from their childhood things, we should all have a feast to commemorate it. Make things like this a big deal, and the child will feel the love of his or her family. It's a moment of celebration for sure for Abraham and Sarah because they recognized the fact that this child is growing without any sort of health complications or issues. He was their miracle child."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ishmael did not like this new child. He felt left out and neglected. It is quite possible that he was. Abraham took the word of the Lord seriously in the matter that Ishmael would not be the one that would take the covenant ( which was given to the children of Israel, but that is still to come). I believe that Ishmael doesn't understand the importance of this child. He either never paid attention when Abraham spoke of it, or Abraham never really clarified to Ishmael who he was. Then again, Jesus was mocked by His own family when His ministry started."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For neither did his brethren believe in him.\" John 7:5 \"And they were offended in him. But Jesus said unto them, A prophet is not without honour, save in his own country, and in his own house.","ref":"Matthew 13:57"}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's obvious here that Abraham still loved Ishmael. It was not an easy thing to figure out on behalf of kicking out his other son. He didn't like the idea of this at all. He had a deep struggle with what to do, and God got his attention about it."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ishmael was made out of a fleshly moment. He was not the one that was promised to Abraham by the Lord. The Lord had made it clear that it would be SARAH that had the child who would lead the promise, and Abraham messed that up in his own unbelief. It could be that Abraham still had a very tough time doing this, but when the Lord bids him, it was wise (especially from what he has learned through Isaac and the other promises God has made) for him to listen and obey no matter how difficult the decision was to be."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There was a promise still given to Ishmael. Ishmael had a very important task as well, but it was not the same as Isaac's task would be. Both would be great nations. Both would have a purpose in the plans of God and the future of humanity. One had the promise of a Messiah. The other had a promise of rebellion. One had the promise of salvation. The other had the promise of wild living. One had the promise of provision from the Lord. The other had the promise of provision from the Earth. We see that this promise is still kept today."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":21,"title":"Ishmael's Near Death","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She must have forgotten the promise that the Lord had made to her about her child. It seems Ishmael was coming to the end of his life and in desperate need. There was no water left for them to take, and so she just thought this was it. Hagar didn't have much faith in God at all, and it shows here pretty plainly."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is where we will end the story of Ishmael. God took care of him, and it is obvious even today as you see the Arab nations continue on. The Quran has a lot of interesting stories in it about Ishmael and his leadership. Obviously, the Bible story is focused on the coming of the Savior, and that will only be through the lineage of Isaac. The Quran's story is focused on their idea of a savior, and he is one that was nothing like the Son of God. Of course, the promised covenant wasn't through Ishmael, so the story of the Quran cannot confess to the truth of God's Word."},{"kind":"para","text":"God's Word is scripture found in the Holy Bible. Why is that? Because from cover to cover of these sixty-six books, it talks about one person, that is Jesus Christ (Immanuel named by the prophets before Him), full of grace and truth. He is God's Word made flesh, and you cannot say that about Muhammad no matter which way you twist it. Does this mean that Arabs can't be saved? Absolutely not. They have rejected the Savior simply because they feel rejected by the Lord, but if they only knew the Bible story and how relevant it is to them, they would know the true God."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Jesus saith unto him, I am the way, the truth, and the life: no man cometh unto the Father, but by me.","ref":"John 14:6"}]}]},{"start":22,"end":32,"title":"A Covenant Well","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abimelech wants to make a deal with Abraham. It should be obvious at this point that they both know the Lord, and so when they are making a deal here, they are making it in good faith towards God and towards each other. That's how you know a deal can be trusted, when both parties are blatantly obviously following the Lord and His Will."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There was an issue of a water source that Abraham felt belonged to him, and so he brought it before Abimelech."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Beersheba\" means \"Well of the sevenfold oath.\" This will be a place mentioned quite often throughout Scripture. Interestingly enough, it is where Hagar and Ishmael were when they were wandering. Could it be the same well that's spoken of here? I would say not. The place had no name before this encounter here, and so when it's mentioned in verse 14, Moses was alluding to what the place would be called later."},{"kind":"para","text":"In verses 20-21 we see that Ishmael grew up in the wilderness and ends up marrying a woman from Egypt. Obviously some time has passed since they started their journey, and so when we get to this portion of Scripture here, Abraham is in the future (of when Ishmael drank from a well there) and he is just now calling it Beersheba because of the covenant made there. It seems the well that Ishmael drinks from is an established one. The one that Abraham makes is one he digs himself."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Philistines will be a very prominent enemy to the Israelites later on. It's a little sad that Abimelech (who is Abraham's friend here) is from this land, considering they turn to hate Israel so much when time passes."}]}]},{"start":33,"end":34,"title":"Abraham Doesn't Belong","entries":[{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Even in the Philistines' land, Abraham was leaving a testimony and calling on the Lord. Obviously, Abraham didn't belong in this land. He would never find a home here, and the next generations would prove that it's not a place that is welcomed to God's people. The Philistines turned into a wicked and mighty nation quite quickly, and the Israelites were tasked with battling them for quite some time. I believe the Philistines were placed here for that specific purpose. They were there to get Israel’s faith back in place. It is only through the conflicts that you know where your faith stands. It's only through the tests that you will grow in faith. Without them, you will not know where to go."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":32},{"start":33,"end":34}]},
    "22": {"chapter":22,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: We last left off seeing Abraham rejoice over his son Isaac, and Ishmael growing in hatred towards Isaac. It is forced at that point for Ishmael to leave and live on his own, separated from the royal lineage. There's not much mention of what COULD have happened if he stayed, but my guess is it would have been pretty bad. Abraham ends up leaving where he is suppose to be, and dwells in a Philistines land. He feels hurt because Ishmael was forced to leave his household (Abraham loved his children, there was no doubt about that)."},{"kind":"para","text":"We pick up in this chapter with God pushing Abraham further after some time has passed. Ishmael was an adult last we read about him, so Isaac has grown in age as well. Abraham had to be very old and brittle at this time, and we will see a very meek soldier of the Lord grown in faith, both through Abraham and Isaac in this chapter."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"A Promised Sacrifice","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now you might be asking, \"Why would a good, loving God tempt His innocent children? Is this temptation into sin?\" Well, we haven't read the temptation yet, but you could argue that the temptation about to take place is a (in a worldly sense) \"sin.\" \"Tempt\" means \"to test, try, to attempt.\" It's not like the world's idea of temptation. James speaks a lot about temptation in His epistle, and the summation of it is, we are going to be \"tried\" quite a bit by the Lord. Those times of trial are times of strengthening. When it comes down to this trial of Abraham, it is no different."},{"kind":"para","text":"God is going to test Abraham. Why? Not because God is curious to see what Abraham might do (God already knows what Abraham will do). Rather, God is testing Abraham so Abraham can increase his own faith even more. He will have to obey and wonder what the Lord means by the test that is coming his way. Sometimes that is the way of our faith as well. We go through a difficult health problem, and instead of questioning God's motives, we should just be trusting His heart."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Moriah\" means \"Chosen by Jehovah.\" We have not been introduced to this land yet. It seems Abraham knows the place in which God is talking about, or perhaps the place was not named until later as Moses is penning this situation down. It is a place that will be mentioned again in 2 Chronicles as the place where Solomon builds the Lord’s house. This place seemed to be a place of significance to the Lord, and it's quite a place of significance to the world today. It's a place that many nations have fought over. Today, Islam has taken much of it over and it is filled with mosques and shrines, and all sorts of unbiblical things. During the time of Jesus, King Herod’s temple stood atop this sight."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when he had made a scourge of small cords, he drove them all out of the temple, and the sheep, and the oxen; and poured out the changers' money, and overthrew the tables;","ref":"John 2:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"The only part of the temple that stands today is what is referred to as the \"Wailing Wall,\" where many Jews still go today for prayer. It is prophesied (and assumed) that a third temple will be built there in the future."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he shall confirm the covenant with many for one week: and in the midst of the week he shall cause the sacrifice and the oblation to cease, and for the overspreading of abominations he shall make it desolate, even until the consummation, and that determined shall be poured upon the desolate.","ref":"Daniel 9:27"},{"kind":"para","text":"This place is significant for sure throughout scripture, and it's something that should be taken with special interest by those of us today because when the third temple is built, we will know the end is near. Furthermore, God is asking Abraham to do quite an impossible task here. This is not an easy thing for Abraham to hear. God has made promise after promise already about His chosen lineage through Isaac, and now He's asking Abraham to kill the promised son? This seems crazy even for the Lord to ask, let alone for Abraham to follow through with..."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":8,"title":"The Lord Provides Himself","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Note the third day. Abraham has fought with the Lord about a lot of the promises that were made already throughout much of the previous chapters that we have read. Yet, here, we see him just obeying. He's going to the place, and he's prepared to sacrifice his own son (the only one he has at the moment; his other son was taken out to a different land). Abraham had to have his doubts. I imagine that is what verse 4 is implying."},{"kind":"para","text":"He arrives at the base of the mount, looks up towards the mountain peak, and thinks, \"I'm trusting you know what you're doing.\" I can't say for sure that this is what he is thinking, but I have the right to believe that he is just like every other sane human being and is confident that this situation is questionable at best. However, we have an idea of what he was thinking in Hebrews."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"By faith Abraham, when he was tried, offered up Isaac: and he that had received the promises offered up his only begotten son, Of whom it was said, That in Isaac shall thy seed be called: Accounting that God was able to raise him up, even from the dead; from whence also he received him in a figure.","ref":"Hebrews 11:17-19"},{"kind":"para","text":"I imagine that Abraham’s thoughts were, \"I have already seen God provide on His promises. If I kill Isaac today, God will just raise him back from the dead.” But the faith story here is much deeper than that."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Isaac is tasked with carrying his own death. Isaac wasn't going to just be an offering. It was not as if Isaac was just simply going to be slaughtered or killed. God told Abraham to make Isaac a \"burnt\" offering. Abraham would have to kill his son, and then set him on fire. This seems cruel beyond measure, and if it were me, I would not be quite as compliant as Abraham is here. I would certainly have questions and conversation with Isaac about it. Surely, Isaac must know what is about to take place... Right?"}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The way Abraham answers here is assuring to Isaac. Abraham could have just said, \"What is it?\" Instead, he says, \"I am here, my son.\" It's a mode of love given to his child. It's a reminder to both Isaac and Abraham that they are here together, and they have a deep care for each other. Abraham LOVES Isaac. Abraham also knows the Lord will not break promises, and he was assured and confident that the Lord was going to do a miracle this day. It seems, however, that Isaac had no idea what was happening this day. He didn't know that HE was the offering for the day. \"Where is the lamb?\" Isaac asks. That's a good question for us to ask as well. If we don't have Christ today, we must ask the question, \"Where is the lamb for a burnt offering?\" Where is the one that will be our sacrifice? If we cannot find Him, we will have to sacrifice ourselves. Do we live up to the standards of sacrifice? Are we perfect and without spot? Are we good enough to be a reasonable sacrifice for ourselves? If not, we must find a substitute."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is an extremely powerful verse written here. In the CSB it says, \"God himself will provide the lamb for the burnt offering...\" The KJV is far more superior in the design of this sentence. This is one that works well with the lineage of scripture because it's spoken in a more Yoda backwards way. Instead of saying, \"God will provide a lamb...\" It says, \"God will provide Himself...\" The truth is (and maybe this was the realization for this moment in Abraham's life) God is our sacrifice. He has provided Himself as our lamb."},{"kind":"para","text":"I want to amplify this if at all possible. The essence of this whole part of scripture is saying, \"And the father of multitude (for that was the promise made to him and it's even in his name) promised, My son, The Rulers/Judges, Divine Ones, The True God (Remember when it's used here it's in a plural sense (not given a number but we can assume trinity), so we could say...) My son, the Trinity will present Himself as a lamb, for a whole burnt offering (or even as a stairway)...\" So to simplify the amplification of each definition here, it would say, \"And the father of multitude promised, My son, The Trinity will present Himself as a Lamb, for our stairway (our connection to Him).\" They went both \"together\" after that. \"Together\" means \"Unified.\" They both trusted that the Lord would provide. Isaac didn't know the full extent of this yet. But he trusted that his dad knew what he was talking about."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":14,"title":"God Will Provide","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that Isaac didn't thrash or question what was happening. It was not as if Isaac was a child going through this test. Isaac was a grown man. He honestly could have just knocked his old man out and walked away. He could have easily resisted and made it difficult on Abraham. Perhaps there were more words spoken here, but they are certainly not recorded. I believe they aren't recorded specifically because Isaac had a strong faith that his father knew what he was doing. His father wasn't just an old and senile man. His father was a faithful man, and his father had never done anything towards him in the past that was not drenched in God's goodness and provision."},{"kind":"para","text":"I know there are a lot of jokes about the amount of family therapy this family would have to have when this was said and done, and I love the jokes myself. But I don't think there is a necessity for therapy here. I believe Isaac was a willing sacrifice. I think he actually wanted to see what God would do here too. He understood the promises that God made as well as his own father, and he hung onto those promises. There may have been a little fear or worry in the eyes of both of them, but there was a much stronger faith that was propelling them forward with the task."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham was ready to comply. Abraham has done a lot of faith building since he's been introduced to us in this book. He was not a man that was very strong in faith, but he desired to see where the Lord would take him. He didn't fully trust all things the Lord would tell him, and it got him into some trouble. Here, though, he is seen trusting it all. His faith is full, and he knows that God will do something great here, even if it seems like a bleak and terrible trial at the moment. That's what real faith is. It's not knowing what will happen next, but just trusting that God knows what He is doing."},{"kind":"para","text":"I've heard a lot of people say, \"So if God tells you to jump off a bridge, would you do it?\" The short answer is, \"yes.\" My long answer is, \"It would have to be evident that it was God telling me, then I would not hesitate to jump off that bridge.\" There are a lot of people today who play the \"God\" card. When asked, \"Why did you rape and murder those thirteen women?\" they say, “Well, God told me.\" No, God did not tell them. God did not commend them into this sin. He did not commend Abraham into a sin either. Isaac was a willing sacrifice, and Abraham directly heard the instructions from the voice of the Lord. God does not speak to His children in this way anymore. He does not give \"new\" commands. His commands have already been given."},{"kind":"para","text":"Mind you, this is before the Ten Commandments are given. Before the Levitical law was penned. Abraham was not bound to God's law in that way. Rather, he was moving by faith. He heard God speak and command, and he was to obey and follow. He knows that God has made a promise, and maybe he was repeating that promise in his mind. He picks up the knife, ready to obey, and thinks to himself, \"In Isaac your seed shall be called...\""}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham must have felt a sigh of relief when the angel of the Lord called out. We, once again, believe this to be Christ speaking to Abraham here. It's fitting for this moment, isn't it?"}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"...now I know...\" So did God not know before? That's not what this is saying. God knew. He was assured that Abraham was built in his faith. He, however, needed Abraham to know how far his faith was willing to go. Isaac was going to come back down the mountain alive this day because of the intervention of the Lord. As it is with us."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Human sacrifice was never permitted by the Lord. He only accepted animal sacrifice. Why? Because there are no humans on this earth who are innocent enough to be sacrificed for anything. The only one worthy of a sacrifice was the Lord, and He gave Himself (willingly, I might add) so that we might have everlasting life through Him. Isaac would not be a justified sacrifice. Only Jesus (the one stopping Abraham from killing his own child) will be."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jehovahjireh\" means \"God will provide.\" It is the place where the temple was built. It was the place of Golgotha, the place where Christ was crucified. It's the place where the world receives salvation. The promise is still true to its name today. God WILL provide, and we know He will because He already HAS."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":19,"title":"A Promise Fulfilled","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was the final seal for the lineage of salvation. It was through this sacrificial act of faith from Abraham that his seed was proven, and our great Lord and Savior will be born. It's a worthy seed, if not for any other reason than for this particular scene here. The moment that Jesus comes, He fulfills what Abraham could not. He fulfills what Moses could not. He fulfills what David could not. He fulfills what Solomon could not. He fulfills what every prophet could not. He fulfills what the Apostles could not. He fulfills what religion cannot. He fulfills what our good works cannot. He fulfills everything, and the promise that God is making here is a promise that has been proven for generations upon generations. Deny scripture all you want, but there is no way to deny the fulfillment of the Lord to all those that accept His sacrifice and believe."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham is dwelling in \"Beersheba.\" Remember, it's called \"the well of the sevenfold oath.\" It sits on the south edge of Israel. We talked about it quite a bit in our last chapter."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":24,"title":"The Lineage of Nahor","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We might have thought that Nahor was a lost lineage, seeing as we are focused solemnly on Abraham and his own journeys. We must not forget that Abraham had left his family at the beginning of all of this, and God was still providing through them in a different way. We won't read into this family too much, but I think it does us well (as we've been doing throughout our study) to give the definitions of these names to know what kind of family they have."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Huz\" means “Wooded.\" \"Buz\" means \"Contempt.\" Huz and Buz are brothers. That's obvious by the reading of this passage, but I just wanted you to say it out loud because it's funny. \"Kemuel\" means \"Raised of God.\" and Kemuel fathered Aram. \"Aram\" means “Exalted.\" It is where Syria comes from."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Chesed\" means \"increase.\" \"Hazo\" means \"Vision.\" Perhaps they had great future hopes for Hazo... \"Pildash\" means \"Flame of Fire.\" He might have been a very difficult child. \"Jidlaph\" means \"weeping.\" All children might be named such. \"Bethuel\" means \"God destroys.\" That's not a very nice name... It could also mean, \"Man of God,\" or \"dweller of God.\" It probably means that more than \"God destroys\" but I couldn't tell you that for sure. It's also something that will be brought up later as a location south of Simeon."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Rebekah\" means \"ensnarer.\" She will become an important character in the life of Isaac later on, but that's a spoiler."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Reumah\" means \"elevated.\" She had four children (I'm assuming while she was in the household of Nahor. It's likely that Nahor is the father of these children…). \"Tebah\" means \"a slaughter.\" Another not very nice name. \"Gaham\" means \"burning.\" \"Thahash\" means “dugong.\" \"Maachah\" means \"oppression.\" If it is Nahor naming these children, he is a very terrible man. I don't believe it was him though. Men are not good at naming children, let's just be real. It would likely be the woman naming them. Milcah had decent enough names for her children. But Reumah was festering some hate and evil, and it was quite obvious through the naming of her children. She probably did not like having children like this. She was being \"abused\" likely. I won't go into accusations, but it's an interesting take when you are reading the names in this way. The lineage of Maachah will become a very hardened people. It will later be them who will be hired to fight David, but again, that's getting ahead of myself."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":24}]},
    "23": {"chapter":23,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Our last chapter saw a close call between Isaac and Abrahams sacrifice. God illustrated the pain it would be for when He sacrifices His own Son on the cross, and this is a reminder to Abraham what this is all for. We may think that God is not working fast enough, or that He doesn't have that great of a plan, but we come to find out that God's timing is always on point. There is no mistakes with how long, or how quickly God shows up or doesn't. Even when God doesn't show up, He still shows up (if that makes sense)."},{"kind":"para","text":"In our chapter today, we get a picture of a very sad and morbid funeral that Abraham and Isaac must have. This chapter doesn't have many \"happy\" things happening in it, and so it's a bit somber, but I believe it has an important message for all of us today (especially knowing Christ and having a hope in eternity)."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"Sarah's Death","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We've seen a lot happen with these two throughout this book so far. This is a heartbreaking way to start this chapter, but it shows the love and heart that Abraham had for Sarah. She was one hundred and twenty-seven years old when she died. She had Isaac at ninety, which means that Isaac (at her death) was thirty-seven years old. He buried her in the land that was promised to him. Sadly, the land won't be theirs for a while, but Abraham knew the promise and trusted God with it. He could have buried her anywhere he wanted, but he chose this land because he knew that God would keep His promise."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":16,"title":"Buying the Burial Plot","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham thinks of himself as a stranger here, even though God promises him the land. In all honesty, this land (no matter where we are residing today) is temporary. We are strangers and pilgrims, just passing through to our true home in eternity. We look forward to the day when we will leave this land to be in a better one."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems these children of Heth knew Abraham well, and they respected him. They were willing to give whatever burial place he decided would be fit for his wife. They even refer to him as \"a mighty prince,\" which means they saw Abraham as royalty. Perhaps it was his wealth that they saw value in? Maybe it was his character? Possibly his victories? I don't know, but they knew there was something special and important about him, and so they let him pick wherever he wanted to bury his wife."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham shows honor to those that were showing honor to him. This was a big deal to Abraham, and an even bigger deal that they were willing to open up their own land for him to utilize for his family."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham knows a spot, but he didn't just want it for free. We read the account, and it seems the children of Heth were willing to give him the land completely for free. Abraham offers a payment for the land because he knows it is not his to utilize (at least not yet). Now, remember, God promised Abraham this land already, but Abraham counted it as a future promise. I think Abraham knew that it wouldn't be him that would take the land, but rather his children that would inherit it. All-in-all, still, Abraham's lineage would take it, and it was a promise made and kept to him specifically."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ephron was there when Abraham said he wanted to purchase the burial spot, and Ephron simply says, \"No, I WANT you to have it.” He knew Abraham was grieving and needing a place for his wife, and so he wasn't willing to offer him a price for it. He gives him an even more impressive title than “prince.\" He calls him “lord.\" Abraham was like a good master to this man, and so the land was freely given."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham wanted to put value in the field. He knew it was a great place to bury his wife. He knew that it would be worth every penny. He knew that the place was valuable and that someday he would be in it with his wife, buried in the land that God had promised to him and his descendants. He was encouraged and humbled by the fact that they were willing to just give it to him, but he couldn't take it without a cost."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Four hundred shekels of silver was worth quite a bit back then. It may not have been the richest place, but it was still worth its weight in silver. Four hundred shekels of silver is worth about ninety-eight dollars in the US today. Many would kill to have that cheap of a burial spot in the place you desire the most."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It is said that all of Abraham's family (except for Rachel, which will be talked about later) are buried there. There is a mosque that is built over that land now, and tourists can go in and see a deep hole where the bodies are buried today. They believed (back then especially, and in some religions still today) that the dead would rise again in this land. That was the belief for a very long time. The dead certainly have risen, but not in the way that the people were thinking it would happen. Jesus proved that with Lazarus."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then said Martha unto Jesus, Lord, if thou hadst been here, my brother had not died. But I know, that even now, whatsoever thou wilt ask of God, God will give it thee. Jesus saith unto her, Thy brother shall rise again. Martha saith unto him, I know that he shall rise again in the resurrection at the last day. Jesus said unto her, I am the resurrection, and the life: he that believeth in me, though he were dead, yet shall he live: And whosoever liveth and believeth in me shall never die. Believest thou this? She saith unto him, Yea, Lord: I believe that thou art the Christ, the Son of God, which should come into the world. And when she had so said, she went her way, and called Mary her sister secretly, saying, The Master is come, and calleth for thee. As soon as she heard that, she arose quickly, and came unto him. Now Jesus was not yet come into the town, but was in that place where Martha met him. The Jews then which were with her in the house, and comforted her, when they saw Mary, that she rose up hastily and went out, followed her, saying, She goeth unto the grave to weep there. Then when Mary was come where Jesus was, and saw him, she fell down at his feet, saying unto him, Lord, if thou hadst been here, my brother had not died. When Jesus therefore saw her weeping, and the Jews also weeping which came with her, he groaned in the spirit, and was troubled, And said, Where have ye laid him? They said unto him, Lord, come and see. Jesus wept. Then said the Jews, Behold how he loved him! And some of them said, Could not this man, which opened the eyes of the blind, have caused that even this man should not have died? Jesus therefore again groaning in himself cometh to the grave. It was a cave, and a stone lay upon it. Jesus said, Take ye away the stone. Martha, the sister of him that was dead, saith unto him, Lord, by this time he stinketh: for he hath been dead four days. Jesus saith unto her, Said I not unto thee, that, if thou wouldest believe, thou shouldest see the glory of God? Then they took away the stone from the place where the dead was laid. And Jesus lifted up his eyes, and said, Father, I thank thee that thou hast heard me. And I knew that thou hearest me always: but because of the people which stand by I said it, that they may believe that thou hast sent me. And when he thus had spoken, he cried with a loud voice, Lazarus, come forth. And he that was dead came forth, bound hand and foot with graveclothes: and his face was bound about with a napkin. Jesus saith unto them, Loose him, and let him go.","ref":"John 11:21-44"},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus is the resurrection to the believers today. We will rise again into eternal life. We are not to worry about an earthly resurrection. We should worry about a heavenly one. What happens and remains with our bodies here on Earth makes no difference to the Lord. The only thing that matters is our souls’ destination."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":20,"title":"He Secures the Property","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Everyone saw the transaction. They had no excuses to say, “Well, we GAVE this land for free to Abraham, so now he owes us...\" or any other sort of bitter talk. Abraham purchased the land in front of them all, and he made it very clear with Ephron there."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"No one could touch that place, because it belonged to Abraham and his lineage. The mosque that is built there today is in dedication to Abraham. We have spoken loosely on this before, but the Arabs believe that they have come from the lineage of Abraham. Surely they have, but it was not through the chosen lineage that God speaks of in Scripture. You can read the Quran today and see that they put a real emphasis on Abraham's lineage. The truth is, however, they were not part of this chosen lineage, but they can still be grafted into the faith just as easily as the rest of us. I may not have been an Israelite, but I am certainly part of the family of God. Not because I am special, or I have found a secret entrance. No, it's because Christ has given access to the whole world to enter into the family of God."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He came unto his own, and his own received him not. But as many as received him, to them gave he power to become the sons of God, even to them that believe on his name: Which were born, not of blood, nor of the will of the flesh, nor of the will of man, but of God.","ref":"John 1:11-13"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":20}]},
    "24": {"chapter":24,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Our last chapter was a bleak one. We see the death of Sarah, and the heavy hearts of Abraham and Isaac. It was not an easy chapter to read by any means."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we are lighted quite a bit with hope and assurance. Abraham is coming to the end of his own life, and he still has some things to tie up before he's gone. One particular thing is giving Isaac someone to have and to hold before he's left alone..."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":9,"title":"Abraham's Servant","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham has had a blessed life (though it has taken a lot of growing pains to get to this point). He is at the point where he knows his own life is coming to an end, and he is desiring to see his son get married. He doesn't want him to marry a Canaanite, however (which is the land he is currently dwelling in (it's also the land promised to him and his lineage to come)), so he speaks to his servant in whom he trusts very much."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham wants to send his servant back to the land in which Abraham started to find a wife for Isaac. He knew that those people would be better suited for him, simply because they were not practicing the same kinds of terrible ethics as the Canaanites (or at least they weren't when he left). This is kind of a bold strategy, seeing as he hasn't seen his family in a very long time. There's no telling what his people have turned into at this point. This was another faith practice for Abraham, but he seems to be doing quite well in trusting that the Lord knows what is best, so he's trusting that the Lord has sustained the families he knew back in Haran."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's a fair question, but Abraham doesn't want Isaac to go to that land. There's a reason that they were in the land of the Canaanites, and Isaac must stay in the promised land. Abraham clears up why in the next verse..."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham was right about what God had promised, and so he's holding tight to the promise of the Lord. God also told him that his lineage would take over four hundred years for them to finally come into the land, and that is ultimately what happens (as we will see in the book of Exodus). But to keep close to God's promises, Abraham doesn't want Isaac to leave the country that God has promised him. Abraham wants Isaac to be familiar with it, and he wants Isaac to pass down the knowledge of this country to his children and so on and so forth until they finally do take the land."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a small gesture of commitment, but it was one that Abraham could bear to make. He's old and can't walk around much like he used to. He might have found a different means before to try and make a promise or a deal with someone (perhaps killing an animal to seal the deal), but he can't do much in his old age, and this is why he's sending his servant to find his son a wife. Otherwise, Abraham would perhaps do it himself. Though he doesn't want to stray far from the land that God has put him in either, he would be willing to do this one small task just to make sure his son is taken care of and so he gets to meet who his son marries. He would be quite thankful if it wasn't a Canaanite."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":28,"title":"The Servant Searches","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a very direct prayer. It seems to me that this might be the servant’s first prayer that he's ever made. He is kind of indirect with it, and he is willing to test God in this matter to see if the one that Abraham serves is in fact the real God of Heaven. Abraham planted a seed in his servant’s heart, and his servant is testing his faith. He doesn't want to mess this up for his master's sake, and so it seems the only wise thing to do is to really see if God is on his side in this or not. He prays that there would be ONE woman that paid attention to him and gave him something to drink. If there was ONE that did this, he would know for sure that God has given this one over for Isaac and he would be able to go and talk to her family about it."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So Rebekah was the one that came out, and it seems it was kind of instantaneous after this servant’s prayer. It happened even before he was done praying to the Lord. She was there, and perhaps right towards the end of his prayer, she stood out amongst everyone else, and it was quite obvious to this servant, but it will be even more obvious in a moment..."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He knows that this is the one. She answered his prayer down to the letter. Everything he wanted to know and see in a woman was literally presented to him right then and there. My assumption is that he was in such awe at how this came to be and the fact that God answered so quickly that he was kind of dazed at the whole matter. This was a seemingly small miracle to everyone else, but quite a big deal to this servant. Everything lined up."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He seems a bit forward. He presents some gold which was worth a lot in their territory as an offering for a place to stay, but if I were this young girl, I would probably think he was a little bit of a strange man. I don't know if I would have trusted him to stay with my family. Rebekah, however, seems far more trusting, and maybe she hasn't run into many who are in need like this man? Plus, the amount of material riches he was willing to offer her may have been a decent sway in her eyes."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It all was well too perfect. This servant was amazed! Everything was exactly right, and he knew it. You can tell by the way he bows his head in worship to the Lord. He can't quite believe what he is hearing and seeing, and because of his unbelief, he says, \"I am so thankful that God has listened to my prayers, even though I didn't believe and I was just in the way...\" What a powerful statement. That is how we are sometimes. We know that God is doing something, and instead of just trusting His leading, we get in the way and are amazed that He's doing something even with us fighting against it. God is too kind for us to resist."}]}]},{"start":29,"end":31,"title":"A Place to Lodge","entries":[{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This servant was rich. I don't think Laban really cared all that much about the fact that Abraham was someone that was related to them. He cared more about the fact that this man had money, and there was plenty of it for them to have if they were kind to this man. Maybe, there would be even more if they could get in touch with Abraham as well..."}]}]},{"start":32,"end":49,"title":"A Recount","entries":[{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He had a job to do, and that was his most important task. This man was a faithful man to Abraham. There are not many in the world today who would be this committed to a leader. This man didn't care about the luxuries that Abraham had and could give. He was just happy to serve Abraham. He seemed to enjoy serving Abraham. Why? I believe Abraham was brimming with the joy of the Lord. Anyone that worked for Abraham would be happy about it because Abraham had a Godly spirit that was worth following."},{"kind":"para","text":"Some Pastors are not worth serving today. I hate to write this down, but it's true nevertheless. There are some that you serve who are not brimming with any sort of joy of the Lord, but rather are just power hungry. A pastor (or any servant of God for that matter) should be able to have a Spirit that goes against everyone else in their area. Sadly, there aren't many that do, and so it is difficult to serve a Pastor with any sort of joy. Most serve because they are expecting something out of it, and that's the type of spirit the Pastor is giving off. Service becomes more of a transaction rather than a desire..."}]},{"start":41,"end":41,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This servant is giving an exact recap of everything that led him here. Maybe there was a different way he could have gone about it, but he wanted to tell every detail because he was still amazed about how this worked out for them..."}]},{"start":48,"end":48,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This recounting of events was exactly spot on. Everything was precisely remembered. When I am retelling an event, sometimes there are details that get lost on me. I have to say, \"Oh yeah, and this also happened.” Not this servant. He was very sharp and keen on remembering all of the details of this day. This recount is quite impressive."}]},{"start":49,"end":49,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He knows his time is not wasted, but as an extra precaution, he says, \"If you think I'm wasting my time, let me know now, and I will leave, and if not, then let me know, and we can talk about the next steps from here.” These people were about to give up one of their children. She certainly was loved by her family. This is not an easy task for any parent. It's especially not an easy task for a parent who has never met the boy their daughter is marrying. I could not just give up my daughter because some man has said he prayed to the Lord about it, even if it was my brother’s children requesting her in marriage. (Actually, in this day and age, that would be a very strange request from family. That's even more of a reason I would not condone or allow this kind of situation to take shape, but it's a very different culture today than it was back then.) Yet, here, we see them come to a point where a decision has to be made, and it seems to be happening all quite fast..."}]}]},{"start":50,"end":60,"title":"Rebekah Leaves","entries":[{"start":52,"end":52,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They basically say, \"If this is from God, we can't do anything to stop it. You have made it clear that God has prospered you and brought you here, and so Rebekah must go with you.” That's a tremendous blessing for the servant to hear. That will be a tremendous blessing for Abraham to hear as well. His family has not left their faith in the Lord, and Abraham will rejoice to hear that news. Furthermore, he will rejoice to hear how all of this took shape and see that his servant has an increase of faith as well."}]},{"start":54,"end":54,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So they rejoiced together about the occasion. He got up the next day ready to leave and wanted their permission to take their daughter:"}]},{"start":55,"end":55,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ten days is a lot to ask. Then again, this was all so sudden. But they knew that the situation was quite urgent, and yet they seem to hesitate here a little bit about this situation. They have a lot of wealth from this single man here giving so much, and we hope that they aren't trying to get more out of him."}]},{"start":57,"end":57,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They were going to leave it up to Rebekah to decide when she should leave."}]},{"start":60,"end":60,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Rebekah was going to bring prosperity to the family name. She certainly will. She will become an integral character of faith, as we will soon see in the chapters to come. She knew what had to be done, and I think she was quite excited about the venture. This was a new stage in her life, and she might have felt a bit stuck where she was staying. She will explore new lands, and she will become the wife of a chosen lineage."}]}]},{"start":61,"end":67,"title":"Rebekah Marries Isaac","entries":[{"start":63,"end":63,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What an exciting moment for Isaac. He goes out to meditate (perhaps on the Lord in prayer), and he is anticipating the arrival of his soon-to-be wife. There was still a chance he wouldn't marry, and so he didn't have his hopes too high (that might be why he is meditating here). But he sees the camels, and hope comes rushing back to him!"}]},{"start":65,"end":65,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I would assume that the servant told Rebekah all about the man that she is going to marry. She probably knows a fair deal about him, but she doesn't know what he looks like. She is seeing him for the first time, and it was common in those days for a woman to hide her face out of respect and honor. She was so excited about meeting this man of God, and it seems he was excited to hear about the journey this servant went on."}]},{"start":67,"end":67,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a really joyous and yet sad ending to this chapter. Isaac had felt quite alone since his mother died. If this servant didn't bring anyone back for him, his heart might have broken. He felt comforted and connected to Rebekah right away, and it made him happy to know that he had someone with him, especially as his father was so far along in age. He knew it wouldn't be too much longer before his father died, and then what would Isaac do? Having a wife produced courage in Isaac, and she gave him comfort. It is how it should be with all of mankind."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is a great connection between God presenting us a spouse here on Earth and God connecting with the church for all of eternity. There are other books we can talk about this in (Song of Solomon, Ephesians, and the likes), but God wants His church to be His bride. Why? Because it brings us courage and comfort when we have Him to rely on."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":49},{"start":50,"end":60},{"start":61,"end":67}]},
    "25": {"chapter":25,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter we saw the setting up (and successful) journey of Abraham's servant to find a wife for Isaac. Rebekah is brought into the picture through a series of miraculous intervention (God makes it very easy for this servant to find who he was looking for) and Rebekah comes back with him to meet her future husband. It seems that they fall in love at first sight, and everything looks blissful and fulfilled as we closed the chapter off."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we get the end of Abraham's life, and the beginning of Isaac's life with his wife Rebekah as they seek to have children of their own to fulfil the familial promises of God."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":4,"title":"Abraham's Other Children","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In Abraham's old age, he found another wife to marry and spend his last days with. I would find it hard to live life alone after my wife had passed away. \"Keturah\" means \"incense.\" Incense was used a lot throughout scripture, but specifically for the Lord's use. It is quite possible that this was something that pleased the Lord, especially when we read what happens next..."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham's family grew immensely! Abraham went from worrying about having ONE son to having SIX sons (seven if you count Ishmael). Notice in the list of the children here we have the mention of two (possibly up to four) noticeable names and lineages. We have the mention of the Midians and the Medans. All of these children have nations come from them, but the Midians will be recognized through scripture coming across the Israelites again. It will be Moses that goes down into the land of Midian for his own wife. Of course, the chosen lineage will be through Isaac, not Midian."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":11,"title":"Ishmael and Isaac Bury Abraham","entries":[{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham gave the most valuable possessions to his son Isaac. His other sons surely got something from Abraham, but Isaac was the one that God wanted to use for the future of His children. Furthermore, Abraham gave to his concubines a parting gift and sent them away specifically from Isaac. He didn't want Isaac to get mixed up in his own flaws."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham lived a long life, and he prospered through his faith. It took him a long time to come to terms with his trust in God, but he eventually continued to push through and prosper. That is how it is for many Christians, mind you. There are a lot of weak-faithed Christians who struggle with sin. I'm saying this in kindness, and not as a means of stabbing or forced conviction. If you have a struggle with sin today, the truth is you have a weak faith. And that's okay! It's okay because you HAVE a faith. Abraham had a weak faith too, and instead of giving up and allowing Satan to influence his mind thinking, \"God will never accept you or use you because of your sin...\" he allowed his faith to have the say-so and moved forward learning and growing. It took him a lot of years, mind you, to build his faith. It will likely take you A LOT of years to build your faith. You may never fully build your faith in this lifetime, but you should still be constantly building it either way. It's good that you STRUGGLE with sin, because it means that God is working on your faith. Be of good cheer that you are not alone in your struggle. Abraham was a great faithful man, but it took quite a lot of lessons and learning to get into the growth of faith."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham loved Ishmael even though Ishmael wasn't a part of the chosen lineage. Ishmael apparently felt and understood that love because he comes back for his father’s funeral. This both encourages my heart and hurts my heart. I know that Ishmael felt plenty of bitterness, and I know that the Muslim faith today expresses their bitterness towards Christians in many facets. They especially hate the Israelite Jews even still today, and there is a great disconnect from the two. It is interesting to me that Isaac and Ishmael are in this together. I truly do believe that the lineage of Ishmael is the one that leads to the Muslim religion for quite a few reasons, but it is extremely evident today by the current Hamas and Israel war that is taking place. It's taking place over the land in which the Israelites will eventually hold in the book of Joshua."},{"kind":"para","text":"You could also ask a Muslim where their lineage comes from and they will tell you Abraham. Obviously not through the lineage of Isaac, and these other lineages are pretty specific as well from Abraham's other sons. The only other son that receives a great blessing from the Lord is Ishmael, and it would make the most sense and historically line up the best if we view it in that light. At this moment, Ishmael (the Muslim lineage) and Isaac (the Jewish lineage) are meeting together one last time to bury their father. They will not be connected again except for war later..."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God chose Isaac for a reason, and that is what Scripture wants to point out. It's not because he was the best of the land; let’s get that clear right now. God could have chosen much mightier men (specifically Melchizedek a few chapters back who was already a king and a priest for the Lord), but He doesn't do that. He chooses Isaac, and at the time that he was chosen, it didn't make sense. But to the Israelites who were reading this story that Moses is penning down, it all added together."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":18,"title":"The Lineage of Ishmael","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here's a deeper analysis on the lineage and founding of Ishmael. Aside from the fact that these names are evidently Muslim-adjacent, we can go based on meanings as well. \"Nebajoth\" means \"heights.\" Heights perhaps reference his stature. He might have been a tall man. Or Ishmael wanted him to be a very powerful man (someone who was above everyone else). \"Kedar\" means “dark,\" and I have reason to believe that this lineage is the same one that pops up again in Psalms, Song of Solomon, 1 Chronicles, Isaiah, Jeremiah, and lastly Ezekiel. I don't know if I could ever really reference Kedar through any of those particular sources of scripture as anything less than \"dark.\" \"Adbeel\" means \"chastened of God.\" Perhaps when Ishmael had this son, he felt a little neglected by the Lord, or he felt that this son might have been a bit of a realization that the Lord conflicted him? Either way, it doesn't seem super pleasant in the life of this son, or he was a son that was very proper and meaningful in his journeys? \"Mibsam\" means \"sweet odour.\" It might seem like a massive jump from chastened of God to sweet odour, but maybe he was in higher spirits at this point. \"Mishma\" means \"a hearing.\" Hearing what? I don't know. Maybe he desired that Mishma would be someone that would give proper judgement? I haven't the slightest. \"Dumah\" means \"silence.\" This is an interesting one, because historically it's proven that he was the founder of the Ishmaelite tribe of Arabia. This further brings the understanding that the Muslim religion has come from Ishmael. Silence is, perhaps, something that Dumah was born with. Maybe he didn't make a sound when he came out. Maybe that was pleasing to Ishmael, and so he thought that this child would be mute, or perhaps bring peace to the land? \"Massa\" means \"burden.\" I don't know what Ishmael was feeling here, but it doesn't seem happy. \"Hadar\" means \"honour.\" \"Tema\" means “desert.\" \"Jetur\" means “enclosed.\" Jetur's descendants will go against Israel later on. \"Naphish\" means “refreshment.\" This is another Arabian tribe. \"Kedemah\" means “original.\" And he makes up the last of the Ishmael’s sons."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now we know that there will be twelve tribes that come from Isaac later on (and these tribes are so named from the offspring of the twelve sons of Jacob (later named Israel, but I'm getting ahead of myself)). But we might not know that there are \"twelve princes\" of Ishmael. Well, I mean until now. These children seem to become quite prosperous just as God had promised. Of course, as we’ve already made clear, they are not the lineage that Scripture focuses on, but they certainly play a role in our society today. It is that religion that has sparked quite a bit of wild battles in the East as well as trying to destroy and conquer land in the West. They fit the description that God had given to them even still today."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the angel of the LORD said unto her, I will multiply thy seed exceedingly, that it shall not be numbered for multitude. And the angel of the LORD said unto her, Behold, thou art with child, and shalt bear a son, and shalt call his name Ishmael; because the LORD hath heard thy affliction. And he will be a wild man; his hand will be against every man, and every man's hand against him; and he shall dwell in the presence of all his brethren. And she called the name of the LORD that spake unto her, Thou God seest me: for she said, Have I also here looked after him that seeth me? Wherefore the well was called Beerlahairoi; behold, it is between Kadesh and Bered.","ref":"Genesis 16:10-14"},{"kind":"para","text":"Interesting here (in Genesis 16) because it mentions \"Kadesh\" which wasn't a place during the time of Abraham, for Kadesh wasn't born yet. But it was certainly a place during the time of Moses, and Moses points out that the location of this well was found between these areas which are named later on. God also has made it clear that Ishmael will have twelve princes in Genesis 17:20. Either way, the picture here is to promote the fact that God's Word always comes to fruition. It might not always happen instantly, but you can be guaranteed that it WILL happen."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We may remember that Ishmael was a wild man. He probably led a wild life. I don't know much else about him other than he died at quite an old age. And in Genesis-like fashion, we get the information about the non-essential lineage first, before we move on to the focal lineage."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, my assumption here is that he was buried in his own land with some other of his family, not with the people of Abraham. I assume his mother Hagar met another man where she ended up and had more children, but those children are not mentioned, and God does not make a point to provide blessings or assurance to any of Hagar’s other children. Assyria is another pretty good insinuation that this is the lineage of the Muslim faith. Assyrians are surrounded almost entirely by Muslim-believing countries even still today, and a good portion of the Assyrians have accepted Islam as their faith of choice. Thankfully, there is a large group of Assyrians who identify as Christians, and so the fight of faith still wages on. It goes to show, though, that just because you might not have been in the chosen lineage, doesn't mean you cannot be grafted in. A topic, no doubt, for a later book of scripture."}]}]},{"start":19,"end":23,"title":"The Lineage of Isaac","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that Rebekah ran into the same problem that Sarah (his mother) did. Sarah couldn't conceive, and it wasn't because she was not a godly woman. God had a very specific plan for Abraham and Sarah, and He needed them to be a proper example. Isaac may have learned a thing or two from his parents, and he goes straight to the Lord in trust and faith, asking that God would grant him a child. God obliged!"}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here's the interesting part, and it will be something that charts the course of the rest of Israel's history (another image of things to come). God grants their prayer, but it wasn't easy on them. He does what they want, and doubles it! There are twins in the womb, and even in the womb, they are wrestling with each other. It seems to be bringing pain on Rebekah, and so she seeks comfort and understanding from the Lord. She asks, \"Why did you answer my prayer like this? Why do I have to be in such pain?\""}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps this is what could have happened if Abraham and Sarah were a little more patient with their waiting for a child. It mimics a lot of the same situation between Ishmael and Isaac, but these will be TWINS born at the same time. God knows that there will be two nations that come from them, and they will be an example of quite a few things. It represents the struggle between light and dark. It represents the struggle of good and evil. It represents the struggle between Spirit and flesh. It represents the struggle between free will and Godly ordinance. The struggle between truth and lies. It also represents the struggle between pride and humility. The struggle between lording and serving. Being a leader and being a follower. We will see that play out in numerous settings, but for Rebekah, that is what she needed to understand at this moment."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":28,"title":"A Split in Family Values","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Shocking! I mean, we knew that when the Lord said it, so it's kind of funny to me that it says, \"Behold\" as if it were a surprise."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Esau\" means \"hairy.\" You could imagine why they called him that. Perhaps it has something to do with how he looked? He is the older son, and it's something we will have to remember when we get further along in this book."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Two babies were coming out at once. I haven't been in many delivery rooms (just the one for the birth of my daughter), but I don't think the twins are supposed to come out together. I think it's usually one, and then there's some more pushing for the other. I could be wrong; I am not a doctor. But here we have a strong grip from this second child, \"Jacob.\" \"Jacob\" means \"heel holder.\" Why was this information important to us? Well, aside from the fact that Jacob was holding onto his brother's heel and so his name matches the description, there is a lot of interesting relation to what God had said before to Rebekah: The elder will serve the younger."},{"kind":"para","text":"God made a guarantee to Rebekah. It didn't matter what would happen; Esau would serve Jacob. We will understand that better as we go through these chapters, but it is something we should remember because Rebekah doesn't seem to get it. Jacob was (perhaps) trying to come out first but did not succeed, and so he is left with nothing more than to cling to his eldest brother for escape. Being first is usually not a good thing by spiritual standards."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"So the last shall be first, and the first last: for many be called, but few chosen.","ref":"Matthew 20:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"You want to be the most useful to the Lord? Be the least."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I actually laughed when I read this. Esau was a \"man's man.\" He knew how to hunt and feed his family. Jacob, on the other hand, was very plain and lived in tents. “Dwelling\" means \"to sit.\" It doesn't seem like Jacob thought he was good at much of anything, and perhaps he didn't receive many encouraging words to do very much from anyone. It was obvious to Isaac (and probably to Rebekah as well) that Esau was going to have a successful future. However, things take quite a change, and the next verse explains why:"}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There's a bit of a dissension between the family here. There are many households that are like this. I believe our household was one of them. My dad was not a man that was easy to please, but it seemed that everything my brother did was far better accepted by him than anything I could have done. The opposite (with my mother) was true. My mother seemed to favor me more than my brother (and this isn't some sort of jab at anyone or anything). I was a bit of a mama's boy, and I am not afraid to admit that in my later stages of life. I believe that is where a lot of the dissension comes from in our family. The truth is, my brother hated that (probably still hates it) and it grows bitterness in his heart to know that. It is likely the case here with Jacob and Esau."},{"kind":"para","text":"Esau wanted to be treated much like his brother with his mother, and Jacob was probably content with being the outcast and only accepted by his mother. He didn't need his father's approval. This causes such strain on the home and it hurts the unity of a family. We cannot favor one child over another. Esau loved his work in this world. It could be said that Jacob was open to more spiritual things than Esau, and he was gaining favor from the people around him because of his work. I believe that will play a vital role later on in their lives (as we will soon see). Jacob was a thinker. Esau was a doer. What a duo they could have made if they worked in unity together and played on their strengths..."}]}]},{"start":29,"end":34,"title":"Selling A Birthright","entries":[{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob didn't do much, remember. He is seen here cooking, and perhaps he was a good cook. Esau came back from field work and was extremely hungry after a hard day’s work. Jacob had food that was nearly prepared, and Esau really wanted to eat. Esau's name is \"Edom\" here, but it doesn't seem to change to that. \"Edom\" means \"red.\" Esau was red when he came out of the womb, and now he is requesting red pottage. Edom will be Esau's descendants and they will be the ones to start the land of Edom, which is southeast of Palestine. We will see this land brought up again in Exodus, Numbers, Joshua, Judges, 1&2 Samuel, and others. It all stems from this very moment, which seems odd at first (why all of a sudden would he receive this name?), but it's because of Jacob’s next disgraceful act that this is important;"}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Esau's birthright was to receive everything that the first child should get (when the father dies). Perhaps Jacob did it to get the approval of his father. Perhaps he did it because he was just being a jerk and wanted to make Esau miserable. Maybe it's a mixture of both. Either way, what Jacob is asking for is pretty despicable and cruel at this moment in time."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Esau said, \"What good is a birthright if I'm dead anyway?\" and so he swore to his brother that he would give him his birthright (which was far more valuable than Esau understood at that time). However, God had already made it clear that the older will serve the younger. Rebekah must not have made mention of this to their children, though."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Esau didn't care about his birthright anymore. He thought of it as very little because he knew that it belonged to his brother now. But he was giving up his position in his household. The firstborn was to be the head of the household when his father passed. His right was to be the leader and provider. His right was to take what was left to him and utilize it for increased growth and provision for himself and his family to follow. Esau (as a young man) didn't think much of it because his father was in good shape, and he had no family to supply for. Later on, he will understand how detrimental that decision was for him."},{"kind":"para","text":"Esau acted as if he didn't care about his calling later in life, and it's a great picture of many of us today. We receive a new birth when we accept the Lord, and it gives us a new \"birthright\" so-to-speak. He says, \"You have now been born again, and this means you will have the calling of ministering, preaching, loving, and teaching.\" Yet there are very few in the church-house today who accept their birthright. In fact, I believe there is a great majority who do as Esau does and just sells it off as quickly as possible because they don't believe it's all that important. As we read through these chapters to follow, we will see how important the birthright is, and we should be wary of selling it off when God has given us a specific calling for His work."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":34}]},
    "26": {"chapter":26,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Our last chapter spoke about the other children of Abraham (it wasn't just Isaac) and the meeting of Isaac and Ishmael for the burial of their father. We get a small glimpse at the lineage of Ishmael and what happens to him as well as the continued lineage of Isaac (which is the focal of scripture here). The problem is, there is a split between values in this family, and it has to do with the twins that were born to Isaac and Rebekah. Esau (the eldest) eventually sells his birthright for some food (he was obviously starving and his birthright seemed unimportant to him at his age)."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we take a small intercession from these kids and go into a chapter more geared towards Isaac specifically. This is really the only time we get to see Isaac's character. While this seems to be mainly focused on digging wells, there is a lot of spiritual application we can pull from this event in history, and I hope that it's an encouragement and blessing as we read."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"Take the Promise","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You will see throughout Scripture that famines were quite common in these days. Every time there is a famine, you can bet any money there is a reason. God uses the famines a lot to shift culture back to Him, and I have no doubt we will see that happen here (it happened with Abraham, and it will happen with Joseph later in this book). Isaac goes to seek out Abimelech, possibly to see if there was any means of provision for his family during this famine. Isaac is a patient man, who came from an impatient father, and we will see Isaac's patience pushed in this chapter."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham, by no means, was a perfect man (we saw much of his imperfections as we've been studying through his life), but he was a faithful man. He always returned to the Lord, and he always accepted correction from his errors. God corrects course for Isaac and tells him NOT to go to Egypt. Isaac was where he needed to be. The land was given to him and his lineage, and that was where he needed to stay. Someone said that Egypt is a symbol of the world in the Old Testament, and I think that's a pretty apt description of it. They had much wealth in the land, and they were ripe with all sorts of worldly things. But our wealth doesn't come from the world; it comes from the Lord. Isaac must learn this."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":16,"title":"The Jealousy of the Philistines","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems the sins of the father are not far past in the lineage. This is the exact same thing that Abraham did (twice), and he was corrected for it. God had to rescue him out of both such instances, and it's happening all over again with Isaac now."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Isaac listened and didn't go to Egypt, but stayed in Gerar. In this land, he felt (seemingly) uncomfortable with the people around him and assumed that these people were going to try and beat him up to steal his wife. He lied about her and asked her to lie about their marriage, and Abimelech (who had already gone through this same circumstance once before with Abraham) saw them \"sporting.\" \"Sporting\" means \"to play.\" It was probably in a somewhat flirtatious way, and it was evident to Abimelech that they were more than just brother and sister. He’d already seen this before somewhere."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abimelech was a godly man (and my assumption is that he still is at this point). He wanted to do what was right by the Lord, and it seems that the Lord keeps throwing this same curveball back at him. At this moment, he likely thought to himself, \"Lord, what is wrong with these people coming in and claiming their wives as their sisters? Why can't we just have God-fearing citizens come in and be normal for once?\" Maybe this was God's way of keeping Abimelech's faith in check. Remember, Abimelech comes from the Philistines, and they become a rather large enemy of the children of God. Abimelech had a faith history, though, and they will have to deny Abimelech's history if they are going to turn that easily."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, in the middle of a famine, Isaac grows crops without an issue. He is given immunity in the community because the king has bid him untouchable (due to his life nevertheless), and he is flourishing in wealth in a land that he is currently not supposed to be in. The Philistines see this and they grow bitter towards Isaac. The king was friends with Isaac and his family, but the rest of the people could not stand him."},{"kind":"para","text":"A good question is, where are their children? At this moment, I really don't know. They must have been left back in the land with a housekeeper or maiden or concubine or something of the sort. They have to be near adulthood at this point. God was still with Isaac and his family, even though Isaac was not very consistent. I believe it's because Abimelech found out the truth and let the truth be known abroad about this man."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Philistines didn't seem to like Isaac all that much. Abraham dug plenty of wells for the Philistines, and now they belong to Isaac, but because the Philistines don't much care for Isaac, they fill all the wells so that he can no longer prosper. The wells were in their land, yes, but they were not wells for them to bury. We are finally starting to see the Philistines culture shift here, and I believe it's because of Isaac that the Philistines start to grow hateful toward Israel. They become jealous of the prosperity that God grants to them. Instead of turning to the Lord and seeking out that same provision, they reject the Lord and reject His chosen people. It is how the world works throughout all of history."},{"kind":"para","text":"It is what happens when Christ comes. It's what happens with the disciples in their ministries (they were all killed for it because of the bitterness and jealousy of the people). It's what happens to countless of the reformers. It's what happens to many missionaries in the world today. It's what happens here in America as people throw things, curse, and yell at the preachers for sharing the gospel. They hate to see others getting something good when they are not receiving it themselves. Even Christians fall into this trap, and it's a very dangerous pit to stay in."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":22,"title":"Water Providence","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So Isaac (instead of returning bitterness with bitterness) goes back out and digs the wells back up and gives the same testimony as his father. It seems, however, that the wells were run dry. We get this idea because they were in a famine, and because of the next verse:"}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They found one well that was giving water during a famine! They had an important source of provision for the people in the middle of the desert. So now the Philistines have two reasons to be bitter towards Isaac. He was growing in produce during a famine, and now he found water during a famine."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Esek\" means \"contention.\" There was certainly a lot of tension building up, and contention for this well. There are people willing to go to great lengths to find this water. There's a lot of deep nuance we can get into from this picture. I believe the best symbol we can take from this is the fact that God's Word is like a springing forth of water!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"That he might sanctify and cleanse it with the washing of water by the word,\" Ephesians 5:26 \"But whosoever drinketh of the water that I shall give him shall never thirst; but the water that I shall give him shall be in him a well of water springing up into everlasting life.\" John 4:14 \"He that believeth on me, as the scripture hath said, out of his belly shall flow rivers of living water.\" John 7:38 \"And he said unto me, It is done. I am Alpha and Omega, the beginning and the end. I will give unto him that is athirst of the fountain of the water of life freely.","ref":"Revelation 21:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"God's Word is certainly deep, and it requires that a man be found faithful to it in digging in. There are plenty of people who dig and find the water source, and it provides nourishment for our souls. Sometimes, when we feel lost without the Lord, and we feel as if He is not present with us, it requires us to dig wells until we find that water, like Isaac and his men are doing here."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Sitnah\" means \"strife.\" So the first well comes, and it is taken pretty quickly, and Isaac seems to just let it go. They find a second well, and (once again) it's taken from him. This is an incredible story here, mind you, because there is no water anywhere else, and yet Isaac is finding water left and right."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now he finds a third well, and they let that one go. \"Rehoboth\" means \"wide places.\" I quite like this name, actually. The other wells were taken, and there was no room for anyone else to draw from them. But because this one was wide open, this well was free for everyone to draw from. Notice that Isaac was peaceful and patient every time a well was taken from him. He had the right and the power to destroy those that stole from him, but he didn't do that. He was a great example in this picture of continuously moving along. He provided water for so many people during this famine and didn't complain about them abusing his work to get it. Perhaps his intent was to provide for those that needed it, but he also knew that there would be others in the future who would need to draw from it, and that is really what he is setting up these wells for. Again, we can turn this and say the same thing about God's Word. We are digging into it to draw from the well of water that God's Word has, and then we are leaving the well and naming it for others to draw from also. The water of God's Word is not just for us to hoard. It's for us to pour out to others."}]}]},{"start":23,"end":25,"title":"Isaac Meets With God","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a big deal for Isaac to experience. So far (as far as I know, and we are told), God has only revealed Himself to Abraham, and Abraham has just passed down stories to his son. Isaac saw God do some amazing things for his family (and with these wells just now, he was likely astounded by the wealth that God was providing for him), but he had never personally visited with the Lord. So here, God comes before Isaac in some way and speaks to him."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So Isaac makes a testimony to the fact that God visits him here and digs yet ANOTHER well! So alters were Abraham's thing, and wells seem to be Isaac’s thing. The alters were a good symbol of God's working, but the wells are a good symbol of God's provision."}]}]},{"start":26,"end":33,"title":"A Meeting With Abimelech","entries":[{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't think Abimelech ever hated Isaac. The Philistines (the people that he was in charge of) perhaps did, but Abimelech had nothing but kindness toward Isaac. In fact, I believe Abimelech sent Isaac out of the land to protect him from all of the Philistines that were growing envious of him. He wanted to make sure that he was taken care of, and he wouldn't be very well taken care of if the whole city was in a revolt against him. Abimelech's hands would be tied at that point. I believe this is why Pilate allows the murder of Jesus. He cannot stop the whole crowd from doing what they want, and if he tried, they would start a revolt, causing anarchy and chaos throughout the land."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abimelech is no fool. He can tell when God is moving in someone’s life. He saw it in Abraham, and now he sees it in Isaac, and instead of becoming bitter and envious towards them, he wants to join hands with them. He wants to be able to be blessed as well. If only the rest of the Philistines would have taken this example to themselves..."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Isaac had made peace with Abimelech, and Abimelech (in good conscience) did what was right by the Lord and by Isaac’s family. They seemed to have a bit of a party in the middle of the desert, and in the morning they left each other with \"peace.\" There was no ill-will between these two men, and there never should be any ill-will between the Philistines and the Israelites. Obviously, that will not be the case."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Isaac dug a well in this place because the Lord visited with him. \"Shebah\" means \"an oath.\" It was where the oath was made between Isaac and Abimelech. They had sworn to prosper together and protect each other. The Lord was their mutual interest."}]}]},{"start":34,"end":35,"title":"Esau's Wives","entries":[{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Esau makes a grave mistake here, and it's evident that he is not following after the Lord in his life. It seems like a common pattern in Scripture (at least as we've been reading) that hunters are more prone to accepting the land as their inheritance rather than searching out the Lord. Esau loved the world and the things that were in the world."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Love not the world, neither the things that are in the world. If any man love the world, the love of the Father is not in him. For all that is in the world, the lust of the flesh, and the lust of the eyes, and the pride of life, is not of the Father, but is of the world. And the world passeth away, and the lust thereof: but he that doeth the will of God abideth for ever.","ref":"1 John 2:15-17"},{"kind":"para","text":"Isaac and Rebekah took notice of this, and they were not happy about it either. It says these wives of Esau were \"a grief of mind\" to his parents. If you don't remember, \"Heth\" was the second son of Canaan, and started the land of the Hittites. They were not in line with the seed of God. They were very much rebellious of that line (in fact), and so it was not a very good thing for Esau to marry these women in this line. Perhaps he did it while his parents were away digging wells so that they wouldn’t be able to stop him. What a scary and sad surprise to come back to, and sadly it only gets worse…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":33},{"start":34,"end":35}]},
    "27": {"chapter":27,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see the faith and guidance of Isaac as he dwells in the land of the Philistines for a time. It is believed that this is where the Philistines begin to resent the lineage of Abraham, and why they will eventually start to hate the Israelites in the future. There's a good assumption that this chapter (in which we are about to read) may play a prominent role in that as well."},{"kind":"para","text":"We see in this chapter the blessings in which Isaac wants to give to his son. We see the stealing of the blessing, the subtlety of sin, and the ignorance of a mother trying to protect her child."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"Esau's Task","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So it seems we are coming close to the end of the story of Isaac now. He is up there in age, and this is the time for him to give the blessings to each of his children. Before we read this blessing, however, we must remember what the Lord has told to Rebekah so many years prior:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the LORD said unto her, Two nations are in thy womb, And two manner of people shall be separated from thy bowels; and the one people shall be stronger than the other people; and the elder shall serve the younger.","ref":"Genesis 25:23"},{"kind":"para","text":"I think it's interesting, too, to see the desire of Isaac is the worldly man, and the desire of Rebekah is the Godly one. But that can be a topic for a different time. Now, at the time when God told Rebekah this, she probably didn't understand what that meant. She might have pondered it, \"the elder will serve the younger.\" but she might not have ever really understood the purpose behind it. When we come to this event in their lives, here, it will make a lot more sense… Back in this time, there was not much one can do to receive an inheritance except by the word of the one giving the inheritance. The words of Isaac are absolute here, so if he says one thing wrong it cannot be made different."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He doesn't call Jacob at all, and only interacts with Esau in his final moments. He likes Esau's food and work ethic, not Jacob’s. He is prominent in giving Esau the greater blessing. He might have, however, desired to give Jacob a blessing of some sort, but nothing like what he was going to give to Esau. Isaac wanted his final meal to be made by his favorite son."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Remember, Rebekah knows that the oldest will serve the youngest, and perhaps that is her motive for this next part. I don't know if Isaac knows this part of God's promise or not, but I will assume he does. Isaac doesn't call the whole family in for a reason. He only calls Esau, and he wants Esau alone to do this task. Yet Rebekah hears it, and so she devises a new strategy."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":17,"title":"Rebekah Encourages Jacob","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Esau was a woodsman. He knew how to hunt and kill. He knew what to look for. He was going to get his own meat by his own hands to give to his father as a blessing to him. Rebekah, however, tasks Jacob with getting food straight from their own flock. They had the means for it, and she knew Jacob wouldn't be quick enough if he went out to hunt his own food (that wasn't how Jacob lived), so she makes a very hasty choice to Jacob so that he can receive the blessing. It's a very sad situation if you think about it. Rebekah (nor Isaac for that matter) should be playing favorites with her kids. She, too, is about to hurt and ruin the life of one of her sons because she loves one more than the other. However, Esau was doing some pretty heinous things against his family, and that is reason enough to hold a conversation with Isaac about the matter. Obviously, there will be no conversation, and all of this will just be action."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob says, \"My father isn't stupid. He will find out that I'm trying to trick him, and then what will I do? Rather than getting a blessing, I'll be getting a beating, and if Esau finds out, it won't be fun.” Another interesting characteristic of Esau is that he's an \"hairy man,\" whereas Jacob is a \"smooth\" man. Perhaps it’s from all the sun he was getting. Either way, Jacob is not just going to \"seem\" like a deceiver to his father; he will BE the deceiver to his father."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is absurd. Why would she put her son into this position? Now, I will point out that these two sons were grown men at this point. It was not as if they didn't understand what was going on and what was taking place. Jacob understood that what he was about to do was steal his brothers’ blessing. He had already done this though, beforehand."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Jacob said, Sell me this day thy birthright. And Esau said, Behold, I am at the point to die: and what profit shall this birthright do to me? And Jacob said, Swear to me this day; and he sware unto him: and he sold his birthright unto Jacob. Then Jacob gave Esau bread and pottage of lentiles; and he did eat and drink, and rose up, and went his way: thus Esau despised his birthright.","ref":"Genesis 25:31-34"}]}]},{"start":18,"end":29,"title":"A Stolen Blessing","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that Jacob has no issue whatsoever with lying here to receive his brothers’ blessing. His heart doesn't seem to be racing. He doesn't seem to think that he's going to get caught. He believes that this is a good idea. He is playing the part of the favorite son to his father when he knows that he can never earn that spot. It is the same with many Christians in the church house today. They play the part of the favorite, but they are just wearing a disguise. I call it the Judas effect. Perhaps I should call it the \"Jacob Effect?” Maybe just the \"Satan Effect?” This is a very Satan thing to do as we will see in a moment."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You can tell that Isaac wasn't very sure about Jacob here. Some things were just not adding up. He might not have been able to physically see, but his conscience could see quite well. He felt in his body that things weren't aligned properly. Two things should have given it away at this point. One is that Esau came back too quickly. There would be no way that he would have found a sheep that fast, killed it, cleaned it, and cooked it for his father in the short time that his father told him to go get him food."},{"kind":"para","text":"The second is found in verse 20, \"Because the Lord thy God brought it to me.” I don't think Esau was a believing man. I am under the impression that Esau is a very worldly man, and that is displayed throughout every action in his life. He had no interest in the God of his father. Jacob, however, seems to. Jacob was an observer of the people around him. I believe that is why Jacob has such an easy time deceiving his father here. I don't think the words \"the Lord thy God brought it to me...\" would ever come out of the mouth of Esau, but Isaac still falls for it. And what really seals the deal is the next verse:"}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here's the blessing that Jacob (NOT Esau) just received. He will have the fatness of the Earth, and the dew of heaven, so he has plenty of corn and wine. In other words, he will be taken care of in his provisions so that he doesn't go hungry. He will have people serving him, nations bowing to him, and he will have leadership authority over his brother (specifically he says, \"...let thy mother's sons bow down to thee…\" I believe he had Jacob in mind for this thought). But the way it's said doesn't really add up. \"Sons,\" and \"Brethren\" would insinuate more than one relative to rule over. However, there were only two kids that Isaac had. Why would he use this terminology?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Well, I believe it's the same blessing that Isaac received from his own father Abraham before his death. He was passing on the same blessing. Now, the Lord had already promised this blessing to Jacob anyway. It is (as it seems) how the Lord works. The oldest always seems to be the servant to the younger (that is a very foreign concept to us today, especially because we want to be served, rather than serve). God doesn't approve, however, the way in which Jacob is going about it..."}]}]},{"start":30,"end":40,"title":"Esau's Blessing","entries":[{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob got out of there just in time. He and his brother barely missed each other."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Esau had just finished working very hard for his father. Jacob didn't have to do much of anything except kill one of his own sheep, and his mom did the rest. Esau had plans and was looking forward to this moment, and it was about to crush him… and Isaac..."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Isaac \"trembled very exceedingly…\" He messed up, and he realized it at this moment. There was nothing that Esau could do about it either, even if he wanted to go and attack Jacob. Jacob was protected by the blessing (and by the Lord, though I believe the Lord would have let Jacob have a little pain for his deception at this moment). Isaac knew that things weren't adding up, and now he was absolutely certain that he had been tricked and it was not a very good feeling..."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Esau is not happy about this and cries uncontrollably (loudly even, and perhaps dramatically). He understands that there is nothing that can be done."}]},{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is why I liken Jacob's actions to Satan. What does scripture say about Satan?"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now the serpent was more subtil than any beast of the field which the LORD God had made. And he said unto the woman, Yea, hath God said, Ye shall not eat of every tree of the garden?\" Genesis 3:1 \"And consulted that they might take Jesus by subtilty, and kill him.\" Matthew 26:4 \"But I fear, lest by any means, as the serpent beguiled Eve through his subtilty, so your minds should be corrupted from the simplicity that is in Christ.\" 2 Corinthians 11:3 \"And said, O full of all subtilty and all mischief, thou child of the devil, thou enemy of all righteousness, wilt thou not cease to pervert the right ways of the Lord?","ref":"Acts 13:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"I think we get the picture. Satan is subtle, and the way he does his business is through his subtlety. He likes to do it out in the open, but not make it obvious enough to know it's him. He's pretty smart for how powerless he really is. It is Jacob in this picture here. Jacob is pretty smart for how powerless he really is. It's Judas in the time of Christ, smart, but powerless."}]},{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"All Jacob has done is held onto the heels of Esau and held him down from being the proper rightful son of Isaac. Esau wants something from Isaac. He wants at least one small blessing. He wants to be blessed by his father. As do we all."}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Isaac says, \"I've literally given every right and every blessing I have to your brother. What can I give you that I haven't given him?” This makes us question what he would have given to Jacob had it been the other way around. Would Jacob have gotten a blessing at all from his father? Would it just show (further) that his father doesn’t care about him?"}]},{"start":38,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"To Esau, it is almost as if he has been cut off from his own family. He has lost connection to his own people. Perhaps that is how it feels to him, and he believes that there is no mending something like this. He is now a lost son without the blessing of his father."}]},{"start":40,"end":40,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Isaac tries to think of any sort of blessing to give to his favorite son, but there was nothing that he could really do other than to cave to God's will on the matter. He knew that Esau would serve Jacob, and so that is the \"blessing\" that he will have to give to Esau here. He lets it be known that he will be taken care of in provision (God is good at that), but he will be a servant to Jacob, not a ruler over him. Esau will become powerful, and Jacob will become burdensome, but there will be a time when Esau will lift that burden from Jacob. Jacob doesn't realize it at the moment, but he won't be able to sit comfortably with the decision he had just made..."}]}]},{"start":41,"end":45,"title":"Jacob Told to Flee","entries":[{"start":41,"end":41,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Esau wants to wait for his father to die before he kills Jacob. Again, the Lord has already guaranteed protection for Jacob, and now Isaac has given him the blessing, so Esau can't really do anything at this moment (or even after Isaac dies). It wouldn't go well for Esau then."}]},{"start":45,"end":45,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Rebekah is under the impression that Jacob will only be gone for a few days while this all passes over. Eventually, Esau will forgive him, and he won't have any more bitterness towards his brother. Little does she know that it will be much longer than a few days that Jacob will be gone. She knows (as well as any) that Jacob will be taken care of. She knows that Esau can't do anything to Jacob. Yet she still takes matters into her own hands and pushes her son away for the purpose of “safety.\" The real sad part is, she claims to love this son and yet leads him in all of these weird directions that go against the Lord's providence and foretelling. Isaac, of course, was no better, but she was not very smart in the way she was handling things. Even sadder is the fact that when Jacob returns, his mother will have already passed away, and he will not see her again after this moment."}]}]},{"start":46,"end":46,"title":"Rebekah Sends Jacob Away","entries":[{"start":46,"end":46,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Rebekah is trying to make an excuse for sending Jacob away (as if she needs to give one for this man-child). She wasn't wrong, though. Esau had already married the heathen women, and so it wouldn't be too long before Jacob didn't have a choice but to marry these people either. So it would be best to go to their hometown and marry within their chosen lineage (for that is the lineage of the Lord) so as not to have two children wandering away from God's Will."},{"kind":"para","text":"I would like to take a moment here to address the term in Romans which says;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"As it is written, Jacob have I loved, but Esau have I hated.","ref":"Romans 9:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"We have a lot of calloused Christians and Calvinists alike who have twisted this verse out of context. Now that we know the blessings of both of the boys, we are going to solidify the truth of what is being said. Esau wasn't hated because God chose to hate him specifically for this cause. Rather, Esau was a man of the world, and God knew that Jacob would be a man of the Lord. It would require some work coming here in the future, but the blessing was on Jacob far before his birth because God already knew where Jacob would be, and what Esau would desire. He \"hated\" Esau because He knew Esau's heart for the world. \"Hated\" means \"to detest\" or to \"love less.” It was not that he didn't \"love\" Esau, but he couldn't love Esau as much as Jacob, because Esau rejected the Lord's Will from the beginning."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":40},{"start":41,"end":45},{"start":46,"end":46}]},
    "28": {"chapter":28,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we got a very sad and terrible picture of a broken family through Jacob's subtle stealing of Esau's blessing. Jacob didn't need to do this, but he chooses to do this anyway through the direction of his mother. Jacob could have just waited for the blessing to be granted but he decided to take things into his own hands. Now we have a broken family, and Jacob is tasked with leaving because all it does it cause his brother harm being in his presence. Esau desires to kill Jacob, and his family doesn't want to have to keep watching them try and avoid each other, so they have to send Jacob away."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we get the send off of Jacob, and what eventually happens to him as he travels to this next place in his life, and starts to come to grips with the reality of God in his life."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"Jacob Sent Away","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think there is a lot of place in scripture to suggest that God is not desirous of His children to marry ungodly people. The Canaanites were NOT a godly sort. Rebekah obviously knew this, and so does Isaac. And so they take recognition that it would not only be wise for Jacob to leave and find a good wife among his own people, but also so that Esau doesn't kill him because of his cruel and sly thievery. Jacob was kind of a jerk, whether it was his mom that told him to do it or not, he was extremely mean and cruel to his older brother."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Isaac knows that the land in which Jacob is supposed to be is to be the land they are currently in. When Jacob leaves this land, Isaac must believe and assume that God will bring him back in some way. For Isaac, it was easier for him to stay in the land because Abraham sent and retrieved a wife for him. In this instance, Jacob must leave. Perhaps they were learning from Esau a few things about what Jacob should do (I mean aside from the fact that Esau wanted to kill his brother). Esau married a couple of Canaanite women, and that was already hard enough. Jacob wouldn't fall for that same trap."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Isaac had to come to terms with the fact that Jacob is the recipient of the blessing. He should have come to terms with the fact about it a long time ago. It was the Lord who told him it would be this way. Honestly, Jacob didn't need to steal Esau's blessing; it was already Jacob’s to begin with, and it was guaranteed to be his no matter what course of action Isaac would take."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob's journey to this land will not be easy. We can kind of miss the part while reading where scripture gives the true nature of the people and their fears if we are not careful. I've learned through reading scripture (straight through) that there's a lot of connection that we can miss when we are reading only one chapter a day. Remember that Esau was the hunter. He was the outdoorsman. He actually enjoyed being outdoors and exploring, hunting, and venturing. Jacob was the complete opposite, and so when he is on his journey, this is truly the first time he has been out on his own without the help of his family or brother. He has to help himself survive here."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":9,"title":"Esau Marries an Ishmaelite","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Ishmaelites were not part of the lineage to Israel. The Israelites are not thought of by anyone on Earth as of yet. Abraham knows that there will be a multitude of children that follow him. Isaac knows it too (that's why he gave the blessing to Jacob). Jacob now knows it (he will be the actual forefront of this nation). But Esau does not understand it at all. So he may have looked at his two Canaanite wives and thought, \"Well obviously they don't please my family, so I will look for wives that WILL please them!” Then he turns around and marries outside of God's chosen lineage again. He thought that because this line comes from Abraham, it must be a decent enough line for his father to accept... However, that couldn't be further from the truth. There is only one line that God has accepted, and there's only one reason that He has accepted it, and as we go along scripture we will see why He has cared so much for this line (and we will also be alongside the Lord in His sorrow for these people as they continually reject Him)."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":17,"title":"A Wild Dream","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, the journey would not be instantaneous. It is going to take Jacob some time to get to where he is going, and it is not as if he has a map to help him get there. This is all new territory for him. It seems he wants to stay the course, though, and perhaps his father told him what things to look for on his journey there."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Aw, nice pillow stones. In those days, it was not very rare for them to be carrying some hide or wool to lay on or help them. Jacob must have forgotten that before he left. The place in which he lands (Haran) will eventually be called “Bethel,\" which is interpreted \"The House of the Lord.” It was about forty miles from home at this point."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob had to be pretty perplexed at this dream at the moment. Not only is he seeing angels ascending and descending up and down a ladder, he is also seeing this light of the Lord appearing above the ladder speaking to him. God tells him that this land will be his and his children to follow, and that will certainly be the case, and still is the case today."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was the same promise given to Abraham, and it was what Abraham gave to Isaac, and then Isaac gave to Jacob. My assumption for this vision is that Jacob didn't really have much faith in the promise. He probably knew what his father said, but didn't really understand that it was literal. Jacob likely believed he would have children and it would kind of end there. But that won't be the whole story. I want to hang on this ladder for a moment. I've read a lot of theories on this ladder and part of scripture that feels a little too over my head. Perhaps there are some truths to it, but I like to try and simplify the things we are reading in scripture to make it a little easier for us to swallow and apply."},{"kind":"para","text":"While this ladder can be a symbol of something far greater for the future of Israel, it's something that can be applied to us today. We get this picture, here, in the loneliest part of Jacob's life of God being right along with him, without him even being aware of it. He may have thought of God like most of the world thinks of Him. He's just a distant being who doesn't really concern himself with the matters of men. But this vision proves that He is close, and He has given a permanent access to Heaven to go back and forth for His work. Charles Spurgeon says, \"The God of Bethel is a God who does concern himself with the things of earth, not a God who shuts himself up in heaven, but God who hath a ladder fixed between heaven and earth.” Jesus is the only access to Heaven, and we know this to be true when we read through the gospel message. This ladder certainly can depict the Christ providing access, and perhaps that is what God is identifying here."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he saith unto him, Verily, verily, I say unto you, Hereafter ye shall see heaven open, and the angels of God ascending and descending upon the Son of man.","ref":"John 1:51"}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Notice that Jacob hasn't said a single thing in this entire vision. Jacob is getting assurance from the Lord, and Jacob is dumbfounded. God makes a promise to be with him no matter where he goes, and promises to bring him back in the end."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I love this verse. He was speechless, and now he's confident after waking up. He saw the Lord, and he had no idea that he wasn't alone. Perhaps he felt alone, but now he feels God's presence. This is a great representation of a lost man. We perhaps feel alone before we come to know the Lord. We are trekking through this life believing we have an overall goal to reach, and a land to get to, just to be stopped dead in our tracks to come face to face with the Lord's salvation. Now we have a new pep in our step. Suddenly we wake up and realize, \"God is with me! What a beautiful day today!” Jacob was feeling a little different after seeing what he just saw, and it wasn't just a random assortment of a dream. Interestingly enough, he will eventually have a son who dreams great dreams like this, and everyone else will think he's crazy. Jacob, on the other hand, will be able to relate to him now."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know of many people who would look at the house of God as “dreadful,\" but Jacob didn't know what to do with this information. Whether he liked it or not, the Lord was there, and the Lord was going to fulfill the promise kept. \"Dreadful\" means \"to stand in awe of,“ \" to cause astonishment,\" and \"to inspire reverence or godly fear.” Perhaps this will be my new saying when I walk into the church house today. I will say, \"How dreadful is this place!” If the people get the reference, then it's my place to be; if they don't... well, then perhaps I continue to look... (I'm kidding, of course.)"}]}]},{"start":18,"end":22,"title":"Vowing to the Lord","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He is leaving a testimony like Abraham did. If someone travels by these stones, they will know something special happened here. If Jacob sees these stones again, he will remember what the Lord did there. How could he forget though? Well, it will be roughly fourteen years before Jacob comes back this way, but that is me getting ahead of myself."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It changed from Luz, which means \"almond tree.” Perhaps there was an almond tree there? Then it changed to Haran, which means \"Mountaineer.\" (This is what it was called when Abraham originally left home on a task for the Lord). Now it is called “Bethel,\" which means \"The house of God.” It was given this name at this moment and sealed by the pouring of oil. The pouring of oil has significance as well, and it will be a symbol of consecration and anointing throughout the rest of Scripture. The anointing of oil is not something we do very much anymore (though I think it has its place still as a symbol of covering in the church today), but it will be a symbol of God's providence and protection on whatever we choose to anoint it to. They still use it on sheep today after the sheep have been shaved because flies come and are attracted to sheep skin. The flies will drive the sheep so mad that if it's not taken care of, the sheep can start ramming its head into walls and eventually killing itself because the flies are bugging it too much. I think there's plenty of symbolism there that we can look into at a different time."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob has transferred his life over to the Lord at this moment, but before he is fully committed, he wants to make sure it was truly the Lord that was giving him this vision. You can never be too careful these days when the Lord calls you to something. We often think, “Well, what if it's just Satan calling me to this task or that task?” I will tell you today, Satan will never call you to a Godly task. He will only call you away from serving the Lord in where God has called you. If you are afraid to move forward for the Lord (because you are afraid Satan is the enticer), think to yourself, \"Does this task please the Lord?” If it doesn't, then it's likely Satan calling you to the task. If it does, then you should have no doubt that the Lord has called you to it. Jacob promises to serve the Lord from this day forward if the Lord is truly going to be with him. He wants to sell out for God, and he wants it to be blatantly clear that God is certainly with him. We will see that his faith (here) will certainly be tested in how serious he is."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":22}]},
    "29": {"chapter":29,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we saw the journey for Jacob commence. We got a picture of a ladder leading to Heaven, and we spoke about God revealing Himself to Jacob, and Jacob not fully understanding it. He didn't grasp the picture he was seeing and so he just chalks it up to God being in that place, but God promised to be WITH him."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see Jacob officially arrive at his destination, and makes some very odd and weird requests and commands right off the batt. It's a strange chapter to be sure, but it's also one that shows that God will always repay deception and will always provide the proper judgement."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":8,"title":"Jacob Arrives","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are several themes highlighted throughout Scripture. One of them is a theme of light, which seems to have a direct reference to the Lord every time. Another one (and it's become quite obvious to me, especially through Genesis) is a theme of water. Revelation talks about this theme being the barriers of safety and boundaries of supplication. When the water supply of the enemy is taken, the enemy is defeated. Interestingly enough, Jesus is our living water."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He that believeth on me, as the scripture hath said, out of his belly shall flow rivers of living water.\" John 7:38 \"For I will pour water upon him that is thirsty, and floods upon the dry ground: I will pour my spirit upon thy seed, and my blessing upon thine offspring:\" Isaiah 44:3 \"For my people have committed two evils; they have forsaken me the fountain of living waters, and hewed them out cisterns, broken cisterns, that can hold no water.","ref":"Jeremiah 2:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"In this picture here, we see the people of the east tending to their sheep with this well. They made a great stone to cover the well when it's not in use (perhaps to keep people, animals, or debris of some sort from falling into it). They come out (probably) frequently to come feed their sheep, and it seems the sheep know to gather around that well. They are anticipating their feeding. We could, of course, liken this image to the grave of Christ and the water of Christ, and I believe it best fits there, but the real important note to make is that wells are life-giving, and they are always linked to God's Word. Jacob is seeing this and not linking this to anything of real value at the moment. He's out, simply, to look for his relative and then find a wife."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob (though not a traveling type) managed to get himself to the right place. Maybe it was more of God's providential leading than it was of his own ability to navigate, but even still, he got to the right place with all odds against him."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know why Jacob thought it was good for him to tell these men what to do here. I don't think Jacob really understands what he's doing either. Maybe he's feeling cocky from the blessings of his father still? Maybe he is feeling confident because of how well he got to his destination? Maybe he's just trying to be a help because he knows they need it? I doubt it though, seeing as these men have been going along fine without him being there. Either way, he is bossing these men around and telling them how to do their job as if he can do it better."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They are waiting for all of the sheep to be together before drinking time. There's a lot I can say about this message here. This set of verses would make for a great sermon. There are churches today who are feeding a small portion of the flock and then rolling the stone over so that no one else gets the water they need. Those churches are usually listening to how another church is doing it. Jacob is telling them how to do it as if he knows better, yet they have been doing it this way nearly their whole lives. Why do they do it this way? Well, it’s so all the sheep get their feeding."},{"kind":"para","text":"It is a wise man's job to feed ALL the sheep, not just part of them. Jacob wasn't thinking, perhaps, and so he is told that it would be smarter to wait for all the sheep to come and then feed them all at once instead of opening and closing the well over and over again for each sheep. I assume the stone that was placed on top of the well was probably very heavy and it would take quite a few men to move it so that they could drink. Perhaps that's another reason for the stone... they didn't want others stealing their water."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":12,"title":"Jacob Is Awkward","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The women in this day were shepherds. They took care of the sheep. I could argue that women are better at taking care of sheep than men are. It is, of course, something that men should learn to do, but women have a more... gentle touch than men do. We run out of patience quite quickly, and that is not a good trait to have when you are tending to sheep. Rachel comes with her sheep, and they prepare themselves for the water they need."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob is being weird here. He tries to tell them what to do, they tell him they want to wait, and then Jacob takes it into his own hands and lifts the stone off the well (it perhaps wasn't very heavy or he was just trying to show off), and then feeds only Laban's sheep (the sheep that Rachel brought). It seems he is enamored with this girl? Whatever the case may be, he is just throwing their entire routine off, and he didn't seem to think about anyone but himself here. He proves it even further in the next verse."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Yet again, super strange. I am sure I am under the same umbrella as most when it comes to dating and girls. I was a worldly child. I didn't come to know the Lord until I was a young adult (in my 20s). The world was quite different back then, but a lot of the same influences still exist today. Because of this, kissing girls was probably something that many of the teens my age would be quite proud of. It seems that Jacob (in his awkward randomness) decides that now would be a good time to kiss this random stranger... and then cry?"},{"kind":"para","text":"If you have ever kissed a girl, you know that crying is likely not the next best step in the process. Maybe he was just really happy to have been around company finally. It has been a long trip, and for the most part of his life he had always been around people, and now he had been alone for quite some time. Maybe his tears are tears of joy? I don't really know. Whatever it may be, Jacob is one awkward fellow."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Another strange thing to say... Jacob is not her father’s brother. In that day, it was a common thing to consider family (no matter where on the family tree) a brother. It is, perhaps, how we should consider the new birth day. If we are in Christ, everyone is our brother in Him. We are held to the same degree as they are, and so we are near kinsmen. To us, that saying is strange and weird. To them, they would know that Jacob is a relative."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":20,"title":"Jacob Loves Rachel","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems they were happy to see a relative come. Rebekah has been gone for some time, and they may have never thought they would meet her children. Suddenly, Jacob shows up, and it could really go one of two ways. They could be disappointed, or they could be happy. Thankfully, they were happy and they embraced Jacob. Jacob gave the family backstory, and there was no mistaking who he was to them."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Laban seems to believe the story, and everything seems to match up. I'm curious to know if Jacob told him about the part where he stole his brother’s birthright? Or maybe about that time he stole his brother’s blessing too? Maybe he just skipped those parts. They trust him enough though, it seems, because he has now been living there for about a month."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob seems to have been serving Laban for this past month (it seems for free), and so now he wants to know how much Jacob is worth. Either that or this is the way in which Laban insinuates that Jacob should start working. Maybe Jacob hasn't done anything for this past month, and Laban wants him to actually help out and get paid."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Tender-eyed\" means \"weak or delicate and in mental qualities.\" Leah wasn't much to look at, while Rachel was beautiful to Jacob. I know and am assured that Laban obviously noticed that Jacob liked Rachel right from the get-go. Perhaps Laban suspected that Jacob would ask for her."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob \"loved\" Rachel, but Jacob didn't really know Rachel. Let’s hope her personality matches her looks... Seven years is a good, long time to serve someone. It's also a call to a future promise."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"At the end of every seven years thou shalt make a release. And this is the manner of the release: Every creditor that lendeth ought unto his neighbour shall release it; he shall not exact it of his neighbour, or of his brother; because it is called the LORD's release.","ref":"Deuteronomy 15:1-2"},{"kind":"para","text":"It will be referenced as the \"Year of Jubilee\" later on in scripture. I don't remember a time in scripture where it was ever really observed, but it was a law given by the Lord that after seven years, debts would be cleared. It could be that it stems from this very moment?"}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Seven years of free labor, and his daughter’s hand in marriage seems like a great deal to him. He accepts."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob was so enamored by this girl that it was not a problem for him to do any sort of backbreaking labor for her. This was a genuine sign that Jacob was serious about this, especially knowing that he was not a working man by any means. He is growing in some areas, and he seems to know that there are some things in this life that are worth the work."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":30,"title":"Laban Tricks Jacob","entries":[{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a grand occasion! Jacob has kept his end of the deal, and now it's time for Laban to pay up. He does so with a feast first and foremost."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's been sufficient to clarify names throughout Genesis, and we shouldn't stop now. \"Rachel\" means \"ewe\" (like a young, innocent lamb). \"Leah\" means \"weary.\" That's a bit of a sad name. \"Laban\" means “white.\" Laban will be mentioned quite a few times in Scripture. \"Zilpah\" means \"a trickling.\" It seems that Laban has a different means of paying up here with his weary daughter and trickling handmaid."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In those days, there was no way to see the woman's face at the wedding ceremony. When they got married (which is what \"go in unto her\" means), their faces were covered heavily, and they did their ceremonies and committed their vows. Jacob didn’t find out until the next morning that he had married Leah. That wasn't fair of Laban to do. Then again, this is the same thing he did to his own brother. He deceived his father by pretending to be the older child, and in this same case, Laban deceived Jacob by pretending his older daughter was his younger. Jacob actually reaped what he sowed. It must not have felt so good for it to happen to him."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Laban got seven more years of free labor. What a bargain!"}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, God doesn't want Jacob to have two wives, but nothing has been really centered around the Lord in Jacob’s life. He doesn't really know the Lord fully yet. He has seen His splendor but has not tasted that the Lord is good. In this moment, he wants the wife he worked for. He has a wife, and so he will have a second wife when everything is said and done. That is, if Laban sticks to his promise now. There was no other daughter to trick Jacob with, so Jacob was pretty confident. Still, fourteen years is a long time to serve someone. It's a long time to stay in one job."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Bilhah\" means \"troubled.\" That's not a good sign. Laban is really trying to inflict some damage on Jacob here by giving him some trouble along with the woman he wants."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ouch. Have you ever loved someone who doesn't love you back? Perhaps you have, and so you feel the hurt from Leah here. It is not a nice feeling. There's no comfort for someone who is hated or loved less."}]}]},{"start":31,"end":35,"title":"Leah's Sons","entries":[{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Reuben is the firstborn. \"Reuben\" means \"behold a son!\" It's almost as if Leah was saying, \"Hey! Look! You have a son! You can love me a little more now!\" It didn't seem to get Jacob's attention, though. He seems to brush it off as unimportant. He really wanted a family with Rachel, not with Leah. It was not by accident or miraculous conception, however, that this son was born. So it seems that Jacob is likely using Leah. Perhaps, she was enticing him? I don't really know."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Leah was not blind to the situation. She knew that the Lord was paying attention to her and gifting her with children. She might be disliked by her husband, but her children wouldn’t turn her away. \"Simeon\" means \"heard.\" Perhaps she was crying to the Lord for her husband, and this was the way in which God answered her prayers."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Three’s a charm, so they say. \"Levi\" means \"joined to.\" Her children were named as a means of hope that her husband would actually start to recognize her and take care of her. This has likely been about three years (nine months per pregnancy), and yet Jacob has learned nothing. These names will go down in history as extremely important, and we will get to that later on."}]},{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This last son of hers was her change of heart from worrying about what her husband thought of her to focusing on what the Lord thought of her. She didn't seek his approval anymore. She sought the Lord. I think this son's name is the best. \"Judah\" means \"Praise.\" Of course, there will be a lot more reference to Judah later on. But from these four sons will come great nations. We should all know that there are eight still remaining, and we will certainly get to that, but for now, we have the sons of Leah here, and they are sons of her sorrow and of her care from the Lord."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":35}]},
    "30": {"chapter":30,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see Jacob arrive after some time of travel (which was a bit of a surprise in itself seeing as he wasn't much of an explorer beforehand), and he does some really awkward things as he gets to know the people of the land. This is when he meets Rachel (still in an awkward way), and desires to marry her. He tells her father that he would work for him for seven years to marry her, and is tricked by Laban and given Leah (his first born) instead. So he works another seven years to get Rachel, and Leah is not happy about her husband marrying her sister as well. To prove her unhappiness, she has children with Jacob knowing that Rachel can't (even though they have tried), and kind of rubs it in her face."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see that sisterly competition continue on where Rachel takes matters into her own hands and forces a means of having children. Leah does the same thing to prove a point to her sister, and it just all becomes a headache and awkward in every way. Jacob was already awkward, and now he is reaping his own awkwardness with his wives."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":8,"title":"Sons of Bilhah","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In these days, children were a measure of wealth. If a woman didn't have a child, it was as if she was not prosperous. Actually, it is very common today to say the same. In the Hispanic culture, the more children you have, the better. In the Jewish culture, it's the same thing. America, Canada, and China have all become nations that find fault in that logic. If my wife and I could, we would have more children. I have a single daughter, and she will grow up quite alone. Not only that, but the more children you have, the better taken care of you will become. Perhaps the Lord doesn't want us to have more kids; I am grateful for the one we have. But I imagine that if we didn't have children, we would feel much like Rachel here, thinking that our life was meaningless and empty without them."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Albeit, Jacob wasn't wrong, but he was also being a jerk about it. There were a lot of graceful ways to speak about this situation. We will see a lot of situations in scripture where women are praying to have children, or they are having conversations with their spouse about it, and there are varying degrees of answered prayer, but they don't seem to be going to the Lord about the matter. Instead, they are just getting upset with each other as if they can control the situation. If I were in Jacob's shoes, I would have just said, \"Well, should we pray to the Lord about it? Perhaps He will listen?\" Of course, I don't believe Jacob really knows the Lord at this particular juncture; that is coming in time."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The apple doesn't fall too far from the tree in this lineage, now does it? I'll give Jacob the benefit of the doubt and assume that he doesn't know the story about Abraham and his concubine. Perhaps he will need to learn the hard way. God doesn't approve of this, and we will see that He doesn't like this idea in a moment."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Dan\" means \"a judge.\" Rachel is right, God is judging her, but not in the way she believes He is. She thinks that this move was a righteous move by her and a pleasing move to the Lord. It isn't, and it never will be."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Naphtali\" means \"wrestling.\" These two sons are born out of jealousy, not out of Godly righteousness. God provides for His children in specific ways on purpose. It is up to the Lord to give children or to withhold. We might not always know the reason why He is withholding, but we can trust and expect that God certainly has a purpose for it. These two children will be used as tribes later on, and we will see that God accepts them in, but it should have never been the case to begin with. Perhaps God would still have had 12 tribes in the end without Bilhah. Maybe He would have had fewer. I don't really know, but I DO know that God will always keep His promise, even if His children are making terrible choices."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":13,"title":"The Sons of Zilpah","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now Leah was making the same dumb mistake as Rachel! I have noticed that when babies are born in the family, all of the other women are shot with doses of emotion that strike desires and necessities to have another child. Perhaps that is what is happening here. Leah can't have any more children, but she is feeling covetous because her husband’s other wife is having children through the means of her maid. So now Leah makes the same move."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Gad\" means “troop.\" Maybe this child had a little more of a difficult time when being birthed. Maybe Leah thought of this child as a strong example that Leah was still the more favored one because she was able to have MORE children than Rachel. I am curious about the maids (now concubines) and their thoughts on this matter, though. They were being treated like baby-making houses. More like animals."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Bilhah\" means \"troubled\" (if you don't remember). She was given to her by Laban (her father), and I believe there was a specific reason he did this. It’s possible she was a troubled person, or maybe she was a person who liked to cause trouble. Either way, her name probably indicated a lot about her, and in this situation, she is far more troubled (and getting into trouble) than before."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Zilpah\" means \"a trickling.\" Once again, she was given to Leah from her father in the last chapter. A \"trickling\" of what? I don't know. A trickling can be a good thing, or it can be a bad thing. A trickling of dew might be a good thing. A trickling of sweet-smelling odor is a pretty good thing. A trickling of water from a leaky pipe is not such a good thing. Perhaps that was a flaw with Zilpah? I'm not there, but if I could put myself there, I would assume these four women were not easy for Jacob to keep up with, and it was getting even more difficult with all of these children running around."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Asher\" means \"happy.\" Leah is satisfied in her sin. Aren't we all... I am often asked the question, \"Why are women looked down upon in Scripture?\" As we've studied throughout Genesis, we have noticed that none of them have been looked down upon at all. In fact, God gives special care to make mention of these women on purpose. The only flaw that I've seen (thus far) is their desire to try and have more than what the Lord has already given them. It is still a very big issue today."},{"kind":"para","text":"Men seem to be content, and women seem to never have enough. I suppose if I had to boil down the most prominent sin a woman struggles with, it has to be her coveting. A man perhaps struggles with lust in an equal sense, and they might walk hand-in-hand, but the woman has a little more of a difficult task in finding ways to be content with where they are. Otherwise, they are attracted to the forbidden fruit. They seem to consistently talk their husbands into doing weird things with their handmaids."},{"kind":"para","text":"Does this mean that women are less superior than men? Actually, I think if we read the Bible in its proper context (which is what we have been trying to do since the beginning of all of this), we see that the women were usually the ones to make the decisions on this matter, and the men simply listened to their leading. No, the point of woman (if you don't remember) was to be equal with man. But there hasn't been any equality since the bite of the fruit, and there likely won't be until eternity. That's why we should take care to make it that way in our households."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":21,"title":"Leah Has More","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These women have such strange ways of resolving problems. They are fighting with each other (as sisters do), but using their husband (whom they shared) as a pawn in their schemes. The real issue here, however, is that Jacob just accepted it. He didn't care as long as they would just get off his back."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Issachar\" means \"there is recompense.\" \"Recompense\" means \"to make amends to someone for loss or harm suffered,\" or \"compensate.\" She thought this is what she deserves for giving Rachel some mandrakes of all things. The trade-off was a child. I don't think this was the reason for the child, but whatever makes her happy, I guess."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Zebulun\" means \"exalted.\" She thinks that because she has so many children, Jacob will love her more and find favor with her over Rachel. We must remember that it was Rachel that Jacob worked so hard for before, and it's Rachel that he still cares deeply about. He has the difficult task of uniting his very conflicted household, and I don't think he ever does it successfully."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Dinah\" means \"judgment.\" Interesting. Leah thought of her daughter as a judgment from the Lord rather than a blessing. To have sons that pass down the family name was important to the men and women of that day, but to have a girl was just as necessary and good for them. She doesn't seem to see her as such, though. The Lord blessed her with a daughter, and it was not God that took the emphasis off of the woman. It was the mother who takes the emphasis off of her."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":24,"title":"Rachel's Son","entries":[{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God was not done with Rachel. Rachel may not have had any children, but God wanted her to have one. This is an important note to make, because if Rachel never had a child, we would hold very different accounts of history in our hands today. This child will become a very important figure in uniting these very torn people. Jacob has a lot to deal with now, and he may feel a bit over-encumbered with the pressure of keeping his family together. They were fighting over every little thing."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Joseph\" means \"Jehovah has added.\" We might recognize a lot of these names that are labelled in these other children, but I can bet any money that we will recognize the name of Joseph the most. Scripture does this intentionally. Some of these children will become very dwindled and painfully ruined by the culture around them, and I believe it's because of Leah and Rachel's insistence on making their handmaids into concubines. Their sin was a very large factor in these tribes’ future problems."}]}]},{"start":25,"end":36,"title":"Jacob Wants to Go Home","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We must remember that this is all happening under the view of Laban, who has still not given freedom to Jacob after he had worked so long. All of these children are now ventured to journey with Jacob and his four wives back into his homeland. Jacob has a lot to deal with at this moment, and he just wants to go home now. Perhaps he felt that if he was not working for his father-in-law any longer, that it might relieve some of the burden or pressure on his shoulders. He has worked long and hard enough for him."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God was blessing Laban because Jacob was there. God had not left Jacob, but Jacob was not paying too much attention to God. God gave Jacob the ability to work above and beyond what he even knew he was capable of doing."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob knows that Laban is not making any strong deals to benefit Jacob. Anything that Laban says will somehow get Jacob stuck in working with him for an even longer time. But that's not what Jacob wants."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob is trying to plead his case. He just wants to go home. Laban is well to do at this point, and Jacob wants to have his family and go back to where he belongs."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob doesn't want anything. In fact, the only thing he will take off of Laban's hands (Laban is being persistent here) is the off-breeds of his herd. These were the breeds that wouldn't produce properly for Laban anyway, and Jacob could still use them if it came down to it. So now, Jacob will be venturing with a bunch of children, four wives, and a lot of off-breed animals."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob won't take any of the perfect ones and will leave them to Laban. And this seems to please Laban. But as we might have learned by now, Laban always has some tricks up his sleeve."}]},{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob did some last-minute work after taking the off-breeds from the flock. Apparently, there was a lot that Jacob was able to take; he is a wealthy man at this point."}]}]},{"start":37,"end":43,"title":"Jacob Tricks Laban","entries":[{"start":39,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They believed in those days that this helped the animals conceive and bear children. I don't think it's anything more than a superstition, but it is an interesting note to make."}]},{"start":41,"end":41,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob was using these methods to make it seem as if these ringstraked ones were not very important (and they were not pure, so they were not important to Laban). He was making his own spotted herd prosperous while making the pure ones a little weaker. Jacob is setting up a trick on his own uncle here (perhaps it's for the tricks his uncle has played on him). Either way, it seems that Jacob has not learned much on this trip except how to get into more trouble than it's worth."}]},{"start":43,"end":43,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob had a lot going for him at this point. Far more than he deserved. He worked hard for his outcome. Ultimately, however, time will tell if it pays off and if what he gained was worth the work. He's got a lot of trials coming his way in the future. He hasn't even thought about his brother's anger in quite some time. He hasn't thought about if his parents are still alive. He hasn't thought about what it's going to take to travel across this land with these children."},{"kind":"para","text":"Just as a recap, I think it's sufficient to give a list of children so we can keep track. Leah had Reuben, Simeon, Levi, Judah, Issachar, Zebulun, and Dinah. Bilhah had Dan and Naphtali. Zilpah had Gad and Asher. And Rachel had Joseph. There is another child coming (Benjamin), but he is not thought of yet. Either way, this gives us a list of eleven boys and one girl (until Benjamin is born). The twelve tribes of Israel are starting to shape up, and I'm sure the children of Israel reading this account understand full well where their lineage comes from."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":36},{"start":37,"end":43}]},
    "31": {"chapter":31,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Our last chapter was a whirlwind. It seems this family is quite... rowdy (for lack of a better word). They are doing some crazy things to get their way, and that includes making Jacob have children with their handmaids rather than waiting on the Lord's providence. Of course, the Lord will use this in the future, but the amount of turmoil this family will go through to get there will be staggering... But I'm getting ahead of myself."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see Jacob finally get the call (and the determination) to leave from Laban's land, and go back home (where he belongs) so his children can be raised in the proper land. He's got quite the caravan with him, and there are still some things that need to be settled before he makes it back."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"Jacob's Call to Leave","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know what these kids were talking about. It seems they were trying to make a mess of things with Jacob and his family at this point because Jacob was prospering in his income working for Laban. Jacob hasn't done anything particularly wrong in this area, and he has kept to his end of the deal so far. So why is it that these children of Laban seem to have a problem with Jacob? Well, I think God has this happen to His children on many occasions for a few reasons. The main reason being that He doesn't want them to feel comfortable where they are anymore."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have noticed that when I have a calling to go somewhere or to do something, God makes me feel uncomfortable in my current situation. He will even use people around me to feel like I'm just no longer welcome there. It's like a tree that's being replanted (my heart being that tree). In order to move the tree, you have to uproot it. You have to dig around the dirt and avoid cutting roots. Once you get the roots with the tree, it can be moved wherever you want to place it. There will be work and resistance, but God does it because He wants us in a different place. That's what God is doing to Jacob here. He is surrounding him with unease, so he feels uprooted from where he currently is because he isn't even supposed to be there. Perhaps God is doing it for Jacob's children's sake. His children will be the spearheads of the twelve tribes after all."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":16,"title":"Jacob's Dream","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob (thus far) has not given the Lord much credit for his prosperity. God has made it clear with a simple message of leaving that it was the Lord who has provided, and Jacob is realizing it now. Slowly but surely, Jacob opens up to the Lord. In verse 9, he points out that it was God that has given him the blessing. This is important to note because Jacob forgets this."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In the last chapter, we talked about some of the superstitions that they used back in those days to try and force breeding of certain types in their livestock. Jacob is telling his wives here that it was God that actually did the miracles here. It was God that performed these things. God told Jacob what to do, and Jacob actually listened and did it. He took God's word for it, and God proved Himself through it. Jacob has had proof of God's moving through every situation, but he is also resistant to fully trusting the Lord. Isaac wasn't really like this. Isaac learned a lot of hard lessons from his father, Abraham. This was the story of Abraham. He had to learn to trust God more and more through every step that he took. Jacob is slowly learning as well."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God makes it clear that the dream Jacob had in Bethel was not just a fluke or accident. He tells him, \"I am the God that spoke to you there and made you promises. It's time for you to go back home where you and your family belong.\" This land and these people were not going to gear Jacob’s boys into a Godly heritage. They would steer Jacob's children in a very different direction had Jacob decided to reject God's calling and stay. God (I believe) would have provided a way for them to get to where they belong, but it would have been an extra work had Jacob not simply listened."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These women believe they deserve an inheritance from their father, and their father hasn't given them a single thing. It seems they feel as if their father has robbed them more than blessed them. Jacob was doing all the real work, and Laban has just abused this family and their living circumstances. The wives are actually agreeing here (for once) with Jacob and the decision to leave."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":21,"title":"False Idols","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was foolish. We do get an idea, however, of what this family and this area were like. \"The images\" weren't pictures of their family trips left on a fireplace mantle. \"Images\" means \"a family idol.\" These were things that her (and her family) worshipped. They were fake gods. Rachel didn't tell anyone she was doing this; she just \"stole\" them, which means she did it without asking, and she likely did it in secret."},{"kind":"para","text":"This was not a good home for any of them to be in, and apparently Rachel hasn't learned anything about the Lord yet for her to take these things. She believed that Jacob had a calling to leave, but she didn't believe in the God of Jacob. She needed her comfort, and her comfort was found in the idols that her family grew up worshipping. Either way, Jacob and his family are geared to leave, and they wait for Laban to go out and shear his sheep before making their journey. Jacob wants to leave without saying goodbye (probably because he knows that Laban will try and strike up a new deal and make things worse)."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Mount Gilead is pretty close to where they need to be. So this was quite a journey at this point. If he could see the mount, he was close to the Jordan River."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":24,"title":"Laban Catches Up","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Laban and his family had to have travelled in a hurry to get here so quickly. It seems they want to get to Jacob before he leaves the land. I don't imagine that Laban had very many kind words for Jacob. Honestly, I think that Laban had more than words to share with him. He was likely on his way to beat Jacob. Laban gets close to Mount Gilead, and that is where he takes a rest before destroying Jacob."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God doesn't tell Laban what to say; he just tells Laban to be smart about what he's doing. He better not hurt Jacob, and he better not try to entice Jacob back either. If I received a message from the Lord like this, I would probably have just thought to myself, \"Is this worth it? Maybe I should just go home?\" Laban was a stubborn man, though, and he didn't want to just let them leave."}]}]},{"start":25,"end":32,"title":"Jacob is Confronted","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Laban is very close to Jacob, and it would probably take a lot for Laban to release his own pride and keep from killing Jacob at this point. Laban believes he has been robbed, and we will see his commentary in a moment. Jacob probably didn't think Laban would catch up to him, however, and that Laban would just let him go. Jacob understood (and saw) that Laban didn't like him even before he left. Now that he has gone and has taken his family, what more does Laban need?"}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Laban is being dramatic here, I want to point out. Jacob is married to his daughters. They no longer belong to Laban. Laban knows this and understands this, but he is treating Jacob as if he stole them away from him and is holding them prisoner. It is well documented that they agreed to leave and (furthermore) they were married to Jacob. That gives the right to Jacob to make decisions with his wives to go wherever they want to go."},{"kind":"para","text":"He then says, \"If you would have let me know, I would have thrown a party! I would have let you guys go without a hitch, and I would have had all sorts of music and fun. You robbed me of that, Jacob!\" No, he wouldn't have. Jacob had been trying to leave for some time, and there was always some sort of deal that Laban would use to keep Jacob still. It was never going to end unless the cycle was broken, and the only way the cycle could be broken was in the way in which Jacob broke it... Simply getting up and leaving."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps Laban really did want to kiss his grandsons and his daughters goodbye before they left. There was a part of Laban that was still human and family-oriented. There has to be in every person. Unless you are pure evil, there has to be some sort of sympathy and love for those that are your own. Even still, this is a ploy from Laban to try and guilt Jacob for what he did. That is the main focus for Laban at this time."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is an honest truth from Laban here. He tells Jacob, \"I was coming to beat you to a pulp. I had no intentions of letting you walk away from this conversation, but your God stopped me.\" Specifically, he says, \"the God of your father spake unto me...\" It doesn't seem to be Laban's God, just Jacob's father’s God. If this didn't cement in Jacob's brain that the God of the universe was fully real and fully present, I don't know what will."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think it's important to know that Jacob was not aware of the gods that were stolen from him. Rachel took them without saying anything. All Jacob can answer for is the fact that he ran away from Laban, and that is all he will answer for next."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob was under the impression that Laban would have made things difficult for Jacob if he hadn’t just run away, and (honestly) I think Jacob is right. Laban wouldn't have let Jacob leave. There would have been some sort of reason for Jacob to stay, because Jacob was bringing profit to Laban and his land. Jacob couldn't stay (especially for the fact that they were worshipping false gods). Jacob makes it clear he doesn't know what happened to his idols, and says, \"Whoever has them, hopefully they don't live. You can be angry with them.\" He didn't want the idols. He had no reason to take them. He says, \"Look through my stuff, and take what is yours that I have stolen, you shouldn't find anything that doesn't belong to me already.\" Well, Rachel decided to stay rather quiet in this moment."}]}]},{"start":33,"end":35,"title":"Rachel Hides the Images","entries":[{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Rachel didn't just want these images for \"sentimental\" value. She was purposely keeping them out of the sight of her father because she truly believed there was power in them. She wanted to implement these fake gods into her family’s traditions. I think this is a good time for me to say, if you are coming to Jesus, you must put away all other ideas of the gods and traditions you once served."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are many who have been intrenched with their religions today who end up rejecting the Lord because they have to give up their traditions. There are some who try and take Jesus and their ideas of God with them and mash them together to make one \"super-god.\" The only way we can receive the Lord is if we are willing to start completely new with our ideas of who we think God is. \"Oh, but I love my rosary. I love my prayers to angels and my candlesticks. I love confessional booths, and sprinkle baptisms.\" You might love these things, but you must let them go if you are to have Christ. Why? Is it because Christ does not love these things? Perhaps He doesn't love them, perhaps He does. I don't know what He specifically thinks about these traditions, but I do know one thing, if it's not the main focus of scripture, it should not be our focus either. Scripture’s main focus is Christ. Period. So when we come to God, that is where we must start first, and that is why we must push all of our old vain religions and traditions out of the way and start new when we begin our relationship with Him. That's what being \"born again\" means."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Jesus answered and said unto him, Verily, verily, I say unto thee, Except a man be born again, he cannot see the kingdom of God.\" John 3:3 \"Being born again, not of corruptible seed, but of incorruptible, by the word of God, which liveth and abideth for ever.\" 1 Peter 1:23 \"Therefore we are buried with him by baptism into death: that like as Christ was raised up from the dead by the glory of the Father, even so we also should walk in newness of life.","ref":"Romans 6:4"},{"kind":"para","text":"When we were born the first time, we brought nothing into this world. If we are to be born again, we cannot bring anything into our new life. We must start new. Rachel did not want to leave her old life behind and wanted to implement her traditions into her family, keeping with the same mundane routines and religious practices of her childhood. She was a fool for it."}]}]},{"start":36,"end":42,"title":"Making a Leader","entries":[{"start":39,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob and Laban begin to fight, and Rachel lets it happen. Honestly, this is what false worship will do to a people. There can never be unity in a house when there are people worshipping the wrong gods and serving their childhood traditions. I think it should be well understood that traditions (in themselves) are not bad. There should just be a reason for them, and the reason should be (alone) to worship God (alone). If it doesn't align with Scripture, and it doesn't bring glory to the Lord (and His sacrifice), it's best to get rid of that tradition."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob makes some great counterarguments against Laban here. Jacob has worked for a long time to suffice Laban, and has been good to Laban through that entire work, and yet Laban has been nothing but difficult. Even when it was Laban's fault for something that went wrong, Jacob would take the hit for it. That is a very important thing to note, because good leaders will take responsibility for their team’s failures. I have a bit of a guilty pleasure. I enjoy watching MasterChef. There's something about it that I find quite amusing. The show (if you haven't seen it) brings a bunch of home cooks from around the world and has them compete with one another (cooking food obviously) until there is one winner left standing (who wins a substantial amount of money as well as some useful tools for their kitchen). There are some episodes where they have what is called \"team challenges\" and in these challenges the contestants are split up into two teams, and must run a kitchen (together) to try and out-cook the other team. One team wins, and the other team faces elimination. When the team facing elimination is pried, it is usually the team’s captain that takes the fall for the team. Even if it wasn't the captain’s fault, they usually take the fall. The judges recognize the real people to blame, and so they ultimately have the decision whether to allow the captain to shoulder the blame, or place the blame on the one who really deserves it. Usually they place the blame on the rightful person."},{"kind":"para","text":"In our society today, it is a very difficult thing to find leaders who will shoulder the blame for their team. They would rather let the whole team fall apart than to just take the pressure and put it on their own shoulders. Laban was this type of person. There are church leaders who do this, and political leaders who do this, and even corporate leaders who do this. If we are in a position of leadership, we should be looking to include ourselves in the issues as well as in the triumphs. We should be able to say, \"We messed up...\" John Maxwell says, \"Bosses drive workers; leaders coach them. Bosses depend on authority; leaders depend on goodwill. Bosses inspire fear; leaders inspire enthusiasm. Bosses say, 'I'; leaders say, 'We.' Bosses fix the blame for any breakdown; leaders fix the breakdown. Bosses know how it is done; leaders show how. Bosses say, 'Go'; leaders say, 'Let's go!'\" The difference here is not just with a change of speech (though that will obviously be the case when you are addressing problems with this mindset. The difference is with a change of heart. We can either boss people around like Laban and let them take the fall for everything, OR you can learn to lead like Jacob and start shouldering the issues so that you help people grow. Jacob has helped Laban grow for twenty years now, and Laban will learn rather quickly how important Jacob is to him."}]},{"start":42,"end":42,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob recognizes that Laban would not have been fair to Jacob after all the work he has put in. He says, \"If it weren't for God, and if it weren't for the fact that you know my lineage, you would have robbed me blind. You have proven that you are not trustworthy for twenty years straight, as you have made shady deals and changed my wages too many times.\" Really, it is only the providence of the Lord that Jacob was able to leave with anything at all."}]}]},{"start":43,"end":44,"title":"Laban's Last Contract","entries":[{"start":44,"end":44,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"No, Laban, they are not. His daughters will (of course) always be his daughters, but he no longer has the right over them. The cattle weren't his either. The cattle were raised and gotten by Jacob alone. Laban didn't do anything to help Jacob in his work. Jacob's children weren't Laban's either. They belonged to Jacob. It wasn't enough for Laban, so there is a final deal that must be agreed to:"}]}]},{"start":45,"end":53,"title":"A Witness Heap","entries":[{"start":45,"end":45,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, these are symbols of testimony. We have seen this a lot in Genesis."}]},{"start":47,"end":47,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I am curious to know how the meal was here. Was there any joy as they sat down to eat? Was it more of a leering look at one another as they ate in this covenant? I don't know, but it could not have been very comfortable. Laban wouldn't be reasonable, and Jacob was tired of bending to Laban’s dictatorship. Laban wants to be the one that comes out on top, and Jacob just wants to be left alone. \"Jegarsahadutha\" is Aramaic, which means \"Witness heap.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"I assume that this contract wasn't very special to Laban. He is just thinking out of anger at this point. Interestingly enough, Jacob also calls the place the same thing. \"Galeed\" is Hebrew, which means \"witness heap.\" They are the same definition, but different meanings. Laban's version is saying, \"This is the location of our witness heap.\" Whereas Jacob's version is saying, \"This is the testimony of our witness heap.\" One was a little more serious than the others."}]},{"start":49,"end":49,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Laban agreed to Jacob's version more. They named the location \"Mizpah.\" \"Mizpah\" means “watchtower.\" So this \"witness heap\" is located at a watchtower. God will take care of the issues between them. He will do justly by both, and they will not interfere with each other’s property anymore. Here’s the basis of the contract:"}]},{"start":53,"end":53,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There was no reason for them to take or rob each other of anything. This \"heap\" was a testimony that God would judge if one or the other broke the promise, and they took the promise seriously. Jacob took the promise seriously for fear of his own father. He knew that Isaac was a godly man, and he knew that he didn't want anything terrible to fall upon his father for breaking a promise like this. It wasn't worth the trials to come to break this covenant. The other way is assured as well. Laban didn't want anything serious to happen to his own daughters if he broke the covenant."}]}]},{"start":54,"end":55,"title":"A Peaceful Resolution","entries":[{"start":54,"end":54,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They sealed the covenant with a sacrifice. That is how all covenants of the Lord were made. God would be a testimony and a covenant keeper where there is sacrifice. That is how we know we are sealed today, because there was a sacrifice made for God's covenant with man. God sacrificed His perfect Son to keep His promise for you."}]},{"start":55,"end":55,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It is a peaceful resolution. It could have been much worse, but everyone walked away with what they wanted today. This includes Rachel, who stole her father’s fake gods. That will not go unnoticed, and there is a reason that scripture includes this information here. We might not see the consequences of that action yet, but you can guarantee that God will reveal the error in her ways in time to come…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":32},{"start":33,"end":35},{"start":36,"end":42},{"start":43,"end":44},{"start":45,"end":53},{"start":54,"end":55}]},
    "32": {"chapter":32,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see the Lord speak to Jacob about heading out of his current living circumstance, and going back to the land that is promised to him. Jacob is not supposed to be here, he is supposed to be home. So he gets ready to leave, and his uncle pursues after him, causing all sorts of drama (including with the idols that his daughter stole, but are never found). They eventually leave off with each other in a pact (or promise) about staying at peace with one another in the future."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see that Jacob is getting closer to being back at home, but he has to wrestle with a few things. Firstly, he must wrestle with his mind as he tries to figure out how to confront his brother (whom he thinks might still be angry, and might try and destroy him). Secondly, he has to wrestle with his faith (which will be put into subjection as we will see in this chapter)."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"Meeting Angels","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is the second altercation with angels that Jacob has experienced. The first time, he simply saw the angels, and the Lord had spoken to him. I don't think he fully understood the purpose of it. He must have had holy leadings from the Lord (whether through dreams or whatever the case may be), but he hasn't come to full circle with following Him quite yet. In this instance, it says that he actually \"met\" with the angels. In other words, he walked through their territory of dwelling."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Angels\" means “messengers.\" Maybe he saw them and recognized their clothing or stature. Most of Scripture depicts angels as genderless beings, but they have the look and representation of men. Paul says we can entertain angels without even realizing we are."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Be not forgetful to entertain strangers: for thereby some have entertained angels unawares.","ref":"Hebrews 13:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"There must be some sort of distinction from regular people, though. I just have no idea what it is. Either way, Jacob and his family are now walking in the midst of the angels, and perhaps the only one that took notice was Jacob, seeing as he's the only one that says anything about it."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So far, the Bible has been great about defining every name of each city. Can we guess what this name might be? If you said \"God's host,\" you are absolutely... wrong. \"Mahanaim\" means \"Two camps.\" I bet you weren't expecting that one. The angels were present with the people of Earth, and that is what Jacob is pointing out here. God is doing a work behind the scenes that no one else seems to be paying attention to. But Jacob sees it. And he is making it known that others should take notice."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's a reminder to us: We may not know what kind of work God and His angels are doing, but we can be guaranteed there is a work taking place. It might take time to see what that work leads to, but it will soon enough come to pass if we hold on and wait patiently. Maybe you will end up walking through the host of Heaven by chance on a journey."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":5,"title":"Jacob Remembers Esau","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We may have forgotten, but Jacob has not. He knows what he did to his brother before he left, and he knows that his brother was not happy about the way things ended. Last we heard of Esau, he was ready to kill Jacob for the kind of deceit he had made. Now Jacob thinks to himself, \"You know what? I should probably do something about my brother before I come back home. He is certainly still upset with my actions.\" Mind you, the actions that Jacob had made ruined a whole life promise for his brother Esau. With that said, Jacob was going to get the promise anyway, because the Lord would have made sure to keep HIS promise to them. It just might have been easier had it been the Lord, rather than a forceful stealing of those promises. So Jacob is sending some messengers ahead of him in hopes that if his brother is still angry, he might take it out on them and know not to head in that direction anymore."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob is using very humbling language here. He makes sure that the \"lord\" title is mentioned twice in his statement. He wants Esau to feel as superior as possible so that he knows that Jacob is looking for peace and not trouble. He had already caused enough trouble to begin with, and he doesn't want Esau to continue to feel bitter about it. Perhaps Esau is a \"lord\" now. I assume he has taken charge of a city, and he is prospering quite well. We see his family dynamic grow through scripture as we read on, but we also know that the main focus is on the Israelites (which are now with Jacob at this time). They (of course) are not called the Israelites yet, but that will be understood in a moment."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":8,"title":"Esau and His Army","entries":[{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ha! Jacob loved to play tricks on his brother, and I have no doubt that Esau is using this tactic as a trick to scare Jacob. We will see in a moment what Esau does when he meets Jacob, but right now, Jacob is being told that there is a whole army coming to approach him. That can't make Jacob feel very easy. He has his family, wealth, and laborers with him. He has a lot to lose here, and not much protection. He DOES have (however) the Lord. And we know (once again) that the Lord has big plans for him in the future."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He's protecting his assets. It was actually a decent enough plan. If a part is destroyed, at least he can escape with his family and fingers still intact."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":12,"title":"Dividing His Possessions","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We haven't seen Jacob as much of a praying man in our previous chapters, but we now get a glimpse of some behind-the-scenes working in Jacob's life. God has been in communication with Jacob (that shouldn't be a doubt). God has told him what to do and where to move, and (so far) Jacob has been quite obedient. Now he feels that he is going to be crushed by his brother, and that the Lord is possibly leading him into trouble."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will notice (in a moment) that the Lord stays silent on this prayer request. There is no comfort given to Jacob. I believe there's a reason for that. Jacob has been commissioned to go home, and he has received a promise from the Lord that He will guide him and use him. That was the promise that Jacob must depend upon. The other stipulations (in that process) were not faith-based."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"(For we walk by faith, not by sight:)","ref":"2 Corinthians 5:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"If God answered Jacob with the solution to the problem, or with the knowledge of what was to come, it would rob Jacob of his faith. I've been noticing a lot (lately especially) that there are two types of people in this world. People who encourage the faiths of others, and people who rob others of faith. We all have a choice to make in every relationship we are a part of. Do we choose to encourage people in their ministry and walk, or do we rob them of their ministries and walk, and take the rewards from them? Jacob did this to his brother. He robbed his brother's faith and blessing. Pastors should not be robbing his congregants of blessings. That is a very dangerous space to be in. Ultimately, this problem is more of a psychological and spiritual one, and the only one that can fight that battle is the Lord. Try as we might (and we will see Jacob try in a moment), God has to be the one that fights the spiritual battles we have no control over. We are not spiritual (yet, in the sense that we can control the unseen). We are physical. We must depend on the Lord for the battle. Jacob will have to depend on God in this moment if he is going to be able to move forward."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":21,"title":"A Present for Esau","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So Jacob is preparing a welcome gift. He is hoping that his brother will reconsider attacking Jacob when he sees the amount of livestock and provisions he has. Jacob certainly has a lot provided to him by the Lord, and Jacob is more than capable of sharing the spoils of it. In total (as an estimation because we aren't sure how many camel colts there are), he is delivering five hundred and fifty animals to his brother. That's a lot of animals. And Jacob STILL has more to offer. If I were in his shoes (as I'm sure was on his mind anyway), my biggest concern is keeping my family safe. He can take all of my material possessions, just don't touch my family."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He splits up each of his servants to walk with the different groups of animals so that Esau (hopefully) will be stopped at least nine times before getting to Jacob. There are nine different animals talked about here. If I were Esau and was stopped after the third group of animals, it would probably just frustrate me more than encourage me. If Esau wasn't coming to kill Jacob, he might just do it now because of how annoying this is going to be. I'm kidding, of course."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob is certainly taking his time to come to meet Esau. His servants have already gone ahead for him a couple of times, and he still hasn't caught up to them. Granted, his servants didn't have thousands of animals and a troop of servants and their own families going with them as well, but there is no reason that it should have taken this long. He's prolonging his pain at this point. The gifts are just hopes that Esau will turn his wrath away."},{"kind":"para","text":"Twenty years is a long time to hold onto bitterness, and it would be silly for Esau to still be bitter towards his brother after all of this. There are a lot of people who hold onto bitterness their entire lives (sadly), and it leaves them in turmoil when it comes to the last moments. They regret not doing something sooner. Today is a good day to forgive someone that you have been holding a grudge against."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":32,"title":"Israel Is Born","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob secures his family and sends everything he has ahead of him. He turns around and goes to think by himself for a while. He realizes his error from his youth, and he regrets making those decisions when he did. God was going to give Jacob a promise whether he did it this way or not. Of course, I believe that God made the promise because He also KNEW what Jacob would ultimately do to his brother, but God didn't want it to happen that way. He wanted to be the provider of the promise, and Jacob didn't just rob Esau of a blessing that night. He robbed GOD of the blessing as well..."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let's consider Jacob’s location at this moment. He is in the middle land. He cannot turn and go back (his uncle was not going to let him return that easily). He's afraid to move forward (his brother could still be angry and ready to kill him). So he's stuck in the middle. And we come to this portion of Jacob wrestling with a random stranger in the middle of nowhere. We are not sure how the altercation takes place, we just simply pick up with Jacob being alone and a man coming and wrestling with him. We will find out who this man is in a moment, but it seems like a very odd thing to take place."},{"kind":"para","text":"We must remember that he is walking through the midst of angels at this point. There are no other people around this area. So we must certainly (if we can agree on anything here) agree on the fact that this \"man\" in which Jacob is wrestling, must be an angel of some sort. But why wrestle with him? Why make ANOTHER enemy? I think perhaps Jacob was not going out of his way to make enemies. He was probably conflicted all around, and this angel (in particular) came to help him with his decisions. The best way to see his issue was to let him fight for it. This will be a useful tool to set the rest of history for the children of Israel. This moment will be the biggest and most important change for the rest of the life of Jacob and his children."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So we are getting a picture of the strength of this opponent here. This \"man\" couldn't help Jacob, because Jacob was very stubborn and very confused, and in order to get Jacob to bend (instead of fight), he touches (probably with a single finger) Jacob’s thigh, and completely disjoints it. The agonizing pain that had to be. Jacob just can't catch a break. This picture gives us a good idea about who it is that Jacob is wrestling with though. It had to be a higher power, because I don't know anyone that can disjoint a fellow with a single touch. I suspect that this was not just an ordinary angel though. We have a lot of speculation about who it is (and we will read a little further into it in a moment), but I believe the person he is struggling with is Christ Himself. But again, we will study deeper into that through the remainder of this chapter and you can make up your own mind about it."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob can't wrestle much at this point (and it doesn't say they are wrestling anymore). The sun is coming up, Jacob is in a lot of pain, and so he just grabs onto this other man for dear life. That is a great place to be in, by the way. If this is Christ (as we will be led to believe), then the best place to be is holding onto Him. Sometimes it requires Christ to put us into submission, but ultimately we should want to be clinging onto Him, requesting a blessing. Jacob was a man that liked to rob blessings, and now he is a man begging to receive one. He has not felt very blessed for quite some time, and he is worried about what the future is about to hold. Where does he fit in? Where does God see him working the best? Well, that is really what he is asking at this moment, and this \"man\" will tell him."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, this \"man\" already knew his name was Jacob. If you don't remember, Jacob means \"holder of the heel.\" That is what he did when he came into this world. He was holding onto his brother’s heel for support. This \"man\" wanted to take account (for Jacob's sake) of the incredible blessing that he is about to receive, and it's why we can all (hopefully) come to agree that this is Christ here."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This \"man\" changes Jacob's name. In Scripture, we find that no one else can change a name except for the Lord. In fact, that is what God does to every single one of His children."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith unto the churches; To him that overcometh will I give to eat of the hidden manna, and will give him a white stone, and in the stone a new name written, which no man knoweth saving he that receiveth it.","ref":"Revelation 2:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"This \"man\" says, “Well, because we have been at this for this time, and because you are eager for a blessing, I will give you what you want. You are quite powerful, and it seems the Lord is on your side because you have continued to strive and not give up.\" We have a pretty strong indication here that this \"man\" knows about Jacob's Lord. The better part of this is the definition of Israel. \"Israel\" means \"God prevails.\" In this moment, God certainly prevails (and He always does if we don't know that by now)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Another indication that Jacob was wrestling with Christ here is by this chosen name. \"Israel\" is a derivative of two Hebrew words which also means \"Wrestles with God.\" We can conclude, then, that Jacob is not just wrestling with any random \"man\" or just a lowly \"angel\" but with the Lord Himself. We have more relation in other parts of Scripture."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Ephraim feedeth on wind, and followeth after the east wind: he daily increaseth lies and desolation; and they do make a covenant with the Assyrians, and oil is carried into Egypt. The LORD hath also a controversy with Judah, and will punish Jacob according to his ways; according to his doings will he recompense him. He took his brother by the heel in the womb, and by his strength he had power with God: Yea, he had power over the angel, and prevailed: he wept, and made supplication unto him: he found him in Bethel, and there he spake with us; Even the LORD God of hosts; the LORD is his memorial.","ref":"Hosea 12:1-5"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"The LORD is his memorial\" means \"the LORD is His name.\" We are then under complete understanding that this \"man\" had to be a prominent worker of the Lord, and not just merely a man. And angels don't have the power to provide blessings. You will not see that in Scripture. Rather, the Lord is always the one that gives blessings."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But my God shall supply all your need according to his riches in glory by Christ Jesus.\" Philippians 4:19 \"The LORD bless thee, and keep thee: The LORD make his face shine upon thee, and be gracious unto thee: The LORD lift up his countenance upon thee, and give thee peace.\" Numbers 6:24-26 \"And God is able to make all grace abound toward you; that ye, always having all sufficiency in all things, may abound to every good work:","ref":"2 Corinthians 9:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"There is very strong evidence that this was not just a mere angel, but a pre-incarnate Christ that Jacob (now Israel) is fighting with."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob maybe didn't know who he was even fighting with. He just knew that he needed something from this man, and this man was the only one that could give it. He knew that this man had a lot to offer if he was a true servant of the Lord. He wrestled the right person. And (might I add), it's okay to wrestle in life today. We are all going to be in a season of wrestling. I don't believe the Lord rebuked Jacob for his wrestling. Instead, he wrestled with him. The Lord can take our shots and hurts. He knows how to deal with us."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For we wrestle not against flesh and blood, but against principalities, against powers, against the rulers of the darkness of this world, against spiritual wickedness in high places.","ref":"Ephesians 6:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"May we wrestle for a blessing, and not for a sinful passion. If we are wrestling with the Lord because we want to please ourselves, we will lose that battle every time, and we certainly won't receive a blessing for it."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Yet another indication that this was not just an average angel wrestling with Jacob here. \"Peniel\" means \"facing God.\" Jacob was confronted with much more than just receiving a blessing in this place, and I think he was blessed above and beyond measure. He is promised, here, that the Lord has blessed him and that he will be a prince with the power of the Lord. That is quite a promise to make to Jacob. The crazy part is, God makes it clear that no one can see His face:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he said, Thou canst not see my face: for there shall no man see me, and live.","ref":"Exodus 33:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord is too holy for us to see Him face to face. Yet, Jacob is assured that he has seen God face-to-face, and has still been preserved from death. He is still alive! Christ, of course, is 100% man and 100% God. There is a lot of indication of that (but it's best left for the gospel accounts)."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I believe there is a much deeper spiritual aspect to this wrestling. I believe the world wrestles with the Lord today. God is consistently wrestling with His creation. The wrestling that is taking place here was to get Jacob into submission. I don't believe that Jacob sought out this fight. I think Christ came and wrestled him. He needed Jacob to trust in the Lord with His heart, not just with his hopes. Christ wanted Jacob’s faith to be spirit-led, not just knowledge-driven. Jacob is seeing the problems surrounding him, and Christ is saying, \"Submit to faith! Don't look at the problems, just trust in the leader of your soul.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"This is, perhaps, the moment that Jacob accepts Christ. This is the moment that Jacob gets to know the God of Abraham and Isaac. That relationship is a bit forced, but it was the only way to get Jacob's commitment. Sometimes that is how God will have to deal with us. He might have to disjoint your hip to get your attention. The clinging to the Lord is a great picture of His dedication and reliance on Him. That is where we all need to be. This handicap that Jacob received will not go away. It will always be a reminder of what he just experienced. Every time he feels a pain in his disjointed hip, he will be reminded of the blessing of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Unto this day\" indicates that this writing is taking place in future times. We have a lot of indication that it is Moses writing these accounts, and we have talked about this before. So Jacob is reminded of this, and his lineage remembers this by not eating the thighs tendon (likely from any animal but especially the animals that are sacrificed). \"The sewn that shrank\" is simply referring to the tendon in the hip area that shrank from just the touch of God. They will memorialize Jacob’s wrestling with what they eat, so they can know where they came from. They do this because everyone has to eat, and what better way to remember something than for us to link or connect it to something we will ultimately HAVE to do. That will be a theme through scripture over and over again (including in the New Testament with Christ)."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":32}]},
    "33": {"chapter":33,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we get the amazing story of Jacob wrestling with God and his name being changed to Israel. We saw he was a bit worried about confronting his brother Esau after so many years, and so he is trying to devise a plan, and wants the Lord to bless his future and give him assurance of what will come next. God certainly provides him with a blessing (and a means of remembering that blessing) and now Jacob must accept the blessing and move forward in faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see Jacob and Esau meet again after so many years, and it is a bit of a nerve-wrecking scene. We have had the assumption that Esau was coming to fight against Jacob, and Jacob has been trying to appease Esau far before they meet together. Now we will see what will ultimately happen when they come face to face with each other for the first time in a long time."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":11,"title":"Jacob Meets Esau","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So Jacob is (hopefully) coming into this with new eyes of faith. He is promised by the angel of the Lord that he will be blessed in our last chapter. The biggest blessing was his name change. Even still, he can't be too careful knowing that his brother is right over the horizon. He sees him at a distance now, and four hundred men with him, and he is still a bit worried about the outcome. He wants his family to be safe, so he separates them."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now Rachel and Leah and their children probably don't know too much about the situation here. I don't know if Jacob had been upfront about all of this before this moment. They are seeing Jacob bow over and over again as he walks along towards his brother, and they are probably thinking, \"Okay, this is strange. He must have really messed up to be acting like this; the first bow was enough.\" Jacob doesn't know what will happen in this moment, and I absolutely love the next verse."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Man, what a powerful verse. Esau and Jacob are twin brothers. They couldn't stay angry at each other forever. Twenty years (plus) is a long time to let hatred like that fester. Esau instantly forgives Jacob and doesn't hold anything over his head. They are seen here, not just embracing and loving on each other, but WEEPING together. What a picture of humility and growth. No one brought up past issues, and they were both just happy to be in each other’s presence again. But it doesn't just end there."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Esau had no idea that Jacob had such a large family and a large abundance. He sees his family for the first time and says, “Well, what do we have here? Are they yours?\" Jacob doesn't take credit for it and simply says, \"The Lord was gracious enough to bless me with this family.\" The whole family shows respect and kindness to Esau as they come up to him. I don't doubt that there was not a single dry eye in this group. They all (I'm sure) were feeling some sort of emotion over this cause and event."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If you don't remember, Jacob sent tons of animals and servants to his brother ahead of time so that his brother might forgive him before he got too close. Esau is questioning it now. Jacob doesn't lie either. He says, \"I sent them so you don't hurt me.\" I find that funny. The next part is amazing as well."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There is a massive change in Esau and Jacob. I believe that Esau had a right to take Jacob's property at this moment. He could have accepted it as a gift. But he seems to be completely forgiving Jacob of anything that he had done in the past. Jacob had robbed Esau several times, and then left him out to dry by himself. Esau, no doubt, was bitter and angry with his brother for years and was thinking about the day in which he would get revenge. Now’s his chance, and instead of doing what he had always thought to do, he instantly forgives him. In fact, Esau doesn't bring up the past issues with his brother at all. It almost seems as if Esau has forgotten about it altogether."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob had spent a life of robbing others of blessings, and now he is paying back sevenfold what he had taken. His persistence finally pays off, and Esau takes the blessing. There is an obvious change in both of these men, but I think the more obvious change here is in Jacob. Jacob has learned the error of his ways and knows that what he did was not kind to anyone, especially to his poor older brother. Esau was still prosperous, and he worked hard for what he got. I believe Jacob worked hard too, but it was mostly handed to him from the Lord. Honestly, we can each say that."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have our necessities met only because the Lord has been gracious to us. If we rob the Lord of glory in our providence, we are ultimately saying that WE obtained our fortune by our OWN strength. That is fine. You can say that. Plenty of people do. But your wages in your own strength are nothing compared to the free gifts that God can provide in HIS strength. I'd rather receive from HIS strength than from my own, because when I am gifted by the Lord, I have plenty to turn around and give away to others who are in need."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":17,"title":"Esau and Jacob Split Up","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Esau wants to guide Jacob back. There are probably a lot of new things that Jacob would not know about. Furthermore, Esau wants to help protect Jacob and his belongings as they travel back. Jacob was wealthy and traveling with his wealth on display. There would certainly be people who would take notice of that and try to rob Jacob."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob has a slow-moving brigade. Esau and his men are not going to like walking slowly back into the land. It would be better if Esau would just go ahead and they would just catch up later, rather than walk at a snail’s pace all the way through."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Esau wanted to leave some of his men, just in case. He felt they might need some protection. Jacob, on the other hand, thought it was better just to walk on through by his own protection. There was plenty of protection from the Lord at this point to get them through. They had made it this far without an issue, and they didn't have much further to go. It looks like Esau is now dwelling in the land of Canaan. He is specifically stationed around Mount Seir. It was a land that was given to him according to Scripture."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Meddle not with them; for I will not give you of their land, no, not so much as a foot breadth; because I have given mount Seir unto Esau for a possession.","ref":"Deuteronomy 2:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"I imagine that the people of Israel were hearing or reading this story of Genesis right outside of the promised land, thinking, \"Wow, how we've all come full circle. God is real here.\" God is real, and we should have an easy time retracing His path when we look through our family history. I believe there is always someone in your family history who has placed their trust in the Lord. They might have left a sour taste to their children about it, or they may have been extremely casual about their faith and their children never took it seriously, but I can guarantee that someone in everyone's family history had trusted in the Lord. We should look into that matter honestly. It might be a blessing to us to know that you weren't the only one."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Succoth\" means \"booths.\" I don't know if this place was called \"Succoth\" before Jacob got there or after he made booths. Either way, this seems to be his place of dwelling for the time being. Everything seems to be too good for Jacob at this moment. It would seem as if Jacob doesn't need to pay for his crimes against his family. God is gracious and good, but there will always be consequences for your actions, and that will rear its head in time. For now, everything seemed to be looking up, and Jacob was back where he belonged."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":20,"title":"The God of Israel","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Shalem\" means \"peace.\" So Jacob is living a peaceful life in the city of peace. Just like light is a constant theme in Scripture, we see God give moments of peace quite often to His children. This was Jacob's moment of peace. \"Shechem\" means \"back.\" \"Padanaram\" means \"field.\" So Jacob had peace in his back (perhaps with his uncle Laban) and looks forward to the field (perhaps his future with his family)."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob buys some property and wants to rest here. It is quite close to where he needed to be. He should have gone to Bethel, but he doesn't seem to make it that far. He is tired and weary, and ready to rest with his family. \"Hamor\" means \"he-ass.\" That's not a fun name to have. I don't know what kind of man this was, but he might not have been all that pleasant to work with. Jacob buys the field here from a man named \"back\" (the son of he-ass) and starts to build his life in the land. He still has some journeying to do, and there is still a lot of trials he will experience in his life before he can sit comfortably. Remember, it is Bethel where Jacob and his family are ultimately meant to be. It was the place where God promised to give him."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And, behold, the LORD stood above it, and said, I am the LORD God of Abraham thy father, and the God of Isaac: the land whereon thou liest, to thee will I give it, and to thy seed; And thy seed shall be as the dust of the earth, and thou shalt spread abroad to the west, and to the east, and to the north, and to the south: and in thee and in thy seed shall all the families of the earth be blessed. And, behold, I am with thee, and will keep thee in all places whither thou goest, and will bring thee again into this land; for I will not leave thee, until I have done that which I have spoken to thee of. And Jacob awaked out of his sleep, and he said, Surely the LORD is in this place; and I knew it not. And he was afraid, and said, How dreadful is this place! this is none other but the house of God, and this is the gate of heaven. And Jacob rose up early in the morning, and took the stone that he had put for his pillows, and set it up for a pillar, and poured oil upon the top of it. And he called the name of that place Bethel: but the name of that city was called Luz at the first.","ref":"Genesis 28:13-19"},{"kind":"para","text":"But for now, he is ready to get off his broken hip."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"EleloheIsrael\" means \"God, the God of Israel.\" The Lord had made Himself obvious to Jacob now. Jacob is owning his new name at this moment. He has established what we have referred to as \"a testimony\" to the Lord in this land. He built an altar, and anyone that saw it would recognize that something had happened here. There was a story that took place in that location. While the passersby didn't know what that meant, we get to understand and be a part of that story today. We see what God did, and we will continue to see how God will build up the Israelites. This is the established future of Israel. This moment was the turning point of the people, as they will see Jacob come to terms with God's promises."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":20}]},
    "34": {"chapter":34,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: These past few chapters have a been a whirlwind in the life of Jacob and his family. Jacob has finally seen his brother Esau after many years of separation. The build up to this moment was intense, and Esau was actually happy to finally see Jacob after so many years. When Esau is giving the green light to head home and Jacob will meet with him later, Jacob takes the occassion to make camp in a place he isn't necessarily supposed to be. He is supposed to go to Bethel, but he doesn't quite reach that place (I believe due to his current handicap)."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we will see why it was a mistake to stop in this land for the length of time that he did, and the snowball effect sin has from one mistake to the next."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"Dinah Defiled","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well, we know that Jacob is not in the right place yet. He has made a stop and will need to move quickly to where he is supposed to be. There is a reason that Esau was cautious in taking care of Jacob and his family, and that reason is going to be displayed in this chapter. Jacob’s daughter Dinah (who seems to be the only daughter he has) thinks it might be fun to see some of the other girls who are staying in this land. Jacob would have been wise to keep his kids close to home and away from the other people (or at the very least go with her to make friends)."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There's no taking this back. Once this kind of damage has been done, it is done forever. No one can go back and fix it."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They certainly did things differently back then, but by no means was this the proper order of operations. It was not the right way to court someone back then, and it's not the right way to do it now. We haven't really changed much as a society today, though if I'm being honest. Most boys and girls believe it's a right of passage in a relationship, and if they can't have this (marital-specific action) now, then they don't want to be in a relationship with you. There's a reason that waiting for marriage is the proper order and response, and the reason is because you can't take it back. Marriage is a forever thing, and intimacy is a forever thing. But this young man wanted to marry Dinah, and I personally think it's horrible for a young girl to marry her rapist."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob is far more controlled than I am (or that his sons will be in a moment). If it were my daughter, I'd look for reasons to kill this man, or at the least get him locked up. Rapists are the worst kinds of criminals. But Jacob... He collected himself and his thoughts before addressing the situation. He was waiting for his children to come back from the field before telling them what had taken place."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":19,"title":"Jacob's Sons Deceive","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These boys were being boys. They were upset at the news, and they wanted something to be done about it. This man's son defiled their only sister, and now he wants to take their sister as a wife? They couldn't believe what they were hearing."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Cultural mixing (especially in those days) was not a very accepted thing in the land. They were to marry their own kind, specifically because they had certain and specific rights for their land, but also because the people of that day were not very friendly to the Jewish way of living. Of course, they aren't necessarily called Jews yet. They aren't even called \"Israelites\" at this moment. But what had been done was still terrible and wrong, and what is the real fix here? Does Jacob allow his daughter to marry this man? Does Jacob take his daughter away and tell him no because of the situation? There was no real law stating what to do, and so they had to be cautious about their next move."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This Shechem man was willing to do whatever he could to have the girl. Now, it might have been a very different story if he was asking for the girl’s hand in marriage BEFORE raping her, but we have a wrench thrown into the middle of the conversation, and so things need to be treaded lightly. This is Jacob's only daughter after all."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Oh boy... We can already see where this is going. I mean, perhaps it's deserved? A little pain for the pain he has caused her. However, their intentions are not to give Dinah up for marriage. In fact, Jacob doesn't seem to speak here at all. He lets his sons do the talking."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, mind you, circumcision is a very serious act of worship to the Lord. It is a proven symbol of these children being set apart. In our modern language, what they are asking Shechem (and all the men of their land) to do was to be converted. That's not a bad thing. However, they are not being circumcised to be converted... They are being circumcised to be accepted by the Israelites. They have missed the whole point."},{"kind":"para","text":"May I just say, marriages like this don't work. If there is one person saved and the other person cannot comprehend Christ, you should not get married. It will be a headache and a heartache for the couple, and it will end in divorce rather quickly. It will end in divorce simply because there is only value in the relationship from one side and not the other, and one side will find power in anger, whereas the other side wants to love like the Lord. The sons of Jacob were abusing their symbol of separation here. They had the opportunity to truly see these people convert (even while they are angry at the fact that Dinah was raped by one of them), but they missed the picture. That should be our true nature. I said earlier that I would be quite angry with the young man who raped my daughter, and I certainly would be, but my first action should be to see if this boy would be saved first. If he is not willing to give me time for conversion, or he is not willing to turn to the Lord, then I will not give him time outside of a prison house."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Shechem is doing an honorable thing. I will give him credit for it here. He is making the best of a wrong right. Even in marriage, he is trying to put the pieces together. I will give him the benefit of the doubt. He grew up in a land where rape was probably a very common practice by his people. He was likely taught that this thing was okay. We saw that it was a common practice in the land of Sodom as well. It seems to be a very common thing throughout most of the land of Scripture, and it's something that encircles the children of Israel quite a bit throughout Scripture. They (the Israelites) even get sucked into this tradition themselves and get themselves into some serious trouble from it. Shechem is trying to prove himself to these people, but these people had no actual pursuit to keep their end of the promise. These brothers were set out to ruin this man's life and will do whatever it takes to make him suffer."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":24,"title":"The Men Circumcised","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These people were hoping to take Jacob's possessions. Not just the children and intermixing, but with his livestock and income. They were hoping that he would be a valuable asset to the land, so much to the point where this family might become a slave to the people. We are in a very interesting territory as we are reading this here, because there are plenty of emotions to go through as we read."},{"kind":"para","text":"How would we deal with this situation at this time? Of course, we look at it from an outside perspective and think, “Well, I would have done this or that...\" but the truth is, we probably wouldn't have done too much differently if we were in their shoes at this time. The boys of Jacob didn't want to intermix with the people of Shechem."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They seem to keep their end of the deal in hopes that Jacob's people will keep their end of the deal."}]}]},{"start":25,"end":31,"title":"Jacob's Sons Murder","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is crazy! This is not okay either. I do feel they were avenging their sister in this act, but they had already given them pain without the intent of giving their sister over. Jacob could have just said (from the beginning), \"No. I will not give my daughter over to be married to your son.\" Instead, he let his sons take the course, and they took it quite far even murdering people who were not involved with this act of injustice. Jacob (I believe at this point) should have known to turn to the Lord in this moment and ask for guidance. Jacob doesn't seem to do that, though, and so now there's a bigger headache due to his children's acts."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"All of Jacob's sons came and helped rob the city of their belongings. This is quite the ordeal, and it just seems to be getting bigger and bigger as each verse passes. None of this is okay, no matter how you twist it. Killing the men was not okay. Raping the women was not okay. Plundering the city was not okay. Not even the forceful circumcision was okay. This is just one big ball of wrong."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now they are taking the widows of the land and using them as slaves... They are making it worse! There is no Godly image pictured in this entire chapter. It seems (if I may) that God is not in this chapter. It's a historical one, albeit, but the Lord seems to be quite absent here. There is usually a glimmer of hope in each chapter, but this chapter is a dark one."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now Jacob is speaking the truth. But the bigger problem here was not the fact that his boys ruined \"Jacob's name.\" Rather, the bigger problem is that these boys had no remorse over the killing that just took place. They just got finished killing a lot of men, and Jacob doesn't seem to mention that at all. He doesn't address his sons’ sins. He only thinks about what this will do to his own reputation. It's a difficult position to be in (I'll give Jacob that), but he has a God who can help him make the right decisions. That is a very important fact for us to consider today as well. We don't have to worry about what the right choice is in our lives when we can go to the Lord and to His Word to get the mind of God and stand firm in the decision of the truth. He doesn’t have scripture to help him, but he DOES have the literal voice of God to depend on."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now... from what I've gathered from this conversation in this chapter, it seems this man was willing to change course and marry their sister. That's a pretty good thing to consider here. Yes, what he did at the beginning was wrong. No, it should not have just been forgiven and forgotten, but it certainly should have been a tool to utilize when addressing this man and his family. They could have seen genuine change in the land, and instead they made the situation much bigger and much worse. The Lord is the avenger, mind you. They should not have taken that upon themselves. That is their reward now. They don't get to see the Lord's moving on this matter."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is the daughter of Jacob, mind you. Whatever came next, the Lord would have His hand upon it. They didn't give the Lord that change, however, and now they will suffer the consequences of their own actions."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Dearly beloved, avenge not yourselves, but rather give place unto wrath: for it is written, Vengeance is mine; I will repay, saith the Lord. Therefore if thine enemy hunger, feed him; if he thirst, give him drink: for in so doing thou shalt heap coals of fire on his head. Be not overcome of evil, but overcome evil with good.","ref":"Romans 12:19-21"},{"kind":"para","text":"None of us (being children of God) should be in the business of taking vengeance in our own hands. I know the struggle of this here (it's so much easier to get even ourselves, and it's instantly gratifying because we get to see evil being taken care of with our own eyes), but we should give place to the Lord to take care of the situation. He WILL take care of it; you can take that to the bank."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":31}]},
    "35": {"chapter":35,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we saw some terrible circumstances befall Jacob's daughter Dinah. She is mistreated, and it leads to some actions taking place that were not any better than Shechem's actions with Dinah. Jacob's sons deceive these people, and while they are trying to earn favor with Jacob's family, two of Jacob's sons go in and slaughter all the men there. The sins of these boys is something that will be dealt with a little later on, but it's good to note these terrible deeds (and before you say that God justified their actions, you might want to hold off until you read this chapter, because they do things that God doesn't speak on again, and it is obvious that God takes notice, and will address these things differently later)."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see this family move on from the land they are currently in. They are to journey to Bethel and make it their homeland. That is where the children of Jacob are to grow and begin to prosper. This chapter is a bit of a rollercoaster emotionally, and there is a lot of beginnings and conclusions that take place."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"Moving On","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, I'm not Jacob, but I believe if I were experiencing the things that were happening to him, I would have moved out of there as quickly as possible. It seems Jacob didn't have any idea about leaving, so God had to speak to him and get him to move. Bethel is where he was supposed to go to begin with anyway. This stop, here, was a very bad mistake, and there was no changing course after that. So God tells him to go to Bethel and make an altar, because that is the place in which God is going to give him to dwell and raise his family."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't think Jacob knew that his wife had her old idols with her before, but now it seems they are placed on the mantle of the home, and he is going to deal with it. He says, \"I don't want these in the home. Put these strange things somewhere else because we are going to go worship the real God.\" Sometimes we all have to come to a point in our life where we look around our homes and hearts and say, \"I think we need to clean up and get rid of some things.\" We often let things casually build up for a long season, and eventually, it becomes a real problem because we have left it unchecked."},{"kind":"para","text":"I had a conversation with a friend about nativity scenes in churches and homes. He believes that they are a sign of idolatry. I don't necessarily disagree with him because there are religious households who would treat these little scenes as such. There are households who have different pictures of angels and pendants hanging from their walls as well. That is all idolatry at its core. I don't really stand to believe that a nativity scene is idolatry in a born-again Christian’s home. Christians have some level of freedom far and above the religionists because we know how to control and avoid abusing our liberties for the Lord. With that said, I do believe there are times in every single Christian’s life where we must do a self-evaluation. We must check the things we are using and see if they have become a problem. If they have, we should do some spring cleaning. We have to get rid of some grudges, or some \"pleasures,\" or some currencies and trade them in for contentment in the Lord. I have no doubt that every single person reading this today has some form of idolatry lingering in their minds and hearts, and it's wise for every single one of us to forsake and repent of those things from season to season."},{"kind":"para","text":"This family is changing course, and I believe the events that took place in the last chapter helped to make that decision very clear. These fake gods weren't providing any security for this family. It would have to be the true God of the universe that would protect them, and that ALONE should be all they put their hope and trust in."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I suppose hiding them is one way of getting rid of them. However, if you have a sin today, you cannot just simply put it to the side to get back later. The wise thing for Jacob to do in this instance was to completely destroy these idols. Not just put them out of sight, but make sure they could never be picked up again. Hopefully, nobody later on finds them and uses them."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I assume that these people were not very friendly people. The Lord seems to have protected Jacob and his family as they were traveling through the land, because something made them turn away from attacking or pursuing after Jacob. I also assume that Jacob still had a lot of wealth that he was just carrying around, and these people likely saw it all. They would (in normal circumstances) be envious of what Jacob had, and would have likely tried to rob him. However, nobody seems to lift a finger, and I don't even know if they were sure why they didn't want to attack. They just had this fear come over them, and they knew that it would be an unwise thing to try anything funny against Jacob or his family."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":8,"title":"Some Sad News","entries":[{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Luz\" means \"almond tree\" in case you don't remember. This is not the name that it remains as. Jacob changes this name to Bethel because that really is the true name of it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he called the name of that place Bethel: but the name of that city was called Luz at the first.","ref":"Genesis 28:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"He changed the name a little over twenty years prior to this particular time, and he was allowed to do that because God promised him the land. We will see that the land's name has a bit of an identity crisis as we continue to read through Scripture. It isn't until the book of Judges where the name is finally lifted from Luz, and just referred to as Bethel."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Elbethel\" simply means \"The God of the House of God.\" So Bethel is the house of God, and Elbethel refers to the God of that house. If you want to question whether or not God was truly in this place, you would simply have to question Jacob. He is making it quite clear that He sees God here, and that God was evidently working in this place."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a tragic scene, no doubt. Rebekah seems to have had a nurse named Deborah, and we are seeing this nurse die. We aren't sure where Rebekah is. Strangely, we don't get any more news about Rebekah after this. It's likely safe to say that Rebekah has passed away, and now that Jacob was so close by, Deborah went (perhaps) to let Jacob know what happened to his mother. That could not have been a very warming thought to Jacob. He is in this mode of worship towards the Lord, and he gets some very sad news."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you don't recall, Jacob was very close to his mother, and it has been over twenty years since the last time he's seen her. He was close enough now to go visit her, but it was already too late. Deborah seems to hold onto her life for as long as possible in anticipation of telling Jacob (maybe that was Rebekah's request to her before her passing), and she finally gets to reveal the news. She's completed her task, and so she can let go of her life and be with the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Allonbachuth\" means \"Oak of weeping.\" So very near to the house of God, there is a place of weeping. That certainly is an allegory for our churches today. I could argue that as soon as you leave the church doors, you are entering into a place of weeping. This whole world is crying and searching for peace, that can only be found in the house of God. The real spiritual work happens there. It seems that Jacob is just being beaten up spiritually and emotionally at this moment. His daughter is abused. His sons are murderers. His mother has passed away. He is living life quite quickly."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":15,"title":"An Accepted Promise","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a good correlation to the fact that the angel in which Jacob fought before was (in fact) the Lord. God makes it clear to Jacob that the fight for a blessing really did occur, and the change of name was a legitimate thing. Jacob was going to take that seriously now, and his name would be changed to Israel. And it will be the image of these people for the rest of their lives (and even still today). The Israelites are still wrestling with God, but aren't we all?"}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The children that Jacob had will not be kings, but they WILL BE tribes. They will be a great multitude of nations, that is for sure. But the kings come much later on, and that promise is seen throughout scripture as we get to know the people of Israel far deeper. The promise of the land will be kept as well, and we will see the land begin to grow over time. Everything in scripture takes time. It takes many years. I believe it takes so long because of our lack of faith, but we know that God will keep His Word, even after the end of the world."},{"kind":"para","text":"At this time, however, this was all quite overwhelming promises, and I don't think Jacob grasped the length of it. I mean, how could he? Being promised nations, and kings, and lands in abundance sounds like an amazing thing, but where's the end of it? How much of it will I actually get to see? Will they be huge? What kind of kings will they be? There is no kingship in this family yet, and Jacob doesn't seem to question any of that, but I know that this was certainly a lot of information being given to him."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He commemorates this place (officially), and it is now Bethel, without question. Other nations might think they have named it correctly, and they might struggle with its new name, but Israel will know this land as Bethel, and Jacob has officiated it. The interesting part here is the drink offering. We have read about several offerings already, but we have not mentioned a \"drink\" offering. What is a drink offering? Well, we don't get much more than this picture here. If we were to liken it to today, I would suppose it to symbolize our life. That is perhaps what it was symbolizing to Jacob in this moment. He is pouring his life out for the Lord in this place and on the testimony that was given here. God made the promise, and Jacob was pouring out everything he had on that promise alone. We should be viewing this life in the same light. In fact, Paul speaks about his life's desire to pour out everything he has on the gospel of Christ."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Yea, and if I be offered upon the sacrifice and service of your faith, I joy, and rejoice with you all.","ref":"Philippians 2:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul is saying he wants to be poured out/\"offered upon\" (that is on top of) the churches’ faith and service. He says he finds joy in being poured out. Every child of God should have a desire to pour out upon the faith and promises of others. I rarely see that today. I see people wanting to be poured into, but rarely do I find Christians willing to pour out. Perhaps this is why a \"drink offering\" is so rare in Scripture as well."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":20,"title":"Benjamin","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ephrath\" means \"Ash-heap.\" I'm not sure this is a very good place to be. It will be a bit sad here... It seems that Rachel was expecting a child. It's an odd bit of detail because it's not mentioned before that she was pregnant. That's great news, because she was about to have her second son."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There is no way to mentally prepare us for this situation. I don't believe Jacob was mentally prepared for this either. So, as a quick recap: His daughter is raped. His sons are killers. His mother (and her helper) have passed. He's received great promises from the Lord. He has another (and final) son. And his wife has now died. This all happens in a fairly short period of time."},{"kind":"para","text":"Rachel wanted to call this son \"Benoni.\" \"Benoni\" means \"Son of my sorrow.\" That is a terrible way to leave this Earth. Jacob would look at his newborn son and instead of thinking of how much he loved his wife, he would think of the pain and sorrow she faced in her last moments giving birth to him because of this name. So, he changes his name. That was not how he wanted to memorialize his late wife. \"Benjamin\" means \"Son of the right hand.\" We will see that Jacob takes special care of the sons of Rachel. We will also see that these two sons (Joseph and Benjamin) will play a very prominent role in the rest of this book of the Bible. Benjamin was not a replacement for Rachel, but he was certainly a reminder of the love he had for his wife. Benjamin (in the eyes of Jacob) was Rachel's departing gift to him."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So this \"ash-heap\" becomes a very prominent place in Scripture. \"Bethlehem\" means \"House of bread.\" It will be a useful place to memorize because it will be brought up over and over again. It will be a place that the Lord uses quite mightily. It is supposed that you can still visit her grave spot today in that same place, and it's labelled and assured that this scene is accurate based on physical evidence, and not just scriptural or historical ones."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We must remember that Moses is the one writing this, so when it says \"unto this day\" it is speaking about the day of Moses. But I guess it's still there today, and it's plainly marked out so there's no mistaking whose grave it is. You would have to visit Bethlehem to find out."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":26,"title":"The Twelve Tribes","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are finally seeing a shift in Jacob's name. It might get a little confusing in the future, but when \"Israel\" is mentioned in this book, it is speaking about Jacob. In the other books, it is speaking about the Israelites (that is the lineage of Jacob). God changed his name, though, and that is the reason we have the nation of Israel today."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Wait, let’s not ignore this sin here... Reuben did something quite serious in this moment, and Israel (that is Jacob) knows about it. What does Israel do? Well, it doesn't seem as if he does much of anything. He doesn't seem to learn his lesson from before. I find it kind of strange that it is sweeping over this information here, but this is a very sad and terrible curse that Reuben is bringing upon himself. Scripture has deemed it worthy to mention it but doesn't give any further explanation or detail about it. That will actually happen quite a bit as we read (it happened with Noah as well in Genesis 9:20)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Then there are other accounts like with Lot and his daughters that give pretty apt detail about the situation. I find it quite odd that there is no more reference to this sin, and we are not told what becomes of it, or how often it happens, or if it was really even an issue. Obviously, this is (of course) an issue, and God will make mention of how wrong this is over and over again as we read through scripture, but it was an issue to have a concubine in the first place. This, however, brings it to a whole different level. It, if anything, showcases how deep the sin was in this family."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So here’s a recap of the family. Leah has six sons. These children are ones that will be quite relevant through scripture (specifically Levi and Judah)."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Rachel has two sons. Both of these will be a real focal of scripture."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Bilhah has two sons."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Zilpah has two sons. These (together) make up the twelve tribes of Israel. All the Israelites hearing or reading this for the first time as Moses is giving it know exactly which tribe they stem from. There are people still today who are able to trace their lineage back to this point. I don't see the use in that, but I find it fascinating to say the least."}]}]},{"start":27,"end":29,"title":"Isaac's Death","entries":[{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Isaac was close to Esau, but not Jacob. That, however, doesn't mean that Isaac didn't love Jacob. He still cared for his son. Isaac had a chance to visit with his boy again after so long apart, and see a little bit of the promise being kept to him."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Esau and Jacob were able to attend their father’s funeral. Jacob didn't get to attend his mother's funeral, but at least he (perhaps) got to hear stories of how things were after he had left. He got to catch up with his father, and I'm sure his father was glad to see (though he was mostly blind) that Jacob grew up to be a man of faith rather than a man of fear. The last time Isaac “saw” Jacob, he was deceitful and weak. Now he sees him, and everything in Jacob's life has changed (including his faith walk with the Lord). Esau doesn't really have that same walk with God, and that will be evident as we continue to read."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":29}]},
    "36": {"chapter":36,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see Jacob receive the call to move on from his currently place of dwelling, and go to the place of Bethel, which is where God originally tells him he belongs. While settling in, and finding places to build, gets news of his mother's passing. God reminds Jacob of His promise and blessing, and Jacob continues to anticipate it. He begins to build and continues his journey when his wife (Rachel) suddenly gives birth. In the process of labor, she ends up dying, and names her child Benoni, but Jacob changes it to Benjamin. We are then confirmed as to who the twelve tribes of Jacob are (as Benjamin is his last child), and he goes to visit his father. At the end of the chapter, we leave off with Jacob and Esau having a funeral for their father."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we focus namely on the family line of Esau. This is somewhat of a rare occurance in scripture, because there isn't too much of a reason to focus on anyone outside of the promise, other than to bring some clarity as to why they are here to begin with. That is really what this chapter is, and it can be a tad bit confusing to us reading today. I want to do my best to clarify, but there isn't too much information I can give, because we are given more information about the legacy of the Israelites."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":8,"title":"Esau's Family","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have been getting a lot of information on the formation of Israel (which is the focal point of Old Testament scripture, and something that is brought up numerous times in the New Testament as well), but we rarely get a glimpse into the other parts of the land. If we want to do a deeper dive into the culture around Israel at this time, we would have to read between the lines a little. We are assured that the world is full of people who are out to do their own thing. We've already seen the world fall away from the Lord over and over again (hence the reason there was a flood during the time of Noah), and we will continue to see that take place throughout scripture. Scripture is giving us some insight into Esau this chapter, and into the type of people Edom becomes."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So we have an idea of the kind of man Esau was. In those days, mixing with the culture around them was in poor taste. It meant they were not staying in the realm of inheritance. This is a good reason why Esau doesn't receive the blessings that Jacob receives (other than the fact that Jacob stole the blessings). But even if Jacob didn't steal them, the blessings wouldn't be on Esau."},{"kind":"para","text":"Notice none of the women he marries is from the Abrahamic lineage through the line of Shem. They come from the lineage of Ham, who Noah was specific regarding the promise of the land. \"Adah\" means \"ornament\" \"Aholibamah\" means \"tent of the high place.\" Perhaps these women were fine for Esau. That was not the desire of his family, of course. Then we have Ishmael's daughter Bashemath. We were told about the other two, but I don't think we ever had the chance to hear about Bashemath (and her relationship with Esau) until now. \"Bashemath\" means “spice.\" That was Esau's desire all this time, I think. He desired to put some \"spice\" into his family lineage, and he certainly does (in ways that his family would not approve of)."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Eliphaz\" means \"My God is fine gold.\" The Lord is even better than fine gold. I am under the impression that it is not speaking about the biblical God here. It is likely (from what I understand about the Canaanites) that they literally worshipped gold as their god. \"Reuel\" means \"friend of God.\" I might be a little biased and harsh on some of these names, but again we don't get too much hope with the Canaanites. Perhaps there was a time when they were actually seeking after the Lord. Perhaps they started off with a rightful view of God. Perhaps they had a head-knowledge of the Lord, but they never had a faith knowledge of Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"We see, over time, that they will eventually be so far gone from the Lord that there is no salvation given to them. It will take (as we've stated before) four hundred years for them to get their act together, and they just simply won't. So maybe Reuel actually was named with the idea that God could be as close to us as a friend could be. Let's look at her family lineage really quick... \"Anah\" means \"answer.\" Perhaps she was an answered prayer to Zibeon? \"Zibeon\" means \"coloured.\" I assume her skin may have been a little bit different. Either way, we seem to start this talk about being God's friend with Reuel, and I assume that is only because they thought of their own gods as very personal. This likely has nothing to do with the God of scripture."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now we know for certain where Aholibamah comes from, so we are assured that it is not under the godly lineage either. \"Jeush\" means \"Assembler.\" \"Jaalam\" means \"concealed.\" \"Korah\" means \"bald.\" Interestingly enough, we get the notion that Esau was a hairy red man, and he has a son who is assumed to be bald. It seems that Esau doesn't have nearly as many children as Jacob does. Five children is still a decent amount, however."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Esau and Jacob can't seem to live together because they each had too much stuff for the amount of land they needed. This same thing happened to Abraham and Lot, if you don't remember, and it caused them to split up. Lot ended up leaving the promised land, and it got him into some deep and serious waters. Now it has forced Esau to have to leave the promised land as well. He doesn't want to have any sort of clashing with his brother anymore than has already been had, so he decides that it's best if they just move on from there and find \"greener\" pastures."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In case you haven't gotten it clear yet, Esau is Edom. Why is that important? Well, that's because Edom will be a very important place to consider in the future. Right now, we see Esau starting this country, and he is going to prosper this country, and so when we come to the Edomites later on, we can remember (who is Edom?) Esau is Edom. It is by Esau that we have this lineage, and it will be by his doing that we see their direction."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":14,"title":"Continuance of Edom","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So we already have had a discussion about this, but the author (likely Moses) wants to make it clear so there is no misunderstanding. But he then continues the line (perhaps for the Israelites to reflect on how they got to this point). Here is how the lineages come about, and how accurate the information Moses has of their family lives after Edom is started (which is happening at this moment in scripture when Esau leaves the land)."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Teman\" means \"South.\" Maybe he spoke with a drawl? \"Omar\" means \"Speaker\" or \"eloquent.\" There was no southern drawl from Omar. He was likely a great speaker. \"Zepho\" means \"Watch-tower.\" \"Gatam\" means \"a burnt valley.\" \"Kenaz\" means \"hunter.\" These last three names don't leave much to the imagination. Their missions in life were pretty well understood at their birth. One would be a great gate-keeper. The other would be a great hunter. But the one in the middle... That doesn't seem promising."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Amalek\" means \"dweller in a valley.\" We will see that the Amalekites will live up to that name absolutely. They will not desire to move out of their comfort zone in their little valley that they think is far more valuable than anything else. This will be a name to remember as we will see in the future chapters of scripture."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he gathered an host, and smote the Amalekites, and delivered Israel out of the hands of them that spoiled them.","ref":"1 Samuel 14:48"},{"kind":"para","text":"The reason for this slaughter is found in the book of Numbers. The Amalekites were not a friendly people to the Israelites."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Nahath\" means \"Rest.\" That sounds like a peaceful name. That is also what Noah's name meant. It meant rest. They had a time of rest during the days of Noah, but the rest of the world was under water and in great turmoil. What rest was happening in the days of \"Nahath?\" I'm not sure. Perhaps it was just rest because of the prosperity of his grandfather Esau. It is understood that they had a lot going for them (otherwise they wouldn't have had to move out of the land). They are starting to grow, and so there is great reason to rest in that comfort. It's a worldly comfort, but this is a worldly lineage."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Zerah\" means \"rising.\" \"Shammah\" means \"astonishment.\" \"Mizzah\" means \"fear.\" Weird dynamic here. We see Reuel finds rest in the first son, \"rising\" in the second son (perhaps that's when more prosperity comes into this family?), astonishment in the third son (maybe this is a sign of amazement more than overwhelming burden), and then FEAR in the last son. What happened with Mizzah? I don't know? We are not told."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So we've already gotten this information. The other two wives (in the family of Esau) only had one son each. This last wife had three sons. I wouldn't go about assuming anything, but I have a suspicious reason to believe that Aholibamah was Esau's favorite. Just a hunch."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":19,"title":"The Royal Family","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Duke\" means \"chief.\" Why were these ones dukes? I don't know. But they took their title extremely seriously, and they have professed themselves as \"chiefs\" of the family in Edom. We don't see any other lineages taking that role in the land. I assume this is the first of its kind. It is a symbol of authority and prosperity. They were the richest in the land, and they are the chief among the inhabitants there. They were the royal family. Jesus has a word for the chiefs..."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And whosoever will be chief among you, let him be your servant:","ref":"Matthew 20:27"}]}]},{"start":20,"end":30,"title":"Another Lineage","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, here, we have some others coming in, and they are joining the family ranks. \"Seir\" means \"hairy.\" Seir (as we may well know) becomes a very significant and prominent place in scripture (especially for the Israelites in their wandering). This is the place they will go when they are let go from Pharaoh in Exodus. This mount Seir will be the place that the law is given to the Israelites. Notice the law is given to them in the land of Edom? They are given the law OUTSIDE of the promised land. There is a lot of significance to that, but I believe it's best saved for Exodus."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Lotan\" means \"Covering.\" \"Shobal\" means \"Flowing.\" \"Zibeon\" means “Colored.\" \"Anah\" means “Answer.\" \"Dishon\" means \"Thresher.\" \"Ezer\" means \"Treasure.\" \"Dishan\" means \"Thresher.\" Dishon and Dishan are two different people, but their names are closely related. It seems there was a lot of threshing in this family. Now we have a different section of dukes. The land was growing enough to have two families of dukes. We will see it grow even more now from here (as they will all have children)."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I want to make it clear (as some might find it weary to go through every name and define them, mistaking the purpose of this message here) that these names are in scripture for a purpose. They are here to define the familial destination (what direction they are going is strongly urged by the names they choose for their children), but also because Moses makes it clear that God allowed these lineages for a purpose. This land is growing, and it would be evident (to the children of Israel standing outside of the promised land reading this) that God placed them all here to direct them to this very moment."},{"kind":"para","text":"It should be understood to us as well who are reading this. We have been sovereignly directed from the Lord. Whether by unbelievers, evil people, kind people, or believers themselves, God has placed each individual person here to guide every single one of us to Him. Ultimately, our leading through this life will always lead to one event in history's future, and that is the second coming of Christ. So before we get too weary at the fact that names are being defined, I think it's important that we reevaluate the purpose of it and the understanding that God has these names here for our information, as well as our understanding of their direction (because we will see in the later chapters that they are directed by these families specifically, and that makes a very big difference). \"Hori\" means \"Cave dweller.\" \"Hemam\" means \"exterminating.\" \"Timna\" means \"restrained.\""}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Alvan\" means \"tall.\" I wonder if he had any chipmunks? (That's a joke, of course...) \"Manahath\" means \"Rest.\" Here, we have a new rest. We have a new \"era\" of rest. Perhaps he provided rest to his family, or perhaps he provides rest to the city. Either way, there was some sort of rest found in him. The rest that is spoken about here, however, is not an eternal one. Noah's name was focused on the rest that God provides. These names (here) are focused on the rest they have in the world. Very different \"rests.\""},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Come unto me, all ye that labour and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest. Take my yoke upon you, and learn of me; for I am meek and lowly in heart: and ye shall find rest unto your souls.","ref":"Matthew 11:28-29"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ebal\" means \"Stone.\" Ebal will have his own mount as well. It will be opposite Mount Gerizim, where the curses and blessings will be placed. Mount Ebal will be where the cursings are displayed, and Mount Gerizim will be where the blessings are displayed. Whether or not the mount is named after this man or not isn't clear, but it is certainly a name providing strength. Cephas' name is “Peter,\" which means \"stone.\" Jesus is the \"stone\" foundation of our faith. These are all things better spoken about in the gospel. Ebal means “stone,\" though, and it is likely that he was a man of some physical strength. \"Shepho\" means \"bold.\" \"Onam\" means \"vigorous.\""}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps this is where we get the connection to the dukes in the line of Esau. They were in the land already? Maybe that is why Esau marries Aholibamah. She was a child of royalty. Or perhaps they started becoming royalty when Esau moved into that land later on. Either way, we get a good look at the family lineage here. \"Ajah\" means \"Falcon.\" \"Anah\" means \"Answer.\" A lot of answers happening in this time. Anah might have answered a very important question to his family and his people, which was likely \"Where are we going to get water?\""},{"kind":"para","text":"In case you didn't know, this was the same Anah that found the mules in the wilderness. What mules? What wilderness? Where was this mentioned the first time? Why does this matter? Well, it seems like a random bit of information, doesn't it? This is the same wilderness (however) that the Israelites will be wandering in. And it will be the same people that find the Israelites in the wilderness. And (if I may) at the time they are wandering, they are acting just like mules (donkeys rather). I don't know what part of the story these \"mules in the wilderness\" is discussing here (I don't remember reading about this situation, so it seems like an odd bit of detail here), but it certainly fits to later parts of scripture..."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, we are likely to take this definition more from the original text. \"Mules\" (here) comes from the Hebrew word which means, \"hot springs.\" I found it odd that it would use the term \"mules\" and then \"asses\" directly after. In fact, there are no other places found in scripture where the term \"mules\" is used. They are always referred to as \"asses.\" If he was feeding the mules, why does it matter if he found the mules? I've spoken to some who have said that we don't need to read the original Hebrew or Greek to get an accurate translation because the KJV is superiorly translated, but then you come across some sources like this that are only empowered by studying the original word. If we are not careful, we can get the wrong idea and context from reading God's Word because our old English friends have had a hard time playing the game of telephone."},{"kind":"para","text":"I want to recognize that this word also means \"mule.\" They weren't wrong translating it this way. But they also muddied the waters by not being more direct with what they are talking about. This man found water in the wilderness. That might have been a bit of a difficult thing to find back then, but he found it, and it was a rejoicing and useful bit of information to the reader today (and to those reading back then). Actually, we see (in the wilderness wanderings) that the Israelites complained (a lot but more specifically) about not having any water."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And all the congregation of the children of Israel journeyed from the wilderness of Sin, after their journeys, according to the commandment of the LORD, and pitched in Rephidim: and there was no water for the people to drink. Wherefore the people did chide with Moses, and said, Give us water that we may drink. And Moses said unto them, Why chide ye with me? wherefore do ye tempt the LORD? And the people thirsted there for water; and the people murmured against Moses, and said, Wherefore is this that thou hast brought us up out of Egypt, to kill us and our children and our cattle with thirst? And Moses cried unto the LORD, saying, What shall I do unto this people? they be almost ready to stone me. And the LORD said unto Moses, Go on before the people, and take with thee of the elders of Israel; and thy rod, wherewith thou smotest the river, take in thine hand, and go. Behold, I will stand before thee there upon the rock in Horeb; and thou shalt smite the rock, and there shall come water out of it, that the people may drink. And Moses did so in the sight of the elders of Israel.","ref":"Exodus 17:1-6"},{"kind":"para","text":"Interestingly enough, God gives them a source of water from a rock. There must have been some water around, somewhere, they just didn't want to look. Either because they are too tired or they are too lazy, but either way, the Lord provides for them, and these springs found in this chapter are another indication (to the reader and to the Israelites anticipating the land) that God had been planning and setting up for future moments since the beginning of time."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We've mentioned this already, but \"Dishon\" means \"Thresher.\" And again, \"Aholibamah\" means \"Tent of the high place.\" The tie-in to Esau starts at Aholibamah. I assume that's how he becomes so prominent in this land. She was already tied-in to some very prominent figures of the land coming from the family line of Seir. We already have the lineage of Aholibamah (through Esau), so it won't mention that again."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Hemdan\" means \"Desire.\" \"Eshban\" means \"fire of discernment.\" \"Ithran\" means \"advantage.\" \"Cheran\" means \"lyre.\" These seem more like names placed on the children by personal experience. Dishon desired a child, then was put through a trial during a very prominent decision, took advantage of a moment (or of a person, you never know in those days), and then played some music? I don't know. Maybe the last one doesn't have anything to do with him more than his love for the lyre."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Bilhan\" means \"their decrepitude.\" “Decrepitude\" means \"elderly and infirm.\" Who Ezer is talking about here, I don't know. Maybe he had this child while his wife was old (or perhaps he himself was old)? Or maybe he is talking about someone close to him? I don't really know, and perhaps we will never know. \"Zaavan\" means \"troubled.\" That's not a good-sounding name for a child. \"Akan\" means \"sharp-sighted.\""}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Uz\" means \"Wooded.\" Another place that is talked about quite a bit in scripture. Specifically where Job lived."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"There was a man in the land of Uz, whose name was Job; and that man was perfect and upright, and one that feared God, and eschewed evil.","ref":"Job 1:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Aran\" means \"joyous.\" That's the first normal, and most flattering name we've heard so far."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So these people were able to pass their royalty along to the next people. Ultimately, this leads them to become kings, as displayed in the next verse."}]}]},{"start":31,"end":39,"title":"The Kings of Edom","entries":[{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Edomites have kings, and it is how they settled their disputes and made their decisions. They had rulers over them to dictate how the nation would be run. The Israelites weren't supposed to be that way. I find it fascinating that this is written here, because if it's Moses writing this, he would not have had to write about the children of Israel having kings over them. It says, \"before there reigned any king over the children of Israel.\" The first king of Israel comes in 1 Samuel. Moses wasn't alive past Deuteronomy. We have about four hundred years between Deuteronomy and 1 Samuel. So this is either an indication that Moses didn't add this note, OR (and more likely) the Lord led Moses to understand that there would be a time that Israel would have a king. Actually, Moses prophecies this in Deuteronomy!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Thou shalt in any wise set him king over thee, whom the LORD thy God shall choose: one from among thy brethren shalt thou set king over thee: thou mayest not set a stranger over thee, which is not thy brother.","ref":"Deuteronomy 17:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"So it's entirely possible that Moses gives us ANOTHER prophecy right here in Genesis. The Israelites reading this at the border of the promised land (or in their wilderness trek) would think, \"What does he mean by that? We don't have a king. We follow the Lord!\" But Moses knows what they will do, and how they will reject God. He calls them out on it, in fact, throughout most of Deuteronomy."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Dinhabah\" means \"Give thou judgement.\" Now you might be thinking, “Well, of course, Bela reigned. Who else was going to take that mantle?\" If you are thinking that, then you are far smarter than me. I have no idea who Beor is. Where did Bela come from? Why is this listed here like it's just a nonchalant additive to the names that have already been given? Moses acts like he had already made mention of Bela before. \"Beor\" means \"burning.\" \"Bela\" means \"Destruction.\" Some have speculated that this Bela is the same as \"Balaam\" mentioned later in scripture."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He sent messengers therefore unto Balaam the son of Beor to Pethor, which is by the river of the land of the children of his people, to call him, saying, Behold, there is a people come out from Egypt: behold, they cover the face of the earth, and they abide over against me:","ref":"Numbers 22:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"Whether these are the same people or not, I couldn't tell you. It's possible that Moses is mentioning the future rule of Balaam (whom they had to face in the wilderness) for the purpose of clarity later on, but it doesn't really give an explanation as to where he came from. We know (in this chapter) that Beor was living in Edom, and somehow his son comes to power as a king. It's possible that Bela just took power and possession at some point, and he wasn't a part of the royal lineage depicted above. Balaam is seen as a man that is \"hired\" by the king of Moab to curse Israel in the book of Numbers. Instead, he ends up blessing the Israelites, because the Lord used Balaam's own desire against him. The Lord protects the Israelites. However, there are some stipulations that come into play, and Balaam eventually uses the Israelites’ own free will to get them to sin against the Lord (this story is best saved for Numbers)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Do I believe these are the same people? No. I personally don't think so. I don't have a way to back up one idea or another. It seems to me that this Bela figure became a king. As far as I know, Balaam never became a king. He reigned in this city named \"give thou judgement\" and he is not made mention of again."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jobab\" means \"a desert.\" And of course, Jobab takes the throne. Why wouldn't he? Everyone loves Jobab. Except, again, we aren't told where he comes from... \"Zerah\" means \"Rising.\" At least we are given a location from Zerah. \"Bozrah\" means \"Sheepfold.\" Where is Bozrah? Again, I don't know. We are just told that it is in Edom."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Husham\" means \"Haste.\" He's from the land of Temani. \"Temani\" means \"southward.\" I assume this place was in Edom towards the south side."}]},{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Where is this coming from? Again, I don't know. The Bible has left this bit of information out (except for right here). I have read in an Apocryphal text that this event took place. In Jasher 62:8-9 it says, \"And Hadad came unto the land of Moab, and Moab and the children of Midian went out to meet him, and they placed themselves in battle array against him in the field of Moab. And Hadad fought with Moab, and there fell of the children of Moab and the children of Midian many slain ones, about two hundred thousand men.\" Our scripture, however, doesn't clarify this scene. I cannot hold to the account given by the Apocrypha, because there are a lot of wrongful portions of it that don't align with the legitimacy of the canonical Bible, nor does it apply to the teachings and work of Jesus Christ. That is really the ultimate reason that it is likely not included in our text today."},{"kind":"para","text":"Scripture doesn't clarify these cases very deeply because this is not the chosen lineage. This lineage is not what God's Word is focused on. What God's Word is focused on is the lineage of Israel, and the coming of the Savior Jesus Christ. These other tribes and events took place, and it is left here for us to know that they certainly did happen, but they are not the focal point of scripture. We can get lost in trying to find every nuance and detail that scripture has left out, and then completely forget the message of the Bible regarding Christ’s sacrifice for the world. THAT is the focus. If scripture doesn't focus on that, then it is not canonical. That is why the Apocrypha is not included in scripture today. It doesn't focus on Jesus' crucifixion, resurrection, and completed work on the cross."}]},{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Samlah\" means \"garment.\" \"Masrekah\" means \"vineyard of noble vines.\""}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Saul\" means \"desired.\" That is what Israel will eventually desire. They will desire a king, and Saul will be a king given to them. Not this Saul, of course. \"Rehoboth\" means \"wide places.\""}]},{"start":38,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Baalhanan\" means \"Baal is gracious.\" We are seeing a shifting of the culture at this point into false worship. I believe the false worship was always there, but they started to worship a fake god whose name is \"Baal.\" \"Baal\" means \"lord.\" It's not the same Lord as the Israelites, however, and even the Israelites got tricked into worshipping this false god. But we can see this land of Edom slowly rotting away into pagan practices because they weren't the chosen lineage. They lived like they weren't the chosen lineage as well, which is why God rejected them to begin with."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have no doubt that God was providing for them behind the scenes, and yet they had found reason to take glory away from the Lord and keep from accepting Him. This is why God calls out the Israelites for His people. They will fall, over and over again, but they always come back to worshipping the Lord. It is the same as why He has called you today if you know Christ. He knows you will fall, but He also knows you will always come back, and He is ready and waiting to accept you."}]},{"start":39,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Hadar\" means \"honour.\" \"Pau\" means \"bleating.\" \"Mehetabel\" means \"favoured of God.\" Again, probably not the same God as the Israelites are worshipping. \"Matred\" means “pushing forward.\" \"Mezahab\" means \"waters of gold.”"}]}]},{"start":40,"end":43,"title":"An Obvious Leading","entries":[{"start":43,"end":43,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, let's not forget Esau. And let's not forget that Esau is what? He is Edom. This lineage account is given here for the sole purpose of the Israelites understanding that the people in the land around them have been set up here specifically for the moment they were currently in. They were there to get them to their destination, and to prove that God is watching and paying attention. They were set in place to move the Israelites a certain way. God knows (right here, in this chapter, as Esau is taking the land and developing a name for himself) that the Israelites will rebel against Him, and they will eventually have to face these people in their harshest state. He is building the faith of His people, but first He has to build up this land to prove His continual providence for His children. That's going to take some time still (four hundred years actually)."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":39},{"start":40,"end":43}]},
    "37": {"chapter":37,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter of Genesis, we took a little break from following Jacob and his family to look at the family and lineage of Esau and the growing kingdoms through his line. Edom was a populous nation, and it seemed to be getting bigger and bigger by the second. Moses recounts his lineage all the way up to the time of Moses (past the story of Jacob and Joseph). It could get a little confusing if we just glance over it and try not to trek with Moses in his account, but when we take our time to look a little deeper, we get a good idea about where they were, what they were doing, and how they get to their position later on."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we go back to Jacob and his sons. We are transitioned from Jacob (now) to Joseph. Joseph will be a very important (and key) member of the family of Israel, and it will be his story that links up so much of the coming happenstances of Israel to the moment of them standing outside the promised land in Deuteronomy. We will see Joseph's story play a huge role throughout the rest of scripture, especially to the leading up to (and after) the crucifixion of the Messiah."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":4,"title":"The Favorite Child","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob has finally made a home for his family in the land that was promised to him. I think he knew where he belonged all along; he just took a while to finally get to it. Perhaps he was concerned about a few things in the land. At this time, the Canaanites were ruling the land. Maybe he was worried about the types of neighbors he was going to have. Or maybe he was just very slow to obeying God. Either way, he gets to where he's supposed to be, and now we are going to have a bit of a transition of characters. So far through Genesis, we have transitioned from person to person and focused on one account of the lineage (all leading up to Moses and the Israelites, of course). We saw Adam, leading to Noah, leading to Abraham, leading to Isaac, leading to Jacob, and now we are transitioning to Joseph, and he will be the focal of the remainder of this book."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph doesn't seem to really fit in with his brothers. Joseph is a teenager at this particular point in time, and he is bringing word to his father about the things his other brothers were doing. It was pretty obvious in the last few chapters that his brothers were troublemakers. Joseph and Benjamin were not. Benjamin was too young to help the boys out, and Joseph was too pure to follow in their antics and terrible lifestyles."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well... Actually... Benjamin really was the son of Jacob in his old age. But Joseph was the firstborn from Rachel, and that is likely why Jacob loved him more. But this is not a good family dynamic. This is literally what drew a wedge between him and his own brother."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Isaac loved Esau, because he did eat of his venison: but Rebekah loved Jacob.","ref":"Genesis 25:28"},{"kind":"para","text":"This caused some serious trouble for the boys in their lives because the parents would fight over rights for the children, and the children knew how to get what they wanted and get away with it. Now Jacob is passing down that frail line of thinking into his own kids, and he has THIRTEEN of them. This is a dangerous idea. Either way, he did it, and he made it very well known that Joseph was the favorite by making this \"coat of many colours.\" “Colours\" (here) means, \"of the tunic reaching to palms and soles.\" It was a coat that extended to his hands and feet. It was a big coat, that was blatantly set apart from any of the other clothes that anyone else would wear."},{"kind":"para","text":"In those days, their clothes didn't even have sleeves. This coat DOES. Their clothes back then were just one piece of cloth that they would drape over their heads (it had a hole in the middle), and they would tie it around their waists. This coat had sleeves sown into it. Perhaps it was colorful too, though I highly doubt it. To add color to the clothes ON TOP OF sowing sleeves and the sorts would be unheard of, but not impossible. Though, I think if it were colorful there would be a lot of people outside the family trying to rob him, because that would be a very expensive coat."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well, of course. This is the type of family dynamic that gets people into bitterness and competition with one another. I think there's already a competition in the family if I'm being honest. With my brother, I have always felt a bit of a competition for attention. As we got older, I think both of us stopped competing. It is quite obvious to me that each of our parents cares more about one child over the other, and I won't say any more than that."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":8,"title":"Joseph's Dreaming","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, if we exclude the circumstances of this dream, this just sounds like his brothers are cynical and ridiculous. Kids dream dreams all the time. To say that Joseph dreamed a dream, and his brothers hated him for having dreams sounds extremely absurd, but that is why we must have context when we read scripture. So here's the context:"}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Obeisance\" means \"to bow down,\" as if in an act of worship to Joseph's work. A sheaf was bound with grain (likely wheat). It is actually a great picture of the people of Israel. Israel will continuously be bound and carried away, and brought back again. This picture is a lot more than just Joseph, but it certainly is a personal thing that will take place in the future. These dreams are not something for us to forget."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph likely didn't understand why he had the dream, but he knew what the dream was symbolizing, and it was a truth that he was telling to his brothers. Obviously, his brothers didn't believe it. They didn't want to believe it because they hated him so much. That is (honestly) how the Christian feels. They are hated so much that no matter what kind of truth they give to the world around them, people will just simply not believe. I believe that Joseph could have had a very different dream of small meaning behind it, and his brothers still would have hated it. They hated the fact that he was dreaming at all. The world’s goal is to snuff out the dreams of a child of God."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":11,"title":"Joseph's Second Dream","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"His father actually fought against the second dream. He had some serious thoughts behind this. One dream of observing Joseph as royalty was enough, but TWO dreams is over the top. As we will learn (throughout the rest of Scripture), these dreams (and the dreams of others) are all used as a sign for things to come. They are prophecies given to these specific men for their own personal lives, but also for the future of the world as a whole. Every dream sequence we see in Scripture is a small view of a larger picture to the end times. It will all lead up to Christ's return, including THIS dream."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And there appeared a great wonder in heaven; a woman clothed with the sun, and the moon under her feet, and upon her head a crown of twelve stars:","ref":"Revelation 12:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"But this dream is also very personal to them as they will be (spoiler alert) bowing to Joseph by the end of this book of the Bible."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"His brothers didn't believe a word he said. They altogether gave up on his silly dreams and thought very little of it. His father (on the other hand) starts to pay attention. He knows there's more to it than Joseph simply having dreams for the fun of it. He probably suspects that the Lord is giving him this dream. Jacob (if you don't remember) has experience in this area."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he dreamed, and behold a ladder set up on the earth, and the top of it reached to heaven: and behold the angels of God ascending and descending on it. And, behold, the LORD stood above it, and said, I am the LORD God of Abraham thy father, and the God of Isaac: the land whereon thou liest, to thee will I give it, and to thy seed; And thy seed shall be as the dust of the earth, and thou shalt spread abroad to the west, and to the east, and to the north, and to the south: and in thee and in thy seed shall all the families of the earth be blessed. And, behold, I am with thee, and will keep thee in all places whither thou goest, and will bring thee again into this land; for I will not leave thee, until I have done that which I have spoken to thee of. And Jacob awaked out of his sleep, and he said, Surely the LORD is in this place; and I knew it not.","ref":"Genesis 28:12-16"}]}]},{"start":12,"end":14,"title":"Feed My Sheep","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If Jacob is living in Hebron at this particular point in time, that means Shechem is about forty miles away from their place of dwelling. That was a long way to travel to feed some sheep. I assume the rest of the field by where they lived was already grazed and gone over, and so they are waiting for it to grow back before they are able to feed the sheep there. There are some very useful tools for us today (even in this verse here)."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord tells His Apostles to feed His sheep several times over. Whether you like to be noticed as such or not, WE are the Lord's sheep. We are in need of feeding. We, however, are fed (just like these sheep here) by the most untrustworthy people in the world. We actually seek for anyone possible to feed us rather than look for someone trustworthy. These boys were not to be trusted in this work, but Jacob had no other choice but to give it to them to deal with. Jacob is old. If Jacob was old when he had Joseph, he's seventeen years older at this point, and he is extremely old now. So he trusts his sons to take care of this very important work, and I don't think his sons take it very seriously."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps Jacob knows that his sons can't be trusted in this task, and so he is questioning it in this moment. They are pretty far away. How is he going to know they are doing what they are supposed to be doing?"}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"His brothers have been gone for some time. Jacob thinks, \"Well, I have one son whom I know will tell me the truth. So I will send him to make sure the work is being done properly. Perhaps he can be a better example for his brothers.\" Joseph's job really was simple. He was to go there, see what they were doing, and then go back home and tell his father what had happened. That's not what is going to happen, though. This moment will be the last time for a very long time that Jacob will see his favorite son again."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":22,"title":"Finding His Brothers","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think Joseph was a bit frantic. He went to where he was supposed to be and couldn't find his brothers anywhere. Concern must have been on his face because this random stranger finds him and says, \"You alright? You need help finding something?\" Joseph was where he was supposed to be, but his brothers were not."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He hopes this man knew where his brothers were, because there was no way he would be able to find them on his own. He has the whole wide world before him and no direction about where his brothers decided to wander off to. He should have printed some directions from MapQuest first..."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Dothan is even further away than Shechem. Maybe they ran out of fields to graze in Shechem as well. I think this stranger paid very close attention to who was in this field quite a bit. Maybe he had future plans for it, I don't know. But the fact that he knew exactly where Jacob's brothers are gives a bit of a testament to the idea that this man was constantly watching and paying attention. He was listening in on their conversation and heard them say they were going to Dothan. That's great news for Joseph, but curious news to this family. Nothing actually becomes of this man, but he is an eyewitness to some of the events that have taken place. Perhaps (if I were him) I'd be asking who his father was and where he came from. But that isn't what happens, and Joseph goes on his way and finds them exactly where the stranger says they would be."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"His brothers hated him so much. Just the very sight of him stirred something up within themselves. Joseph is simply on his father’s business and nothing more. If I were Joseph, I would have seen my brothers and thought, \"Okay, they are here. I will go tell my father that is what I was told to do, and I will do no more than that.\" But as it seems, Joseph thought it was best to come and talk to them for a little bit. Maybe he was going to try and walk home with them so he wasn't walking all alone. His brothers conspired (however) to kill him."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is a lot of connection here to Christ that I hope we aren't missing. They ridiculed Christ, they mistreated Him, they sought to kill Him (eventually achieving that goal), they disregarded His sayings, they didn't believe his acclamations, they hated Him. These are all signs of the same thing in Joseph's life. Joseph is simply following orders from his father, and his brothers hate that. They hate that Jacob loves Joseph so much. The world hates that God loves Jesus so much. They hate those that trust in Him."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Strangely enough, Reuben is the only voice of reason here. The other boys sought blood. They wanted to literally kill their own brother out of jealousy (a theme that has already played its course in this book (Cain and Abel, Noah's children, Jacob and Esau…)). I'm sure these stories had been passed along, and I guarantee these brothers heard about these events, yet they are falling for the same dumb traps as their ancestors. We still fall for this same dumb trap today."},{"kind":"para","text":"Reuben was a voice of reason, and that is quite a big deal in this situation. Imagine if Reuben had said nothing. This story would go a very different way, and there would be no redemption for Israel, or the rest of the world had Reuben kept his mouth shut. Reuben planned on bringing Joseph back. I think his main concern was with their father, more than with Joseph. Reuben hated Joseph too, but he loved his father enough to not do that to him."}]}]},{"start":23,"end":28,"title":"Joseph Is Sold","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Dothan\" means \"Two wells.\" I suppose there were likely two wells in this area. One probably had water, and the other one was run dry. I'm sure that the one that ran dry was taken apart and left there. Now there is just a very large hole in the ground that has no water. Joseph is in this bit. There would be no way to climb out. He would have to be lifted out, or helped out of it now. They took his coat off (that was probably all he had on), and threw him into the pit to die there. They had no remorse for their actions (that's how much they hated their own brother). Get this picture with me because I think it's important that we see the heinous countenance of these brothers. Even Judas Iscariot had remorse for his decision to betray Christ."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Saying, I have sinned in that I have betrayed the innocent blood. And they said, What is that to us? see thou to that.","ref":"Matthew 27:4"},{"kind":"para","text":"If Judas recognizes his act as a sin, then the brothers’ act (here) is several times more evil. Of course, we can look at all the people chanting for Jesus' crucifixion as even more heinous than this act, but these are Joseph's own brothers. These were people that have grown up with Joseph for the last seventeen years. It was not as if they only got to know Joseph for three years before they decided to kill him. No, they knew Joseph for nearly their whole lives. This was a terrible act indeed."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Judah says, \"Hey, we could probably make money off of Joseph. Maybe he could actually be of value to us if we sold him to this Ishmaelite.\" They knew that this Ishmaelite would take Joseph and sell him to the Egyptians as a slave. They knew that this would be the last time they would hear from Joseph. They wouldn't have to kill their own brother, but instead they could just sell him into slavery and it would be nearly equivalent. In fact, it would be better because they could get paid for doing it."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We seem to have a connection of merchantmen. I assume that the Midianites and the Ishmeelites worked together quite often. Both of these people come from Abraham, mind you. The Ishmeelites coming from Ishmael and the Midianites coming from Midian (if that was not obvious enough). These men were actually about to buy one of their own kinsmen and sell their own people into slavery. Whether they knew that or not at this point obviously doesn't concern anyone involved, but they were all in agreement with this plan."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Ishmeelites meet with the brothers first, and they say something like, \"Hey, we have found someone. He is a slave, and we cannot keep him any longer. Can you help us get him out, and we will sell him to you.\" The Ishmeelites think, \"Wonderful, if I can get a deal for this slave, I can sell him for more and make a greater profit.\" The Midianites are walking by and see that some things are taking place. Perhaps the Midianites were going to meet with the Ishmeelites for some light trading as well. Maybe they were going to meet up together in Egypt later on. There's a lot of background information that is not made mention of here because the focus is on Joseph. So the Midianites help the Ishmeelites get Joseph out of that hole in the ground, and Judah sells his brother to the Ishmeelites. The transaction was that easy and painless. Joseph couldn't even think as fast as things were moving. He thought he was going to go home."}]}]},{"start":29,"end":36,"title":"Jacob Is Heartbroken","entries":[{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Where did Reuben go? Perhaps he was helping to finish the feeding of the sheep and was planning on coming back to get Joseph as soon as that was done. Maybe he was sidetracked and ended up wandering for a bit. Either way, he comes back and he begins to panic. \"What am I going to do?\" he asks his brothers. We don't know if Reuben ever finds out about what happened to Joseph. I assume that his brothers would have no problem telling him in time, but for right now they are devising a plan to help make sense of their brother’s disappearance when they come to their father."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There was a sacrifice made here for their sins. They didn't do this intentionally. They killed an innocent goat for this purpose, here, to try and help them get away with their lie. They sold their innocent brother into death, and killed an innocent goat from their father. The two pictures are parallel in many ways. It's so sad that they were able to think of this so fast."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob knew the coat he gave his son. They knew he knew. Even if it were true that an animal came and killed Joseph, these kids would know exactly who that coat belonged to."}]},{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is such a sad scene here. Jacob's sons didn't really want to comfort their father. They didn't care about how their father actually felt about this. They just destroyed their own father’s countenance and heart without a single bit of pity for him. They are blessed that Jacob doesn't have a heart attack in this moment. I likely would have. My heart would have burst for the amount of sorrow I felt over my child being taken from me. There would be nothing I could do. Later (when they find that Joseph is still alive), my heart would have been broken by the fact that my children chose to lie to me about it. Perhaps it's never mentioned to Jacob again (we don't know, and we will study that better when we get there), but if I were these boys (specifically Reuben), I would be horribly pained by my father’s hurt here. I couldn't sleep at night knowing that it was my brothers who did this. I wouldn't trust a single one of them. My conscience would eat at me, and I would have to tell the truth to my father, especially because he is so undone. These boys have no remorse, though, and they don't care about their father’s hurt in the least bit. Jacob was right to shew them away from trying to give him phony comfort."}]},{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So the Midianites and the Ishmeelites seem to be working together in their tradeings. They were going to split the money between each other. Maybe they walked together for protection (they had a lot of merchandise they were carrying around, and anyone would be privy to try and rob them). We know for sure that the brothers sold Joseph to the Ishmeelites (it specifically says that), but we see the Midianites completing the job here. So, I have reason to believe they were working together and splitting the profits. Either way, we get a clear picture of where Joseph goes, and he is placed into the hands of Potiphar, who was very close to the leader of Egypt."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":36}]},
    "38": {"chapter":38,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we got a bleak look at this family and their hatred towards their brother. They mistreated Joseph (the youngest in their family, aside from Benjamin, who was not old enough for them to hate yet), and they can't stand that their father loves him so much. When they are out feeding the sheep (or whatever it is they are really doing), they see Joseph and think it's sufficient to kill him now. They strip him of his fathers coat, and throw him into a pit determining what to do with him. They find some merchants and sell him into slavery."},{"kind":"para","text":"I would like to say that this chapter shows what happens to Joseph now that he is sold into slavery, but the Lord (and Moses) saw it fit to take a small break from that story and describe a very dismal and terrible story of Judah. Specifically Judah. I do find it interesting that this story pops up here (because it almost seems random), but I think it's useful because it's the lineage we will be following along with the most throughout the rest of scripture."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":11,"title":"The Sin of Judah","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This might seem like a very strange thing to bring up. When we read through this chapter, it seems rather out of place. Judah is one of the twelve brothers, and he is about to do something that is frowned upon in their culture (and any culture today, but it's probably more normalized now). He goes to see an Adullamite woman for some sort of purpose, it seems, and while he is there, he meets a Canaanite woman. \"Adullam\" means \"justice of the people.\" Perhaps he was going to this woman to see about his family’s standing after his brother has been left behind. I could not say for sure. \"Hirah\" means \"a noble family.\" These people were law people. They were people who could help you out of a bind (or so it seems). He was likely checking his standing in case he gets caught for selling his own brother into slavery."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Canaanites were people that were not part of the chosen lineage. Noah made that clear, and we have studied that over and over again. The Israelites were to stay within their own family lineage and grow from there, but they will ignore that pretty frequently as we will see. \"Shuah\" means \"wealth.\" She was likely a rich person who didn't really need to do much for what she is seen doing here. Or her family just thought having children made them wealthy (that is pretty normal in those days; the more children, the richer the person). And so she has this daughter who grabs the attention of Judah in some way. Judah was foolish and let his own lust get the better of him, and we will see how that plays out for him."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Er\" means \"awake.\" This will certainly wake up Judah from his sin, right? Well, apparently not, because he comes back to have more children."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So now we have several children in this family line. \"Onan\" means \"strong.\" \"Shelah\" means \"a petition.\" And he was born at Chezib. \"Chezib\" means \"false.\" There was nothing genuine about this family line. For Er, Judah found a woman for his firstborn whose name is Tamar. \"Tamar\" means \"palm-tree.\" He did this specifically because it was his firstborn. But he messed up in the way he received these children. I believe it's important to note this lineage specifically because it's the one that leads to Jesus Christ. I truly believe that's why we have this account here. We will see the great sin of this lineage in the verses to come. But for reference, Matthew gives us an account:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Abraham begat Isaac; and Isaac begat Jacob; and Jacob begat Judas and his brethren; And Judas begat Phares and Zara of Thamar; and Phares begat Esrom; and Esrom begat Aram;","ref":"Matthew 1:2-3"},{"kind":"para","text":"It interesting to note how these people started off."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Er was a terrible person, but Judah was no better. Er is killed quite quickly because of his sin and wickedness. We've seen a lot of wicked people throughout the book of Genesis, but this Er fellow must have been pretty bad. It was custom (in those days) that if a man dies, the brother was to marry the wife and continue the lineage (if they didn't have children prior). This is why Judah commissions Onan to do this. He was making a fool of this lineage already, and we will see that Onan doesn't comply with the way in which his father desired. Yet, he tries to go and do as his father says, but he chooses to change his mind at the last moment."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Was it displeasing because he didn't listen to his father? Did God just kill this young man because he didn't continue the lineage through his brother’s wife? I think it's deeper than that. He still sleeps with her. He defiles his brother’s wife, and keeps back from continuing his brother’s lineage. He backs out of the marriage, and he backs out of taking care of his brother’s wife, and so she is just used and abused at this point. The Lord is not pleased with this family at all. He isn't pleased with Er, He isn't pleased with Onan, and He certainly isn't pleased with Judah and His brothers. These men were despicable."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There's quite a bit happening here. We are seeing death after death, and it hasn't been a good time for this family. The thing that makes me extremely confused is the countenance of Judah. He seems to have no cares at all about this situation. I mean, obviously he cares enough because he is trying to help his daughter-in-law, but we don't get an account of how Judah is feeling after the deaths of each of his sons. Every other character of faith in the Bible is distraught when their son dies before them, and Judah doesn't seem to have a worry in the world. Perhaps he is feeling a bit hurt by it all, but it just doesn't show it in scripture. Moses didn't think it was worth mentioning. Whatever the case may be, Judah tells Tamar to wait until his last son is old enough to marry, and hopefully he'll be the one to redeem her (unless he dies as well). So Tamar went back home."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":23,"title":"Tamar Forgotten","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that the business Judah has with Hirah has to do with sheep. He has told Tamar to wait, and it has been some time. Apparently enough time has passed that Shuah's daughter passes away. I am not sure how to interpret this, so I'm going to assume that Shuah's daughter is the one that Judah becomes infatuated with and has children with. We never get her name. Shuah was the mother's name. I don't know what the daughter’s name is, and the Lord seems to purposely leave it out. So Judah is left without a significant other at this moment. He is being comforted by Hirah, and is brought to shear his sheep when Tamar finds out that he is coming towards her father’s house. He wouldn't be too far away, and she has some questions about what to do next (she doesn't want to live as a widow forever)."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Shelah was old enough to become her husband, but Judah hasn't said a single thing to her about it. She wants to continue her lineage. So instead of talking to Judah about it, she dresses up like a harlot in disguise."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Judah is a gross man, I will say that much. He is enamored by the wrong women, and Tamar uses this to take advantage of this family who has largely taken advantage of her. He wants to reserve her, but she requests some payment up front. She says, \"What will my payment be?\" He says, \"I'll give you a goat.\" She wants a downpayment in case that goat never comes, and he obliges by giving her his signet, bracelets, and staff. These ONLY belonged to Judah. These are pretty valuable pieces that he was loaning for the night. That's how much this harlot meant to him."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He sends his friend to go make the trade back to him. The woman couldn't be found, however, because she had taken her harlot wear off. She was not stupid."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Harlots are not a fun thing for women to be called in any day or age. They still exist today, but we do not refer to them as harlots. In these days, it was a very disgraceful thing to have a harlot in your midst. They tried to keep them separated from the rest. They would have all known if there was a harlot living among them."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He should be shamed for his acts. He is trying to hire a woman right after losing the mother of his children, and has no shame or remorse for any of it. He keeps messing things up, and doesn't have any motive or desire to get his life together and veer onto a godly path. He just wants to enact upon his flesh, and I feel this is justified from Tamar. He was the one that brought Tamar into this family, after all, and was given some evil children for the cause. It is evident to the reader (and obviously to Tamar) that Judah had completely forgotten about Tamar. He had no intentions of helping her along later on."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":26,"title":"Tamar Is Pregnant","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let me get this straight. It was okay for Judah to try and sleep with a harlot, but because Tamar was actually BEING a harlot, it was not okay? He was going to burn her at the stake for it. She is pregnant from HIM specifically. He is the biggest hypocrite in this chapter. None of this is okay. Not a single ounce of it, and I hope we understand that. We cannot justify one side or the other. But the things in which Judah was doing were casually ruining the life of a woman who took her life seriously. Judah may not have cared about life in general, but he can't just toy around with other people's lives."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is what you do when you enact on prostitution, by the way. You are toying around with a person’s life (whether consenting or not). This is why marriage is such an important institution. Otherwise, we get a lot of boys with the mindset of Judah here. That is a good portion of our world today. All women (in the eyes of boys in our generation) are just products and pleasures. This was wrong back then, and it's still wrong today (whether we want to normalize it or not)."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here's a very sad reality. Judah knew that he was ignoring Tamar. He made promises that he never intended to keep. It was not as if he just downright forgot; he simply ignored his promise. Tamar certainly wasn't \"righteous\" in her actions, but she was doing much better than he was. Judah was a complete fool, and there are no redeeming qualities found in him in this chapter. Now, it was customary in those days to give the brother to the widow, but we have a very different dynamic happening here. We have the father-in-law having a child with his daughter-in-law. That was very frowned upon (probably worse than impregnating a prostitute). She really played this smoothly. Again, none of this can be condoned by the Lord. All of them (including Judah) should be killed for their actions. However, God knows the plans for the future, and He has them set in stone. Matthew's account is being written before this account takes place. That's the sovereignty of God, friend."}]}]},{"start":27,"end":30,"title":"Tamar's Lineage","entries":[{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Two children. I don't think twins were all that common (even back then), but we have seen through this lineage that twins were happening quite a bit. A scarlet thread is an interesting choice, isn't it? A hand pops out, and she puts a red-colored thread on the wrist to differentiate the children. The firstborn would have the thread! When we see \"the scarlet thread” (which will be mentioned a few times in scripture), we get the picture of Christ there."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is the lineage of Christ, if you don't remember. It would be really difficult for us to remember that based on their sin in this chapter. We might think, \"Boy, God chose the wrong lineage for this. These people are broken and sinful.\" You are quite right in your assessment. However, that is who Jesus came to seek. He came from a very broken and sinful lineage, and He never sinned! He broke the chain of his family. The scarlet thread is a reminder of that!"}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Pharez\" means \"breach.\" He took the spot of the firstborn (which seems to be the scarlet thread throughout Scripture (you see what I did there?)) He came out first, even though his brother had his hand out first."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Zarah\" means \"rising.\" It will be through the lineage of Pharez that Christ comes into the scene. It's through the chosen lineage (the technically second-born). It worked the same way with Jacob and Esau as well. It's through the seemingly \"lesser\" lineage. David will be the \"lesser\" of all his brothers, and he will be in this lineage as well. Solomon will be the \"lesser\" of all his brothers, and he will be in this lineage as well. It's always the lesser lineage that seems to be the one that God pays the closest attention to. He can bring up those that are weak. That is a good thing to remember."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":30}]},
    "39": {"chapter":39,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we took a bit of a break from the life and lineage of Joseph to look at the sin and disgrace of Judah. Things did not look well for this family. Obviously there was something mentally and psychologically wrong with the entire family, but Moses (led by the Spirit of course) thought it was sufficient to add this little story about Judah and his problems right in the middle of Joseph's testimony. It seems quite random and strange, and I don't know if it was necessary, for anyone at this time, but it is certainly something that is necessary for us today (for that is the lineage of the Lord mind you)."},{"kind":"para","text":"In our chapter today, we pick back up with Joseph and his movement in slavery. It was a difficult thing to be a slave in those days. They were worked (literally) to death. It was highly unlikely that you would survive very long if you were sold into slavery. It was pretty much assured that there would be no prosperity for a slave. We get a very different look (however) at the life of slave through Joseph, and we will notice that God can use and protect His children, even in the most bleakest of situations."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"Potipher's Possessions","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You might have noticed that we are no longer talking about Judah in this chapter. It seems like a strange thing to just bring up that information about Judah and continue on the story with Joseph. I honestly don't know what the purpose was for this. I do know that life was happening behind the scenes while Joseph was moving through the chain of slavery, and perhaps that is the purpose for illustrating what was going on with Judah. All the brothers had something else going on, though, so we won't fully know the whole picture with everyone else because now we are getting to see what happens to Joseph."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord (without Joseph knowing it) has placed Joseph in a very important position by bringing him to Potiphar. There (I'm sure) were several people who would have gladly bid on Joseph, but Potiphar gets to him first. This is, of course, not by accident. These were pagan people, and they did not follow the real Lord of creation, but as far as a slave goes, being in the household of Pharaoh was about as much of a luxury a slave was ever going to get in Egypt. \"Potipher\" means \"belonging to the sun.\" Just his name alone bleeds paganism."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I have no doubt that Joseph was not comfortable in his position as a slave. This is not where Joseph was supposed to be. His brothers sold him into this, but this was not God's will on the matter. However, God will deal with the brothers in His way and time, and Joseph will be taken care of, even in the midst of great turmoil like this. Joseph is a Godly son, so even in the midst of slavery (which was sure death for whoever was sold into it), he was going to be the best slave he could. God uses him, and it is evident to everyone around him that God is with this young man. Remember, Potipher isn't a believer. So the fact that even Potipher recognizes that God is with Joseph says a lot about Joseph's work ethic and attitude."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't think it was a very common thing for a slave to be promoted, but here is Joseph (the slave in Egypt) being promoted in Potiphar’s house to start taking care of his most important essentials. This is a very big deal! Potiphar trusted Joseph so much that he put him over everything in his own household. We will see later that the Egyptians and the Syrians (Israelites as they will be called later) were not keen on sharing a table together, let alone to work together or be put in charge over each other’s belongings. This certainly says a lot about the character of Joseph."},{"kind":"para","text":"Through these small examples, we will see that God is painting a picture of who Joseph was, and why he was the chosen one to be thrown into slavery. That is something we might want to remember. While it was not in God's Will that the other brothers threw Joseph into slavery, God could have rejected the brothers from this circumstance. He could have made it impossible to sell Joseph. He could have intervened (perhaps in the life of one of the people passing by to buy Joseph). But He doesn't. He lets this happen. He lets it happen for a reason, and we will find that out in the end."},{"kind":"para","text":"For those of us who face troubles today, that is what we must remember. God has entrusted you with this situation. He has entrusted you with this trial and turmoil. He, perhaps, could have had another one of Jacob's children face this trial, but Joseph was the lucky one."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Potipher trusted him with everything. If the Lord is in it, He will grow it. If the Lord is with you, He will be obvious. It seems that (according to verse 6) Potipher doesn't even request an account of what Joseph was taking care of. It grew to a point where Potipher just placed what he had in Joseph's hands, and trusted that Joseph would prosper in it and take care of it. This helped Potipher with his own work, because he knew that everything at home was being taken care of. This could also be a dangerous thing (as we will see in a moment). Potipher is getting busy with his work for Pharaoh, and so his home life is slipping by him because he knows that Joseph will take care of things while he's busy with his work."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":20,"title":"Potipher's Wife","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When you are more busy in your work and less busy in your family, it causes a strain on the relationship. I've seen a lot of pastors who are very deeply motivated to take care of their churches and their ministries. They spend a lot of time in their work and their toils. They LOVE their church. They love it so much (in fact) that it makes their relationship with their families look like hate. That is a problem. A minister of God should not be loving His church more than His family. This is something we will learn over and over again throughout Scripture, but it's a lesson that will have to be learned by Potipher (and even Joseph) right here. Joseph was in charge of Potipher's entire household, and I have reason to believe that even meant Potipher's wife. Now, it was not Joseph's job to be her husband (that is a role that no one else could fill), but it was his job to make sure her needs were taken care of. This particular need (however) would be a sinful one, and so Joseph is faced with a decision here."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph was a young man. He still lived in the flesh. He had a sinful side (even if he is a follower of God). As a young man, I have no doubt that he likely pondered the idea. He didn't let his flesh get the better of him though, and he stood on his integrity and character. His identity was in the Lord, and he knows that this would not be pleasing to God specifically. He knows it wouldn't be pleasing to Potipher either, but Potipher likely wouldn't question it or even think too deeply about it. Joseph was a man that understood his moral standards, and was not willing to waver on them just for a little fun from his boss’s wife. If Joseph had followed through with her desires, and listened to his flesh here, this story would be catastrophic. Thankfully, Joseph knows who he is, and is not willing to change for a small season of sin (that wouldn't be worth much in the end anyway)."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems she doesn't let up. This had to be a great hindrance to Joseph. He probably enjoyed his work for Potipher. Potipher was giving him reason to rejoice in the work he was called to do. But knowing that his wife was going to be there to try and entice him had to really bring Joseph down. I have worked in several situations where there was a person on the job who was just... not friendly. They made it difficult to want to work. I loved my work, but knowing that they were going to be there made me go to work in a burden. It didn't matter how much I tried to avoid this person, he would always go out of his way to burden me some more in some way. I imagine that it's about as equivalent to being punched in the stomach going to work each day. You could see how annoying and tiresome that could be if it were a daily thing."},{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph is likely growing quite weary of this woman. I believe every day before work, Joseph got up and felt refreshed. He went to work, put things in order, accounted everything in Potipher's house, set things up to help him flourish, and... oh, here comes Potiphers wife again. She asks him if he's up for it today, and he flutters with a dilemma again. He thinks to himself, \"Maybe if I just do this, she'll leave me alone. No. I can't do this. This is not my identity. That is not who I am, and it's not who I want to be.\" He turns to her and says, \"Now listen here, you know very well I can't do that. Stop trying to entice me with it.\" and walks away. He finishes his work for the day, and goes to lie down, and just before falling asleep remembers he will have to deal with her again tomorrow. I imagine Joseph lays there and prays that God would give him the strength to reject her again tomorrow, and God grants that prayer. But how long will it go on?"}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This time it was a little different. She wasn't just using her words anymore. She was pulling him in. She actually put her hands on him this time. He had already positioned himself in his mind that this was not going to happen, and so he took his garment off quickly and left quickly. He \"got him out\" of there as fast as he possibly could. She was like Satan in the wilderness when Jesus had fasted for forty days. Consistent and annoying. I don't believe this was her first rodeo either (we will notice that in a moment). Satan is always looking to entice the ones that are the most prosperous in the Lord's work. Those are the ones that he knows are worth attacking."},{"kind":"para","text":"Someone once said, \"The enemy attacks the strongest fortresses because he knows that there is much treasure to find.\" God builds His children up, and Satan is always pacing to see how to tear God's children down. We must take the example of Joseph here, who was preparing himself for the future attacks of Satan. The first time he was enticed, I believe it caught him off guard. I don't think he was prepared for Potiphar’s wife to ask something so bluntly. After that, he prepared his heart and spirit to reject it every single day. Today would be very different, even though he was successful at rejecting her."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She is accusing Potipher for bringing in the wrong person. Her and her husband must not have a very strong marriage together. Joseph knows that marriage is sacred and important, but it's quite obvious that Potipher's wife doesn't have a care in the world for her marriage. This is why I believe it's not her first time. She thought of this quickly, and she is using this as a means to pass accusation onto her husband. She was waiting for a time to blame her husband for something. I think if Joseph did decide to lay with her, she would have still thrown him under the bus. Perhaps not. Perhaps she was just cynical because he didn't fall for her enticements. Either way, she was locked and loaded and ready to pass blame and try and cover her own tracks."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She was ready to throw it in her husbands face."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a very big accusation from her. I think it's interesting (and humorous in my own opinion) that Moses doesn't give her name here. We have been given nearly every name under the sun of all sorts of ungodly people, but this lady's name was not worth mentioning. Potipher is named, but his wife remains nameless. That's how shameful the Lord sees this lady. But you can notice that she is quite keen on blaming her husband for this fault."},{"kind":"para","text":"Usually when someone is lying about something, a very simple sign to know they are lying is if they are quick to pass the blame. We can recognize that psychology from the get-go with Adam and Eve. Eve passes the blame onto the snake, Adam passes it to God."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the man said, The woman whom thou gavest to be with me, she gave me of the tree, and I did eat. And the LORD God said unto the woman, What is this that thou hast done? And the woman said, The serpent beguiled me, and I did eat.","ref":"Genesis 3:12-13"},{"kind":"para","text":"Cain and Able."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the LORD said unto Cain, Where is Abel thy brother? And he said, I know not: Am I my brother's keeper?","ref":"Genesis 4:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham and Sarah."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Sarai said unto Abram, My wrong be upon thee: I have given my maid into thy bosom; and when she saw that she had conceived, I was despised in her eyes: the LORD judge between me and thee.","ref":"Genesis 16:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"The list goes on, even to Isaac and Rebekah, Jacob and Esau, Joseph and his brothers, and now to this moment (and more to come). It's evident that liars are quick to pass blame. That's an important lesson for us to get into our brains while we read the rest of Scripture, but it's a useful tool for you today as you search out the truth in your own life and circumstances."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know if Potipher is really angry at Joseph. I think Potipher knew that Joseph was an upright man, and he wasn't the type to do this thing. Honestly, I think he trusted Joseph more than he trusted his own wife. I think what Potipher was really angry at was his wife and her blame-shifting. I think he takes it out on Joseph because there's not much he can do against his wife, but I think he was far more upset with her than he was with Joseph specifically. Potipher could have done a great deal of things to Joseph. He could have beaten him nearly to death. He could have even killed him if he so chose. He had that kind of authority over Joseph. This is what he does, however:"}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This prison was not like the other prisons in the land. This prison was for special people. The king's prisoners were treated a little differently than the other prisoners around. They were still treated as prisoners, but they were stationed there with a future goal of release (mostly of the time by death more than likely). Notice that Joseph hasn't said a word. Potiphar doesn't go and ask him what happened. Potiphar doesn't even accuse Joseph of anything. He just gets him out of there and throws him into the prison. How long he will stay there? It was anybody's guess."},{"kind":"para","text":"It seemed as if Joseph finally caught a break, and now he is back at square one again. It should be Potiphar’s wife who is thrown into prison, not Joseph. But Joseph is the slave, not the wife, and so the blame has to be shifted onto Joseph. That should sound familiar to us all. Christ is not to blame for your sin. Yet He took the punishment for it and is not given the time of day to state his own case. Evil people always think they have won, but we all know they will never succeed. The Jews who committed Christ to the cross think they won, but we all know He rises back from the dead."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":23,"title":"A Prison Captain","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Even in the middle of prison, Joseph was prospering. That seems like such an odd verse. Joseph had traced God's mercy and presence through every difficulty he had confronted. I believe that is why it was so easy for him to keep his integrity in the face of enticements. He knew that if he didn't stay strong in his faith, he would distance himself from the mercy and presence of the Lord. That is not something worth doing. He depended (wholeheartedly, I might add) on the Lord's guidance, and he knew that his life as a slave would be in complete shambles and quite lonely without Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"We must remember this as well. Through every difficulty you face, you can trace God's mercy and presence if you are standing on integrity and in His Will. A litmus test is used in several settings. One setting is in chemistry. You use this test to see whether or not a solution is acidic or not. It's a good way to make sure you are not going to make a bomb when you mean to make an aspirin. It's used in politics as well to determine if the candidate is someone who qualifies for his position. It's also used by the people around you every day to see if you are genuine in your character or not. Oh, you didn't know that you are being tested? Let me inform you: people are testing you. They want to see how far you can be pushed and if your character is genuine. That is the goal of this world. They like to test you and prod you to see what color your blood is. The truth is, we all bleed the same, just some of us have different pain tolerances."},{"kind":"para","text":"Comment 3: I believe my wife has a higher pain tolerance than anyone else I know. She is super sensitive to being tickled but would not be fazed by being kicked in the shin like I would be. I don't know if that is something that she has developed over time or that is something that her brain has just processed differently than mine, but it's obvious that she can take a lot more pain than me (or most men, for that matter)."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think the Lord allows these tests for a great deal of purposes, but as we have drawn out through our study of Genesis, He has been allowing these tests to develop some strength in certain areas. He wants us to have some thick skin so when we are tempted (like Joseph), we can successfully reject it. If Joseph's other brothers were in this same position, I don't believe a single one of them would have made it very far after their first week. It seems that Joseph was the only one considerate about the Lord's presence in his family (other than Jacob, of course). The Lord had grabbed his attention out of all of his brothers because his brothers were prideful and arrogant men. They wouldn't listen to the Lord even if God was making it obvious to them. They would have just thought themselves as someone even more special with God's presence in their life. Joseph is facing every litmus test possible in the world, but the Lord was there with him in all of it. Joseph knows that, and sees it, and it couldn't be any more obvious to those around him that God is with this young man."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph prospers in prison. He prospers as a slave, and he prospers as a prisoner. This seems like an absolutely crazy sentence to write out. If we looked at slaves today, we would not see them as prosperous. They are just as mistreated (they were probably treated worse in Egypt, honestly), and you don't think of them as prosperous. Same with prisoners. You don't look at a prisoner and say, \"Wow, God is in that man's life. I think he should be in charge of organizing the other prisoners and putting things in their place.\" Yet, we see both of those things happen in the life of Joseph. There is no denying that Joseph is innocent and godly."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This prison keeper (like Potipher) utterly trusted Joseph with everything. He didn't ask for an account from Joseph either, because Joseph was honest and trustworthy. I think this gave Joseph even more encouragement that the Lord was with him. Only the Lord could make a prisoner and slave feel at home in those circumstances. But that is the great encourager to us today, friend. If Joseph can serve the Lord in some of the most bleak of situations, what is stopping us from giving our heart to God in the seasons of true difficulty?"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":23}]},
    "40": {"chapter":40,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see a bit of a high lifting of Joseph and things are actually looking pretty good for him. He is placed in charge of Potipher's possessions (a man who was captain to Pharaoh so he had a lot of authority). He seems to trust Joseph with everything he has, and Joseph does right by him. However, his wife is not a faithful person to her husband and he tries to sway Joseph into betraying her husband. He declines, and yet is accused due to her evilness. Potipher is upset enough that he throws Joseph into prison, even though Joseph has been a real benefit to him. Joseph is made a captain in prison, but it seems like this might be the end of Joseph's life and freedom until his death."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, I wish I could say things start to look up, but they really don't. This chapter takes a little break from Joseph's moving, and focuses on a couple people who enter into the prison with some heavy burdens themselves. Thankfully they are close to Pharaoh, and this will serve (later on) as a useful tool to Joseph to eventually (and hopefully) give him a way out of prison."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":4,"title":"The Baker and Butler","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know what they did to offend this man, but I don't think it would have taken too much to offend this Pharaoh. I assume the power high of Pharaoh made him quite greedy and self-authoritative. No one could say one wrong thing without it coming off as if they were trying to take Pharaoh's place. I'm willing to bet that is why both of these men were in prison to begin with. But I digress."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Pharaoh’s anger was enough to not just get mad at the ones making him mad. He was getting mad at those that were in charge of them. Maybe they made his birthday cake wrong?"}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now Joseph has some new friends. While this might seem like a very bleak circumstance, this will actually work out for Joseph in the future. Joseph wasn't thinking of these new inmates as his access to Pharaoh; he was just seeing them as more poor souls stuck within his life destiny (which at this point was likely death in prison)."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph had a lot of power even in the middle of prison. Jesus came to Israel during a time of great persecution to the Israelites. They were people that could barely afford to live because of the Romans’ customs and ruling, and they were looking for someone to get them out of their financial and physical strain that they were facing. Instead of Jesus relieving them of that, He relieved them of a greater debt. But notice that Jesus was in the middle of the trial with all of them. People rejected His help, but He was still there facing the same persecution as the rest of the Jews. Yet, when we see Jesus, He is living as a free man in the middle of bondage. Joseph is living as if he is free even though he's literally in bondage. You, today, can live in freedom too in a very dark and bleak world. What a beautiful gift that the Lord has given to us."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":8,"title":"A Couple Dreamers","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, the butler and the baker probably didn't think too much of their dream when they woke up. They probably got to talking, and one said, \"Hey, I had a little bit of a strange dream...\" The other said, \"Wait, that is pretty similar to MY dream! What are the odds? I wonder if that's a sign of something?\" And it shifted their countenance completely. It makes them feel uncomfortable."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph was a caring man. He came from a family who was calloused to emotions and circumstances, but Joseph was certainly built a very different way. He sees these two men and he notices that they are not feeling well. So he takes it upon himself to ask questions in hopes that he can be some sort of encouragement or help."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is an important scene to analyze here. Joseph didn't have to say a single thing to these two men. He hardly knows them at all. Yet, with the little he knows, he feels sorry for them simply by their countenance. He has a heart for people. That is the true sign of a child of God. They notice the hurts of other people, even if the other people don't know the Lord personally. I don't believe this baker, nor this butler knew the Lord. They could have, but I highly doubt it. Yet, Joseph still loves them enough to check on their pains. He is spending his morning helping their hurts. What a great example of a minister. That is a very rare thing to find today (inside or outside of the church)."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph knows all about what it means to have a dream. His dreams got him into trouble, but they were his dreams. Their dreams may get them into trouble, but it will be God that makes that call for them. We should notice that Joseph is not taking the power away from the Lord. He says, \"Hey, don't be sad, let's bring these thoughts to God together and get to the bottom of it. God will answer.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, I assume these men probably thought that Joseph was talking about one of several gods they worship. But Joseph wasn't. Joseph knew the TRUE God of this world, and that is the one that will interpret their dreams. They don't understand that, so when Joseph says this they probably didn't think too highly of his assumption that he would talk to God."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":15,"title":"The Butler's Dream","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This doesn't seem like a dream to be worried about. Even if there was no interpretation at all, this butler was simply pressing grapes into the cup of Pharaoh. That is tame enough. Perhaps the butler wasn't too concerned about his dream. Maybe he was more concerned about the fact that the other man's dream seems to lock in with his own. Either way, whatever he saw in this dream, he remembered, and it concerned him. I have plenty of dreams (I assume every time I sleep). I hardly remember any of my dreams unless they are nightmares. The Lord really wanted the butler to remember this dream though, because it will all lead to Joseph's eventual letting go from prison (but that's a spoiler)."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph saw this as an opportunity to get into the ears of Pharaoh. He interpreted the dream, and he was assured it would come to pass. He was saying, \"When this happens, and you are restored to your position, help me get out of here. I don't belong here. I don't even belong in Egypt, and I miss my family very much. But I certainly don't belong in this dungeon. I have done nothing wrong.\" Joseph was pleading that this butler would \"shew kindness\" just like Joseph did to the butler. Joseph didn’t show kindness to him because he was expecting something from them. He showed kindness because he genuinely wanted to help. It just so worked out in his favor that there was someone that would eventually be out of here (in three days), and so it might be nice if this butler would show kindness in return. We will see that the butler will not, for quite some time still, however..."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the sad reality of our modern society. You can be the kindest person in the entire world, but that will not guarantee that others will be kind back to you. It is a very difficult thing to show kindness these days. People abuse others’ kindness. They use it for self-gain and personal recognition. If you choose to be kind, others will use it to build themselves up and leave you in the dust. The LORD will notice your kindness, and He will lift you up in due time. Hold on to that promise."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":19,"title":"The Bakers Dream","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Another dream that doesn't seem to need an interpretation. The only thing that seemed to be connected in this dream was the number three, and the Pharaoh they were serving. But carrying baskets doesn't seem like a very interesting and memorable dream to have. Yet, the Lord allows this dream to be remembered, and because the first interpretation was such a good thing, it brought his countenance up a little and was kind of happy to receive the interpretation of his own dream. That smile will fade in a moment."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I can almost bet that the news of this to the baker made both these men assume that Joseph didn't know what he was talking about. People always reject others who give bad news. They don't like to hear the truth. I believe that is why when they are released from prison, they completely forget about Joseph and his willingness to help them. They probably threw his advice out the moment he gave a negative interpretation about the baker’s dream."},{"kind":"para","text":"I want to notice one other thing which I find quite satisfying to the prominence of this chapter. All things, of course, lead to Christ, and I believe this chapter is no different. The man giving the interpretation is labelled as a type of Christ (something we have spoken about before, and we will speak about again in the future). But even these dreams are linkages to Christ. Both seem to be happy dreams, leading to something that each of them loves. The baker is not known as the baker because he hates baking. The butler likely didn't \"enjoy\" his job, but he was satisfying himself with serving this crazed ruler to the best of his ability. One has life in three days, the other has death in three days. Christ was in the grave for three days, and the options were the same for both of them. The baker likely didn't have a choice about his outcome. Perhaps he could have requested to stay in the prison, but that would be directly rejecting Pharaoh's rule to let him go, and that would probably lead to his death anyway. Maybe he could have run. Still, Pharaoh would have pursued and killed him. Maybe there was more to this man's story than we realize."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think the baker might be a little bit of a troublemaker, and that is what got him and the butler into prison to begin with. The butler wasn't looking to make trouble; he just got mixed up with the wrong people. Pharaoh will see that apparently... Either way, they both provide a choice. There will be obvious choices like this throughout all of Scripture. The choice that leads to death, or the choice that leads to life. That is what Moses will say in Exodus to Deuteronomy. That is the law that Joshua will display in the book of Joshua. That is the picture of the two kings of Israel, Saul and David. That is what it will be to the two thieves on the cross with Christ when He is crucified. That will be the choice for all of humanity after the Lord has resurrected in those three days. There are two choices for us still today. It's a choice we have every morning. Life, or death. The Lord leaves it to you to choose."},{"kind":"para","text":"The baker could have likely asked Joseph to pray for him. He could have said, “Can’t your God have mercy on me? I don't want to die! I don't want to lose everything!\" But the baker doesn't say a single thing. That is likely because he doesn't believe Joseph's interpretation. That is what leads to his death. That is what leads to our death today. When people reject the gospel message of Christ, they sign their own death warrant. You gave the news, and it's up to them if they want to accept or reject it."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":23,"title":"Correct Interpretation","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So I guess they weren't thrown into prison for making his birthday cake wrong (unless he is having a second birthday). I find it interesting that they are celebrating Pharaoh's birthday here. I don't see that mentioned too much in scripture, but it was a pretty important occasion back then apparently. Maybe only important to the country’s leadership, but it was still important. Joseph didn't know it was Pharaoh's birthday though, and so his interpretation was based solemnly off of the fact that God provided him with the meaning. In the moment (as this was happening) they probably didn't think about their dreams being interpreted. Everything was happening so fast and they are on their way to their positions."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It happened just as he said. Maybe the butler didn't see the death of the baker. To Pharaoh, the baker was easily replaceable. I assume he couldn't find another butler like this man. This man was good at his job. He was a useful man. The baker, however, was not useful to Pharaoh. The important fact is that everything happened just like Joseph said, and the butler should have taken that as a sign that Joseph was a man of God. That should have sparked his memory."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think the Lord would have allowed this butler to be killed too for his wickedness had it not been for Joseph still in prison. He was going to eventually get Joseph out of there, but he needed the butler to do that. He allows the baker to die, and there is no telling what this baker did. We are assured that the Lord knows what He is doing, however, and the purpose of the death of this baker (if not for anything else) would be a solid proof that Joseph is an innocent man who is only speaking the truth."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He completely forgets Joseph. Again, I don't believe they took Joseph's words very seriously. I don't think they put much stock into what he had said. I'll give the butler the benefit of the doubt and assume that he didn't know the baker had died. I'll also assume because of the quickness of all of this that he was just so happy to be out of prison that his brain was focused on everything he had to catch up on and not on who helped him interpret his dream. The Lord will get a hold of this butler later on, but for now it seems as if all hope is lost on Joseph."},{"kind":"para","text":"This leaves off on a bit of a cliffhanger. We are still left with Joseph in prison, and he is in distress. He truly feels as if this is the end of the road for him. This is his death place. While he is still able to have quite a bit of power in prison, he doesn't belong there. He knows he doesn't belong, and he certainly misses his family. How could he not? He misses his father, and even his wayward and calloused brothers. Whether they missed him or not is not ever cleared up, but it is doubtless that they felt guilty for their actions that they can never reverse. Though, if I were them, I would be going to Egypt to look for my brother. They likely thought he was dead at this point anyway, or that he was going to die quickly. That was the ultimate goal for them anyway. They wanted their brother to die, they just didn't want to be the ones to shed his blood (though they are still responsible for it by selling him directly into slavery). Things will start to look up for Joseph. This book isn't over yet!"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":23}]},
    "41": {"chapter":41,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we saw two new people come into prison under the watch and care of Joseph. This was a baker and butler for Pharaoh. These were people that would have been close to Pharaoh. We aren't told exactly what they did wrong, but it seems that whatever it was did not sit well with Pharaoh. They both dream dreams that will cause them to feel uneasy, and Joseph interprets the dreams for them. The butlers dream has a good ending (it shows that he will get out of prison) but the bakers dream has a bad ending (he will get out of prison and then he will die). Joseph wants the butler to remember Joseph when he finally free, but the butler forgets about him."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see that the Lord gets the attention of Pharaoh either way. There is a great famine headed in their direction, and God wants Joseph in a specific position so that the Lord can do something miraculous. I believe the famine was coming specifically because of the sins of the people, but it was also (and specifically (spoiler alert)) to get His children back in unity. Joseph was not supposed to be here, and his brothers were not supposed to be living the lives they were living. This will ultimately unite them, and that is why the Lord sends famines quite a bit through scripture."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":8,"title":"Pharaoh's Dreams","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So the butler was to be the one to remind Pharaoh about Joseph, but he failed to do so. Now the Lord gave a dream to Pharaoh to call the butler’s attention back to Joseph. I think if the butler had just said something to begin with, Joseph would already have been out of prison. But the Lord worked this out for a much bigger purpose, and it will be so Joseph can save a whole slew of people."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Kine\" means \"cows.\" He saw seven well-fed cows, and seven skinny cows. The dream is pretty self-explanatory at the moment, and it doesn't seem like there is much more to it than that. Yet it's something that Pharaoh will notice, and I think it will be because of what he sees next."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know if you've ever seen cows eating other cows, but I don't think that is very normal. I imagine that the dreams were much more vivid than the picture we are getting here. We can only have a word picture of the events, but Pharaoh legitimately saw cows eating other cows. It was a gruesome picture, I have no doubt about that."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, this is not a normal thing to dream about. Just like the skinny cows, the skinny corn was eating up the fat corn. Obviously, these dreams are connected, and Pharaoh is starting to become a bit concerned about what is happening to him at night."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Pharaoh doesn't know the Lord. Egypt was like most of the other countries of their day. They worshipped everything except for the God of the universe. You would be hard-pressed to find any other nation worshipping the Lord in that time. Abraham and his lineage were quite alone in this venture. We do see, however, that there are some who pop up here and there and follow the Lord. The king of the Philistines was one. The king priest during Sodom and Gomorrah was another. But that is about all you will see as far as those following the Lord outside of Abraham’s family. The Lord has plenty more followers today, but there is still not enough."},{"kind":"para","text":"Pharaoh was a powerful man, and he had a lot of powerful resources, but he didn't have the most powerful at his disposal, and that was the reliance and direction of the Lord. So he calls all of these people (magicians specifically) and \"wise\" men to give him the interpretation of his dream, and nobody can do it. These people were \"wise\" by the standard of the world. They knew quite a lot of things, but they didn't know the wisdom from above."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the wisdom that is from above is first pure, then peaceable, gentle, and easy to be intreated, full of mercy and good fruits, without partiality, and without hypocrisy.","ref":"James 3:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"There was a man in their midst who has that wisdom, however, but the butler has to tell them about him."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":13,"title":"Remembering Joseph","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The butler is missing a few things in his retelling, but at least he remembers Joseph. God got this butler to do what he was supposed to one way or another. Joseph was a follower of the Lord, and so the Lord works in his favor to get him out of prison. But the butler doesn't remember (or realize) that Joseph didn't just interpret the dream for him. He specifically tells him that it is the Lord who does it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And they said unto him, We have dreamed a dream, and there is no interpreter of it. And Joseph said unto them, Do not interpretations belong to God? tell me them, I pray you.","ref":"Genesis 40:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"But it's no surprise that this butler forgets this part, he completely forgets about Joseph literally a few days after he is told not to forget about him."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":24,"title":"God Interprets","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph was as sure as dead when he was sold into slavery. He survived that. Then he was as sure as dead when he was placed into prison. He survived that. Now he is facing the ruler of Egypt, and if he messes up and lies to the Pharaoh (which would be a strange action for Joseph to take), he WILL be as sure as dead. He gets a clean shave, puts on the proper apparel, so he can stand before the king distinguished."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph has a choice here. This will determine his death sentence. Joseph has been the face of dream interpretation with this butler, and (in all honesty) Joseph has every right to feel a little betrayed by the Lord. For some reason, Joseph never turns away from the Lord and steals glory from him. He knows that God is still there. How he knows this, I'm not sure. I assume it's because God did not leave him hanging when he interpreted their dreams to begin with. Maybe his own dreams were dreams that gave him great reminders of God's goodness and provision. Maybe it's the fact that he can prosper in prison of all places. I don't really know, but he has a choice at this moment to either claim that he is capable of interpreting the dream, or give it all back to God like he has done before."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I love this about Joseph. He is unapologetically a soulwinner. Yet no one around him is willing to believe. He has told his previous master about this God (letting it be known that if he sleeps with his wife, it would be a sin against the Lord). He has told the butler and the baker about this God, but they didn't believe it (even though the things he said came to pass). He is telling Pharaoh about this God. None of these people are willing to believe this God's existence though. They all think they know gods of all sorts, and so this (to them) must just be another one of those gods. It's a \"dream interpreter\" god. One that can just be added onto their shelf. Either way, this God is going to give an answer, and it will give Pharaoh some peace of mind knowing that his dream was interpreted (whether good or bad)."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There is a bit more depth to his dream here. We did not know that the skinny cows still remained skinny after eating the fat ones before. These cows were not getting sustenance no matter what they did or ate. These were diseased cows that could not be cured."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The weather killed the “good” corn, and the bad corn killed the good corn. There was none to declare the truth to Pharaoh because he was seeking the truth in all the wrong places. You can say what you will about Christian men and women, but if they are genuine Christians, they will give straight truth. They won't desire to deviate (or shy away) from what they know is good and right. Some are also even willing to point out your flaws and be brutally honest with you. Some people like honesty like that. Others don't."},{"kind":"para","text":"Whatever the case, real Christians aren't afraid to give you the truth, and they aren't willing to speak from their own thoughts either. They search out the truth for themselves before they give you an answer. They want to make sure that the most accurate information is given to the person they are speaking to. The magicians and \"wise\" men gave up hope because the dreams were too difficult for them to understand. They don't know the Lord to seek Him out. Joseph does, though, and that will be the saving grace of Pharaoh and the land."}]}]},{"start":25,"end":37,"title":"The Interpretation","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Both dreams are connected."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"The fat cows and the good corn represent the same thing, and the ill cows and the ill corn represent the same thing.\" That is from the Jonathan Translation."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They have seven years to get their act together before the famine comes. May we remember (once again) that when there is a prophecy (even like this), it is speaking about the current situation, the future situation, and the end times situation. This is something that will become a problem again when the Lord returns. The years of famine will be longer, however. It will be more dreadful. In those days, the Lord will make sure his children are well fed, just as he has always done before. He will provide food for His own. His children just have to trust that. His children have a hard time trusting that though. We have seen that problem arise again and again. Abraham fell for that trap when he was coming into the promised land. That is why he goes to Egypt. He sees a famine, and trusts in what he can see, rather than trust in the Lord who is guiding him."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph is very insistent that it is GOD who is providing this interpretation. It is God who gave the dream to Pharaoh, and it will be God who makes it all come to pass. The Egyptians (and the world) could not control what was going to come. It was coming, and they had better prepare themselves."}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph had been prepared for this moment. His time with Potipher, and in prison were not wasted time. He was building up knowledge about how to deal with situations just like this. He was wise in his accounts, and how to take care of valuable things. In this moment, the most valuable thing to the people will be food. There will be a time when food will be scarce, and there is a fix to this before that time comes. What Joseph is insisting upon is that they start storing up a little food for the next seven years. He says, \"a fifth part.\" At the beginning, a fifth part won't seem like very much. But a fifth of the food over seven years will be above and beyond what they could ever use. This was a very smart way to address their situation. He not only interprets their dreams, he even gives them a college course on how to take care of their provisions. Pharaoh is astonished, and even the servants around Pharaoh were amazed."}]}]},{"start":38,"end":45,"title":"Joseph Put In Charge","entries":[{"start":38,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is (perhaps) the first time that Pharaoh has even thought about the Lord in his entire life. He sees God in Joseph. He can tell that Joseph is a follower of a much higher being, and he is assured that Joseph is not just making it up. The wisdom that is flowing out of Joseph's mouth is evidence that he is being led by something. How else would Joseph be able to figure all of this out in this way (in such a short time)? Joseph was a slave, and he was a prisoner, and he has been in Egypt from a young age, so there is no way he learned all of this wisdom from anyone in Egypt. No body would have taught him this, and especially taught him to seek after the Lord in matters like this. There was only one explanation for Joseph's wisdom, and that was that he must be under the authority of a much higher power."}]},{"start":43,"end":43,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This escalated so quickly. I don't think Joseph was prepared for this treatment. He was just trying to be a help. The Lord puts Joseph (rightfully so) in a very high position of power. Godly men should be in high positions. We don't have many (if any) godly men in ranks of power these days. That is why there is so much turmoil in our politics, our government, and our country. They are not people who are seeking after wisdom from the Lord. Most are not even people who have experienced any of the trials that would set them up for success."},{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph has been in some very dark places throughout these chapters of his life. He knows what it's like to be at the very bottom. That is what will make him a good, honest, and compassionate leader now that he is at the very top. His wisdom grew from personal experience. That is where great wisdom comes from. It comes from what you have gone through and have dealt with. Pharaoh was still in charge, but Joseph will be just under him. He will be nearly as powerful as Pharaoh."}]},{"start":45,"end":45,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Zaphnathpaaneah\" means \"the revealer of secret things.\" Joseph just rolls off the tongue better, but what do I know? He was given a wife from one of the priest’s daughters. \"Asenath\" means \"belonging to the goddess of Neith.\" That is a very sad name to have. She is marrying a very godly man, and that name doesn't say \"I will be a godly wife.\" But her life will surely change, and Joseph will lead her the right way."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is (of course) not the lineage that Joseph was supposed to marry into, but that is what will happen here, and there isn't much of a choice in the matter. I'm sure Joseph thought that he would never go home anyway. His family didn't care about him, and this was his new life now. So he prospers in this land, and he is given a very high reverence from the Pharaoh and a place to lead."}]}]},{"start":46,"end":52,"title":"Joseph's Family","entries":[{"start":46,"end":46,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph was not very old at this time. He was still in his prime. I am currently thirty-one (as of this writing), and I am \"old\" but not in the sense of not being able to continue to grow and do things. I still have the ability to move, act, and do. I know the acts and lack of energy Joseph probably feels at his current age, but he had a very prominent position of power as a young man. He could build that power for the rest of his life, and he will become a great representation of the Lord in the land of Egypt."}]},{"start":49,"end":49,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They started off counting everything, but there was so much of it that Joseph eventually gave up counting. He couldn't even keep track anymore. It was plenty, he knew that much."}]},{"start":52,"end":52,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Thankfully, these two sons were born before the famine, because having children in the middle of a famine can be scary. My wife is currently pregnant with our second child. We are facing a financial crisis in America. Inflation is so high that it's very difficult to even travel anywhere. Our gas prices (when we were living in Texas) used to be about $2 (sometimes less) per gallon. We are now seeing gas prices (in California) for a little over $5 per gallon. That is a huge increase, and it has stayed that increase for a few years now. I don't know how much more we could deal with increased prices like that. Sadly, everything has gone up, and it seems like a very difficult thing to fathom that we are going to have a child right in the middle of all of it."},{"kind":"para","text":"With that said, I know that God is faithful, and Joseph knows it too. So even if he had children right in the middle of the famine, he is confident that God will take care of him and his family. God would see them through. \"Manasseh\" means \"Causing to forget.\" That's not a very prosperous name, but I think he felt sentimental towards his own family. These would be sons (in Joseph’s eyes) that would never be seen by his own family. He was a \"forgotten\" child. This son will also help Joseph get over the fact that his own family is completely separated from him. He is thankful that God has given him children and is able to start him up with his own family. \"Ephraim\" means \"I shall be doubly fruitful.\" He is fruitful to have two children. In the middle of Joseph's pain and harsh living, God has provided and given Joseph a life above and beyond what Joseph thought he was even capable of having. These children were beautiful pictures of Joseph's life. They were a reminder of God's goodness and provision to Joseph. Joseph knew (and thought) he would never have a life. He thought that his life would be spent as a slave, and he would eventually die from that work. That is not what the Lord did with him, however."}]}]},{"start":53,"end":57,"title":"The Lord Provides","entries":[{"start":55,"end":55,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph was the man in charge of the prosperity of Egypt, and Pharaoh knew that Joseph could be trusted to distribute what was proper and good. He knew that Joseph would be a wise steward and would not see Egypt crumble from the famine."}]},{"start":57,"end":57,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Corn (of all things) became insanely valuable. We had an increased price of eggs not that long ago. Eggs. You know, the thing that chickens lay by the dozens. Apparently, something with the pandemic made it hard for chickens to lay eggs or something. At that time, I had just quit my job working for a chicken farm (believe it or not). I was able to get a box FULL of eggs for completely free. If I had known, I might have held onto the job a little longer. But the eggs went up to about $12 a carton. That is highway robbery if you ask me. I stopped eating eggs for a while. The price has gone down significantly since then, but it was strange to think that eggs would be so valuable to people."},{"kind":"para","text":"Corn is valuable to the world during this famine, and they were willing to give up whatever they had to get some. Thankfully, Egypt had plenty of it, and so Joseph was able to start trading and selling to the people so that they could scrape by during this very great and difficult famine. Notice that it is Joseph that they all have to meet up with. God didn't give the people any other choice than to go to a man of God to find provision. These people were going to be told about the Lord's provision, and they will all have their chance to see that God was doing a work in this land because of this man Joseph. That should be enough for everyone around them to turn to the Lord... Of course, that won't happen, but it would be wonderful if it did!"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":37},{"start":38,"end":45},{"start":46,"end":52},{"start":53,"end":57}]},
    "42": {"chapter":42,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see things start to look up for Joseph. Pharaoh has a dream, and Pharaoh's butler remembers that Joseph is able to interpret dreams. Because of this Pharaoh takes Joseph out of prison so he can explain to him what his dreams were about. Joseph interprets the dream as flourishing land for seven years, and then terrible famine for seven years after that. He gives them a means and route to avoid being affected by the famine, and Pharaoh is so impressed with Joseph's tact and skill that he makes him in charge of the provisions of the land. Joseph is basically co-pharaoh at this point."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see that people from all walks of life are coming to Egypt to receive provisions so they can survive. The famine seems to be world reaching, and Egypt is the only place that has anything to keep people alive. It even brings Joseph's brothers in to try and find sustenance, and Joseph recognizes them instantly and must do something with them in order to get their attention and see where they stand."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"Egypt's Providence","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are two things that need to be addressed at this point. First, God will never let your sin go unpunished. These boys thought that the selling of Joseph was the last of the story. They would never have to worry about it again, and they would never have to deal with Joseph again. I honestly believe that this great famine is happening because these boys were not where they were supposed to be. The Lord moves the whole world for His children. That is proven throughout all of history. They needed to be in proper standing with the Lord, and at the moment they are not. They actually won't even consider the Lord until the day they come back and see Joseph. But I don't want to get ahead of myself."},{"kind":"para","text":"Second, God always has a way of meeting the needs of His own children. He will take care of those that He loves. Whether they love Him or not is a completely different story. Egypt’s prosperity is well known at the moment, and Jacob wants to send his sons to go get them food so they can survive. I imagine that they all felt pretty weary and worried. They had a lot going for them at one time when they left Laban's house if you don't remember. They were basically rich and had plenty to survive off of. Apparently that all ran pretty thin during this famine, and so they are forced at this point to find provision somewhere else."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":17,"title":"Joseph and His Brothers","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob has already lost a son, and that seems to haunt him even into his old age. Benjamin was not a little boy anymore. None of them are little boys. They are all full-grown men capable of making their own decisions with their lives. Jacob did not want to chance losing the only son left of Rachel, and so he commands that Benjamin stay home. That won't work, though. It won't work because the Lord wants to unite all His children. We will see that God necessitates unity in the tribes over and over again throughout the Old Testament (and I believe even in the New Testament if I can be honest), and He demands unity in the church today. He doesn't want a single person to be left behind (no matter how old or how young they might be)."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph recognizes his brothers right off the bat. He doesn't want his brothers to know that it's him, and so he treats them roughly. He makes them feel a bit uncomfortable around him. He is doing this to get some information from them to see why they are here. They explain that they are there to buy food because it seems to have depleted in their land (just like it has everywhere else)."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He remembered the dreams. That is key in this moment. His dreams are coming to fruition."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For, behold, we were binding sheaves in the field, and, lo, my sheaf arose, and also stood upright; and, behold, your sheaves stood round about, and made obeisance to my sheaf. And his brethren said to him, Shalt thou indeed reign over us? or shalt thou indeed have dominion over us? And they hated him yet the more for his dreams, and for his words.","ref":"Genesis 37:7-8"},{"kind":"para","text":"Just as his dream had envisioned, it is exactly how things are falling into place. Those dreams were not just Joseph's subconscious making up wacky stories. Those dreams were God's provision for Joseph, and that is what I believe kept Joseph going for so long. I don't think he ever forgot about the dreams he had. I believe it was those specific dreams that got him into trouble in the first place, and that is ultimately why he ends up as a slave in Egypt. Now the brothers are doing exactly what they were getting upset at Joseph about before. What an eye-opening moment for Joseph, and there's nothing he can say or do to them to make them see how right the Lord had been all this time. So he plays into their presence a little further and claims them to be spies. This could get them into a lot of trouble with Pharaoh because they could end up in prison. I don't believe that is what Joseph wants, though. He does not want to make them suffer the same fate that they made him suffer."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They are telling the truth. Honestly, I think they are telling the truth because they are at their wits’ end. This is the only chance they have at the moment to survive. If this deal goes sour, they may as well kiss their father and brothers goodbye. Any other time, I don't think you would see them telling the truth. I think they would have had no problem lying to try and get their way. But God has them in a corner, and they must do whatever it takes to get food to their family."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Egypt isn't naked at the moment. It's the only place that is prospering. It's a bit odd that Joseph goes about this situation in this way. Obviously, I believe Joseph is trying to find out if they are genuine or not. It has been some time since he has seen his brothers, but that doesn't mean they have truly changed. He wants to make sure there was a genuine heart change in them before he reveals himself."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think Joseph had taken a tally and noticed that Benjamin wasn't there. That was not lost on him. They, once again, speak more truth, and they further tug at Joseph's heart by mentioning him (even if it is briefly)."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph wants to see Benjamin. That is his blood brother, mind you. These other brothers are his half-brothers. Benjamin is his closest brother, and he wants to see how he is faring in this world. If his brothers could be so cruel to Joseph, what was stopping them from being so cruel to Benjamin?"}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"One brother will go and get Benjamin. The rest of the brothers will stay in prison until Benjamin is there. Again, the Lord wants to unite His children. That WILL happen, whether these brothers (or even Jacob) agree with it or not. They were all placed in prison, but they all were together while in their cell. Joseph had a lot of pull in prison, so I'm sure for those three days they were taken care of better than Joseph ever was."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":25,"title":"Repentant Brothers","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That should have shot a red flag in their brains. Something about an Egyptian saying that should not have sat well with these men. \"I fear God\" is a very rare thing to come out of the mouth of anyone that was not in the household of Abraham. They don't seem to take note of it though. I think it's because they don’t fear God. At least they don't yet. That time is coming quickly though."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now Joseph is letting them all go except for one. He is going to keep one brother as collateral until Benjamin is there. If they want to free their brother, they must act quickly."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They believe that the way in which they treated Joseph is what has gotten them into this mess today. Honestly, they are kind of right. They should have treated him much better. They should have loved their brother and had been willing to die in his place. They forsook him, however, and sold him into slavery, and couldn't care less about the feelings their younger brother had on the matter. They are chalking this up to reaping what they have sown."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's a good reminder to us. If we sow the proper harvest today, we will not have to worry about what we are going to reap. I think there are a lot of people who don't think about the reaping part. They think that their actions will just stay where they are and there is nothing that will come about it later on. That has never been true throughout history. People always reap what they sow, and these brothers are about to find that out the hard way."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph was speaking in an Egyptian language, and the brothers were speaking in a Hebrew language. They obviously don't know that it's Joseph, and so they think that Joseph isn't going to understand them while they talk about this. This was probably a bit of vindication for Joseph. He was probably happy at this moment that they were feeling guilt for their previous actions. Maybe he didn't even know about Reuben’s plea for him. He sees that they are in distress, and this had to have made Joseph feel better about the circumstances. This was something they were feeling guilty about, and that is a good sign."},{"kind":"para","text":"Repentance and forgiveness can come if we are able to address our conscience in the right way. Strangely enough, there are a lot of people in this world who don't know how to address their conscience, and so they ignore it and no change comes from it. These men likely ignored it for a long time, but they are tasked with addressing it today."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph is feeling all sorts of emotions, but he has to keep his course and show them that he means business. He took a moment to walk away and cry, and then he gained his composure and bound Simeon as a sign of how serious he is about this. Why Simeon? Well, I actually don't know why. I think it was probably the brothers that chose him, and Joseph just agreed to it."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They don't know that this is happening. Joseph makes this call to provide for his family for free. He is dropping little hints to them so they could figure it out on their own. When they find out about this, it should have been another red flag for them, but they don't seem to connect any dots. They just become even more fearful."}]}]},{"start":26,"end":28,"title":"Money Restored","entries":[{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"At least they are giving God the glory here. That has been a rare thing in their lives. It is obvious to them now that the Lord is the one that is putting in this work towards them. They are thinking this is a very bad thing and that God is punishing them, but the exact opposite is true. They don't deserve these mercies that the Lord is performing, and they certainly don't deserve forgiveness from Joseph, but that is exactly what they are receiving. They are receiving mercy and forgiveness. That is what we all receive from the Lord, and it is quite undeserved."}]}]},{"start":29,"end":34,"title":"A Recap","entries":[{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Just by the fact that Jacob saw one less child when they returned was enough to (I'm sure) concern Jacob. There's no way he felt good about seeing nine children return. He has already lost one, and now there is a second one gone, and they are saying they want to take his youngest? This seems absolutely insane."}]}]},{"start":35,"end":38,"title":"Jacob Is Distraught","entries":[{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I find this a bit comical. They thought that only ONE of their money sacks was restored. They assumed they must have just counted wrong and they would likely never be noticed. What a surprise to find that none of them paid for any of the food they just took. They are under the impression (at this moment) that they just stole a ton of food from Egypt. Egypt also has one of their brothers, and if word gets out that they stole all this food, their brother would be killed for sure, and the Egyptians would be following after them. They are all afraid at this point, and Jacob has no idea what to do."}]},{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Because of the money being restored (in Jacob's eyes), Simeon is as good as dead. These sons of his were just killing each other left and right. He knows that it's only around their brothers that trouble befalls them, and so it seems like a ridiculous venture to take Benjamin on this journey just to lose him too. Benjamin is the last of Rachel's children. If Jacob loses him too, it would certainly make Jacob's heart burst from sadness."}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Reuben, for some reason, has been the only voice of reason since the beginning of this. It would have been wise for the other brothers to have listened to him when they were deciding on what to do with Joseph. It is wise of them to listen to Reuben now. He is willing to sacrifice his two sons (which is how many I assume he has) if Benjamin doesn't return. He is confident that he will not allow anything to happen to Benjamin. They have to make things right, and they have to try and rescue Simeon at all costs. There can't be any more death in this family."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think that Jacob is feeling pressure at this moment. He has a faith in God, but that faith was being tried. He had to have thought that God was punishing him for the things he had done in his past. He must have remembered his thievery and past sins that he committed against his own brother, and he likely thought that the Lord was dealing with him because of it now. The Lord would comfort him in due time, but the Lord is somewhat tricking him as well. He is under the impression that Joseph is dead. What a surprise and joy it will be to find out that Joseph is still alive, and that he has been trying to connect to his family for a long time."}]},{"start":38,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob is not convinced. He does not want these boys to take Benjamin. He is willing to allow Simeon to die then to risk the life of Benjamin or the lives of Reuben’s two children. We will never know what happens next because this chapter ends here. If only there was a way we could turn the page and read on..."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":34},{"start":35,"end":38}]},
    "43": {"chapter":43,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, the brothers of Joseph come to the land of Egypt to look for provisions. They are struggling with the famine, and they have no other choice but to go to Egypt to figure out what to do next. Egypt is the only place that has the provocation that they need to continue to survive. Of course it's because of Joseph. Joseph really takes care of these men, but they don't see it as him taking care of them. They think they have somehow stolen their money back, plus the food that was given to them, and they return home to their father to tell him the story of what took place. One brother is in jail (Simeon) while they go to get Benjamin and present him to Joseph. That should have been a question they had before they left. Why does this strange man want to see their youngest brother so badly? It couldn't have just been about proof, there had to be something deeper than that."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, the family is seen expending all of their produce and coming down to the bare minimum with no choice but to take Benjamin with them and go and see Joseph to try and rescue their brother and get more food for them to survive."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":14,"title":"Decisions To Make","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think it was fairly obvious that God was doing a work in this land for this time. He was preparing the unity of the tribes of Israel, and He couldn't let it go with everything else taking place. They still have a brother in prison, and a father who is distraught. If there was even a thought to them that it would just be better off to leave their brother in prison, the Lord wouldn't allow it. They had to travel back, if not for their brother, then at the very least for more food. They had a lot of food given to them, but it still wasn't going to be enough as we will see in the next verse."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So it seems that some time had passed before they decided to go back and get more food. If I were them, I would have made this task far more immediate. They don't know what was going to happen. They are probably under the impression that their brother was already dead in prison, and so they didn't make too much haste for the occasion."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The reason they haven't gone back (at least according to Judah) was because Jacob wouldn't allow Benjamin to go with them. Benjamin was the last remaining child of the woman he really loved, and he wasn't willing to gamble the life of Benjamin. Jacob could have just travelled with them, but he is up there in age, and getting him there would be quite the venture. It would have been worth it, though, just to get my other son back out of prison. What do I know though? I am not living in this same situation."},{"kind":"para","text":"For me, Hell is a place that I would not desire any one in this world to be stuck in. I do not wish it upon my worst enemies. People wish Hell on people for very little today, but the Christian should not desire a single person to go there. I would go to the depths of Hell (if I were allowed) to bring my relatives out and into salvation. Of course, that's not the way it works. I have to bring them out while they are here on Earth. I have to keep them from going there today if I want them to receive eternal life. Sadly, that is not going to be the case. Many of them have rejected it even against my pleading for them. I pray for them, and I still love them, and I am wrapping my arms around their ankles, begging them to see the salvation that is given to them right before their faces. They just don't want to."},{"kind":"para","text":"This family has the chance to rescue the boys, and it would be wise of them to take that chance while they have it for the sake of eternity, and for the sake of their lineage (specifically the promises that God has made towards them, and they know that He is GOING to keep)."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They hadn't originally planned to talk to anyone. It should have been a pretty big hint to them that this man who was pressing them knew a little bit more than they realized. The moment he asked about a brother and a father being alive should have sparked some curiosity in their brains. It didn't, and it doesn't even seem to faze them here either. They just think that this is a normal question and a normal situation that took place, and now they just have to deal with it like normal people."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Seriously. If Jacob had just let them go to begin with, they would have been back already. It's either let them chance bringing their brother home and continue life, or risk killing everyone due to the difficulty of the famine. Dying of starvation is not nearly as easy as dying in prison or dying from being killed by Egyptian rulers. Starvation sounds like one of the most painful ways to go. Especially for children. The Lord has opened up providence for this family, and it would have been extremely wise if Jacob had just gone to God in prayer and asked about this matter."},{"kind":"para","text":"We may have noticed that Jacob has not said much to the Lord nearly this entire time he has been walking. Abraham had a very close relationship with God. That was something that should have been passed down to Isaac. Isaac doesn't seem to have too much conversation with the Lord either, and so that same mentality is passed down to Jacob, and while Jacob knows the Lord, he doesn't depend wholeheartedly on him. Jacob was true to his new name. He had a constant wrestling with the Lord even in his old age."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Yes, that is where he should have been looking all this time. He should have been saying from the get-go \"God Almighty, give you mercy...\" He is giving everything that he has left. Again, there's a famine, so the fruits, balm, honey, spices, myrrh, nuts, and almonds were extremely valuable commodities at this time. He tells them to take \"a little\" with them because there isn't much left over. This will be everything he has, and he is seen FINALLY trusting that the Lord will do what is right in this situation."},{"kind":"para","text":"God is a God of mercy, I think that should be well established. Jacob is depending on God's mercy here. He is relying on the fact that God has a merciful side. These sons, honestly, don't deserve mercy from the Lord. The things they have done up to this point prove the necessity of God's wrath. They have been mistreating their own flesh and blood, and completely butchering the name of Israel. But God is going to give mercy to those that seek His mercy, and at this time they had no other choice but to rely on the mercy of God or die."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":25,"title":"A Lunch Date","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph has been waiting for this moment for a very long time. They don't recognize him (his face has been mostly covered anyway), and he is giving the impression that he doesn't even speak their language. He is playing the part of a stranger quite well, but it's all leading to revealing himself soon. He wants to see where these men are at spiritually. He wants to bring them prosperity through their humility. He wants them to learn their lessons, and I think this is the only way they would have. They don't seem to learn any lessons any other way except through possibly losing a brother or two..."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They are going to the house of a very highly favored government ruler in Egypt, and they are scared of it. To them, they see this as a sign of suffering. This is a pleasure, but they don't see how this could be any good. Last time they spoke to Joseph, he was a little harsh on them (though they didn't know his kindness behind the scenes). They can't think of any good reason that they would be invited there unless it was to make them into slaves. It must be a terrible thing to constantly live with guilt in your life. You have made so many mistakes that you think your mistakes are coming back to haunt you. You are reaping what you have sown, and you are worried about it now. This is why it's important to pay attention to what you are sowing today. Otherwise, you might live in guilt, thinking that the Lord will give you what you deserve. God is merciful, though, and we will see his mercy played out through Joseph soon enough."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They are desperately pleading for their lives now. The steward knows what took place; they don't have to explain themselves to him. They think by trying to plead their case, it will get them out of harm’s way. They just don't want to be enslaved here (especially with Benjamin, because they had made a promise to bring Benjamin back, and if they don't, it will surely give their father a heart attack)."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This steward was giving more glory to God than the Israelites likely ever did at this moment. Joseph seems to have touched the heart of this steward for the Lord, and it shows by his answer to them here. He says \"It was your God who gave you the money back\" instead of \"Pharaoh's right-hand man has given you your money back.\" This, honestly, should have given them comfort after hearing this. It should have also been another red flag that something was up here. Then Simeon comes out, and that should be another sign of comfort to them. Simeon wasn't killed, and he is now sitting freely with the rest of his brothers."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They had a lot to give to Joseph at this moment (as if Joseph needed any of it). They really don't want to be stuck here, and so they are going to make as much of an effort as possible to sway Joseph into mercy. The Lord has been trying to get their attention over and over again through every step that they have been taking for the last few chapters now. They seem to continuously miss the Lord's grace and mercy towards them, and it is upsetting to us who read these accounts today. They miss the obvious signs, and they go back to depending on themselves. That will be the life story of the Israelites throughout the rest of Scripture. It is the life story of many of us reading this today as well. God makes His presence and grace known, and we seem to miss all the signs leading up to it. There will be a time for the brothers to see the obviousness of it, but for the present moment, they are missing it, and continuing to strive by their own hands and thoughts."}]}]},{"start":26,"end":34,"title":"Joseph See's Benjamin","entries":[{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I'm sure this threw Joseph off a little bit. He was trying to do a kindness towards them with this lunch, and he is being lavished with gifts before he even has the chance to relax in his own home. He wants to see where his brothers are spiritually set with this lunch."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, RED FLAG! Why would this man care about their father so much? Why does he keep bringing it up? It would have been something I would have asked about, but they don't seem to think anything special about it. They are all in fear of their own lives, and their father was safely at home at the moment, so they don't think anything more of it."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They are really grasping here. Whatever they can do to make Joseph feel like the one in charge and continually right."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"RED FLAG, RED FLAG, RED FLAG! An Egyptian extending God's grace is unheard of in this time. He is making it so obvious to them, and they are not grabbing any of the hints. Of course, Joseph knows this is Benjamin, but he can't let his identity be known quite yet. This is his only full-blood brother, and he is extremely excited to see him and know that they have not mistreated him."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Oof. I can't imagine the emotions that Joseph is feeling in this moment. He hasn't seen his family in some time, and his youngest brother Benjamin is no longer that young. He is a young man, and it's likely that Benjamin doesn't even recognize Joseph without a mask on, let alone with the covering. Joseph is having a hard time keeping himself together, but he doesn't want to spoil anything yet before getting to see where they are at and pushing the cause a little further. So he finds a place to cry for a moment and collect his thoughts. I believe this cry was not just an emotional one. I believe it was a spiritual one. It was a cry of thanksgiving to the Lord. He was not sure he would ever see his brothers again and has prayed that there would be a moment that God would allow him to address these things that have transpired, and the Lord is answering that prayer."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Another red flag. Why wouldn't Joseph eat with the Egyptians? They likely thought that this character (who they don't know is Joseph) was an Egyptian, yet he has his own table away from the rest of the Egyptians. They don't seem to take into account how strange this is, and so they are all sitting down for a meal, anticipating the next moves, and uncertain about how this will end (or if it will end well still). Maybe this whole lunch is for comforting the Israelites, so they can be taken easier? Maybe it was a ploy to try and make them out to be spies like they were accused of before. Maybe it was a way for Egypt to try and conquer and control Jacob and his family. They had a lot of questions, and no way for them to be answered."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They hadn't eaten like this in a very long time. The famine got them to a point where they were lucky to even have corn with their meal. They were starving. This meal was above and beyond what they deserved, and it was another obvious mercy of God. God's presence is in the fact that they were being so well taken care of in this meal, and they are missing it again. They are enjoying God's good gift to them, but they don't see it as God's good gift. They are seeing it as Pharaoh's good gift, and that further shows their low spiritual level (though God has made it very well known that He is present with them for the sake of Joseph)."},{"kind":"para","text":"We should be sensitive to God's presence in our lives. He gives us so much goodness and we seem to let it go right over our heads. His name is even present with us, and yet we don't seem to connect the two together. God has given you mercy and goodness, and it would be wise of us to recognize it when it happens. Honestly, it's a daily thing. We can find room to thank the Lord every single day for something. We should be doing that and writing it down in a simple journal so we can remember that God has always been good even in the middle of uncertainty."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":34}]},
    "44": {"chapter":44,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, the brothers finally convince their father to take Benjamin down to recieve more food, and return the money they think they stole. They are, instead, brought into Josephe's house for a meal and a good time. They are cautious, but they are grateful. We also noticed that God was continously providing His presence through every step and they seemed to be missing it over and over again. Even still, we will find that God has subtely influenced them in this chapter."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, they released, but not without one more test of prodding from Joseph. It seems like a bleak situation to these brothers, but to those of us who know the whole story, we get the birds eye view of the purposes and decisions being made here."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"Stealing Cups","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph is doing the same thing that he did before. He's doing this out of kindness. I know that for sure, but he is also doing this to toy with them and see if they have changed. He adds one more thing this time around."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This seems like a strange addition, but it's going to play right into Joseph's hands. While the brothers are all enjoying their time with the feast, Joseph has been devising a plan. He doesn't want this to be the last time his brothers are here. He does this specifically to Benjamin's bag. Why? Well, he is the one closest related to Benjamin. Benjamin is also the youngest. If they had no hard time throwing Joseph into slavery, what would they do to his youngest? Are they really changed? Do they really have remorse for their actions? This will be a very important test to them."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If you don't remember in the last chapter, they were concerned about their own well being (as well of that of their asses)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the men were afraid, because they were brought into Joseph's house; and they said, Because of the money that was returned in our sacks at the first time are we brought in; that he may seek occasion against us, and fall upon us, and take us for bondmen, and our asses.","ref":"Genesis 43:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"They were concerned about the lifestyle of their donkeys. Honestly (if I can be rash in saying so), their donkeys were a great representation of their mental and spiritual nature. They were stubborn and rebellious. The humor is not lost on me (if this is not humorous, I feel like the Lord could have reworded this differently). We will see donkeys represent the lifestyle of Israel quite a bit through scripture (specifically the Old Testament). That is not the state in which God wants His children to remain. He wants His children to be sheep. Most of us act like donkeys though."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It was a setup, of course. Again, the purpose is to see what lengths they are willing to go to for their brother Benjamin. If they didn't care about Rachel's firstborn, what makes them think they will care for the lastborn? Joseph doesn't know, however, that Jacob was serious about bringing Benjamin back. They would have to be sure Benjamin came back with them if they didn't want to kill their father and lose everything. But this will also teach them a valuable lesson about life and unity altogether. It is sad that it will have to take this much effort to break their wills, but it is sometimes how God must do things in order for us to be more sensitive and to have a better heart and compassion for our neighbors."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":13,"title":"Benjamin Caught","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Good point, but they don't realize that the cup was in their possession. I want to draw attention to the \"God forbid\" remark here. That is interesting to me. They have not made too much mention of the Lord in all this time, and they are now pleading that the Lord would attest to their innocence. The short time they were in the house of Joseph has influenced their mindset."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They are confident that they are innocent. They had no reason to take anything."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Only the one with the cup will be held responsible. The rest can go free."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This servant knew exactly where the cup was. He did this for dramatic effect. He did this to make them all feel confident of their innocence, and make them feel as if they were doing quite alright. They will feel good throughout all ten of the first brothers, and then it gets to Benjamin... The emotions that must have gone through their minds at that moment."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Benjamin was taken, and they were in great distress. They started \"renting\" (or tearing) their clothes (that was a common gesture in those days), and were not sure what else to do but go back to Egypt. They were not going to go home without Benjamin. They couldn't. They would destroy their own families and then some if that took place. They needed to get Benjamin back for their father's sake. God is going to teach them a lesson through this as well, as we will see in a moment."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":17,"title":"Judah Pleads for Benjamin","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is where the rubber meets the road. The men come straight to Joseph to plead with him. They have not talked about a strategy from here. They just know that Benjamin is not going to stay in Egypt; he is going to go home with them, or they will all die together. Joseph must have felt something stir up when his brothers returned for Benjamin. He must have seen the look of pain on his brothers’ faces and known that they were genuinely concerned about Benjamin. He still wasn't sure if it was because they just wanted their brother back or because they actually cared about their brother. But there will be a clearcut answer to their spirits’ desire in the next verse."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's interesting that it's Judah who is pleading here. He does not have a track record of greatness in this family so far. Of course, we know that this is the kingly lineage in the new land, but they didn't know that at this time. He is just a serious sinner like the rest of the world, and now he is pleading that God would show mercy. Not just Joseph, but God Himself. He has come to the end of himself (finally). I think they all have now."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph says, \"No, I don't need all of you to be a slave. I just need Benjamin. He was the one that had the cup.\" I think this was another prod for them to prove their seriousness about this. I can't see how it would be any more obvious to Joseph at this point, but he was looking for one specific revelation in his brothers that they hadn’t presented yet."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":34,"title":"Judah Willing to Trade Places","entries":[{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a verbatim retelling of the events. Judah was paying attention. It may seem as if none of the brothers were really bored with the decision that Reuben was making, but they all seem to be quite concerned about the matter. Maybe they weren't really concerned up until this point. It was starting to become too serious for them to just remain quiet about. Reuben had put his own children on the line for Benjamin. Now it was the rest of the brothers’ turns. Notice that they explain the situation about Joseph as well. Joseph didn't know what they had said to their father after he was sold into slavery. Judah opens up about it here and says that he told their father that their brothers were rent into pieces. Joseph now knows that they lied to their father about him, and so he thinks that Joseph is dead. Joseph didn't know this information before, and so it's being revealed to him right now. His father doesn't know he is alive."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Judah is volunteering himself to take the place of Benjamin. What a truly remarkable offer this brother makes. I believe they were all willing to give themselves up to save Benjamin. That is what real love looks like."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Greater love hath no man than this, that a man lay down his life for his friends.","ref":"John 15:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"These brothers were giving their own lives for one young man. They were willing to give up everything to see that Benjamin runs free back to their father. They are finally turning to what is right. They have come to the realization of how vitally important they all are to each other. They have messed up so many things, and they are tired of living that lifestyle of constantly trying to fix and bash through everything with lies and deceit. They are fessing up to their sins, and they are finally taking responsibility."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Judah can't even bear the thought of how his father would react to Benjamin not returning. He can't stand to think of the distraught and horrors that his father would have to face. He is growing compassion for his father's sake because of what he had done to him so many years ago now. He can't do it again. He won't. He isn't willing to play with his father's mind and heart like that."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob was a bit of a strange man. We know that his favorite was Joseph (that should not have been the case, but we can also see why Joseph was his favorite based on the lives each of the children led after Joseph was out of the picture). He was a pretty lax father as well. He didn't get after his children too much. He literally let them get away with murder. His children are now seeing how cruel they have been to the honor of their father, and he didn't deserve that (though his past shenanigans would say otherwise). God has dealt with Jacob enough for his past sins, and the brothers are realizing that now."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think it is a very important connection to realize that the desire of Judah will be actually fulfilled later on in scripture. Christ comes from this tribe, remember. It is this tribe and its lineage that brings in the Savior. It will be Jesus who gives His life a ransom not just for the tribe of Joseph, but for the whole world. He will give His life a ransom for imprisoned souls. His life will be given for those stuck in slavery. Given for the innocent and hurting. He will give His life to keep from the evil that will befall His Heavenly Father. He will give His life to keep us from being imprisoned for all of eternity in a place of torment and torture. That promise will be kept through the line of Judah."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are left with yet another cliffhanger. These past three chapters have seen no conclusion, and it's getting deeper and deeper every time we read. We will get the full scope and purpose of these events in the next chapter, but it's not without its emotional relevance. With that said, tissues might be in line for the next chapter."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":34}]},
    "45": {"chapter":45,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Joseph prods his brothers a little further by putting his cup in Benjamins bag and sending them on their way. They are stopped by Joseph's servant who finds the cup and brings Benjamin back to be imprisoned. The brothers don't let him go that easily, and Judah is seen requesting a trade... His life for Benjamins. Benjamin was not going to stay in the land of Egypt, and I think all of the brothers were willing to give their lives over to protect their brother Benjamin."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Joseph finally caves to all of the loyalty and repentance of his brothers, and gives over to the urge of proving who he is to them."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":15,"title":"Joseph Reveals Himself","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Judah was last seen begging Joseph to take the place of Benjamin, and it seemed (to the brothers) that all hope was lost. Now, suddenly, Joseph is crying. They must have felt a little weirded out by this. What could they do now?"}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's a bit of a somber moment, but I find it a little funny. These brothers did not expect this to come about. They were the ones that were crying, and suddenly this man standing before them is bawling his eyes out. They were sitting there watching this man suddenly break down in tears and have all the other Egyptians leave the room. The Egyptians still heard him crying (that's how loud he was crying), so I don't know the full reason for making them leave the room. I suppose I would rather people hear me cry than see me cry. I don't know if we ever view someone as beautiful when they cry. Though, there are people who you have compassion for through their tears. There are some people, however, who are ugly criers. Maybe Joseph was one of them."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well, yes... They said his father was alive. He reveals who he truly is and throws a question in the middle of it. Instead of the brothers jumping and hugging Joseph, they are left perplexed. They are seen as dumb sheep now, instead of wild donkeys. They are really trying to calculate what is going on here. I believe everything from the past few chapters is starting to flash before their eyes right now, and all those red flag moments are becoming increasingly obvious to them. They assumed Joseph was dead. Instead, they see Joseph as a very prominent leader of Egypt! More so, he has been in their presence this entire time!"}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph already told them this, but there must have been something on their face that didn't really believe it. He has to reiterate because they are very confused, and it's obvious to Joseph. It's funny because he says, \"Look at me, I am Joseph... You know, your brother... The Joseph that you sold into Egypt?\" As if they can mistakenly identify which Joseph was talking about here. They aren't confused about who he is; they are confused about why he is. Why he is wearing those clothes. Why he is still alive. Why he is so powerful and prominent in the land. Why he is revealing himself to them now. Why he is going through this much effort to unite with his brothers."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, I believe these brothers were living with guilt for a good majority of their lives. Any trouble they faced, they probably wrote it off as karma that they deserved because of what they did to Joseph. They didn't see it as stepping stones leading to their ultimate provision; they saw it as well-deserved judgement for their terrible life choices. Joseph says, \"Don't be angry with yourselves because of what you did...\" They had the right to be angry with themselves. They were not doing anything good. But the premise of this statement is that Joseph has already forgiven them. They didn't know they were forgiven, but Joseph is making it clear that they have been forgiven a long time ago."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was obvious to Joseph. It was not obvious to the brothers, but they will see that God has been drawing their attention for quite some time now. They will look back and say, “Boy, am I stupid... I see Him now. I realize that I have missed every sign that the Lord was present with me. I forsook Him...\" That is the same with all of us. We can look back after a great trial in our lives and say, \"Ah... God was there, and I didn't realize it.\""},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Jacob awaked out of his sleep, and he said, Surely the LORD is in this place; and I knew it not.","ref":"Genesis 28:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"May we realize where the Lord is as we travel through difficult terrain. We can always find places of safety and deliverance if we are searching out the Lord in the middle of pain."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God's providence played the biggest role in all of this. Joseph didn't deserve to be given into slavery. He did nothing wrong. Yet God allowed it because it meant salvation for Egypt and his family back home (as well as many others in the land around them). Jesus didn't deserve to be delivered into crucifixion. He did nothing wrong. Yet God allowed it because it meant salvation for the whole Earth. God sending Joseph to Egypt in that time actually saved a TON of people. It didn't just protect Joseph's family and the people of Egypt. It says:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And all countries came into Egypt to Joseph for to buy corn; because that the famine was so sore in all lands.","ref":"Genesis 41:57"},{"kind":"para","text":"God delivered such a large portion of people through the obedient struggle and wrongdoing of one man. You can see how the Bible folds into itself. Everything is a lead-up to Jesus Christ and His salvation given to the world. This is a small example of the promise of Christ, but it's an obvious one."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Goshen\" means \"drawing near.\" That is an interesting name for such an interesting occasion. Another providential foretelling of the Lord, no doubt. Why Goshen, though? Why not just go back to his homeland? Well, he answers that in the next verse."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are still a lot more years of hard famine to go through, and they would not survive outside of Egypt. Egypt had providence; the Lord provided that for them. So it would be wiser and easier for the family to move that way. He didn't offer them a place inside Egypt, though. He didn't have THAT much privilege. The Egyptians don't have dealings and dwellings like this with the Hebrews."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And they set on for him by himself, and for them by themselves, and for the Egyptians, which did eat with him, by themselves: because the Egyptians might not eat bread with the Hebrews; for that is an abomination unto the Egyptians.","ref":"Genesis 43:32"},{"kind":"para","text":"There were some things that were just not good for them, and Joseph didn't want to put them in any sort of distress with the Egyptians, even though they might be okay with it for a time. He set them up in land that was close by, though. Canaan is a land that is promised to them, but it has some work that needs to take place. This land in Goshen will be their land for now, and eventually, Egypt will do something about this land once the Israelites start increasing in number. That's a study for Exodus, though."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They still don't know what to say. Maybe they are pinching themselves. They are not sure if this is all real or not. So much information is being thrown at them at once, and they evidentially reel from a plethora of emotions at the moment. But the proof was literally staring them in the face. They couldn't deny what they were actually seeing."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So Benjamin was also told that Joseph was killed. That was the story he grew up believing. Yet he is getting all of this information for the first time and is filled with all sorts of emotions on the matter. He is likely angry, excited, confused, and happy all at once. If I were Benjamin, I would be pretty dizzy at the moment. The only thing he could let out was a cry."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They haven't said much, and now their adrenaline is starting to taper, and they are realizing what is going on. They cry, and kiss, and FINALLY talk. They are catching up, I imagine. Putting together a strategy and plan to get Jacob over to them. Figuring out how to tell Jacob exactly what occurred (because he is still under the impression that his son is dead). There was a lot to go through at this point, and it would not be very easy."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":24,"title":"Pharaoh's Offer","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph was such a likable guy that Pharaoh believed the rest of his family would be just as likable and prominent. Pharaoh doesn't know them, though. At this point, however, I believe they are ready for a change. A change in scenery, a change in lifestyle, a change in spirit, and a change in heart. They have learned some valuable lessons in these past few chapters quite quickly. It has been a rollercoaster for all of them. Pharaoh is pleased with Joseph, and so he is pleased that Joseph's family has found him. I don't know if Pharaoh knew that his brothers were the ones that sold him into slavery to begin with or not. But that won't matter too much at this point."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Pharaoh gave them personal permission to move that way. He was giving them the best of the land for the sake of Joseph. Joseph truly was their saving grace. If it were not for the Lord leading Joseph all this time, they would certainly die from this famine. He is sending wagons (which is the first we are hearing about this) to get their belongings and their families to make the trip over to this new land easier and quicker. The Pharaoh was excited to have more people like Joseph in this land."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that Joseph enjoyed giving Benjamin five times as much more than the others. Benjamin had never turned against him. Benjamin never had the chance to. Because of this, Joseph takes special care of Benjamin every time we see him. Even during the feast a couple of chapters back;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he took and sent messes unto them from before him: but Benjamin's mess was five times so much as any of theirs. And they drank, and were merry with him.","ref":"Genesis 43:34"},{"kind":"para","text":"It would be evidence for their father when they go to tell him what they just experienced."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was funny. Joseph says, \"Don't run away with the goods, please.\" The brothers still had time to mess this up. They won't. They will be sure to keep everything in order to make sure that this amazing miracle gets back to their father, and they are able to survive in the land of Egypt."}]}]},{"start":25,"end":28,"title":"Jacob Is Convinced","entries":[{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I hurt for Jacob's circumstance here. There are a few things I want to address, but before I do, may we be in this somber moment with Jacob. He is so calloused and hurt by his boys. They have done some very destructive and dumb things, and he can't even believe them when they are telling the truth. He wants proof now. Just bringing up the name of Joseph was a pain in his heart. He didn't want them to mess around like that; it was not good for his spirit or his health."},{"kind":"para","text":"We see that he is called \"Jacob\" in this verse. We have seen him called Israel several times over in the past few chapters."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Israel said, Wherefore dealt ye so ill with me, as to tell the man whether ye had yet a brother?","ref":"Genesis 43:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"Why is scripture going back and forth like that? Some scholars believe it is because Jacob represents his old sinful nature, and Israel represents his new nature in the Lord. How much stake you can claim in that can be debatable. It holds some merit. I won't argue one side or the other, but I believe that it's intentional and illustrates Jacob's continuous struggle with the Lord. His heart was in a struggle here, and he was feeling hurt by his sons’ constant lies and deceit."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob had a dwindling spirit. He was feeling weak. Notice it says his spirit \"revived.\" In the next verse, he is not referred to as Jacob anymore."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The proof of all the provisions from Egypt was enough to convince Jacob that his son was still living. His spirit was restored, and he was ready to see his son whom he always hoped was still alive. I think there was always a thought in his head that Joseph was never actually dead. I think he always knew that his sons were lying to him. I think he knew that something had happened (and he didn't know the full story), but he knew that it wasn't the complete story. There didn't have to be too much convincing here. He was sold when he heard the story and saw the provisions. Another cliffhanger. At least this one is a positive one to leave off on. We are anticipating Jacob seeing his son again after so many years of thinking he was gone forever."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":28}]},
    "46": {"chapter":46,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Joseph finally revealed his true identity to his brothers after prying and testing them for so long. He desires that they would go home and get his father and bring everyone they have back here to Egypt to live with them during the famine. They would be provided for with the watch of Joseph, and Pharaoh would no doubt be thrilled to have more help. It took some convincing for the sons to get Jacob on board with the idea of going to Egypt (and to believe that Joseph was still alive) but eventually he trusts in their story."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see Jacob do a little bit of tying up loose ends in his faith. He dedicates himself fully to the Lord at this moment. He is a changed man because of the salvation of his son Joseph, and the opportunity to see him again. He realizes that God has been with him since the beginning and starts to get this straight. We get a count of how many people are living with Jacob at this point, and we finally get to see Joseph and Jacob see each other for the first time in what seems like forever."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"God and Israel","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob spent a good portion of his life wrestling God, let's be honest. He left Beersheba when he was much younger without the fear of the Lord. He ran into the Lord in Bethel and was very undone ever since. We don't see him have very many \"faith-filled\" moments with God other than when we find him wrestling with God just before seeing his brother. He is told what land to stay in, and he finally gets there after so many years of trouble."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have come full circle at this point. He has seemingly lost so much. The Lord prospered him, but his children were doing wicked and heinous things. Behind the scenes, however, God was setting up and devising a plan to protect this family, and Joseph gets them the protection they needed all along. He only does this (mind you) through the provision of the Lord, and Joseph has made it abundantly clear that he recognizes that. So now Jacob returns back to his hometown, and the first thing he does when he gets there is sacrifice to the God of Isaac. That is his God too, by the way. He is finally acknowledging Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"I find it kind of fascinating that Joseph has a relationship with the Lord, seeing as there really is no fruit of it anywhere else in his family. His dreams may have played a pretty big role in that, I don't really know. Either way, this whole situation with Joseph and the brothers finally gets Jacob in a mode of worship to the Lord. It's something that Jacob should have done a long time ago, the moment that his son Joseph was said to have been killed. That's where most men go when they are in great distress. Not stubborn Jacob, though."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know if Jacob had a fear to go down there. It's interesting that Jacob gets this vision this night though. He hasn't heard much from God, and now that he has done something to grow closer to the Lord, God begins to speak to him. That is an important message to bring here. There are times when it might seem like the Lord is not very near. The truth is, we might be harboring some sort of sin that keeps us from going to Him and worshipping Him. Jacob didn't go to God because he never felt like he needed the Lord. He never thought of God as someone he can personally speak and interact with. He had glimpses and sparks of faith, but never fully dwelled in his faith. That is a danger for all of us reading this today."},{"kind":"para","text":"God doesn't go anywhere, you see, and Jacob just had to call out to Him for the Lord to open up. That is all it takes for us as well. If we have the Lord, and we are saved today, all we must do is call out to God in order for the Lord to reveal Himself to us. He will answer our prayers through Scripture. He will show you the truth of His Word. It just requires that first step of faith. It wasn't the sacrifice that got God's attention, it was Jacob's faith finally coming to a reliance on God. If there was any doubt in Jacob's mind before about going to Egypt, there should be no doubt in his mind now. God has given him the approval. He tells him it's a good idea that he goes down and He will prosper him there."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let's take a step back to what God told Jacob before when he first encountered the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And, behold, I am with thee, and will keep thee in all places whither thou goest, and will bring thee again into this land; for I will not leave thee, until I have done that which I have spoken to thee of.","ref":"Genesis 28:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"All the places thou goest\" is a pretty clear-cut promise. It was not as if the Lord just simply said, \"I will go with you.\" He openly says, \"I will go with you EVERYWHERE you go.\" Now God reassures him again that He will be with him when he goes to Egypt as well. He has a plan for the Israelites there. He says, \"I'm going to bring you there, but then I'm also bringing your body back when you die.\" He will also bring his lineage back into the land. He reassures Jacob that they will not remain there forever. The land of Canaan was promised to the line of Jacob through Isaac and through Abraham. That promise will be kept."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Egypt sent these wagons to bring them and everything they had back with them. They were a gift of grace and mercy from Pharaoh. If only Egypt remained with a Pharaoh like this. Of course (as a spoiler, but we should already know), the very next book shows a different Pharaoh who does not treat the Israelites kindly at all. At this moment, the God of Jacob and Isaac was extending grace and a path for His children here. He was getting them into a position for when the time came to take the land of Canaan (the land of promise)."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't imagine he had too much to bring back with him. There was a great famine, let's not forget. They had a lot of trouble in this time, and it was not as if they could make multiple trips back and forth every once in a while to pick up different people. If anyone was left behind, they would certainly die. He took his children, and their children, and anything that was remaining to them with him as he travelled back to Egypt in faith and obedience to the Lord. He was leaving home again. This time he was leaving it by the direction of the Lord. Last time he was leaving it by the fear of his sin."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":15,"title":"Leah's Lineage","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"First up, we have the lineage of Leah. Reuben has been an interesting fellow. He has made some downright terrible mistakes, but there were some good things to report about him. He was a man that had a heart. Out of everyone in this family of brothers, he was the only one that didn't want to kill Joseph. He wanted to bring Joseph back home. He was also the only one that was saddened by the idea that Joseph was sold into slavery. \"Hanoch\" means \"Dedicated.\" \"Phallu\" means \"Distinguished.\" \"Hezron\" means \"Surrounded by a wall.” \"Carmi\" means \"My vineyard.\" These could easily be names that are transferable to the Lord's provision and protection. Dedicated to the Lord, distinguished from the world around him, surrounded by God's protective wall, a part of God's fruitful vineyard. I'm sure that was not the intent behind the names originally, but they could be now."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jemuel\" means \"day of God.\" Interesting to see that he has named his child in commemoration of the Lord! \"Jamin\" means \"right hand.\" This is another name that could be easily transferable to the Lord's trusted. \"Ohad\" means \"United.\" That is what God has done, by the way. He has united these brothers back together again. That is something He seems to continually do throughout Scripture. \"Jachin\" means \"He will establish.\" The Lord establishes His people, and He is doing that by sending them to Egypt at this moment. \"Zohar\" means \"Tawny.\" \"Tawny\" means \"Orange-brown color.\" Kind of like Esau, who was red when he came into this world. \"Shaul\" means \"desired.\" This was the son of a Canaanitish woman. That was not a very pleasing thing to the Lord. God did not like the idea of them intermingling outside the chosen lineage. We can speak more about that in Deuteronomy and Joshua."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Gershon\" means \"Exile.\" That is a prophetic name in multiple facets. That is kind of interesting to me. \"Kohath\" means \"Assembly.\" Once again, quite poetic. \"Merari\" means \"Bitter.\" I don't imagine that Levi was in the proper mindset when naming this child."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Judah is a tribe to pay attention to. We can't forget that. This is the tribe in which Christ comes through. That is why we should take special note of it. We have made mention of these names before, but I will give them again just for clarification’s sake and ease. \"Shelah\" means \"A petition.\" Hey, the Lord has petitioned for your soul! He petitions for the souls of all of mankind! \"Pharez\" means \"Breach.\" We get a further lineage in this line with Hezron and Hamul. \"Hezron\" means \"surrounded by a wall.\" It seems Pharez liked the name and decided to name his child the same thing. \"Hamul\" means \"Spared.\" God has spared His children and His people. That is an obvious fact. Then back to Judah's last child: \"Zarah\" means \"Rising.\""}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Tola\" means \"Worm.\" That's not a very nice name. \"Phuvah\" means \"Splendid.\" I wonder which child he liked the most? \"Job\" means \"Persecuted.\" This is a different Job, obviously. Perhaps both share a similar story, though. \"Shimron\" means \"Watch-height.\""}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Sered\" means \"fear.\" \"Elon\" means \"Mighty.\" \"Jahleel\" means \"God waits.\" There are a lot of things that God is good at, but He is certainly good at waiting. He knows how to wait."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Thirty-three on this side of the family. That's a lot of family from one man. He had twelve children though, and we are still not done yet. Next we have Zilpah's children."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":18,"title":"Zilpah's Lineage","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ziphion\" means \"Lookout.\" \"Haggi\" means \"Festive.\" \"Shuni\" means \"Fortunate.\" \"Ezbon\" means \"Hasting to discern.\" \"Eri\" means \"Watchful.\" \"Arodi\" means \"I shall subdue.\" \"Areli\" means \"Lion of God.\" Actually, the Lion comes through the tribe of Judah. But we will see in Deuteronomy that Moses calls attention to a lion again through this line."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And of Gad he said, Blessed be he that enlargeth Gad: he dwelleth as a lion, and teareth the arm with the crown of the head. And he provided the first part for himself, because there, in a portion of the lawgiver, was he seated; and he came with the heads of the people, he executed the justice of the LORD, and his judgments with Israel.","ref":"Deuteronomy 33:20-21"}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Jiminah\" means \"right hand.\" \"Ishuah\" means \"He will resemble.\" \"Isui\" means \"he resembles me.\" \"Beriah\" means \"with a friend.\" Beriah had some children as well. \"Heber\" means \"comrade.\" \"Malchiel\" means \"my king is God.\" That should be the theme of all of the children of Israel, by the way. It should be our theme as well. Then Asher had a daughter as well. \"Serah\" means \"the prince breathed.\""}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we can add sixteen more members. For those of us keeping track, that equals forty-nine."}]}]},{"start":19,"end":22,"title":"Rachel's Lineage","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Rachel only had two children. These were the children that Jacob surely cared the most about. These are not the tribes in which will be the focus of the rest of Scripture, however. These specific men will go down in history as very important and integral characters of the faith, but it will be the line of Judah that will be the continual focus throughout the Old Testament leading into the New. It will be Judah, the one that starts off so rocky and disastrous that rakes in the royal lineage. Praise the Lord that He can use such a broken family for His glory ultimately."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Manasseh\" means \"causing to forget.\" \"Ephraim\" means \"I shall be doubly fruitful.\" Joseph will be in a moment when he meets with his father for the first time in a very long time."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Belah\" means \"destruction.\" Well, that's a little chaotic, I think. All of these other names are a little different, but this is just out there. \"Becher\" means \"Young camel.\" Was he just running out of names? \"Ashbel\" means \"A man in God.\" This is an interesting name! A man in God is what the Lord desires all men to be. It is what will provide strength to a man, mind you. \"Gera\" means \"A grain.\" \"Naaman\" means \"Pleasantness.\" \"Ehi\" means \"My brother.\" \"Rosh\" means \"Head.\" \"Muppim\" means \"Serpent.\" \"Huppim\" means \"Protected.\" These are fun names to say over and over again. I might want two children in the future to be named this. I would be calling them to me for no reason just so I could say their names together. You have to give points to Benjamin for creativity. \"Ard\" means \"I shall subdue.\""}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Fourteen more makes it sixty-three! This is quite the amount of people traveling to Egypt at the moment, and we are STILL not done."}]}]},{"start":23,"end":27,"title":"Bilhah's Lineage","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Hushim\" means \"Who makes haste.\" It seems that Dan only had one child. It is a small tribe to be sure."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jahzeel\" means \"God divides.\" God will divide in the later books of Scripture. When the church starts, we will find God MULTIPLYING. That is really how the Lord leads. \"Guni\" means \"My defender.\" In the Strong's concordance, there is a question mark next to this definition. It reads like \"My defender (?)\" I assume this name is presented as more of a question than a statement. Perhaps Naphtali was wondering who his defender was. His defender is the Lord. \"Jezer\" means \"Forming.\" \"Shillem\" means \"repaid.\""}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we are adding seven more, which brings our grand total up to seventy! Seventy people traveling back to Egypt. In the middle of a famine! I don't know if Pharaoh was expecting this many mouths to feed (I don't even know if Joseph was expecting this much following behind into Egypt), but boy are they going to be in for a surprise. The land was plenteous, though, and God gave them assurance about going, so there is no doubt in anyone's mind that this was the right call and decision both on Pharaoh and Joseph's part, AND on Jacob and his children's part."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Threescore and ten equals up to seventy. My math was right as we read in case you were wondering. Moses is sure to put the total down for us as well to save us the work of adding the people. I just did it for fun. It also helps us to do it so we can see how accurate and precise Scripture is. Go back and count them yourself if you don't believe my math or Moses' math."}]}]},{"start":28,"end":34,"title":"Shepherds of Israel","entries":[{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Judah is sent forward to set up provisions and make way for these seventy people coming into the land to make camp. I don't know of any spiritual tie-in to this, other than to say that it will be the case with our Lord. He is sent forth ahead to prepare for us a place."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And if I go and prepare a place for you, I will come again, and receive you unto myself; that where I am, there ye may be also.","ref":"John 14:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"Praise the Lord for sending His Son to prepare a place for you and me today. That is a great hope for us while we are dealing with and trekking through this world. If we didn't have that assurance, this life would surely drag on with uncertainty."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This should tug on your heartstrings. What a moment recorded here. We have a long-awaited meeting between Jacob and his most prized son of Rachel. I can't imagine the emotions. It says they wept and hugged for a \"good while.\" I don't assume that means a few minutes. I assume it means that it lasted a good while. I wouldn't want to move if I were there. I wouldn't believe this to all be real."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was a weird first thought. It's another funny verse to me. Jacob says some funny things. He finally gets to see his son after quite some time, and he says, \"Well, I'm so happy about this event that I can die now.\" Maybe Jacob was holding onto hope for some time because he always knew there would be some clarity and grace from the Lord. Maybe he always felt that Joseph never actually died. He wanted some final proof. Perhaps the Lord wouldn't let him die for that reason. They both needed this long hug that they had been waiting for a lifetime to make. Jacob was ready to be with the Lord. Notice his name is \"Israel\" here. He is in absolute faith in the Lord. He is confident about his eternal destination and is pleased that the Lord has let him live to see this day. He's ready to go to his true home now."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph seems to ignore his father's comment about dying. He is planning a life for all his family over here in this land. Pharaoh could use some handy men in livestock. Jacob was great at tending sheep, and I believe all his sons were good at it too. That is what they were last seen doing before selling Joseph. Joseph picks back up where they left off. This time they are going to do it right."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Shepherds were looked down on in Egypt. That's funny because they can use that meat and that provision for great gain in their clothes and in their sustenance. The Israelites were going to be shepherds for Egypt. Think about that for a moment. If the Israelites are to be a representation of the church in the Old Testament, we have a great representation of what we are as the church. We may not be tending to literal sheep, but we are tending to the Lord's flock. He is the owner of the field. He does not see the shepherds as a disgrace. He sees them as the most important workers. He puts them in positions and entrusts them with his most important ministry. Attending sheep requires the ministers to be patient and kind. They are to be willing to work together and confident in their leadership. Shepherds are important to keep the sheep safe and well-fed. That will be Israel's job in the new Egypt (we will see this new Egypt in our next book), and that will be our job in this world as the called-out ministers of the Lord."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":34}]},
    "47": {"chapter":47,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we finally get to see Jacob trust and rely on the Lord. He gives a sacrifice to God and gets a vision from him about moving to Egypt. The Lord is okay with it, but there will be a time when they will have to come back. They won't really do it on their own though, and so God will place a situation and path for them to get back into the land they belonged in (that's for a future study). He meets Joseph for the first time in a long time, and it is a very warming and touching section of scripture."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Joseph introduces his family to Pharaoh, deals with the toughest parts of the famine, and then sets up his family for success in this land of Egypt. It ends with the final promise from Joseph to bury his father in the land of promise."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":10,"title":"Jacob and Pharaoh","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"At the end of the last chapter, Joseph literally says this is what he is going to do. It's refreshing to know that his words and dignity are not tainted by his circumstances. Honestly, if anyone had a right to become hardened and withdrawn from moral values and general life consistency, it would have been Joseph. We might notice that throughout this entire time of Joseph's journey, he has constantly been presentable to the Lord. He has made every effort to honor God, and yet we have not seen God (at least through these words) visit with Joseph one time. Joseph's faith pleased God, and so by that alone, God worked."},{"kind":"para","text":"You might complain today and say, “Well, God has never shown Himself to me. He showed Himself to everyone else in Scripture, but He never shows up when I need Him.\" Look at Joseph. Joseph could have easily said that same thing, but he doesn't, because he fully trusts that the God of his fathers is real and present, and he has seen the Lord work in his life even without seeing the Lord specifically."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was a trust moment for the brothers here. Joseph could have told them to say this just to get them into trouble. He could have twisted the script and thrown them under the table like they did to him so many years ago. I'm curious to know which five men Joseph chose. He likely chose five men that were the most prominent in throwing him into the pit before. That would have made the most sense for me. It was a show of forgiveness. Judah was likely one of them. I couldn't say for sure, though. They take Joseph's advice either way and call themselves shepherds."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Pharaoh is even trusting them with his OWN cattle. That is how much trust and respect he has for Joseph. It was for Joseph's sake that they have this opportunity and privilege to live in a thriving location at this moment. The Egyptians didn't want to deal with the shepherding aspect of taking care of the animals, and so these people were perfect for that job if they were already doing it anyway. They actually were good at it. They knew how to do it already. They might not have done it in quite some time, but they grew up doing it, and Joseph knew that they would fit well in there, and they would have a job that kept them busy, and kept Egypt thriving."},{"kind":"para","text":"Some might be under the impression that Joseph made his brothers lie about their occupation. I don't believe that to be the case. We have seen them take these matters seriously before, and they are going to need to take it seriously now if they are going to live in this new land and survive. Otherwise, they will be cast out and aside, and their whole lineage will die off."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Pharaoh likely didn't know much about what a \"blessing\" was. He gladly accepted it, though, and Jacob gave him a proper one. He wanted Pharaoh to know how thankful he was. Pharaoh was blessed for sure, and he will be blessed as long as he lives. Sadly, everyone's life comes to an end, and that is what Pharaoh is focused on right now, looking at Jacob."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems (at least to Pharaoh) that Jacob was quite an old man. It must have looked pretty strange to Pharaoh that this man was still alive. What was he even staying around for? Pharaoh doesn't know the full story of Joseph and his brothers, and so this will be the only bit of information he is going to receive."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He's one hundred and thirty years old, and he calls his life \"few and evil.\" That's funny to me. The things he has gone through have worn on him immensely, and he is certainly feeling it at his old age. We will see that he has a few years more to go. He will die at one hundred and forty-seven years old. He gets an extra seventeen years with his son, Joseph."},{"kind":"para","text":"He gives homage to his father here and says, \"My life pales in comparison to them.\" He doesn’t go into any further detail. That's all he wanted to talk about. He is one hundred and thirty, and his life has been difficult. By all means, he had some pretty great highs in his life. He had a lot going for him and plenty of children to boast about. But he doesn't do that because he realizes that the Lord had been humbling him since the beginning of his trickery."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob gave him another blessing. He didn't want to hash up his past. He just wanted to be a blessing to the people that were giving him a blessing. That is what a man of God looks like, honestly. They are looking to be a blessing."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":12,"title":"Provisions For Everyone","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So they are living in this land of Goshen. Rameses was another name for it, apparently, and they have the best of the land for their job titles. Their livestock would have plenty to graze on for quite some time. We will see that they will become a great multitude of people in Egypt because of this moment here. It sets up the course of history for the Israelites and the rest of this book of the Bible."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The land had plenty for the animals to eat from, but Joseph and Egypt had plenty of provisions stored up for the families to eat from. That is the main thing that Joseph is focused on. He wants to keep his family alive. He wants to bring them into prosperity with him. He doesn't want to leave anyone behind. Through all of those terrible circumstances he faced, he has grown more compassionate for his family, and has fallen deeper in love for the hurting souls around him."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":20,"title":"Running Low","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The bread seemed to have depleted in Egypt as well now. Of course, Canaan ran out of food; we saw that happening before they even left. Egypt still has plenty gathered though, if it's not bread. It would still be a bit of a stretch, but they would make it work because the Lord would provide. God had already promised it, and God always keeps His promise. It's a good thing that Jacob and his family didn't stay in the land of Canaan. They wouldn't be alive at the moment."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The food had to be purchased somehow, and they were running out of funds and out of food. Their money was becoming worthless because it was just being recycled, and they were losing more than they were gaining. They needed food more than the money anyway. So they are pleading and begging with Joseph to help them in provision. Joseph never said that this famine would be easy. He set up everything possible to provide and keep them floating for the seven years that they will face this famine, and I believe he did a great job at that. He's in a bit of a bind here though, and so he is looking to try and get a better solution to help and prosper these people."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The cattle are valuable. Joseph has a whole family that can take care of that now. They didn't have that kind of provision before Jacob and his children moved in. That would be extremely valuable later on. The people couldn't afford to keep their animals alive anyway, so what good would it do them to keep the cattle when it wouldn't feed them?"}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a miracle right here. We were just told in verse 13 that there was NO bread. There was nothing left in the land to give. Somehow the Lord provided bread. Joseph made a deal, and the deal was kept by some sort of miraculous provision. Whether Joseph knew that he would be gifted by the Lord's abundance or not, I could not say. You might read this and say, \"Surely there was more of an obvious reason for that. Maybe there is a translation error?\" No. I don't believe that to be the case. I believe there was no bread, and somehow the Lord multiplied bread by the faith of Joseph. This is the first time we see the Lord do this, but it's not the last time."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"When Jesus heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities. And Jesus went forth, and saw a great multitude, and was moved with compassion toward them, and he healed their sick. And when it was evening, his disciples came to him, saying, This is a desert place, and the time is now past; send the multitude away, that they may go into the villages, and buy themselves victuals. But Jesus said unto them, They need not depart; give ye them to eat. And they say unto him, We have here but five loaves, and two fishes. He said, Bring them hither to me. And he commanded the multitude to sit down on the grass, and took the five loaves, and the two fishes, and looking up to heaven, he blessed, and brake, and gave the loaves to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude. And they did all eat, and were filled: and they took up of the fragments that remained twelve baskets full. And they that had eaten were about five thousand men, beside women and children.","ref":"Matthew 14:13-21"},{"kind":"para","text":"It should not be an astounding thing to us to believe that God can multiply food out of nowhere. God blessed the land, and it was because Joseph had faith in the Lord. That has been obvious to us since first reading about Joseph."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Traveling back and forth was not an option for these people anymore. There was nothing left to give to Egypt except themselves. Their money failed, and they had no more herds to give, so now the only thing left was to work for Pharaoh. They likely believed they would be let go after they got on their feet, but once you are a slave to Egypt, it was like you were signing your life away. There was no leaving that life behind. They should have seen the faith of Joseph and turned to the God of Joseph. Perhaps Joseph should have told them to turn to the Lord as he was providing this last bit of provision for the people. The Lord never reveals that necessity to him, though. He doesn't say much of anything at all, but just tries to find earthly solutions to the famine problem. This would certainly be the last line that they could possibly give."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph doesn't seem to address enslaving the people. I think he is trying to avoid that problem altogether. He wants to find a solution that doesn't involve these people giving their lives to be forever indebted to Pharaoh because that is a passed-down problem. But there will be no choice if they want to come and survive under Pharaoh’s roof. So Joseph owns the lands, and he is able to do something with them."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":26,"title":"Indebted For Life","entries":[{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph gets the people closer to the food supply. He stations them right on the borders of the central areas of the provisions. He doesn't touch anything belonging to the priests, though. Apparently, Pharaoh had special provisions for them for some reason. You would think that the Pharaoh would have turned everyone to the God of Joseph, but he doesn't seem to question it or mind it. He has his vices, and he is not willing to get rid of them, even if it means prosperity for his people."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The land was owned, and the people had no place to go, and so Joseph put them to work in the land. They will now grow crops. In a famine, crop growing would be nearly impossible. This is ANOTHER miracle. There is only one year left of the famine, and they just have to hold out a small bit longer before they will start to see prosperity again. Joseph is trusting that these crops will grow, and if (or when) they do, a fifth of it will be taxed to Pharaoh. They will be responsible for the rest (using it to grow more food and distributing it throughout their households). That should be enough for everyone to survive from. I'm sure they were wondering how the grain would grow in the famine. It hadn't been growing before, so what makes Joseph think it will work now? I don't even know if Joseph knows for sure that it will work. He is just praying for a blessing and miracle and that the famine will end in the year that the Lord gave him warning about. By this time next year, there should be fresh grain for all the people to survive off of."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph didn't just get these people to become slaves to Pharaoh; he is trying to help these people. The grace of Joseph was making people feel indebted. Grace should make everyone feel this way. I have met a lot of graceful men, and they have done some astoundingly abnormal things for me and my family. One man bought furniture and a toy car for my daughter when we first moved out to Texas. He didn't expect anything in return. He just wanted to be a blessing to me and my family and extend an act of grace for this poor California family who was way out of their element. Another man paid for my daughter’s camp this last year. She was so excited to go, and we did not have the funds to do it. I was not going to let her go (though she had helped with fundraisers throughout the last couple of years, but that's besides the point), but there was a kind, graceful man that paid her way through. They did not expect anything in return. In both of these instances (and I can name plenty more), I felt extremely indebted. What can I do to ever repay them back?"},{"kind":"para","text":"The grace of our Lord should make us feel indebted as well. We should be so overwhelmed by the fact that Jesus gave His life for us that we want to do something in return for it. We should not be sitting idly thinking we deserve the grace of God. Rather, it should move us to action because we certainly don't deserve it. We could never pay it back, of course, but that does not mean we shouldn’t desire to give what we can to the Lord for His goodness to us. These people were indebted to a graceful character, and they were wanting to do whatever it took to keep their families alive and to serve the one that has been gracious enough to help them survive this long."}]}]},{"start":27,"end":31,"title":"The Burial Promise","entries":[{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"While the rest of the land was falling apart and facing severe consequences from the famine, you have Jacob and his lot over here growing and MULTIPLYING EXCEEDINGLY. They were no longer affected by the famine."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob had seventeen years to spend longer with Joseph. He certainly made up for all those lost years, and while most of his life was \"evil and few,\" he had seventeen good years of abundance and good faith. The Lord was always gracious to him, though, whether he wanted to see it or not. God took care of this family. It should have seemed (to Jacob anyway) that God was specifically focused on him. It was Joseph, SPECIFICALLY (from his own family), that kept him and the rest of his children alive. The Lord had preserved this family, and they were growing in the middle of a famine. God took special note of this family, and that should have been a great sign to Jacob a long time ago. He has seventeen more years to realize that, though, and I believe he finally does."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The putting the hand under the thigh thing was a sign of respect and promise. We have seen this happen before a few times in this book of the Bible. The interesting thing is that Jacob didn't want to be buried in Egypt. He wanted it to be a promise that he will be buried in the land that he belongs in. He is keeping a promise from the Lord. He does not belong in Egypt, he belongs in the land of promise! He also doesn't want his lineage to be comfortable in Egypt."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will see soon that there are promises and provisions that Jacob is going to give to each of his children in regards to that very thing. He wants there to be a reason that they end up leaving Egypt, and going to where they belong. They might forget the promise of the Lord giving them a land and freedom. Most probably do forget, but not all of them. Jacob held to the hope of God's promises, and so he is believing by faith that God will keep it. He wants his body to be buried in the land of promise because it was a sign that he believed God's promise. He trusts Joseph the most with keeping this promise to return him to the land, and it will be Joseph that buries him there."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":31}]},
    "48": {"chapter":48,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Joseph was setting up to help the nations around him prosper during the most difficult parts of the famine. They were all coming to him for direction because he was the only one with provisions in all of the land. He couldn't just give it out for free, because then it would all be gone before they had the chance to help as many as possible. So instead he gave them ideas about what to do and how to keep thriving. They follow his commands, and end up getting to a place where they are in necessity to work under Pharaoh to try and save their land. The chapter ended with Joseph making a promise to bury his father in the land by in Canaan (A promise that will be kept much later on)."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we pick up with Jacob very much up there in age and desirous to give blessings to Joseph. Rather than giving blessings to Joseph specifically, he adopts Joseph's two sons and provides the blessings to them giving Joseph a double portion of blessing because of it."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"Adopting Grandsons","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Even in his sickness, Jacob was able to muster up some strength to see his son again. He didn't have much strength, but there were some things he wanted to establish in these final moments that would set the course of history. He had a promise from the Lord, and that promise is going to have to be passed down now, just like it was passed down to him and passed to Isaac before him. The Lord has made some promises to this lineage, and the promises are going to be remembered. Jacob illustrates this by using the last of his strength to speak to Joseph."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It was the land of Canaan that they were inheriting, by the way. They are not currently in that land because of the famine, so Joseph is understanding that it is not the land of Egypt that they are supposed to stay in. Jacob had a lot of back and forth with the Lord, and most of the time it would seem as if he was just ignoring God altogether, but he is illustrating here that what God has said did not go unnoticed. He remembered it whether he believed it at the time or not. He has come to a point in his life where he has seen God make good on promises, and he is ever trusting in the Lord now that his favorite son was alive. He must be thrilled to share this information now. I don't know if this was ever shared with his children before. It doesn't seem like it was. It seems as if Jacob held this in his heart for so many years, and now that he sees Joseph as such a Godly man, he feels wise enough to explain his own experience with the Lord."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a very important verse to understand. Joseph doesn't have a tribe if you don't notice. His two sons get their own tribes, which would make the total number thirteen rather than twelve. We will notice that Levi is usually left out of the counting of the tribes as well. Their inheritance was not in the promise of the land. Their inheritance will be in the promise of the Lord. They will inherit God. But Jacob adopts his two grandchildren and counts them as one of his own. Just like Reuben and Simeon are Jacob’s, so will Ephraim and Manasseh be his."},{"kind":"para","text":"You might say, “Well, why Reuben and Simeon?\" Well, they were the firstborns, so they get the highest privilege. Except that is not how it is going to work in this family. Jacob was good at stealing blessings before, but now he will be good at dishing them out. These two sons of Joseph are not a part of the original lineage because they were born as Egyptians, not as Hebrews. But they will be counted as tribes, and they will gain an inheritance by Jacob (which is ultimately by the Lord)."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He is speaking about other children that Joseph will have. We didn't know that Joseph had other kids. Perhaps he doesn't at this moment, but there will be a time when he does. Jacob cannot make this same promise to them because they are not a part of receiving the inheritance. So the claim can't be given to them, because they are not born yet. So if they want to be blessed, they will have to take place under their brothers Ephraim and Manasseh. Jacob can't adopt any of the other sons of Joseph after he passes, so the buck has to stop at these two."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Bethlehem is a very important place for us to keep track of and consider as we read through Scripture. It's a place that will be brought up over and over again. It's the place in which our Savior will be born."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now when Jesus was born in Bethlehem of Judaea in the days of Herod the king, behold, there came wise men from the east to Jerusalem,","ref":"Matthew 2:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"It's also where Rachel died and was buried. The greatest kings will come from this town, mind you. It's where David will be born, and it's where Ruth will meet Boaz, and many victories and miracles will take place. It's a little town with a lot of great history."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":16,"title":"Giving a Blessing","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, it was not as if Jacob didn't know that Joseph had children. Jacob wasn't able to see very well. His father couldn't see very well in his last days either. It's interesting because Isaac wanted to bless Esau when he couldn't see, and Jacob tricks him. In this instance, Jacob wants to bless Joseph's children, and he isn't tricked. He deserves to be, but Joseph doesn't have that kind of heart or desire. He wants his children to be blessed by his father."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I am sure that Jacob was thrilled to have his favorite son back in his life. Even more thrilled to call Joseph's children his own. This moment is not just a fleeting one. It's not just a grandfather hugging his grandchildren. This is a genuine love for this family. He actually genuinely loves these children. He is not just hugging them, he is embracing them. \"Embraced\" means \"to hold someone closely in one's arms and accept them.\" They are now a part of the family of Jacob. They were his children. This was likely a serious and somber moment, and these children (now teenagers at this point) were feeling pretty overwhelmed. They probably didn't really understand what was going on here. Thankfully, the tribes of Manasseh and Ephraim will read this later on and consider themselves blessed that this moment takes place in history."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a statement. Jacob was under the impression that Joseph was dead and figured he was never going to see Joseph again. Now he is seeing his grandchildren's faces as well, thanks to the Lord. God preserved them. Jacob recognizes that and gives God the glory. You can tell that Jacob has come quite a long way in his faith since we were first introduced to him. He likely would have never spoken about the Lord like this before if these things hadn’t happened with Joseph. It taught Jacob a thing or two about the importance of faith and trust in the Lord."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I guess these two boys felt a little uncomfortable about the situation. It seems they were trying to get away from it, but the blessing they receive will be well worth the kisses and embraces their grandfather is giving them. Joseph bows down in adoration to his father on behalf of the goodness of God. He also recognizes that God was providential in their meeting again, and that He was the reason that Jacob had not died after so many years later. He is honoring his father here for the blessings he is bestowing on his children."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The right hand and left hand are significant to honor and power in a family (or in a business). We say, \"I like so-and-so, he is my right-hand man.\" That means he is the most trustworthy. He is the one that has the higher honor over everyone. The left-hand man still matters, but he is not in the same position as the right-hand man."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Manasseh deserved the place of higher honor because he is the firstborn. Joseph thinks that Jacob has mistaken his hand placement and is almost giving the blessing to the wrong person. Notice he had to cross his arms in order to place his hands. It was not as if he couldn't just have moved the children to the proper positions; rather, he placed his hands on each boy’s head to signify something that we will open up more in a moment."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Capital “A,\" mind you. They were receiving equal blessings, but the placement of the hands will be something that Joseph takes notice of. He is identifying God being in his life since the beginning here, though. It was God that brought him through his hardships. It was God that fed him his entire life. God sent His Angel (that is Christ) to redeem him. It will be God's gift to the whole world in due time. The God of Abraham and Isaac will be blessed with children above and beyond just the tribes of Israel."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":22,"title":"The Older Serves the Younger","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph wasn't happy that Jacob was doing this. The honor belongs to the older child, not the younger. But Jacob knows what he is doing. He has learned a lesson from his own past. He has learned that it will be the last first and the first last in the house of the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"So the last shall be first, and the first last: for many be called, but few chosen.","ref":"Matthew 20:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph thinks it's a mistake, but Jacob will make it clear that it is no mistake."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He is not dumb. He is doing this on purpose. The nation of Ephraim will have a multitude of nations, and Manasseh will be a great people. They will both receive blessings, but Ephraim is getting the bigger blessing here. That is intentional. Why? Well... I don't know. I assume he is mimicking what he has learned in his own life in regards to being the servant of his brother. He was to lead his own older brother, and his older brother was to be blessed because of it. That is still the case in this situation. That is what Jacob wants to do for this family, as a symbol and representation that the things that befell Jacob and Esau are evidenced by the promise here being kept. Ephraim will become a much mightier nation than Manasseh."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are assured that Ephraim will be the more blessed, but the people will desire that others would be blessed like them both. They will say, \"God make thee as Ephraim and as Manasseh\" when they are directing a blessing on others. That is what the blessings of these two look like. Whether one was greater than the other didn't matter; they were both going to be greatly used and greatly blessed, and everyone else around them would recognize that."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It will not be Joseph who goes by the way. It will be Moses, a son of the Levitical tribe. He will be the one that brings the tribes back to where they belong. He will have quite the journey ahead of him, though. That's a conversation for a later chapter."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph will have a greater inheritance through his two sons than any of his other brothers. He went to Joseph first to give him the blessings first. Joseph was not the firstborn in the family, but he was the favored one. It was through Joseph that Jacob wanted to give the greater blessing because he was a man of God. He was a faithful man. His brothers did not live lives like that."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then cometh he to a city of Samaria, which is called Sychar, near to the parcel of ground that Jacob gave to his son Joseph.","ref":"John 4:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"He is given special and specific land. He would be buried there. Because he is buried there and it was given to him, that land could not be given to anyone else. That was his for keeping. It belonged to his family (Ephraim and Manasseh specifically). It's an interesting place to note as well. This is a place where Jacob's well is located in Scripture. Jacob will be buried here later on."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the bones of Joseph, which the children of Israel brought up out of Egypt, buried they in Shechem, in a parcel of ground which Jacob bought of the sons of Hamor the father of Shechem for an hundred pieces of silver: and it became the inheritance of the children of Joseph.","ref":"Joshua 24:32"},{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob seems to have earned this place by taking it from the Amorites. It seems they took it by force, and he takes it back by force. This land is hard-fought land, and it's hard-fought land still today. It's known as Askar today. It's all over the news since the current war happening in Israel and Hamas."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":22}]},
    "49": {"chapter":49,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: It has been a wild and crazy ride with these sons of Jacob. We have seen so many things take place so far (especially with Joseph and his life in Egypt). Jacob spent the last chapter speaking alone with Joseph and his two grandsons Ephraim and Manasseh. He wanted to provide a special blessing to them and adopt them into the family before he delivers the rest of the blessings to his children."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is what this chapter is about. He is going to take the last moments of his life (and the little strength he has left) to sit up and give the future blessings and promises to the rest of his children. It is a fascinating chapter that can be used to dive very deep into the different tribes of Israel (which I won't do too much diving in that manner), but it's one that provides us with some interesting forethoughts about what we study later on in scripture. The things mentioned in this chapter all end up become very real and prominent in each of these tribes and lineages."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"Jacob Gathers His Sons","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We will get into the bigger promises of the tribes in Deuteronomy 33, but for now we are going to get the promises to the sons of Jacob that will influence the tribes. When it speaks about \"the last days\" here, it is not speaking about the end of times. It is speaking about the last days of Israel specifically. There are some guarantees and promises that Jacob is going to make to each one of his children which will have a direct and exact impact on their future, and on the leading of each tribe. Joseph has already been given a blessing to Ephraim and Manasseh in our last chapter. He will now give the blessings to each child through the rest of the family starting with his firstborn."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Gather yourselves together\" is something that should be (and will be) continually reminded to God's children. We will see that God continuously desires to bring His children together and unify them throughout the Old Testament. We will see that is what God wants for the church in the New Testament. Sadly, both of these people struggle with this very thing. If we are able to have a strong head of followership on our shoulders, it would be much easier to remind your brothers and sisters around you to follow the Lord. May we take the command to gather very seriously and prominently. That is the hardest thing for our churches to do these days... I am going to do my best to reference the promises of Moses from Deuteronomy 33, and match them up to our tribes here today."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":4,"title":"Reuben's Strength","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The promise of Moses reads:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let Reuben live, and not die; and let not his men be few.","ref":"Deuteronomy 33:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"Reuben has some power. He is the son of Jacob's youth, when he was able to do things a little easier. Honestly, Jacob and Reuben are a lot alike. He calls him \"unstable as water.\" There weren’t many moments in Jacob's life where you could refer to him as \"stable.\" He was constantly in battle with his flesh and with his faith. Abraham had a lot of battles with the Lord as well. Jacob, I felt, battled harder. It took him longer to trust God. Reuben is a pretty close image to Jacob's constant fighting with God, and he will \"not excel.\" He will run the race, but he will also be content to just stay where he is. He won't excel any further than that. It's because of the sin of going to \"his father's bed to defile it.” If you don't remember, this is spoken about in Genesis 35."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it came to pass, when Israel dwelt in that land, that Reuben went and lay with Bilhah his father's concubine: and Israel heard it. Now the sons of Jacob were twelve:","ref":"Genesis 35:22"},{"kind":"para","text":"We spent some time talking about it there, so I won't talk about it here. Because of this, he will have a bit of a challenge in his life, and his lineage will face that battle throughout. It is as if Reuben was now demoted from being firstborn. (Of course, you can't demote anyone in your family, but that was the equivalence of it because he was not going to get the blessings of a firstborn, but Joseph's children receive those blessings.) All of these children kind of ruined their blessings in some way or another."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":7,"title":"Simeon and Levi's Cruelty","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Moses' blessing to Levi reads:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And of Levi he said, Let thy Thummim and thy Urim be with thy holy one, whom thou didst prove at Massah, and with whom thou didst strive at the waters of Meribah; Who said unto his father and to his mother, I have not seen him; neither did he acknowledge his brethren, nor knew his own children: for they have observed thy word, and kept thy covenant. They shall teach Jacob thy judgments, and Israel thy law: they shall put incense before thee, and whole burnt sacrifice upon thine altar. Bless, LORD, his substance, and accept the work of his hands: smite through the loins of them that rise against him, and of them that hate him, that they rise not again.","ref":"Deuteronomy 33:8-11"},{"kind":"para","text":"Moses' blessing to Simeon reads: ...nothing. Moses didn't give a blessing to Simeon. We will talk about that more in a moment. These two killed a whole town if you don't remember, because a man from there raped their sister. It was an interesting set of events. Jacob points out that this was wrong. They are instruments of cruelty."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We will see that the Levites will certainly be scattered throughout the land. They will not inherit any land when the tribes enter in. However, they have a promise from the Lord to inherit Him. That is far better than inheriting land in my personal opinion. I will say, having a cursing from your father is not a very fun thing to receive. Anger is a very big problem that will be addressed extensively throughout Scripture. It's a problem that the Israelites will face over and over again, and it's a problem that Christians face today. We are told to be angry and \"sin not.\""},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Be ye angry, and sin not: let not the sun go down upon your wrath:","ref":"Ephesians 4:26"},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know of many (if any) who are able to be angry and not sin. Usually when you are angry you are not thinking straight. That is the problem with these boys. Their anger is providing them with a curse. Simeon will be basically swallowed up by Judah when the land is divided. The curse that is spoken about here remains true. Levi is scattered, and Simeon will be separated from them. Once Simeon is surrounded by Judah, Judah will eventually (seemingly anyway) disperse this group and they will be scattered throughout that land. Interestingly enough, Simeon's tribe will not be mentioned in Deuteronomy 33. I assume because they are mostly dwindled at that point anyway. There wasn't much for them to inherit. Joshua gives some land to them in Judah, but that won't last forever."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":12,"title":"Judah's Praise","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Moses' blessing to Judah reads:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And this is the blessing of Judah: and he said, Hear, LORD, the voice of Judah, and bring him unto his people: let his hands be sufficient for him; and be thou an help to him from his enemies.","ref":"Deuteronomy 33:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"Christ is coming through this tribe. That is why it's important that we pay attention to them. If we are going to pay attention to any of the tribes, it should be this one first. We might read through scripture twelve times and pay attention to one tribe at a time when we do it, but if you are going to pay attention to a tribe this first read through, it should be the tribe of Judah. They will be a leader in constant battle with Israel's enemies, and it will be through them that the great kings of Israel will come out of."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Lions will be a picture of Judah's strength over and over again throughout scripture. It will be the reference that is made to Christ as well!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And one of the elders saith unto me, Weep not: behold, the Lion of the tribe of Juda, the Root of David, hath prevailed to open the book, and to loose the seven seals thereof.","ref":"Revelation 5:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"He is a growing lion, and in the end he will be a victorious lion, but it is going to take some battles to get there (and my, how Israel and this tribe battle throughout the next few thousand years)."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a fantastic prophecy given here. \"Shiloh\" means \"that which belongs to him.\" It's the ruler of the place. That would be Jesus Christ. Jesus will gather His people in those days. Until then, Judah will be the lawgiving and law-keeping nation, and it will be the tribe with the sceptre rule. They will be in charge."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Shiloh\" also means \"tranquillity.\" Christ will be the one that brings rest to His children. He will bring rest to the tribes when He comes. This promise that Judah is given here has been a promise that is passed down for generations now. It was passed down from Adam to Seth, Seth to Noah, Noah to Shem, Shem to Abraham, Abraham to Isaac, and Isaac to Jacob. Jacob now takes that promise and passes it onto Judah. Judah is not innocent, by the way. He has made some terrible mistakes as well. We got a whole chapter dedicated to his mistakes. But Jacob chooses this particular son for this promise, and I believe it is through the leading of the Lord that he chooses him."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is absolutely crazy what he is saying here. This is another prophecy of Christ yet again. Christ is the one that’s riding on a donkey. His garments are washed in his blood, that is what the wine is likely referring to. Wine stains do not come out easily. Neither do blood stains. You say, \"That's a bit of a stretch, how can we associate wine with blood?\" Well, scripture does it quite a bit. They do it in Revelation:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the winepress was trodden without the city, and blood came out of the winepress, even unto the horse bridles, by the space of a thousand and six hundred furlongs.","ref":"Revelation 14:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"And in Isaiah:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherefore art thou red in thine apparel, and thy garments like him that treadeth in the winefat?","ref":"Isaiah 63:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"And in the gospels for the Lord's last supper:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For this is my blood of the new testament, which is shed for many for the remission of sins. But I say unto you, I will not drink henceforth of this fruit of the vine, until that day when I drink it new with you in my Father's kingdom.","ref":"Matthew 26:28-29"},{"kind":"para","text":"It would not be much of a stretch to assume that this is speaking about the blood of Christ here."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"title":"Down By The Sea","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Moses' blessing to Zebulun (Issachar is included with this one as well) reads:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And of Zebulun he said, Rejoice, Zebulun, in thy going out; and, Issachar, in thy tents. They shall call the people unto the mountain; there they shall offer sacrifices of righteousness: for they shall suck of the abundance of the seas, and of treasures hid in the sand.","ref":"Deuteronomy 33:18-19"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is true. We will see Zebulun living on the \"haven of the sea.\" They will possess some land along the shoreline of the coast north in Israel. Why that was chosen for this tribe, I could not say. They would be a very useful tribe for trades on the sea though, and that was a guarantee from Jacob. Maybe they were already good at making trades, and so Jacob used what he saw in his son to bless him further in the future?"}]}]},{"start":14,"end":15,"title":"The Stubborn Bearers","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well, this is not something I would like to be called by my father. He is calling Issachar stubborn. They were strong, though, and so they would use their strength to bless the nation of Israel."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The tribe of Issachar will be useful for this backbreaking labor. They will be responsible for some pretty serious and heavy lifting. We will see that to be true. It is interesting that we don't pay too much attention to this tribe when we read. It is easy to miss them, honestly. But they will be the heavy lifters for Israel."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":18,"title":"Dan Is Judge","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Moses' blessing to Dan reads:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And of Dan he said, Dan is a lion's whelp: he shall leap from Bashan.","ref":"Deuteronomy 33:22"},{"kind":"para","text":"It is (in fact) Samson that comes from this tribe of Dan. He will be a pretty significant judge. When we think of the book of Judges, we usually think about Samson first. He wasn't even the first judge, and yet he is the most remembered. I suppose because he is the most ridiculous."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Dan will become a problem though later on. They will be very much against the workings of the Lord. They will be a major cause of turning the children of Israel away from God. I believe that is why Jacob says this next:"}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They should be praying for the Lord's salvation. We will see in Revelation, that this tribe is not mentioned as part of the one hundred and forty-four thousand saved in the end."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Of the tribe of Juda were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Reuben were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Gad were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Aser were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Nepthalim were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Manasses were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Simeon were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Levi were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Issachar were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Zabulon were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Joseph were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Benjamin were sealed twelve thousand.","ref":"Revelation 7:5-8"},{"kind":"para","text":"That is an interesting thought. Why are they left out? Well, I don't know. I assume it has more to do with the fact that they were a lost tribe. They were a tribe that did not desire to turn to the Lord. They were very prominent in going against God about everything and helping as many as possible do the same thing. I don't believe those numbers and tribes are literal if I can be honest, but they are significant for a reason. I think they are a demonstration of the modern church today. We can talk more about that in Revelation."}]}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"title":"Gad Overcomes","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Moses' blessing to Gad reads:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And of Gad he said, Blessed be he that enlargeth Gad: he dwelleth as a lion, and teareth the arm with the crown of the head. And he provided the first part for himself, because there, in a portion of the lawgiver, was he seated; and he came with the heads of the people, he executed the justice of the LORD, and his judgments with Israel.","ref":"Deuteronomy 33:20-21"},{"kind":"para","text":"We will see Gad be struck quite a few times during our study of scripture. But they will be a tribe that helps Israel quite immensely."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"These were of the sons of Gad, captains of the host: one of the least was over an hundred, and the greatest over a thousand.","ref":"1 Chronicles 12:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is an interesting look at our Christian life if I can be honest. We will enter into our walks with God in hopeful adoration, knowing that we can stand with the Lord, and we will be taken down a few times. But we will always overcome at the last thanks to the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ!"}]}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"title":"Asher's Bread","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The promise from Moses reads:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And of Asher he said, Let Asher be blessed with children; let him be acceptable to his brethren, and let him dip his foot in oil.","ref":"Deuteronomy 33:24"},{"kind":"para","text":"Asher's land will provide the Israelites with some luxuries that they may have never thought they would get before. It is through these blessed luxuries that will ultimately lead to Israel's downfall. They depend too much on the providence and not enough on the provider. They think they are completely okay without the Lord because they have the tribe of Asher to provide some bread for them. That is what happens with the modern man and money. They lean on their finances and forsake the providence of God. Eventually, your providence will let you down. Your provider never will, though."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"title":"Goodly Words","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Moses' blessing to Naphtali reads:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And of Naphtali he said, O Naphtali, satisfied with favour, and full with the blessing of the LORD: possess thou the west and the south.","ref":"Deuteronomy 33:23"},{"kind":"para","text":"We will find that Jesus does quite a lot of ministry in this part of the land. It is written largely about in the book of Matthew (which is the book of what Jesus said)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now when Jesus had heard that John was cast into prison, he departed into Galilee; And leaving Nazareth, he came and dwelt in Capernaum, which is upon the sea coast, in the borders of Zabulon and Nephthalim: That it might be fulfilled which was spoken by Esaias the prophet, saying, The land of Zabulon, and the land of Nephthalim, by the way of the sea, beyond Jordan, Galilee of the Gentiles; The people which sat in darkness saw great light; and to them which sat in the region and shadow of death light is sprung up.","ref":"Matthew 4:12-16"},{"kind":"para","text":"They should be people of great ministry and preaching. I don't know if they ever have any sort of preachers in the land, but it is proven that they had one excellent preacher at the least."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":26,"title":"Joseph's Fruit","entries":[{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Moses' blessing to Joseph reads:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And of Joseph he said, Blessed of the LORD be his land, for the precious things of heaven, for the dew, and for the deep that coucheth beneath, And for the precious fruits brought forth by the sun, and for the precious things put forth by the moon, And for the chief things of the ancient mountains, and for the precious things of the lasting hills, And for the precious things of the earth and fulness thereof, and for the good will of him that dwelt in the bush: let the blessing come upon the head of Joseph, and upon the top of the head of him that was separated from his brethren. His glory is like the firstling of his bullock, and his horns are like the horns of unicorns: with them he shall push the people together to the ends of the earth: and they are the ten thousands of Ephraim, and they are the thousands of Manasseh.","ref":"Deuteronomy 33:13-17"},{"kind":"para","text":"Interesting enough, the children of Joseph will develop into a land that will later be known as Samaria. It will be a land of Gentiles, and the Jews will not be very fond of them. In fact, they will have a disdain for the Samaritans. Crazier is that it will be \"by a well\" that Jesus first reaches out to this land of Samaria. Joseph's blessing is a blessing of fruit. I am so thankful that the Gentiles have received salvation. For that is the camp I belong in."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Their own people will be against them eventually. They will be shooting bows at them. They will have a lot of power at that point though. So much so that they will cause a division in the land. That is the division we see when the Lord starts His ministry on Earth. But God is the strength of that land. He's the strength of all lands today. If a land is not prospering, it is because they don't have the Lord as their strength. They must turn to Him in trust and faith through Jesus Christ, and their land will prosper."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't mean to sound insensitive when I say this (and I am going to (no doubt) catch flak for it, but I can't help myself), but I truly believe a poor nation can prosper if they would trust the Lord. All they have to do is turn to Him, and He will prosper and provide miraculously above and beyond what they thought was even possible. That's the theme of history, and it’s factually and scientifically proven."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God will bless these people. God has blessed these people. We see God blessed them by the fact that they were still around during the ministry of Christ. Christ did a good portion of His ministry in that land. We are the benefactors of that blessing if you are saved today. Praise the Lord for the blessing of Joseph!"}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Progenitors\" means \"become pregnant\" or \"to conceive.\" They will be blessed by God and included in the family if they just come to Him. God accepts any and every person that genuinely turns to Him by faith. All we must do is simply turn to Him. We must simply believe in His Son, and we are grafted into the family of God. These boys of Joseph were Egyptians, remember. They are adopted in. The Samaritans will be adopted into God's family too. WE are adopted in if we would simply believe. We must follow the Lord and trust in His salvation. This is a message of pleading to anyone reading that doesn't know of their salvation. You can come to God today if you just turn to Him. That is all the Lord wants, and it is my heart’s desire for you as well."}]}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"title":"Benjamin The Wolf","entries":[{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The blessing from Moses reads:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And of Benjamin he said, The beloved of the LORD shall dwell in safety by him; and the LORD shall cover him all the day long, and he shall dwell between his shoulders.","ref":"Deuteronomy 33:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"Benjamin has some secrets, I think. We don't get too much of a picture of Benjamin. Maybe he is a tame lad, and Jacob is speaking about the future dwellings of this tribe. I don't think so. I mean, he certainly is, but I also think that Benjamin was a very hardened man. We will see that a lot of hardened men come from this tribe."},{"kind":"para","text":"Ehud \"But when the children of Israel cried unto the LORD, the LORD raised them up a deliverer, Ehud the son of Gera, a Benjamite, a man lefthanded: and by him the children of Israel sent a present unto Eglon the king of Moab. But Ehud made him a dagger which had two edges, of a cubit length; and he did gird it under his raiment upon his right thigh. And he brought the present unto Eglon king of Moab: and Eglon was a very fat man. And when he had made an end to offer the present, he sent away the people that bare the present. But he himself turned again from the quarries that were by Gilgal, and said, I have a secret errand unto thee, O king: who said, Keep silence. And all that stood by him went out from him. And Ehud came unto him; and he was sitting in a summer parlour, which he had for himself alone. And Ehud said, I have a message from God unto thee. And he arose out of his seat. And Ehud put forth his left hand, and took the dagger from his right thigh, and thrust it into his belly: and the haft also went in after the blade; and the fat closed upon the blade, so that he could not draw the dagger out of his belly; and the dirt came out. Then Ehud went forth through the porch, and shut the doors of the parlour upon him, and locked them.\" Judges 3:15-23"},{"kind":"para","text":"Saul \"Now there was a man of Benjamin, whose name was Kish, the son of Abiel, the son of Zeror, the son of Bechorath, the son of Aphiah, a Benjamite, a mighty man of power.\" 1 Samuel 9:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul \"And Saul was consenting unto his death. And at that time there was a great persecution against the church which was at Jerusalem; and they were all scattered abroad throughout the regions of Judaea and Samaria, except the apostles. And devout men carried Stephen to his burial, and made great lamentation over him. As for Saul, he made havock of the church, entering into every house, and haling men and women committed them to prison.\" Acts 8:1-3"},{"kind":"para","text":"You can read a bit deeper about their countenance in Judges 19 and 20. It's also a bit strange, seeing as Benjamin was the closest related to Joseph. We will see this tribe stick very close to Judah, which is the tribe we will place a lot of focus on as we study. We will find Benjamin is always around and siding with Judah through every situation."}]}]},{"start":28,"end":33,"title":"Jacob Has Finished","entries":[{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They have all received their blessings now. They were not going to argue over who gets what. It was final. What Jacob says to them is set in stone. It will be provenly set in stone in the future (we should know that at this point). God will keep these blessings and promises throughout the generations to come. He will honor His word here."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob has already made it clear that he wants to be buried there. His children will keep this promise (we will study that better in the next chapter). He has made it a very trying matter that he is to be buried back in this land that they owed. He is assured that one day he will be raised to live in that land again. He believed there would be an Earthly resurrection. Will there be one? I don't really know."},{"kind":"para","text":"Some have said different things in speculation without cold, hard facts about it. I know there will be a Heavenly resurrection. Our souls will raise again. An Earthly one seems a bit like a strange thing to take place, but I don't put anything against the Lord if there is proof to back that up. We know we will certainly return with the Lord when He comes to Earth for His second coming. Whether that is in body or in Spirit, doesn't matter to me. As long as I am by the Lord's side, I am content."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For since by man came death, by man came also the resurrection of the dead. For as in Adam all die, even so in Christ shall all be made alive. But every man in his own order: Christ the firstfruits; afterward they that are Christ's at his coming.","ref":"1 Corinthians 15:21-23"}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob was resting assured that God would keep His promises. He had seen glimpses of what God can do, and He was trusting that God WILL do the rest. He won't be here to see it, but he will be in paradise with the Lord. He will be living up there and waiting for the Lord to continue to move forward. He is up there today rejoicing in the Savior. He is satisfied with the knowledge that God has kept His promises already. He is ready to see the Lord finish His work. We are all ready for it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For we know that the whole creation groaneth and travaileth in pain together until now. And not only they, but ourselves also, which have the firstfruits of the Spirit, even we ourselves groan within ourselves, waiting for the adoption, to wit, the redemption of our body.","ref":"Romans 8:22-23"},{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob trusts God by faith at this point. He believes the promises of God and gives those promises to his sons. Now it's time for him to finish his fight and lay down. When he finally does, it is when he passes into a better promised land."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":33}]},
    "50": {"chapter":50,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Jacob was giving the final blessings to his children after giving blessings to Joseph's children. He is close to his death, and he wants to solidify some things before he passes on. These promises and blessings can be traced through scripture as being accurate and true. We will study these tribes further as we go along (they are used as an example for things to come in the New Testament), and we will see how these promises effect their future."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we get the burial of Jacob, and the life of the Israelites after his passing. This final chapter is a pretty heavy one, so I want to try and bring as much levity to it as possible without completely shying away from their hurts. Hopefully we can join these men in mourning for the loss of Jacob and the life continued after."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"Embalming Israel","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I couldn't imagine this moment for Joseph. This had to be hard. Twenty years was not enough for Joseph. It was enough for Jacob, however. He was ready to be home with the Lord. So many lost years in the eyes of Joseph, though. He could have spent a better portion of his life with his father, and he missed it all over the sin of his brothers. Of course, he is not going to hold that against them. He now knows it was all for a purpose, but it still had to be hard to not allow bitterness to root into his life. He is a great forgiver."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know if I could have forgiven them if I were in his shoes. Perhaps my spirit would have been different, though. Maybe I would have been more prone to forgiveness. All I know is that this is a touching scene, and it has been such a long and dramatic journey with this family."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Egyptians had the ability to embalm a body. I don't know if any of the other bodies were done in this manner in this family’s past. I doubt it. They didn't have the ability to do it, but Egypt was rich, and they embalmed their people. We can see that their embalming was effective even still today. There are mummies that are fully preserved because of this. It is likely that we could find Jacob’s body buried in those caves today. His body might still be intact. I suppose we won't know, though, because that is all very dangerous territory to walk around on."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Forty days to embalm a body is a very tedious process. There are a lot of “forty-day\" moments in scripture that should be taken into consideration. It takes forty days for Moses to pray before the Lord about the people in the wilderness."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he was there with the LORD forty days and forty nights; he did neither eat bread, nor drink water. And he wrote upon the tables the words of the covenant, the ten commandments.","ref":"Exodus 34:28"},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus fasted for forty days before HE entered the wilderness."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when he had fasted forty days and forty nights, he was afterward an hungred.","ref":"Matthew 4:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus also showed himself for forty days after he resurrected."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The former treatise have I made, O Theophilus, of all that Jesus began both to do and teach, Until the day in which he was taken up, after that he through the Holy Ghost had given commandments unto the apostles whom he had chosen: To whom also he shewed himself alive after his passion by many infallible proofs, being seen of them forty days, and speaking of the things pertaining to the kingdom of God:","ref":"Acts 1:1-3"},{"kind":"para","text":"There are other accounts of fasts for forty days, and forty-day journeys, and forty-day changes. It's obvious that God utilizes forty days to make a difference. It will likely take about forty days to actually make a difference for something even still today."},{"kind":"para","text":"When I worked in construction, there were times when my dad would get a call from someone about something they didn't like that we had built. They asked for it that way, and we showed them what it would look like in the design, and they signed off and approved the design before we actually started making it. It would take a while before we actually got to the making process. Once it was completed, they called my dad and said, \"We have been looking at it, and we are not sure if we really like it.\" They either don't like the placement of it, the design of it, or the color of it usually. My dad would always say, \"Give it thirty days, and if you still don't like it, you can call me back, and we will come and change it.\" I don't think anyone has ever called my dad back after the thirty days were up."},{"kind":"para","text":"Forty days is a solidified standard. If you can make it to forty days, you can make it to a lifetime. It takes forty days to \"embalm\" Jacob. \"Embalm\" means \"preserve.\" Forty days to embalm Jacob. A lifetime to preserve Israel. It was worth every second spent in the eyes of both Egypt and Joseph because it was going to preserve the body of Jacob (who assumed he would raise again in that land). Joseph was respecting his father's last wish and preparing to transfer him back to his homeland to be buried where he had asked to be buried. But it's interesting that the Egyptians mourned for him too. He must have touched the hearts of many, or they just very highly respected Joseph and could not bear to keep a strong countenance when Joseph was so terribly hurting."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":6,"title":"Let My People Go","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph is asking to let his people go to bury their father. He is asking Pharaoh. That's an important note to make, because the next Pharaoh will be asked a similar question and the outcome will be vastly different. Joseph has favor with this Pharaoh, and it should be no problem for him to go and bury his dead father. Even this Pharaoh had to have sympathy for him. This is also a good reminder to us that Joseph is still not free. Just because he has his family back doesn't make him free from Egypt. He will be enslaved to Egypt for the rest of his life. Thankfully, the Lord allows favor with this Pharaoh; otherwise, the outcome would be a lot like what we will experience in Exodus (spoiler alert)."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Pharaoh didn't even have to think about it. He knew that this was the needful thing to do. Joseph is going to keep his word anyway. Pharaoh is trusting a slave to go away and come again. He is trusting that Joseph is not going to run for the hills to never return. Joseph is a man of his word."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":14,"title":"Burying Jacob","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well, if Joseph didn't return, their little ones would be the ones to bear the burden of that. There is a whole procession going with them, though. There are servants of Pharaoh, and elders of his (Joseph's) house, and all the elders of Egypt, and then Joseph's brothers, as well as Joseph's sons, and his wife, and their wives were all going with them to bury Jacob. This is quite the funeral. You can tell a lot about whether a man was loved based on the number of people who attend his funeral. Jacob was a man that was loved. This proves that."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Seven days of weeping and mourning over this man, Jacob. They were sad, to say the least. It was a seven-day-long funeral. We have a funeral for one day, and everyone goes home, and the family who lost their loved one sits in silence, thinking about their life with whom they just lost. These people stuck together and mourned for seven days straight, and didn't complain about it a single time. They truly loved this man. It was a \"great and very sore lamentation.\" It had to be some pretty intense weeping happening here."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Abelmizraim\" means \"meadow of Egypt.\" The Canaanites even felt sympathy for this funeral— that's how enormous this weeping was. Jacob lived in the land of the Canaanites for quite a while, and they didn't seem to be affected by Jacob at all. It is this moment of his funeral that they suddenly take notice. Even then, they are not affected other than to note that \"Egypt\" was mourning. It wasn't just Egypt mourning this day, though. It was the Hebrews as well. It was their near kinsmen. If only they knew these Israelites better, it could have made a world of difference for what is to come for them later on. But I am getting ahead of myself."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They owned this land. It was theirs to bury their people. Jacob was resting with the rest of the Patriarchs in his lineage. He was resting in a land that no one else could take from them. They owned it, and it was their rightful place to bury their loved ones. It was where he deserved to be buried. It's important that we note the unity taking place between the children of Israel here. It's a theme we must pay attention to throughout the rest of our reading of Scripture. God unites His people. Always. All the time. For all of history. Someday, when this world is far gone, we will all be united with those that have already passed in the clouds with the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then we which are alive and remain shall be caught up together with them in the clouds, to meet the Lord in the air: and so shall we ever be with the Lord.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 4:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"That is where we will remain. We will remain forever in unity. This is the start of their unity journey here. They are unified to keep a promise to their father. They will unify again later on to keep the promise of the Lord, and they will unify again when they have a good king, and again when the Savior has come. Today, they are not united except for one thing. They are united to hate the Lord Jesus Christ, and blaspheme His precious name. I pray that they don't stay that way. I pray that they come to know Christ and unite with the body of believers in Christ. We can unite with them. They will have a bigger family with bigger blessings."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph kept his word and returned back to Egypt. Where else was he going to go? There was still plenty to build up back in Egypt. Eventually, though, they would have to return to this land. They would have to take the promise of Jacob. They would have to obey the promise that God had given to them. They would have to do it by faith."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":21,"title":"God Meant It For Good","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems they are not very confident in Joseph. They think he was lying about his compassion and forgiveness for them this entire time. They think there is nothing holding Joseph back from letting them have it now. That is the problem with sin, by the way. Sin will make you feel guilty forever. You must learn to accept the forgiveness of the Lord before you can accept forgiveness from others. That is the only way to truly battle sin. God has forgiven you, and that is a good reason to resist the flesh."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He wept for two reasons more than likely (possibly even three). The first being that they didn't believe his forgiveness was genuine. He felt hurt by the fact that they couldn't just accept that Joseph wanted to start fresh with them. The second being that they sort of lied about this moment here. They say it was their \"father\" that wanted to relay this message, when it was really THEM that wanted to relay it. They could have just been honest about it. The third being that he is weeping out of compassion. That is how the Lord wept, by the way."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Jesus wept. Then said the Jews, Behold how he loved him!","ref":"John 11:35-36"},{"kind":"para","text":"All they had to do was speak to him in his face, and he would have been more than willing to forgive them over and over again. I think Joseph has proven how serious his forgiveness was. Yet, their sin is keeping them from fully accepting it."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They are truly repentant here, and it is showing. I don't know if they were ever truly repentant before, but they are seriously desiring forgiveness for what they have done. Joseph is a compassionate man. He is a forgiving man. He is a great representation of who Christ is. Christ died on the cross to forgive you and me of our sins. We sinned against Him specifically, and yet He forgives us by giving His life for us. Joseph has already forgiven them. There was a purpose for the events that unfolded, and Joseph has not forgotten it."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"No, Joseph is not in the place of God. He is not God, and He cannot damn them or judge them. He knows that they are genuine in their desire to be forgiven, and he tells them to \"fear not.\" They don't need to be afraid of what he might do, because Joseph is not going to abuse his own power against his own brothers. He wouldn't do that to anyone, let alone his own family. The next verse is the most powerful one, though."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Take this into consideration when you read this. Jesus Christ has this same message. We thought evil against the Lord, but God meant it for good to bring to pass today and save as many people as possible. We meant it for evil, but God meant it for good. The religionists meant to put the Lord on the cross for evil, but God meant the death of His son for good. Joseph's brothers thought to do evil to Joseph, but God allowed it to happen for something far greater than their sin. God used the children of Israel's sin to save people. That is a very interesting thing. He used the sin of the world to prove His love on the cross. He uses evil to prove His own good. I can't even fathom that."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He gave them kind words of comfort. Joseph wanted them to truly know that he had forgiven them. He would not hold onto bitterness for what they had done. He was willing to forgive at the drop of a hat. He forgave them even before they showed up at his doorstep begging for food. He had to physically forgive them to their faces at that point. Now he is solidifying his forgiveness for them. He is making it abundantly clear that it was finished. Their debt had been paid, and there was nothing they had to worry about except to believe that he was telling the truth. They just had to trust him."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":26,"title":"A Promise of Deliverance","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph had a good, long life, even to see the third generation in his family. He was able to see his great-great-grandchildren. He is growing old now, and it's time to pass on to freedom in paradise. He will be getting to go see his father again and live with him for all of eternity. He had never thought to see his dad again. They were reunited, and then separated once more. Now they will be reunited for all of eternity, never to be separated again."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joseph will be buried in the promised land. He will not stay here. He belongs in the land of Canaan. It will be some time before his bones are buried in that land because they will be stuck here for quite some time, but it will be guaranteed that his bones will be buried with the rest of his relatives in the land that they own. At this time, however, we have a send-off for Joseph, and this is how Genesis ends."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have experienced so much through this book and have gotten to know this family of Israel quite well. It is a bittersweet ending, and there is still plenty more for us to study. This book has been a rollercoaster, though, starting off with the first sin of the world and the effects that it has had on everyone's life since then. We are still feeling the effects of sin today. But there is a hopeful conclusion. Thankfully, we don't just get the end of Israel right here. Joseph gives a blessing to them by promising that God is not done with them yet. He promises that God will take them out of this land and bring them to the land they are promised by the Lord. God will do that. It will just take some time before He does."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":26}]},
  },
};
